window.DOMINANCE_TOPICS = [{"slug":"cv-introduction","title":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"1-6","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system","title":"1. Why do we need a circulatory system?"},{"id":"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways","title":"2. The two major circulatory pathways"},{"id":"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers","title":"3. The heart and its four chambers"},{"id":"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right","title":"4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?"},{"id":"5-systole-and-diastole","title":"5. Systole and diastole"},{"id":"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system","title":"6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system"},{"id":"7-understanding-check","title":"7. Understanding Check"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Introduction to the Heart and Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system\">1. Why do we need a circulatory system?</h2>\n<p>Every cell in the body needs a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients. At the same time it keeps making waste, including carbon dioxide, which must be carried away.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can't cells simply rely on diffusion?</strong> Over very short distances, diffusion is fast: oxygen crosses the few micrometres from a capillary to a nearby cell in a fraction of a second. But the time diffusion takes grows with the <strong>square</strong> of the distance. Moving oxygen 1 mm by diffusion alone takes minutes, and 1 cm takes hours.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Distance</th>\n  <th>Rough time for oxygen to diffuse</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>10 µm (capillary to a nearby cell)</td>\n  <td>about 0.02 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>1 mm</td>\n  <td>a few minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>1 cm</td>\n  <td>several hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A body many centimetres thick could never be supplied that way. So the body uses <strong><span data-term=\"bulk-flow\">bulk flow</span></strong> instead. The heart pumps blood quickly through vessels to within a few micrometres of almost every cell, and diffusion only has to cover that last tiny step. The lecture sums up the heart's job simply: to move the blood about, sending it to all the tissues.</p>\n<p>The <strong>circulatory system</strong> has three parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> the pump.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood vessels:</strong> the pipes that carry blood to every tissue and back.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood:</strong> the carrier. It transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a tissue's needs rise, the circulation responds. Working muscle during exercise receives more blood, which brings extra oxygen and removes extra carbon dioxide.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Diffusion times are approximate (time ≈ distance² ÷ 2D, with D for oxygen in water). They are foundation material added to explain why a pump is needed. They are not on the lecture slides.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">2. The two major circulatory pathways</h2>\n<p>Blood travels through two circuits, one after the other:</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> The right side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs. There, carbon dioxide leaves the blood and oxygen enters it. The oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> The left side of the heart pumps oxygen-rich blood to the tissues of the whole body. The tissues take up oxygen and add carbon dioxide, and the blood returns to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-01\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size pulmonary and systemic circulation diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of systemic and pulmonary circulation, showing blood travelling between the right heart and lungs, then the left heart and body organs.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>The two circuits are <span data-term=\"in-series\">in series</span>.</strong> Blood must pass through one circuit before it can enter the other: body → right heart → lungs → left heart → body. There is no other route.</p>\n<p><strong>Why must both ventricles pump the same amount?</strong> Because every drop the right ventricle sends through the lungs arrives at the left ventricle. Over any period longer than a few beats, the right and left ventricles must pump the same volume per minute: about 5 L/min each at rest. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more than the left for a long time, blood would pile up in the lungs. If the left pumped more than the right, the lungs would slowly drain. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">→ Cardiac Output, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Within the systemic circuit, the organs sit side by side (<span data-term=\"in-parallel\">in parallel</span>).</strong> The aorta divides into branches that supply the brain, the heart muscle, the gut and liver, the kidneys, the skeletal muscles and the skin. Because each organ has its own branch:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>every organ receives fresh, oxygen-rich blood from the arteries, not blood another organ has already used;</li>\n<li>the flow to each organ can be turned up or down on its own, by narrowing or widening that organ's small arteries, without starving the others. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">→ Haemodynamics, §8</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lungs are the exception: all of the blood passes through them, in series.</p>\n<p><strong>The heart feeds itself the same way.</strong> The heart does not take its oxygen from the blood inside its chambers. Its muscular wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers to most of its cells. So the heart muscle has its own parallel branch from the aorta: the <strong><span data-term=\"coronary-arteries\">coronary arteries</span></strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\">→ Coronary Circulation, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"circuit-map\"><figcaption>Series and parallel</figcaption><p>One loop runs right heart → lungs → left heart → aorta, labelled in series. The aorta then splits into side-by-side branches for the brain, the heart muscle (coronary arteries), the gut and liver, the kidneys, skeletal muscle and the skin, labelled in parallel. The branches rejoin in the veins and return to the right heart. Gut and liver are drawn as one branch because blood from the gut passes through the liver.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture gives about 5.5 L/min for each side of the heart; 5 L/min is also widely quoted. The parallel arrangement of organs is foundation material for the regional-circulation chapters.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\">3. The heart and its four chambers</h2>\n<p>The heart is a muscular organ that works as <strong>two pumps side by side</strong>. The right heart pumps blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps blood to the body. Each pump has two chambers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right atrium:</strong> receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae.</li>\n<li><strong>Right ventricle:</strong> pumps that blood into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs.</li>\n<li><strong>Left atrium:</strong> receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Left ventricle:</strong> pumps that blood into the aorta, which carries it to the whole body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>atria</strong> mainly receive blood and top up the ventricles. The <strong>ventricles</strong> do the main pumping: they generate the pressure that drives blood into the arteries.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-02\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size labelled heart anatomy diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" alt=\"Sectional anatomy of the human heart showing four chambers, cardiac valves, great vessels, interventricular septum, and the thicker left ventricular wall.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>How does blood flow through the heart?</strong> Follow the path:</p>\n<p><strong>Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What keeps blood moving one way?</strong> Four valves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>inlet valves</strong> sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the <strong>tricuspid</strong> valve on the right and the <strong>mitral</strong> valve on the left. Together they are the <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV (atrioventricular) valves</span>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>outlet valves</strong> sit between each ventricle and its artery: the <strong>pulmonary</strong> valve on the right and the <strong>aortic</strong> valve on the left. Together they are the <span data-term=\"semilunar-valves\">semilunar valves</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Valves contain no muscle. They open and shut <strong>passively</strong>, pushed by blood pressure. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts the moment that reverses. So blood can only move forward. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Arteries and veins are named by direction, not by oxygen.</strong> The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. This is because <strong>arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood towards it</strong>, whatever its oxygen content.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why can't diffusion alone supply the cells of the body?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Diffusion stops working once blood starts to flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Oxygen cannot cross cell membranes without a pump</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Cells use oxygen faster than any blood flow can bring it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Diffusion time rises steeply as the distance grows</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance. Micrometres take a fraction of a second, but centimetres take hours. The circulation carries blood close to every cell, so diffusion only covers the last few micrometres.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> For a week, a person's right ventricle pumps slightly more blood per minute than the left. Where does the extra blood collect?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the systemic veins, which hold most of the blood volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the pulmonary vessels, between the two ventricles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the right atrium, because the tricuspid valve shuts</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the aorta, which stretches to store the extra volume</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The circuits are in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps goes through the lungs to the left heart. If the left ventricle sends on less than arrives, the difference builds up in the lung vessels.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does the brain still receive oxygen-rich blood when the leg muscles are working hard?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood reaches the brain before it reaches the leg muscles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The brain extracts oxygen faster than the leg muscles can</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Each organ has its own branch from the aorta, side by side</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart pumps a separate circuit just for the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Systemic organs are supplied in parallel. The brain's branch carries fresh arterial blood, not blood the muscles have already used. Flow to each organ can also be adjusted separately.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A vessel carries oxygen-rich blood towards the heart. What is it?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A pulmonary vein, because it carries blood towards the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A pulmonary artery, because its blood is rich in oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A systemic artery, because oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A coronary artery, because it supplies the heart muscle</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Vessels are named by direction. Veins carry blood towards the heart, and the pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\">4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?</h2>\n<p>Both ventricles pump the same volume each minute (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">§2</a>). Yet the left ventricle's wall is two to three times thicker than the right's. The difference is <strong>pressure, not volume</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What sets the pressure a pump must make?</strong> Blood flows only when there is a pressure difference to push it. The more the vessels oppose flow (their <strong><span data-term=\"resistance\">resistance</span></strong>), the bigger the pressure difference needed to keep the same flow. The lecture puts it as: flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=2-what-actually-determines-flow\">→ Haemodynamics, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> a vast network of small, muscular arteries and arterioles, so total resistance is high. The left ventricle must reach about <strong>120 mmHg</strong> with each beat.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> short, wide, thin-walled vessels, so resistance is low. The right ventricle needs only about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Generating more pressure takes more muscle, so the left ventricular wall is thicker. <strong>Same volume per minute, different pressure.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why is it useful for the lungs to have low pressure?</strong> Lung capillaries are extremely thin, so that gases can cross them quickly. High pressure inside them would push fluid out into the lung tissue and air spaces. This is <strong><span data-term=\"pulmonary-oedema\">pulmonary oedema</span></strong>, and it gets in the way of gas exchange. Low pulmonary pressure keeps the lungs dry. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">→ Microcirculation, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-systole-and-diastole\">5. Systole and diastole</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">Systole</span></strong> means contraction and <strong>diastole</strong> means relaxation. Always say which chambers you mean, because the atria and ventricles contract at different times.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> the atria contract and push the final portion of blood into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial diastole:</strong> the atria relax and fill with blood returning from the body and lungs.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole:</strong> the ventricles contract and eject blood into the pulmonary trunk (right) and the aorta (left).</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular diastole:</strong> the ventricles relax and fill for the next beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The order matters.</strong> The atria contract <strong>at the end of ventricular diastole</strong>, just before the ventricles contract. By then, most ventricular filling (about 70% in the lecture) has already happened passively. Atrial contraction only tops it up.</p>\n<p>At a resting heart rate, one beat lasts about 0.8 s. The ventricles spend about 0.3 s in systole and 0.5 s in diastole. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system\">6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system</h2>\n<p>To pump well, the heart needs two things: <strong>force</strong> and <strong>timing</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Force: cardiac muscle (myocardium).</strong> Cardiac muscle cells are electrically linked to one another. An impulse therefore spreads from cell to cell, and the muscle of each chamber contracts as one unit. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">→ Cardiac Muscle, §2</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Timing: the conducting system.</strong> The impulse starts in the <strong><span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span></strong>, at the junction of the superior vena cava and the right atrium. It spreads across the atria to the <strong><span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span></strong>, which holds it back briefly. It then races down the <strong>bundle of His</strong> and the <strong><span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">Purkinje fibres</span></strong> through both ventricles. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §2</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture notes that every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. The SA node normally sets the pace because it fires <strong>fastest</strong>, so it triggers the rest before they can fire themselves. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Electrical activation always comes first; contraction follows.</strong> The order in which the impulse spreads sets the order of contraction: the atria first, then, after the AV-node delay, the ventricles.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Lecture connection: Circulation 1, opening slides (the heart's structure and function, conducting tissues, systole and diastole). The diffusion explanation and the parallel arrangement of organs are added foundations.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Both ventricles eject about 70 mL per beat. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It must eject a larger volume of blood each minute</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It must generate higher pressure against higher resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It contracts for longer because the aortic valve is smaller</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It must also pump blood through the coronary arteries first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The two ventricles move the same volume, but the systemic circulation offers far more resistance. The left ventricle needs about 120 mmHg against the right's 25, and more pressure needs more muscle.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is low pressure in the pulmonary circulation an advantage?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It lets the right ventricle pump less blood than the left</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It slows the blood so that more carbon dioxide can be made</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It stops the pulmonary valve from opening during systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It keeps fluid from being pushed out of the lung capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Thin lung capillaries leak fluid if pressure inside them is high, which causes pulmonary oedema. Low pressure keeps the lungs dry. Both ventricles still pump the same volume.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> During atrial systole, what are the ventricles normally doing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Contracting, so the atria and ventricles squeeze together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Ejecting blood into the aorta and pulmonary trunk at once</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Relaxed, finishing filling just before their own contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Relaxing after ejection, with all four valves still shut</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. They top up the relaxed ventricles just before the ventricles contract; the AV-node delay keeps that order.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why does the SA node normally set the heart rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It fires fastest, so it triggers the others first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is the only part connected to the nervous system</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It lies in the left ventricle, the strongest chamber</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It holds the impulse back so the atria contract first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The fastest pacemaker wins: the SA node fires before the others reach their own firing point. D describes the AV node. The SA node lies in the right atrium.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-understanding-check\">7. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> For several minutes, a patient's left ventricle pumps less than the right ventricle. Where does blood build up first?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the lungs, raising the pressure in the pulmonary vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the leg veins, causing swelling around the ankles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the right ventricle, whose wall thickens to cope</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the pulmonary veins, which then carry oxygen-poor blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The lungs sit between the right and left ventricles. If the left side sends on less than the right side delivers, blood accumulates in the lung vessels first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which statement about the heart valves is correct?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Valves open when their muscle fibres contract</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Valves open and shut because of pressure differences</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Valves are opened by impulses from the AV node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Valves shut as soon as the atria begin to relax</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Valves have no muscle and no nerve control. Pressure behind a valve opens it, and reversed pressure shuts it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood into the pulmonary trunk. Why is the pulmonary trunk still called an artery?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It has a thick muscular wall like the aorta</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It carries blood at the highest pressure in the body</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It carries blood away from the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It carries blood back to the left atrium</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry it towards the heart, whatever the oxygen content.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Diffusion only works over micrometres.</strong> The circulation brings blood within a few micrometres of almost every cell.</li>\n<li><strong>The pulmonary and systemic circuits are in series.</strong> Both ventricles pump the same volume per minute (about 5 L/min at rest). A lasting mismatch makes blood pile up in one circuit.</li>\n<li><strong>The systemic organs are in parallel.</strong> Each gets fresh arterial blood, and flow to each can be adjusted separately. The heart muscle has its own branch: the coronary arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Four chambers:</strong> the atria receive blood and top up the ventricles; the ventricles generate the pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Valves are passive.</strong> They open and shut with pressure differences, keeping flow one-way.</li>\n<li><strong>Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry it towards the heart</strong>, whatever its oxygen content.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle is thicker because it needs more pressure</strong> (about 120 vs 25 mmHg), not because it pumps more volume. Low lung pressure keeps the lungs dry.</li>\n<li><strong>Systole is contraction; diastole is relaxation.</strong> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation (SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje) comes first and sets the order of contraction.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation 1. Why do we need a circulatory system? Every cell in the body needs a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients. At the same time it keeps making waste, including carbon dioxide, which must be carried away. Why can't cells simply rely on diffusion? Over very short distances, diffusion is fast: oxygen crosses the few micrometres from a capillary to a nearby cell in a fraction of a second. But the time diffusion takes grows with the square of the distance. Moving oxygen 1 mm by diffusion alone takes minutes, and 1 cm takes hours. Distance Rough time for oxygen to diffuse 10 µm (capillary to a nearby cell) about 0.02 s 1 mm a few minutes 1 cm several hours A body many centimetres thick could never be supplied that way. So the body uses bulk flow instead. The heart pumps blood quickly through vessels to within a few micrometres of almost every cell, and diffusion only has to cover that last tiny step. The lecture sums up the heart's job simply: to move the blood about, sending it to all the tissues. The circulatory system has three parts: Heart: the pump. Blood vessels: the pipes that carry blood to every tissue and back. Blood: the carrier. It transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat. When a tissue's needs rise, the circulation responds. Working muscle during exercise receives more blood, which brings extra oxygen and removes extra carbon dioxide. Source note: Diffusion times are approximate (time ≈ distance² ÷ 2D, with D for oxygen in water). They are foundation material added to explain why a pump is needed. They are not on the lecture slides. 2. The two major circulatory pathways Blood travels through two circuits, one after the other: Pulmonary circulation: The right side of the heart pumps blood to the lungs. There, carbon dioxide leaves the blood and oxygen enters it. The oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart. Systemic circulation: The left side of the heart pumps oxygen-rich blood to the tissues of the whole body. The tissues take up oxygen and add carbon dioxide, and the blood returns to the right side of the heart. Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation. Image credit OpenStax College, original illustration, CC BY 3.0; unmodified. The two circuits are in series. Blood must pass through one circuit before it can enter the other: body → right heart → lungs → left heart → body. There is no other route. Why must both ventricles pump the same amount? Because every drop the right ventricle sends through the lungs arrives at the left ventricle. Over any period longer than a few beats, the right and left ventricles must pump the same volume per minute: about 5 L/min each at rest. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more than the left for a long time, blood would pile up in the lungs. If the left pumped more than the right, the lungs would slowly drain. ( → Cardiac Output, §1) Within the systemic circuit, the organs sit side by side ( in parallel). The aorta divides into branches that supply the brain, the heart muscle, the gut and liver, the kidneys, the skeletal muscles and the skin. Because each organ has its own branch: every organ receives fresh, oxygen-rich blood from the arteries, not blood another organ has already used; the flow to each organ can be turned up or down on its own, by narrowing or widening that organ's small arteries, without starving the others. ( → Haemodynamics, §8) The lungs are the exception: all of the blood passes through them, in series. The heart feeds itself the same way. The heart does not take its oxygen from the blood inside its chambers. Its muscular wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers to most of its cells. So the heart muscle has its own parallel branch from the aorta: the coronary arteries. ( → Coronary Circulation, §1) Series and parallel One loop runs right heart → lungs → left heart → aorta, labelled in series. The aorta then splits into side-by-side branches for the brain, the heart muscle (coronary arteries), the gut and liver, the kidneys, skeletal muscle and the skin, labelled in parallel. The branches rejoin in the veins and return to the right heart. Gut and liver are drawn as one branch because blood from the gut passes through the liver. Source note: The lecture gives about 5.5 L/min for each side of the heart; 5 L/min is also widely quoted. The parallel arrangement of organs is foundation material for the regional-circulation chapters. 3. The heart and its four chambers The heart is a muscular organ that works as two pumps side by side. The right heart pumps blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps blood to the body. Each pump has two chambers: Right atrium: receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae. Right ventricle: pumps that blood into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs. Left atrium: receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Left ventricle: pumps that blood into the aorta, which carries it to the whole body. The atria mainly receive blood and top up the ventricles. The ventricles do the main pumping: they generate the pressure that drives blood into the arteries. Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart. Image credit Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., original illustration, CC BY 3.0; unmodified. How does blood flow through the heart? Follow the path: Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues. What keeps blood moving one way? Four valves: The inlet valves sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the tricuspid valve on the right and the mitral valve on the left. Together they are the AV (atrioventricular) valves. The outlet valves sit between each ventricle and its artery: the pulmonary valve on the right and the aortic valve on the left. Together they are the semilunar valves. Valves contain no muscle. They open and shut passively, pushed by blood pressure. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it, and shuts the moment that reverses. So blood can only move forward. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §2) Arteries and veins are named by direction, not by oxygen. The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. This is because arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood towards it, whatever its oxygen content. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why can't diffusion alone supply the cells of the body? A. Diffusion stops working once blood starts to flow B. Oxygen cannot cross cell membranes without a pump C. Cells use oxygen faster than any blood flow can bring it D. Diffusion time rises steeply as the distance grows Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance. Micrometres take a fraction of a second, but centimetres take hours. The circulation carries blood close to every cell, so diffusion only covers the last few micrometres. 2. For a week, a person's right ventricle pumps slightly more blood per minute than the left. Where does the extra blood collect? A. In the systemic veins, which hold most of the blood volume B. In the pulmonary vessels, between the two ventricles C. In the right atrium, because the tricuspid valve shuts D. In the aorta, which stretches to store the extra volume Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The circuits are in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps goes through the lungs to the left heart. If the left ventricle sends on less than arrives, the difference builds up in the lung vessels. 3. Why does the brain still receive oxygen-rich blood when the leg muscles are working hard? A. Blood reaches the brain before it reaches the leg muscles B. The brain extracts oxygen faster than the leg muscles can C. Each organ has its own branch from the aorta, side by side D. The heart pumps a separate circuit just for the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Systemic organs are supplied in parallel. The brain's branch carries fresh arterial blood, not blood the muscles have already used. Flow to each organ can also be adjusted separately. 4. A vessel carries oxygen-rich blood towards the heart. What is it? A. A pulmonary vein, because it carries blood towards the heart B. A pulmonary artery, because its blood is rich in oxygen C. A systemic artery, because oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs D. A coronary artery, because it supplies the heart muscle Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Vessels are named by direction. Veins carry blood towards the heart, and the pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. 4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right? Both ventricles pump the same volume each minute ( §2). Yet the left ventricle's wall is two to three times thicker than the right's. The difference is pressure, not volume. What sets the pressure a pump must make? Blood flows only when there is a pressure difference to push it. The more the vessels oppose flow (their resistance), the bigger the pressure difference needed to keep the same flow. The lecture puts it as: flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance. ( → Haemodynamics, §2) Systemic circulation: a vast network of small, muscular arteries and arterioles, so total resistance is high. The left ventricle must reach about 120 mmHg with each beat. Pulmonary circulation: short, wide, thin-walled vessels, so resistance is low. The right ventricle needs only about 25 mmHg. Generating more pressure takes more muscle, so the left ventricular wall is thicker. Same volume per minute, different pressure. Why is it useful for the lungs to have low pressure? Lung capillaries are extremely thin, so that gases can cross them quickly. High pressure inside them would push fluid out into the lung tissue and air spaces. This is pulmonary oedema, and it gets in the way of gas exchange. Low pulmonary pressure keeps the lungs dry. ( → Microcirculation, §5) 5. Systole and diastole Systole means contraction and diastole means relaxation. Always say which chambers you mean, because the atria and ventricles contract at different times. Atrial systole: the atria contract and push the final portion of blood into the ventricles. Atrial diastole: the atria relax and fill with blood returning from the body and lungs. Ventricular systole: the ventricles contract and eject blood into the pulmonary trunk (right) and the aorta (left). Ventricular diastole: the ventricles relax and fill for the next beat. The order matters. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole, just before the ventricles contract. By then, most ventricular filling (about 70% in the lecture) has already happened passively. Atrial contraction only tops it up. At a resting heart rate, one beat lasts about 0.8 s. The ventricles spend about 0.3 s in systole and 0.5 s in diastole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §1) 6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system To pump well, the heart needs two things: force and timing. Force: cardiac muscle (myocardium). Cardiac muscle cells are electrically linked to one another. An impulse therefore spreads from cell to cell, and the muscle of each chamber contracts as one unit. ( → Cardiac Muscle, §2) Timing: the conducting system. The impulse starts in the SA node, at the junction of the superior vena cava and the right atrium. It spreads across the atria to the AV node, which holds it back briefly. It then races down the bundle of His and the Purkinje fibres through both ventricles. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §2) The lecture notes that every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. The SA node normally sets the pace because it fires fastest, so it triggers the rest before they can fire themselves. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) Electrical activation always comes first; contraction follows. The order in which the impulse spreads sets the order of contraction: the atria first, then, after the AV-node delay, the ventricles. Source note: Lecture connection: Circulation 1, opening slides (the heart's structure and function, conducting tissues, systole and diastole). The diffusion explanation and the parallel arrangement of organs are added foundations. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Both ventricles eject about 70 mL per beat. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker? A. It must eject a larger volume of blood each minute B. It must generate higher pressure against higher resistance C. It contracts for longer because the aortic valve is smaller D. It must also pump blood through the coronary arteries first Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The two ventricles move the same volume, but the systemic circulation offers far more resistance. The left ventricle needs about 120 mmHg against the right's 25, and more pressure needs more muscle. 2. Why is low pressure in the pulmonary circulation an advantage? A. It lets the right ventricle pump less blood than the left B. It slows the blood so that more carbon dioxide can be made C. It stops the pulmonary valve from opening during systole D. It keeps fluid from being pushed out of the lung capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Thin lung capillaries leak fluid if pressure inside them is high, which causes pulmonary oedema. Low pressure keeps the lungs dry. Both ventricles still pump the same volume. 3. During atrial systole, what are the ventricles normally doing? A. Contracting, so the atria and ventricles squeeze together B. Ejecting blood into the aorta and pulmonary trunk at once C. Relaxed, finishing filling just before their own contraction D. Relaxing after ejection, with all four valves still shut Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. They top up the relaxed ventricles just before the ventricles contract; the AV-node delay keeps that order. 4. Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why does the SA node normally set the heart rate? A. It fires fastest, so it triggers the others first B. It is the only part connected to the nervous system C. It lies in the left ventricle, the strongest chamber D. It holds the impulse back so the atria contract first Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The fastest pacemaker wins: the SA node fires before the others reach their own firing point. D describes the AV node. The SA node lies in the right atrium. 7. Understanding Check 1. For several minutes, a patient's left ventricle pumps less than the right ventricle. Where does blood build up first? A. In the lungs, raising the pressure in the pulmonary vessels B. In the leg veins, causing swelling around the ankles C. In the right ventricle, whose wall thickens to cope D. In the pulmonary veins, which then carry oxygen-poor blood Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The lungs sit between the right and left ventricles. If the left side sends on less than the right side delivers, blood accumulates in the lung vessels first. 2. Which statement about the heart valves is correct? A. Valves open when their muscle fibres contract B. Valves open and shut because of pressure differences C. Valves are opened by impulses from the AV node D. Valves shut as soon as the atria begin to relax Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Valves have no muscle and no nerve control. Pressure behind a valve opens it, and reversed pressure shuts it. 3. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood into the pulmonary trunk. Why is the pulmonary trunk still called an artery? A. It has a thick muscular wall like the aorta B. It carries blood at the highest pressure in the body C. It carries blood away from the heart D. It carries blood back to the left atrium Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry it towards the heart, whatever the oxygen content. 8. Chapter Essentials Diffusion only works over micrometres. The circulation brings blood within a few micrometres of almost every cell. The pulmonary and systemic circuits are in series. Both ventricles pump the same volume per minute (about 5 L/min at rest). A lasting mismatch makes blood pile up in one circuit. The systemic organs are in parallel. Each gets fresh arterial blood, and flow to each can be adjusted separately. The heart muscle has its own branch: the coronary arteries. Four chambers: the atria receive blood and top up the ventricles; the ventricles generate the pressure. Valves are passive. They open and shut with pressure differences, keeping flow one-way. Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry it towards the heart, whatever its oxygen content. The left ventricle is thicker because it needs more pressure (about 120 vs 25 mmHg), not because it pumps more volume. Low lung pressure keeps the lungs dry. Systole is contraction; diastole is relaxation. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation (SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje) comes first and sets the order of contraction."},{"slug":"cardiac-muscle","title":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"4 and 18-20; added foundations","coverage":"supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different","title":"1. What makes cardiac muscle different?"},{"id":"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together","title":"2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together"},{"id":"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential","title":"3. The cardiac muscle action potential"},{"id":"4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force","title":"4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force"},{"id":"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax","title":"5. How does cardiac muscle relax?"},{"id":"6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus","title":"6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?"},{"id":"7-how-do-the-sympathetic-and-vagal-nerves-change-cardiac-muscle","title":"7. How do the sympathetic and vagal nerves change cardiac muscle?"},{"id":"8-why-these-properties-matter-for-pumping","title":"8. Why these properties matter for pumping"},{"id":"9-understanding-check","title":"9. Understanding Check"},{"id":"10-chapter-essentials","title":"10. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Muscle Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different\">1. What makes cardiac muscle different?</h2>\n<p>Heart muscle has to do something no other muscle does: contract about once a second for a whole lifetime, without tiring, and relax completely between beats so the chambers can refill. Its special features follow from those demands.</p>\n<p>Like skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle is <strong>striated</strong>. Its actin and myosin filaments are arranged in repeating units called <strong><span data-term=\"sarcomere\">sarcomeres</span></strong>, which shorten when the muscle contracts. Unlike skeletal muscle, it is <strong>involuntary</strong>, and its cells are short and branched, usually with one central nucleus.</p>\n<p>Every heartbeat follows the same chain of events, and this chapter follows it link by link:</p>\n<p><strong>electrical signal → calcium enters the cell → the cell's calcium store releases much more → actin and myosin pull → calcium is removed → the muscle relaxes.</strong></p>\n<p>Four features make this chain work in the heart:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intercalated discs</strong> join the cells, so the signal spreads and the cells pull together (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">§2</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>A long action potential with a plateau</strong> lets calcium in and stops the muscle from locking in contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential\">§3</a>, <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">§6</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Calcium-induced calcium release</strong> links the electrical signal to contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">§4</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Many mitochondria</strong> supply a constant stream of ATP. Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism, so it needs oxygen all the time. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\">→ Coronary Circulation, §1</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A few specialised heart cells can fire on their own; this is <strong><span data-term=\"automaticity\">automaticity</span></strong>. Ordinary working muscle cells normally don't: they wait to be excited by their neighbours. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together</h2>\n<p>One heart muscle cell on its own cannot pump blood. Thousands must contract together, and each cell's pull must pass to the next. Both jobs happen at the <strong>intercalated discs</strong>, where neighbouring cells meet end to end.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Structure</th>\n  <th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fascia adherens</strong></td>\n  <td>Anchors the actin filaments of neighbouring cells, so the pull passes from one cell to the next</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Desmosomes</strong></td>\n  <td>Hold neighbouring cells firmly together, so they don't tear apart under repeated force</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Gap junctions</strong></td>\n  <td>Tiny channels that let ions (electrical current) flow straight from one cell into the next</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>When one cell is excited, current flows through its gap junctions into its neighbours and brings them to threshold. The signal spreads from cell to cell, and the whole chamber contracts almost as one. The other two junctions hold the cells together while they pull.</p>\n<p>So heart muscle behaves as a <strong><span data-term=\"functional-syncytium\">functional syncytium</span></strong>: <em>syn</em> (together) + <em>cyt</em> (cell), meaning separate cells working as one unit. The atria form one syncytium and the ventricles another. Between them lies a ring of fibrous tissue that does not conduct. The only normal electrical route across it is the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span>, which is why the AV node controls when the ventricles contract after the atria. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" alt=\"Branched cardiac muscle fibres and an enlarged intercalated disc showing desmosomes and gap junctions.\" width=\"1018\" height=\"476\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential\">3. The cardiac muscle action potential</h2>\n<p>An <strong>action potential</strong> is a brief electrical change across the cell membrane: the inside of the cell swings from negative to positive and back again. It is the signal that tells the cell to contract.</p>\n<p>In ventricular muscle, the cell rests at about <strong>−90 mV</strong>. Its action potential lasts about <strong>200–300 ms</strong>, roughly 100 times longer than a skeletal muscle action potential. That extra length comes from a long, flat <strong>plateau</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Phase</th>\n  <th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>0: Rapid upstroke</strong></td>\n  <td>Fast Na⁺ channels open. Na⁺ rushes in, and the inside of the cell becomes positive within about a millisecond.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>1: Small dip</strong></td>\n  <td>The Na⁺ channels close, and a brief outflow of K⁺ pulls the voltage down slightly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>2: Plateau</strong></td>\n  <td>L-type Ca²⁺ channels (L = long-lasting) let Ca²⁺ in, while K⁺ leaks out. The two roughly cancel, so the voltage stays nearly flat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>3: Repolarisation</strong></td>\n  <td>The Ca²⁺ channels close, the K⁺ outflow wins, and the inside returns to negative.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>4: Rest</strong></td>\n  <td>The cell stays steady at about −90 mV, held there by K⁺ channels that are open at rest.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does the plateau matter?</strong> It has two jobs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>It lets calcium in.</strong> The Ca²⁺ entering during the plateau is the trigger for contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">§4</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>It keeps the cell <span data-term=\"refractory-period\">refractory</span></strong>, meaning it cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over. This prevents tetanus (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">§6</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>You can see the action potential on the ECG.</strong> The ECG lecture links the flat <strong>ST segment</strong> to the plateau, when all the ventricular muscle is depolarised. The <strong>QT interval</strong> roughly matches how long a ventricular action potential lasts. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/ecg?section=4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">→ Electrocardiography, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" alt=\"Ventricular action potential and refractory period with a comparison of skeletal and cardiac muscle electrical and mechanical activity.\" width=\"1837\" height=\"2019\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> this is the action potential of a working ventricular muscle cell. Pacemaker cells in the <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> have no steady resting voltage, and their upstroke is driven mainly by Ca²⁺, not by fast Na⁺. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Neighbouring cardiac cells stay firmly attached but no longer contract together. Which junction has most likely failed?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Desmosomes, which hold neighbouring cells together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Gap junctions, which let current pass between cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Fascia adherens, which anchor the actin filaments</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. T-tubules, which carry the signal into each cell</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Gap junctions carry the electrical current from cell to cell. Without them the signal can't spread, so the cells stop contracting together, even though desmosomes and fascia adherens still hold them firmly attached.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What keeps the ventricular action potential flat during the plateau?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Fast Na⁺ channels staying open for about 200 ms</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The Na⁺–K⁺ pump stopping until contraction ends</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Ca²⁺ flowing in while K⁺ flows out at a similar rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Gap junctions holding the voltage of neighbouring cells</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Inward Ca²⁺ through L-type channels balances outward K⁺, so the voltage barely changes. The fast Na⁺ channels close within a few milliseconds of the upstroke.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> On the ECG, which part matches the plateau of the ventricular action potential?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ST segment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The P wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The QRS complex</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The T wave</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> During the plateau, all the ventricular muscle is depolarised at once, so no current flows between regions and the ECG trace stays flat. The QRS complex is the upstroke spreading through the ventricles, and the T wave is repolarisation.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"ecc\">Excitation–contraction coupling</span></strong> is the chain that turns the electrical signal into force. The link between the two is <strong>calcium (Ca²⁺)</strong>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The signal travels into the cell.</strong> The action potential spreads along the cell surface and down the <strong><span data-term=\"t-tubules\">T-tubules</span></strong>, tubes of surface membrane that dive deep into the cell.</li>\n<li><strong>A little Ca²⁺ enters.</strong> The change in voltage opens <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> in the T-tubules, and a small amount of Ca²⁺ flows in from outside the cell.</li>\n<li><strong>That Ca²⁺ releases much more.</strong> The incoming Ca²⁺ opens the release channels (<strong>RyR2</strong>, ryanodine receptor type 2) of the <strong><span data-term=\"sarcoplasmic-reticulum\">sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR)</span></strong>, the cell's internal calcium store. The SR pours out far more Ca²⁺. This is <strong><span data-term=\"cicr\">calcium-induced calcium release (CICR)</span></strong>: the entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger, and the SR supplies most of the Ca²⁺ that drives contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>Ca²⁺ unlocks the filaments.</strong> Ca²⁺ binds to <strong>troponin C</strong> on the actin filament. This shifts <strong>tropomyosin</strong>, uncovering the spots where myosin can grab actin.</li>\n<li><strong>Myosin pulls.</strong> Myosin heads attach to actin and pull, using energy from ATP. The sarcomeres shorten and the cell contracts.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"calcium-cycle\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>The calcium cycle, step by step</figcaption><p>One simple drawing of a heart muscle cell shows the cell membrane with a T-tubule, an L-type channel, the SR with its release channel (RyR2) and its pump (SERCA2a), the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger (NCX), and one actin–myosin unit. Next/Back steps through seven stages. At each stage the part involved lights up, with one plain sentence underneath: (1) the signal arrives down the T-tubule; (2) a little Ca²⁺ enters through the L-type channel; (3) the SR releases much more Ca²⁺; (4) Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, uncovering actin; (5) myosin pulls, and the cell contracts; (6) SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR; (7) NCX pushes Ca²⁺ out of the cell, and the cell relaxes. Ca²⁺ is shown as dots that move between compartments. The text explains everything without the widget.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-03\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" alt=\"Diagram showing cardiac T-tubule L-type calcium channels, ryanodine receptor, sodium–calcium exchanger, sarcoplasmic reticulum, SERCA and phospholamban.\" width=\"756\" height=\"567\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PeaBrainC</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why does heart muscle depend on calcium from outside the cell?</strong> In skeletal muscle, the voltage sensor in the T-tubule is mechanically linked to the SR's release channel, so the signal opens the store directly; no outside Ca²⁺ is needed. Heart muscle has no such direct link. Ca²⁺ must first enter from outside to open the store. <strong>Skeletal muscle uses a direct mechanical link; heart muscle uses CICR.</strong> So anything that reduces Ca²⁺ entry, such as calcium-channel-blocking drugs, weakens the heart's contraction, even if the SR is still full.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax\">5. How does cardiac muscle relax?</h2>\n<p>To relax, the cell must lower its Ca²⁺ again. Ca²⁺ leaves troponin C, tropomyosin covers the binding spots on actin again, and myosin lets go.</p>\n<p>Two main pumps remove the Ca²⁺:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"serca\">SERCA2a</span></strong> (the sarco-endoplasmic reticulum Ca²⁺ pump) is a pump in the SR membrane, powered by ATP. It pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, ready for the next beat.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"ncx\">NCX</span></strong> (the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger) sits in the cell membrane. It uses the Na⁺ gradient to swap Na⁺ in for Ca²⁺ out, removing roughly the Ca²⁺ that entered during the beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other pathways, including a Ca²⁺ pump in the cell membrane, play smaller parts. <strong>Relaxation therefore uses energy, just as contraction does.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What if SERCA2a works too slowly?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relaxation is delayed.</strong> Ca²⁺ stays high in the cell for longer, so the muscle stays partly contracted and the ventricle fills less well.</li>\n<li><strong>Later beats can be weaker.</strong> Less Ca²⁺ goes back into the SR, so less is available to release on the next beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Good pumping needs <strong>both a strong contraction and a fast, complete relaxation</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?</h2>\n<p>In skeletal muscle, the action potential is over in a few milliseconds, long before the twitch ends. A second stimulus can arrive while the muscle is still contracting, and rapid stimuli add up into one sustained contraction: <strong>tetanus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Heart muscle can't do this. As the lecture puts it, it is impossible to tetanise cardiac muscle because of the <strong>long refractory period</strong> of its action potential. The refractory period lasts almost as long as the contraction itself. By the time the cell can fire again, it has already begun to relax.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> a heart locked in sustained contraction could not refill, so it would pump nothing. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Figure 02 shows how the refractory period and the contraction line up.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Ca²⁺ still enters through L-type channels, but the SR's release channels (RyR2) fail to open. What happens to contraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It is much weaker, because the SR's large Ca²⁺ store is not released</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is normal, because the entering Ca²⁺ is enough on its own</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It is stronger, because more Ca²⁺ stays in the extracellular fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It becomes sustained, because Ca²⁺ can no longer be pumped away</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The entering Ca²⁺ is only the trigger. Most of the Ca²⁺ that activates the filaments comes from the SR, so without RyR2 release the rise in Ca²⁺ is far too small.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> SERCA2a pumps work more slowly than normal. What is the most likely immediate effect?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The action potential upstroke becomes slower</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Gap junctions close, so cells contract one at a time</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart can now be tetanised by rapid stimuli</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Relaxation is delayed because Ca²⁺ in the cell stays high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> SERCA2a is the main route for removing Ca²⁺ after a beat. If it slows, Ca²⁺ stays bound to troponin for longer and relaxation is delayed. Over later beats, less Ca²⁺ is stored for release.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can't a rapid series of stimuli make cardiac muscle contract continuously?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Cardiac muscle stores no calcium in its SR</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Gap junctions block stimuli that arrive too quickly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its long refractory period lasts most of the contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The vagus nerve relaxes the muscle between beats</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The cell cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over, so contractions cannot add up into tetanus. This guarantees relaxation and filling between beats.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-how-do-the-sympathetic-and-vagal-nerves-change-cardiac-muscle\">7. How do the sympathetic and vagal nerves change cardiac muscle?</h2>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation</strong> prepares the heart for exercise or stress: stronger beats, faster relaxation and a faster rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Noradrenaline</strong> (released by sympathetic nerve endings) and <strong>adrenaline</strong> (released by the adrenal medulla) both act on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> on heart cells:</p>\n<p><strong>β₁ receptor → G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP rises → PKA is switched on</strong></p>\n<p>Here <strong>cAMP</strong> (cyclic AMP) is a messenger molecule inside the cell. <strong>PKA</strong> (protein kinase A) is an enzyme that switches other proteins on or off by adding phosphate to them. PKA acts on the Ca²⁺ machinery in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stronger contraction.</strong> L-type channels let in more Ca²⁺, so the SR releases more, and more myosin heads pull on actin.</li>\n<li><strong>Faster relaxation.</strong> PKA disables <strong><span data-term=\"phospholamban\">phospholamban</span></strong>, a protein that normally acts as a brake on SERCA2a. With the brake off, Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the SR faster.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"xanthines\">Caffeine and theophylline (xanthines)</span></strong> work further along the same pathway. The enzyme <strong>phosphodiesterase</strong> normally breaks cAMP down. Xanthines block that enzyme, so cAMP builds up and contraction gets stronger.</p>\n<p><strong>Vagal stimulation</strong> works the other way. Acetylcholine released by the vagus nerve slows the SA node (a slower heart rate) and weakens <strong>atrial</strong> contraction. Its direct effect on the strength of the ventricles is small, because few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Three words for three effects:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Term</th>\n  <th>Word parts</th>\n  <th>What it describes</th>\n  <th>Positive effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">Inotropy</span></strong></td>\n  <td><em>ino</em> = fibre</td>\n  <td>Strength of contraction</td>\n  <td>Stronger contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"lusitropy\">Lusitropy</span></strong></td>\n  <td><em>lusi</em> = loosening</td>\n  <td>Speed of relaxation</td>\n  <td>Faster relaxation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span></strong></td>\n  <td><em>chrono</em> = time</td>\n  <td>Heart rate</td>\n  <td>Faster heart rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> positive inotropy means the muscle contracts more strongly <strong>at the same filling</strong>. A ventricle that contracts harder simply because it holds more blood is showing the Frank–Starling effect, not increased inotropy. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">→ Cardiac Output, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture slide says adrenaline comes from sympathetic nerve endings and noradrenaline from the adrenals. It is the other way round: sympathetic nerve endings release mainly noradrenaline, and the adrenal medulla releases mainly adrenaline. Both act on β₁ receptors. The lecture also lists a negative inotropic effect of vagal stimulation; this effect is mainly on the atria.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-why-these-properties-matter-for-pumping\">8. Why these properties matter for pumping</h2>\n<p>Each property of heart muscle supports one part of the pumping cycle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gap junctions</strong> make each chamber contract as one, giving a strong, coordinated squeeze.</li>\n<li><strong>The long refractory period</strong> guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats.</li>\n<li><strong>Calcium handling sets the strength of each beat.</strong> More Ca²⁺ means more myosin heads pulling, a stronger contraction and a bigger stroke volume. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=7-what-changes-contractility\">→ Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Fast relaxation matters most at high heart rates.</strong> Diastole shortens most as the rate rises, so the ventricles must relax quickly to have time to fill. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §10</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Contraction is not the same as ejection.</strong> The ventricles start contracting a moment before blood leaves: pressure must first rise above the pressure in the aorta or pulmonary artery. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §4</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Lecture connection: Circulation 1 (cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised; contractility through β₁ receptors and cAMP; xanthines; vagal effect) and the ECG lecture (ST segment and QT interval). The cellular detail (intercalated discs, phases of the action potential, CICR, SERCA2a and NCX) is standard physiology added as foundation.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-understanding-check\">9. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> How does β₁ stimulation make heart muscle both contract more strongly and relax faster?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The plateau is shortened, so less Ca²⁺ is needed for each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. More Ca²⁺ is released, and SERCA2a is freed to pump it back faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Gap junctions open wider, so the cells contract and relax together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Vagal fibres are switched off, which removes their braking effect</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> PKA increases Ca²⁺ entry and release, giving a stronger beat. It also disables phospholamban, the brake on SERCA2a, so Ca²⁺ is pumped back faster and the muscle relaxes sooner.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> According to the lecture, why can caffeine and theophylline make heart muscle contract more strongly?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They block the breakdown of cAMP, so cAMP builds up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They open fast Na⁺ channels, so the upstroke is larger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They block β₁ receptors, so noradrenaline acts longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They slow SERCA2a, so more Ca²⁺ stays in the cell</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Xanthines block phosphodiesterase, the enzyme that breaks down cAMP. More cAMP means more PKA activity and stronger contraction.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A ventricle contracts more strongly because it was filled with more blood. Is this a positive inotropic effect?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Yes, because any stronger contraction counts as inotropy</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Yes, because extra filling releases more Ca²⁺ from the SR</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. No, because inotropy only describes the heart rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. No, because inotropy means stronger at the same filling</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> A stronger beat caused by more filling is the Frank–Starling effect. Inotropy is a change in the muscle's own strength at the same filling.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Strong vagal stimulation mainly affects the heart by:<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Weakening ventricular contraction more than heart rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Blocking the gap junctions between ventricular cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Slowing the SA node and weakening atrial contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Shortening the refractory period of the ventricles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Acetylcholine slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle, so the direct effect on ventricular strength is small.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Why does fast relaxation matter more at high heart rates?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Diastole shortens most, so filling must happen faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Systole lengthens, so contraction must end sooner</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The refractory period disappears at high heart rates</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Ca²⁺ stops entering the cells when the rate is high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> As the rate rises, diastole is cut far more than systole. A ventricle that relaxes slowly has too little time left to fill.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-chapter-essentials\">10. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The chain of events:</strong> electrical signal → Ca²⁺ enters → the SR releases more Ca²⁺ → myosin pulls on actin → Ca²⁺ is removed → the muscle relaxes.</li>\n<li><strong>Intercalated discs:</strong> gap junctions pass current from cell to cell; desmosomes and fascia adherens hold the cells together and pass on their pull. The cells act as one unit (a functional syncytium).</li>\n<li><strong>The atria and ventricles are separate units.</strong> Fibrous tissue insulates them, and the AV node is the only normal electrical route between them.</li>\n<li><strong>The ventricular action potential:</strong> phase 0 is Na⁺ rushing in; phase 2, the plateau, is Ca²⁺ in balanced by K⁺ out; phase 3 is K⁺ out. On the ECG, the plateau is the ST segment.</li>\n<li><strong>CICR:</strong> a little Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2, and the SR releases much more. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, and myosin pulls on actin.</li>\n<li><strong>Relaxation needs energy:</strong> SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, and NCX pushes it out of the cell.</li>\n<li><strong>No tetanus:</strong> the long refractory period lasts most of the contraction, so the ventricles always relax and refill.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic (β₁ → cAMP → PKA):</strong> stronger contraction and faster relaxation (phospholamban released). Xanthines raise cAMP by blocking its breakdown. Vagal stimulation slows the SA node and weakens the atria.</li>\n<li><strong>Inotropy</strong> is strength at the same filling; <strong>lusitropy</strong> is speed of relaxation; <strong>chronotropy</strong> is heart rate.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology 1. What makes cardiac muscle different? Heart muscle has to do something no other muscle does: contract about once a second for a whole lifetime, without tiring, and relax completely between beats so the chambers can refill. Its special features follow from those demands. Like skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle is striated. Its actin and myosin filaments are arranged in repeating units called sarcomeres, which shorten when the muscle contracts. Unlike skeletal muscle, it is involuntary, and its cells are short and branched, usually with one central nucleus. Every heartbeat follows the same chain of events, and this chapter follows it link by link: electrical signal → calcium enters the cell → the cell's calcium store releases much more → actin and myosin pull → calcium is removed → the muscle relaxes. Four features make this chain work in the heart: Intercalated discs join the cells, so the signal spreads and the cells pull together ( §2). A long action potential with a plateau lets calcium in and stops the muscle from locking in contraction ( §3, §6). Calcium-induced calcium release links the electrical signal to contraction ( §4). Many mitochondria supply a constant stream of ATP. Heart muscle depends almost entirely on aerobic metabolism, so it needs oxygen all the time. ( → Coronary Circulation, §1) A few specialised heart cells can fire on their own; this is automaticity. Ordinary working muscle cells normally don't: they wait to be excited by their neighbours. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) 2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together One heart muscle cell on its own cannot pump blood. Thousands must contract together, and each cell's pull must pass to the next. Both jobs happen at the intercalated discs, where neighbouring cells meet end to end. Structure What it does Fascia adherens Anchors the actin filaments of neighbouring cells, so the pull passes from one cell to the next Desmosomes Hold neighbouring cells firmly together, so they don't tear apart under repeated force Gap junctions Tiny channels that let ions (electrical current) flow straight from one cell into the next When one cell is excited, current flows through its gap junctions into its neighbours and brings them to threshold. The signal spreads from cell to cell, and the whole chamber contracts almost as one. The other two junctions hold the cells together while they pull. So heart muscle behaves as a functional syncytium: syn (together) + cyt (cell), meaning separate cells working as one unit. The atria form one syncytium and the ventricles another. Between them lies a ring of fibrous tissue that does not conduct. The only normal electrical route across it is the AV node, which is why the AV node controls when the ventricles contract after the atria. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §5) Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs. Image credit Image: OpenStax · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. 3. The cardiac muscle action potential An action potential is a brief electrical change across the cell membrane: the inside of the cell swings from negative to positive and back again. It is the signal that tells the cell to contract. In ventricular muscle, the cell rests at about −90 mV. Its action potential lasts about 200–300 ms, roughly 100 times longer than a skeletal muscle action potential. That extra length comes from a long, flat plateau. Phase What happens 0: Rapid upstroke Fast Na⁺ channels open. Na⁺ rushes in, and the inside of the cell becomes positive within about a millisecond. 1: Small dip The Na⁺ channels close, and a brief outflow of K⁺ pulls the voltage down slightly. 2: Plateau L-type Ca²⁺ channels (L = long-lasting) let Ca²⁺ in, while K⁺ leaks out. The two roughly cancel, so the voltage stays nearly flat. 3: Repolarisation The Ca²⁺ channels close, the K⁺ outflow wins, and the inside returns to negative. 4: Rest The cell stays steady at about −90 mV, held there by K⁺ channels that are open at rest. Why does the plateau matter? It has two jobs: It lets calcium in. The Ca²⁺ entering during the plateau is the trigger for contraction ( §4). It keeps the cell refractory, meaning it cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over. This prevents tetanus ( §6). You can see the action potential on the ECG. The ECG lecture links the flat ST segment to the plateau, when all the ventricular muscle is depolarised. The QT interval roughly matches how long a ventricular action potential lasts. ( → Electrocardiography, §4) Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction. Image credit Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Important distinction: this is the action potential of a working ventricular muscle cell. Pacemaker cells in the SA node have no steady resting voltage, and their upstroke is driven mainly by Ca²⁺, not by fast Na⁺. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §3) OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Neighbouring cardiac cells stay firmly attached but no longer contract together. Which junction has most likely failed? A. Desmosomes, which hold neighbouring cells together B. Gap junctions, which let current pass between cells C. Fascia adherens, which anchor the actin filaments D. T-tubules, which carry the signal into each cell Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Gap junctions carry the electrical current from cell to cell. Without them the signal can't spread, so the cells stop contracting together, even though desmosomes and fascia adherens still hold them firmly attached. 2. What keeps the ventricular action potential flat during the plateau? A. Fast Na⁺ channels staying open for about 200 ms B. The Na⁺–K⁺ pump stopping until contraction ends C. Ca²⁺ flowing in while K⁺ flows out at a similar rate D. Gap junctions holding the voltage of neighbouring cells Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inward Ca²⁺ through L-type channels balances outward K⁺, so the voltage barely changes. The fast Na⁺ channels close within a few milliseconds of the upstroke. 3. On the ECG, which part matches the plateau of the ventricular action potential? A. The ST segment B. The P wave C. The QRS complex D. The T wave Show answer and explanation Answer: A. During the plateau, all the ventricular muscle is depolarised at once, so no current flows between regions and the ECG trace stays flat. The QRS complex is the upstroke spreading through the ventricles, and the T wave is repolarisation. 4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force Excitation–contraction coupling is the chain that turns the electrical signal into force. The link between the two is calcium (Ca²⁺). The signal travels into the cell. The action potential spreads along the cell surface and down the T-tubules, tubes of surface membrane that dive deep into the cell. A little Ca²⁺ enters. The change in voltage opens L-type Ca²⁺ channels in the T-tubules, and a small amount of Ca²⁺ flows in from outside the cell. That Ca²⁺ releases much more. The incoming Ca²⁺ opens the release channels ( RyR2, ryanodine receptor type 2) of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), the cell's internal calcium store. The SR pours out far more Ca²⁺. This is calcium-induced calcium release (CICR): the entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger, and the SR supplies most of the Ca²⁺ that drives contraction. Ca²⁺ unlocks the filaments. Ca²⁺ binds to troponin C on the actin filament. This shifts tropomyosin, uncovering the spots where myosin can grab actin. Myosin pulls. Myosin heads attach to actin and pull, using energy from ATP. The sarcomeres shorten and the cell contracts. The calcium cycle, step by step One simple drawing of a heart muscle cell shows the cell membrane with a T-tubule, an L-type channel, the SR with its release channel (RyR2) and its pump (SERCA2a), the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger (NCX), and one actin–myosin unit. Next/Back steps through seven stages. At each stage the part involved lights up, with one plain sentence underneath: (1) the signal arrives down the T-tubule; (2) a little Ca²⁺ enters through the L-type channel; (3) the SR releases much more Ca²⁺; (4) Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, uncovering actin; (5) myosin pulls, and the cell contracts; (6) SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR; (7) NCX pushes Ca²⁺ out of the cell, and the cell relaxes. Ca²⁺ is shown as dots that move between compartments. The text explains everything without the widget. Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling. Image credit Image: PeaBrainC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Why does heart muscle depend on calcium from outside the cell? In skeletal muscle, the voltage sensor in the T-tubule is mechanically linked to the SR's release channel, so the signal opens the store directly; no outside Ca²⁺ is needed. Heart muscle has no such direct link. Ca²⁺ must first enter from outside to open the store. Skeletal muscle uses a direct mechanical link; heart muscle uses CICR. So anything that reduces Ca²⁺ entry, such as calcium-channel-blocking drugs, weakens the heart's contraction, even if the SR is still full. 5. How does cardiac muscle relax? To relax, the cell must lower its Ca²⁺ again. Ca²⁺ leaves troponin C, tropomyosin covers the binding spots on actin again, and myosin lets go. Two main pumps remove the Ca²⁺: SERCA2a (the sarco-endoplasmic reticulum Ca²⁺ pump) is a pump in the SR membrane, powered by ATP. It pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, ready for the next beat. NCX (the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger) sits in the cell membrane. It uses the Na⁺ gradient to swap Na⁺ in for Ca²⁺ out, removing roughly the Ca²⁺ that entered during the beat. Other pathways, including a Ca²⁺ pump in the cell membrane, play smaller parts. Relaxation therefore uses energy, just as contraction does. What if SERCA2a works too slowly? Relaxation is delayed. Ca²⁺ stays high in the cell for longer, so the muscle stays partly contracted and the ventricle fills less well. Later beats can be weaker. Less Ca²⁺ goes back into the SR, so less is available to release on the next beat. Good pumping needs both a strong contraction and a fast, complete relaxation. 6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus? In skeletal muscle, the action potential is over in a few milliseconds, long before the twitch ends. A second stimulus can arrive while the muscle is still contracting, and rapid stimuli add up into one sustained contraction: tetanus. Heart muscle can't do this. As the lecture puts it, it is impossible to tetanise cardiac muscle because of the long refractory period of its action potential. The refractory period lasts almost as long as the contraction itself. By the time the cell can fire again, it has already begun to relax. Why this matters: a heart locked in sustained contraction could not refill, so it would pump nothing. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Figure 02 shows how the refractory period and the contraction line up. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Ca²⁺ still enters through L-type channels, but the SR's release channels (RyR2) fail to open. What happens to contraction? A. It is much weaker, because the SR's large Ca²⁺ store is not released B. It is normal, because the entering Ca²⁺ is enough on its own C. It is stronger, because more Ca²⁺ stays in the extracellular fluid D. It becomes sustained, because Ca²⁺ can no longer be pumped away Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The entering Ca²⁺ is only the trigger. Most of the Ca²⁺ that activates the filaments comes from the SR, so without RyR2 release the rise in Ca²⁺ is far too small. 2. SERCA2a pumps work more slowly than normal. What is the most likely immediate effect? A. The action potential upstroke becomes slower B. Gap junctions close, so cells contract one at a time C. The heart can now be tetanised by rapid stimuli D. Relaxation is delayed because Ca²⁺ in the cell stays high Show answer and explanation Answer: D. SERCA2a is the main route for removing Ca²⁺ after a beat. If it slows, Ca²⁺ stays bound to troponin for longer and relaxation is delayed. Over later beats, less Ca²⁺ is stored for release. 3. Why can't a rapid series of stimuli make cardiac muscle contract continuously? A. Cardiac muscle stores no calcium in its SR B. Gap junctions block stimuli that arrive too quickly C. Its long refractory period lasts most of the contraction D. The vagus nerve relaxes the muscle between beats Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The cell cannot fire again until its contraction is nearly over, so contractions cannot add up into tetanus. This guarantees relaxation and filling between beats. 7. How do the sympathetic and vagal nerves change cardiac muscle? Sympathetic stimulation prepares the heart for exercise or stress: stronger beats, faster relaxation and a faster rate. Noradrenaline (released by sympathetic nerve endings) and adrenaline (released by the adrenal medulla) both act on β₁ receptors on heart cells: β₁ receptor → G protein → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP rises → PKA is switched on Here cAMP (cyclic AMP) is a messenger molecule inside the cell. PKA (protein kinase A) is an enzyme that switches other proteins on or off by adding phosphate to them. PKA acts on the Ca²⁺ machinery in two ways: Stronger contraction. L-type channels let in more Ca²⁺, so the SR releases more, and more myosin heads pull on actin. Faster relaxation. PKA disables phospholamban, a protein that normally acts as a brake on SERCA2a. With the brake off, Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the SR faster. Caffeine and theophylline (xanthines) work further along the same pathway. The enzyme phosphodiesterase normally breaks cAMP down. Xanthines block that enzyme, so cAMP builds up and contraction gets stronger. Vagal stimulation works the other way. Acetylcholine released by the vagus nerve slows the SA node (a slower heart rate) and weakens atrial contraction. Its direct effect on the strength of the ventricles is small, because few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle. Three words for three effects: Term Word parts What it describes Positive effect Inotropy ino = fibre Strength of contraction Stronger contraction Lusitropy lusi = loosening Speed of relaxation Faster relaxation Chronotropy chrono = time Heart rate Faster heart rate Important distinction: positive inotropy means the muscle contracts more strongly at the same filling. A ventricle that contracts harder simply because it holds more blood is showing the Frank–Starling effect, not increased inotropy. ( → Cardiac Output, §4) Source note: The lecture slide says adrenaline comes from sympathetic nerve endings and noradrenaline from the adrenals. It is the other way round: sympathetic nerve endings release mainly noradrenaline, and the adrenal medulla releases mainly adrenaline. Both act on β₁ receptors. The lecture also lists a negative inotropic effect of vagal stimulation; this effect is mainly on the atria. 8. Why these properties matter for pumping Each property of heart muscle supports one part of the pumping cycle: Gap junctions make each chamber contract as one, giving a strong, coordinated squeeze. The long refractory period guarantees that the ventricles relax and refill between beats. Calcium handling sets the strength of each beat. More Ca²⁺ means more myosin heads pulling, a stronger contraction and a bigger stroke volume. ( → Cardiac Output, §7) Fast relaxation matters most at high heart rates. Diastole shortens most as the rate rises, so the ventricles must relax quickly to have time to fill. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §10) Contraction is not the same as ejection. The ventricles start contracting a moment before blood leaves: pressure must first rise above the pressure in the aorta or pulmonary artery. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §4) Source note: Lecture connection: Circulation 1 (cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised; contractility through β₁ receptors and cAMP; xanthines; vagal effect) and the ECG lecture (ST segment and QT interval). The cellular detail (intercalated discs, phases of the action potential, CICR, SERCA2a and NCX) is standard physiology added as foundation. 9. Understanding Check 1. How does β₁ stimulation make heart muscle both contract more strongly and relax faster? A. The plateau is shortened, so less Ca²⁺ is needed for each beat B. More Ca²⁺ is released, and SERCA2a is freed to pump it back faster C. Gap junctions open wider, so the cells contract and relax together D. Vagal fibres are switched off, which removes their braking effect Show answer and explanation Answer: B. PKA increases Ca²⁺ entry and release, giving a stronger beat. It also disables phospholamban, the brake on SERCA2a, so Ca²⁺ is pumped back faster and the muscle relaxes sooner. 2. According to the lecture, why can caffeine and theophylline make heart muscle contract more strongly? A. They block the breakdown of cAMP, so cAMP builds up B. They open fast Na⁺ channels, so the upstroke is larger C. They block β₁ receptors, so noradrenaline acts longer D. They slow SERCA2a, so more Ca²⁺ stays in the cell Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Xanthines block phosphodiesterase, the enzyme that breaks down cAMP. More cAMP means more PKA activity and stronger contraction. 3. A ventricle contracts more strongly because it was filled with more blood. Is this a positive inotropic effect? A. Yes, because any stronger contraction counts as inotropy B. Yes, because extra filling releases more Ca²⁺ from the SR C. No, because inotropy only describes the heart rate D. No, because inotropy means stronger at the same filling Show answer and explanation Answer: D. A stronger beat caused by more filling is the Frank–Starling effect. Inotropy is a change in the muscle's own strength at the same filling. 4. Strong vagal stimulation mainly affects the heart by: A. Weakening ventricular contraction more than heart rate B. Blocking the gap junctions between ventricular cells C. Slowing the SA node and weakening atrial contraction D. Shortening the refractory period of the ventricles Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Acetylcholine slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Few vagal fibres reach ventricular muscle, so the direct effect on ventricular strength is small. 5. Why does fast relaxation matter more at high heart rates? A. Diastole shortens most, so filling must happen faster B. Systole lengthens, so contraction must end sooner C. The refractory period disappears at high heart rates D. Ca²⁺ stops entering the cells when the rate is high Show answer and explanation Answer: A. As the rate rises, diastole is cut far more than systole. A ventricle that relaxes slowly has too little time left to fill. 10. Chapter Essentials The chain of events: electrical signal → Ca²⁺ enters → the SR releases more Ca²⁺ → myosin pulls on actin → Ca²⁺ is removed → the muscle relaxes. Intercalated discs: gap junctions pass current from cell to cell; desmosomes and fascia adherens hold the cells together and pass on their pull. The cells act as one unit (a functional syncytium). The atria and ventricles are separate units. Fibrous tissue insulates them, and the AV node is the only normal electrical route between them. The ventricular action potential: phase 0 is Na⁺ rushing in; phase 2, the plateau, is Ca²⁺ in balanced by K⁺ out; phase 3 is K⁺ out. On the ECG, the plateau is the ST segment. CICR: a little Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels opens RyR2, and the SR releases much more. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, and myosin pulls on actin. Relaxation needs energy: SERCA2a pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, and NCX pushes it out of the cell. No tetanus: the long refractory period lasts most of the contraction, so the ventricles always relax and refill. Sympathetic (β₁ → cAMP → PKA): stronger contraction and faster relaxation (phospholamban released). Xanthines raise cAMP by blocking its breakdown. Vagal stimulation slows the SA node and weakens the atria. Inotropy is strength at the same filling; lusitropy is speed of relaxation; chronotropy is heart rate."},{"slug":"cardiac-conduction","title":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"7-10","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system","title":"1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?"},{"id":"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction","title":"2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction"},{"id":"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker","title":"3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?"},{"id":"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm","title":"4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?"},{"id":"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction","title":"5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?"},{"id":"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid","title":"6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?"},{"id":"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity","title":"7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?"},{"id":"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances","title":"8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances"},{"id":"9-chapter-essentials","title":"9. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system\">1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?</h2>\n<p>Pumping works only if the chambers contract in the right order and at the right moment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>the atria before the ventricles</strong>, so the atria can top up the ventricles first;</li>\n<li><strong>all parts of each ventricle together</strong>, so pressure builds fast enough to eject blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That gives the heart two electrical jobs: <strong>start each beat</strong>, and <strong>deliver the signal in the right order</strong>. The specialised conducting system does both.</p>\n<p>Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a nerve impulse for every contraction. Specialised heart cells fire on their own; this is <strong><span data-term=\"automaticity\">automaticity</span></strong>. The nerves only speed this built-in rhythm up or slow it down (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\">§7</a>). As the lecture summarises, depolarisation spreads from the SA node to the AV node, then down the bundle of His to the rest of the heart, and contraction starts after depolarisation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\">2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction</h2>\n<p><strong>SA node → atrial muscle → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Structure</th>\n  <th>Where it is and what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node (sinoatrial node)</span></strong></td>\n  <td>In the wall of the right atrium, where the superior vena cava joins it. Starts each beat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Internodal pathways</strong></td>\n  <td>Carry the signal across the right atrium to the AV node. Bachmann's bundle also carries it to the left atrium.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node (atrioventricular node)</span></strong></td>\n  <td>Low in the right atrium, in the wall between the two atria, just behind the tricuspid valve. Holds the signal back briefly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Bundle of His</strong></td>\n  <td>Carries the signal from the AV node through the fibrous barrier into the wall between the ventricles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Right and left bundle branches</strong></td>\n  <td>Run down each side of the wall between the ventricles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong></td>\n  <td>Spread through the inner walls of both ventricles and hand the signal to the muscle.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Sectioned heart showing the SA node, atrial pathways including Bachmann's bundle, AV node, His bundle, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres.\" width=\"2021\" height=\"1354\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>What are the internodal pathways?</strong> The lecture names three: <strong>anterior</strong> (including Bachmann's bundle), <strong>middle</strong> (Wenckebach) and <strong>posterior</strong> (Thorel). They are routes the signal prefers, not insulated wires; the signal also spreads through ordinary atrial muscle. <strong>Bachmann's bundle</strong> matters most because it carries the signal across to the left atrium, so both atria contract almost together.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The three internodal tracts are named as in the lecture. Most anatomists now describe them as preferred routes through atrial muscle rather than separate insulated bundles.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?</h2>\n<p>Because it reaches threshold first.</p>\n<p>A working ventricular muscle cell sits at a steady −90 mV until a neighbour excites it. An SA node cell never sits still. After each beat, its voltage immediately starts <strong>creeping upwards by itself</strong> until it reaches threshold and fires. This slow climb is the <strong><span data-term=\"pacemaker-potential\">pacemaker potential</span></strong>, also called spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: <em>spontaneous</em> (by itself), <em>diastolic</em> (between beats), <em>depolarisation</em> (becoming less negative).</p>\n<p><strong>The three phases of the SA node action potential:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase 4: the slow climb.</strong> At its most negative, about −60 mV, the cell opens special channels that let positive ions, mainly Na⁺, leak in. This leak is the <strong><span data-term=\"funny-current\">funny current (I_f)</span></strong>, and its channels are called HCN channels. It is called &quot;funny&quot; because most channels open when a cell becomes <em>less</em> negative, but these open when it becomes <em>more</em> negative. Later in the climb, Ca²⁺ channels start to open too, and the outward K⁺ leak fades. The voltage rises steadily towards threshold, about −40 mV.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 0: the upstroke.</strong> At threshold, <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> open and Ca²⁺ rushes in. This upstroke is slower than a ventricular cell's, which is driven by fast Na⁺ channels. The SA node never gets negative enough to use its fast Na⁺ channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 3: repolarisation.</strong> The Ca²⁺ channels close, K⁺ flows out, and the voltage falls back to about −60 mV. The climb then starts again.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is no phase 1 or 2: an SA node cell has <strong>no plateau</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" alt=\"Side-by-side SA nodal pacemaker and ventricular cardiomyocyte action potentials: spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation and calcium upstroke in nodal cells contrasted with sodium upstroke and plateau in ventricular cells, above an ECG.\" width=\"1500\" height=\"1125\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells13010004\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jonker, Barnett, Boink &amp; Christoffels, <em>Cells</em> (2024), Figure 1</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Voltages are typical approximate values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture covers the SA node's location and its role as pacemaker; the ionic detail is foundation.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does an SA node cell fire again without any nerve signal?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The vagus nerve releases acetylcholine that triggers each new beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its voltage creeps up by itself after each beat until it reaches threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Neighbouring atrial muscle cells excite it at the end of each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Its resting voltage stays at −90 mV until stretch opens Na⁺ channels</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The SA node has no steady resting voltage. The funny current and later Ca²⁺ currents push the voltage up after every beat until it reaches threshold, so it fires again on its own.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is the funny current called \"funny\"?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It flows only when the heart rate is unusually high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is carried by Ca²⁺ instead of Na⁺ like other currents</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its channels open when the cell becomes more negative</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It flows outwards even though it makes the cell less negative</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Most channels open as a cell becomes less negative. HCN channels open as it becomes more negative, just after a beat, and let positive ions in, which starts the next climb.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why is the SA node's upstroke slower than a ventricular cell's?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It must wait for the AV node to release the signal first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its gap junctions slow the entry of Na⁺ from its neighbours</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It starts from −90 mV, so it has further to climb</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is driven by Ca²⁺ through L-type channels, not fast Na⁺</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The SA node never gets negative enough to use fast Na⁺ channels, so its upstroke relies on slower L-type Ca²⁺ channels.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm\">4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?</h2>\n<p>The lecture puts it simply: all parts of the conducting system can fire on their own, but they are controlled by the SA node, the pacemaker.</p>\n<p>The reason is speed. The SA node climbs to threshold fastest. Each beat it starts spreads through the conducting system and fires the slower cells <strong>before</strong> they reach threshold on their own. That resets them, so their own rhythm never gets a chance. <strong>The fastest pacemaker wins</strong>; the others still have automaticity, but they are always beaten to it.</p>\n<p>Being driven repeatedly at a faster rate also temporarily suppresses the slower cells' own firing. This is <strong><span data-term=\"overdrive-suppression\">overdrive suppression</span></strong>, and it is why there can be a short pause before a backup pacemaker takes over.</p>\n<p><strong>What if the SA node fails?</strong> The next fastest pacemaker takes over and produces an <strong><span data-term=\"escape-rhythm\">escape rhythm</span></strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Pacemaker</th>\n  <th>Typical rate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>SA node</td>\n  <td>60–100 beats/min (normal resting rhythm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>AV junction</td>\n  <td>about 40–60 beats/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricles (Purkinje fibres)</td>\n  <td>about 20–40 beats/min</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An escape rhythm keeps the ventricles beating, but more slowly, so it may not pump enough blood.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?</h2>\n<p>The AV node conducts <strong>slowly</strong>. Its cells are small, they have few gap junctions, and like the SA node their upstroke is driven by Ca²⁺. The signal takes about a tenth of a second to get through.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is that delay useful?</strong> It gives the atria time to <strong>finish contracting</strong> before the ventricles start. The atrial kick pushes the last portion of blood into the ventricles while the mitral and tricuspid valves are still open. Most filling, about 70% in the lecture, has already happened passively; the kick only tops it up.</p>\n<p><strong>You can see the delay on the ECG.</strong> The lecture describes the <strong><span data-term=\"pr-interval\">PR interval</span></strong>, normally 0.12–0.20 s, as the time from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crawling through the AV node. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/ecg?section=4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">→ Electrocardiography, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why can't the signal simply spread from the atria to the ventricles?</strong> A ring of fibrous tissue between them does not conduct. The AV node and bundle of His are the only normal route across. Because the AV node can only pass impulses so fast, it also protects the ventricles from being driven by a very fast atrial rhythm.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?</h2>\n<p>After the AV node, the signal enters the <strong><span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">His–Purkinje system</span></strong> and speeds up dramatically:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Tissue</th>\n  <th>Approximate conduction speed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>AV node</td>\n  <td>about 0.05 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ordinary ventricular muscle</td>\n  <td>about 0.3–0.5 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Purkinje fibres</td>\n  <td>about 2–4 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Purkinje fibres are fast because they are <strong>wide</strong>, packed with <strong>gap junctions</strong>, and use <strong>fast Na⁺ channels</strong> for their upstroke. They spread the signal over the inner walls of both ventricles in a few hundredths of a second, so the whole ventricular muscle is activated within about 0.1 s, the width of the QRS complex.</p>\n<p>The order is roughly: the wall between the ventricles first, then the inner walls near the apex, then outwards through the muscle to the outer surface, and the base last. The details belong in the ECG chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Slow in one place, fast in another.</strong> The two speeds do two different jobs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slow AV conduction gets the timing between chambers right:</strong> the atria finish before the ventricles start.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast His–Purkinje conduction gets the coordination within the ventricles right:</strong> all the muscle contracts together, so pressure builds quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"conduction-timeline\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Follow one heartbeat's signal</figcaption><p>What it shows: a simple heart outline with the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres labelled. Below it, an ECG strip. Controls: Play sends a glowing signal along the pathway, and the ECG strip draws in step with it: the atria light up with the P wave; the signal pauses at the AV node, labelled \"AV delay\", along the flat PR segment; it then races down the His bundle and the Purkinje fibres, and both ventricles light up together with the QRS complex. Step buttons let students stop at each stage. Schematic timing.</p></figure>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"ScZVGHDisa0\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ScZVGHDisa0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Conduction speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture gives the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s).</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why doesn't the AV junction set its own rhythm?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Its own firing is blocked by the fibrous skeleton</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Each SA impulse fires it before it reaches threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It only fires when the vagus nerve stimulates it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It fires faster, but its beats are too weak to count</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The SA node's impulse arrives and fires the AV junction before it can reach threshold by itself, resetting it every beat. The fastest pacemaker wins.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The SA node stops firing, and the ventricles keep beating at about 45 beats/min. What is most likely setting this rhythm?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The SA node, firing more slowly because of vagal tone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The Purkinje fibres in the ventricle walls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A backup pacemaker in the AV junction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Bachmann's bundle in the wall of the atria</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The AV junction's own rate is about 40–60 beats/min, so it is the next pacemaker in line. A ventricular escape rhythm is usually slower, about 20–40 beats/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> On the ECG, which interval mostly reflects the AV-node delay?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The QT interval</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The QRS duration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ST segment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The PR interval</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The PR interval runs from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crossing the AV node.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why must conduction be slow in the AV node but fast in the Purkinje fibres?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Slow AV conduction lets the atria finish first; fast Purkinje conduction makes the ventricles act together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Slow AV conduction saves energy; fast Purkinje conduction lets each ventricle contract twice in every beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Slow AV conduction prevents tetanus; fast Purkinje conduction shortens the ventricular refractory period</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Slow AV conduction lets the SA node recover; fast Purkinje conduction raises the overall heart rate</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The AV delay gets the timing between atria and ventricles right. The fast Purkinje network makes all the ventricular muscle contract together.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\">7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?</h2>\n<p>The key rule: <strong>the faster the phase 4 climb reaches threshold, the sooner the next beat.</strong> The nerves change heart rate by changing that climb.</p>\n<p><strong>At rest, the vagus is already braking the heart.</strong> An SA node with no nerve input fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest, the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine all the time (<strong><span data-term=\"vagal-tone\">vagal tone</span></strong>), holding the rate down to about 60–80. The lecture's evidence: heart rate is controlled mainly by this constant vagal discharge, so cutting the vagus (vagotomy) or blocking it with <strong>atropine</strong> makes the heart rate rise.</p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation speeds the climb.</strong> Noradrenaline acts on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> and raises cAMP inside the SA node cells. More funny current and more Ca²⁺ current flow, the phase 4 climb gets <strong>steeper</strong>, threshold comes sooner, and the heart rate rises. Conduction through the AV node also speeds up, so the PR interval shortens.</p>\n<p><strong>Vagal stimulation slows the climb.</strong> Acetylcholine acts on <strong>M₂ receptors</strong>. It lowers cAMP, so there is less funny current. It also opens extra K⁺ channels, so the cell starts each climb <strong>lower</strong> (more negative) and climbs <strong>more slowly</strong>. Threshold comes later, and the heart rate falls. AV conduction slows, so the PR interval lengthens.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"pacemaker-slope\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>How nerves set the heart rate</figcaption><p>What it shows: the SA node voltage over 3 seconds, with a dashed threshold line. Controls: four buttons: Strong vagal (starts lower, shallow climb, about 41 beats/min); Rest (vagal tone, about 75 beats/min); No nerve input (about 100 beats/min); Sympathetic (steep climb, about 150 beats/min). The trace redraws for each, the beats-per-minute readout updates, and a one-line explanation names what changed (the slope, the starting voltage, or both). Schematic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Two words for two effects:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span></strong> (<em>chrono</em> = time): an effect on <strong>heart rate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"dromotropy\">Dromotropy</span></strong> (<em>dromo</em> = running): an effect on <strong>conduction speed</strong>, especially through the AV node.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation is positively chronotropic and dromotropic; vagal stimulation is negatively chronotropic and dromotropic.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The intrinsic SA node rate of about 100 beats/min is a standard textbook value. The lecture (Circulation 2) states that heart rate is controlled mainly by tonic vagal discharge, and that vagotomy and atropine raise it.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances</h2>\n<p>Electrical problems come in two kinds: the pacemaker fails, or the signal is delayed or blocked on its way.</p>\n<p><strong>The SA node fails.</strong> It fires too slowly or pauses. A backup pacemaker may take over with a slower escape rhythm (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm\">§4</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>The signal is blocked at the AV node (<span data-term=\"heart-block\">heart block</span>).</strong> Think of it as three levels of trouble:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First degree: every impulse gets through, but late.</strong> The PR interval is longer than 0.20 s.</li>\n<li><strong>Second degree: some impulses don't get through.</strong> Some atrial beats are not followed by a ventricular beat; those beats are &quot;dropped&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Third degree (complete): no impulses get through.</strong> The atria keep beating at the SA node's rate, and the ventricles beat independently at a slow escape rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In complete block, the atria sometimes contract while the tricuspid valve is shut. Blood is pushed back into the neck veins, giving the <strong>giant &quot;a&quot; waves</strong> the lecture describes in the JVP. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses\">→ Pulses and JVP</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Both kinds of problem reduce pumping: the heart rate may be too slow, and the atria and ventricles no longer work in step.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> According to the lecture, atropine (which blocks vagal acetylcholine) raises the resting heart rate. What does this show?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Atropine directly speeds up the funny current in the SA node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Sympathetic nerves cannot act on the heart at rest</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. At rest, the vagus nerve continuously slows the SA node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The SA node needs acetylcholine before it can fire</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Removing the vagal brake lets the SA node rise towards its own rate of about 100 beats/min. So at rest, constant vagal tone is holding the rate down.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does noradrenaline make the SA node fire faster?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It makes the cell rest at −90 mV, closer to the Na⁺ threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It steepens the slow climb of phase 4, so threshold comes sooner</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It adds a plateau, which shortens the time between beats</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It slows the AV node, so the SA node has less to wait for</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases the funny current and Ca²⁺ current. The climb gets steeper, so each beat starts sooner.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Vagal acetylcholine opens extra K⁺ channels in the SA node. Why does this slow the heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. K⁺ entering the cell makes phase 0 much larger and slower</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. K⁺ blocks the gap junctions between the SA node and the atria</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The plateau lengthens, so the refractory period lasts longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The cell starts lower and climbs more slowly, so threshold comes later</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> K⁺ leaving the cell pulls the voltage down, so each climb starts more negative. With less funny current the climb is also shallower, so it takes longer to reach threshold.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> In complete heart block, the atria beat at 75 per minute and the ventricles at 35 per minute. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles follow their own slower escape pacemaker</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The SA node fires twice for every ventricular beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The AV node lets only every other impulse through</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The vagus nerve slows the ventricles but not the atria</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> In complete block no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. A ventricular escape pacemaker takes over at its own slow rate, independent of the atria. Letting every other impulse through would be second-degree block.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\">9. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pathway:</strong> SA node → atria → AV node → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Automaticity:</strong> specialised cells fire on their own; the nerves only change the rate.</li>\n<li><strong>The SA node</strong> sits where the superior vena cava joins the right atrium. It is the pacemaker because it reaches threshold first.</li>\n<li><strong>The SA node action potential:</strong> phase 4 is a slow climb, mainly the funny current (Na⁺ in, through channels that open when the cell is most negative); phase 0 is Ca²⁺ in; phase 3 is K⁺ out. There is no plateau.</li>\n<li><strong>The fastest pacemaker wins.</strong> If the SA node fails, the AV junction (about 40–60/min) or the ventricles (about 20–40/min) take over with an escape rhythm.</li>\n<li><strong>The AV node</strong> sits just behind the tricuspid valve. It conducts slowly (about 0.05 m/s), so the atria finish contracting first. On the ECG this is the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s).</li>\n<li><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong> conduct fast (2–4 m/s), so both ventricles contract together, within about 0.1 s (the QRS).</li>\n<li><strong>Vagal tone:</strong> the SA node's own rate is about 100/min. At rest the vagus holds it at 60–80, which is why atropine or vagotomy raises the heart rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Nerves change the phase 4 climb.</strong> Sympathetic (β₁, cAMP) makes it steeper and the rate faster. Vagal (M₂, more K⁺, less funny current) starts it lower and makes it shallower, so the rate is slower.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart block:</strong> first degree, late; second degree, some dropped; third degree, none get through, with the atria and ventricles beating independently.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity 1. Why does the heart need a conducting system? Pumping works only if the chambers contract in the right order and at the right moment: the atria before the ventricles, so the atria can top up the ventricles first; all parts of each ventricle together, so pressure builds fast enough to eject blood. That gives the heart two electrical jobs: start each beat, and deliver the signal in the right order. The specialised conducting system does both. Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a nerve impulse for every contraction. Specialised heart cells fire on their own; this is automaticity. The nerves only speed this built-in rhythm up or slow it down ( §7). As the lecture summarises, depolarisation spreads from the SA node to the AV node, then down the bundle of His to the rest of the heart, and contraction starts after depolarisation. 2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction SA node → atrial muscle → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle Structure Where it is and what it does SA node (sinoatrial node) In the wall of the right atrium, where the superior vena cava joins it. Starts each beat. Internodal pathways Carry the signal across the right atrium to the AV node. Bachmann's bundle also carries it to the left atrium. AV node (atrioventricular node) Low in the right atrium, in the wall between the two atria, just behind the tricuspid valve. Holds the signal back briefly. Bundle of His Carries the signal from the AV node through the fibrous barrier into the wall between the ventricles. Right and left bundle branches Run down each side of the wall between the ventricles. Purkinje fibres Spread through the inner walls of both ventricles and hand the signal to the muscle. Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system. Image credit Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. What are the internodal pathways? The lecture names three: anterior (including Bachmann's bundle), middle (Wenckebach) and posterior (Thorel). They are routes the signal prefers, not insulated wires; the signal also spreads through ordinary atrial muscle. Bachmann's bundle matters most because it carries the signal across to the left atrium, so both atria contract almost together. Source note: The three internodal tracts are named as in the lecture. Most anatomists now describe them as preferred routes through atrial muscle rather than separate insulated bundles. 3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker? Because it reaches threshold first. A working ventricular muscle cell sits at a steady −90 mV until a neighbour excites it. An SA node cell never sits still. After each beat, its voltage immediately starts creeping upwards by itself until it reaches threshold and fires. This slow climb is the pacemaker potential, also called spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: spontaneous (by itself), diastolic (between beats), depolarisation (becoming less negative). The three phases of the SA node action potential: Phase 4: the slow climb. At its most negative, about −60 mV, the cell opens special channels that let positive ions, mainly Na⁺, leak in. This leak is the funny current (I_f), and its channels are called HCN channels. It is called \"funny\" because most channels open when a cell becomes less negative, but these open when it becomes more negative. Later in the climb, Ca²⁺ channels start to open too, and the outward K⁺ leak fades. The voltage rises steadily towards threshold, about −40 mV. Phase 0: the upstroke. At threshold, L-type Ca²⁺ channels open and Ca²⁺ rushes in. This upstroke is slower than a ventricular cell's, which is driven by fast Na⁺ channels. The SA node never gets negative enough to use its fast Na⁺ channels. Phase 3: repolarisation. The Ca²⁺ channels close, K⁺ flows out, and the voltage falls back to about −60 mV. The climb then starts again. There is no phase 1 or 2: an SA node cell has no plateau. Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials. Image credit Image: Jonker, Barnett, Boink & Christoffels, Cells (2024), Figure 1 · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. Source note: Voltages are typical approximate values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture covers the SA node's location and its role as pacemaker; the ionic detail is foundation. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why does an SA node cell fire again without any nerve signal? A. The vagus nerve releases acetylcholine that triggers each new beat B. Its voltage creeps up by itself after each beat until it reaches threshold C. Neighbouring atrial muscle cells excite it at the end of each beat D. Its resting voltage stays at −90 mV until stretch opens Na⁺ channels Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The SA node has no steady resting voltage. The funny current and later Ca²⁺ currents push the voltage up after every beat until it reaches threshold, so it fires again on its own. 2. Why is the funny current called \"funny\"? A. It flows only when the heart rate is unusually high B. It is carried by Ca²⁺ instead of Na⁺ like other currents C. Its channels open when the cell becomes more negative D. It flows outwards even though it makes the cell less negative Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Most channels open as a cell becomes less negative. HCN channels open as it becomes more negative, just after a beat, and let positive ions in, which starts the next climb. 3. Why is the SA node's upstroke slower than a ventricular cell's? A. It must wait for the AV node to release the signal first B. Its gap junctions slow the entry of Na⁺ from its neighbours C. It starts from −90 mV, so it has further to climb D. It is driven by Ca²⁺ through L-type channels, not fast Na⁺ Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The SA node never gets negative enough to use fast Na⁺ channels, so its upstroke relies on slower L-type Ca²⁺ channels. 4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm? The lecture puts it simply: all parts of the conducting system can fire on their own, but they are controlled by the SA node, the pacemaker. The reason is speed. The SA node climbs to threshold fastest. Each beat it starts spreads through the conducting system and fires the slower cells before they reach threshold on their own. That resets them, so their own rhythm never gets a chance. The fastest pacemaker wins; the others still have automaticity, but they are always beaten to it. Being driven repeatedly at a faster rate also temporarily suppresses the slower cells' own firing. This is overdrive suppression, and it is why there can be a short pause before a backup pacemaker takes over. What if the SA node fails? The next fastest pacemaker takes over and produces an escape rhythm: Pacemaker Typical rate SA node 60–100 beats/min (normal resting rhythm) AV junction about 40–60 beats/min Ventricles (Purkinje fibres) about 20–40 beats/min An escape rhythm keeps the ventricles beating, but more slowly, so it may not pump enough blood. 5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction? The AV node conducts slowly. Its cells are small, they have few gap junctions, and like the SA node their upstroke is driven by Ca²⁺. The signal takes about a tenth of a second to get through. Why is that delay useful? It gives the atria time to finish contracting before the ventricles start. The atrial kick pushes the last portion of blood into the ventricles while the mitral and tricuspid valves are still open. Most filling, about 70% in the lecture, has already happened passively; the kick only tops it up. You can see the delay on the ECG. The lecture describes the PR interval, normally 0.12–0.20 s, as the time from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crawling through the AV node. ( → Electrocardiography, §4) Why can't the signal simply spread from the atria to the ventricles? A ring of fibrous tissue between them does not conduct. The AV node and bundle of His are the only normal route across. Because the AV node can only pass impulses so fast, it also protects the ventricles from being driven by a very fast atrial rhythm. 6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid? After the AV node, the signal enters the His–Purkinje system and speeds up dramatically: Tissue Approximate conduction speed AV node about 0.05 m/s Ordinary ventricular muscle about 0.3–0.5 m/s Purkinje fibres about 2–4 m/s Purkinje fibres are fast because they are wide, packed with gap junctions, and use fast Na⁺ channels for their upstroke. They spread the signal over the inner walls of both ventricles in a few hundredths of a second, so the whole ventricular muscle is activated within about 0.1 s, the width of the QRS complex. The order is roughly: the wall between the ventricles first, then the inner walls near the apex, then outwards through the muscle to the outer surface, and the base last. The details belong in the ECG chapter. Slow in one place, fast in another. The two speeds do two different jobs: Slow AV conduction gets the timing between chambers right: the atria finish before the ventricles start. Fast His–Purkinje conduction gets the coordination within the ventricles right: all the muscle contracts together, so pressure builds quickly. Follow one heartbeat's signal What it shows: a simple heart outline with the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres labelled. Below it, an ECG strip. Controls: Play sends a glowing signal along the pathway, and the ECG strip draws in step with it: the atria light up with the P wave; the signal pauses at the AV node, labelled \"AV delay\", along the flat PR segment; it then races down the His bundle and the Purkinje fibres, and both ventricles light up together with the QRS complex. Step buttons let students stop at each stage. Schematic timing. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. Source note: Conduction speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture gives the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s). Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why doesn't the AV junction set its own rhythm? A. Its own firing is blocked by the fibrous skeleton B. Each SA impulse fires it before it reaches threshold C. It only fires when the vagus nerve stimulates it D. It fires faster, but its beats are too weak to count Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The SA node's impulse arrives and fires the AV junction before it can reach threshold by itself, resetting it every beat. The fastest pacemaker wins. 2. The SA node stops firing, and the ventricles keep beating at about 45 beats/min. What is most likely setting this rhythm? A. The SA node, firing more slowly because of vagal tone B. The Purkinje fibres in the ventricle walls C. A backup pacemaker in the AV junction D. Bachmann's bundle in the wall of the atria Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The AV junction's own rate is about 40–60 beats/min, so it is the next pacemaker in line. A ventricular escape rhythm is usually slower, about 20–40 beats/min. 3. On the ECG, which interval mostly reflects the AV-node delay? A. The QT interval B. The QRS duration C. The ST segment D. The PR interval Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The PR interval runs from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crossing the AV node. 4. Why must conduction be slow in the AV node but fast in the Purkinje fibres? A. Slow AV conduction lets the atria finish first; fast Purkinje conduction makes the ventricles act together B. Slow AV conduction saves energy; fast Purkinje conduction lets each ventricle contract twice in every beat C. Slow AV conduction prevents tetanus; fast Purkinje conduction shortens the ventricular refractory period D. Slow AV conduction lets the SA node recover; fast Purkinje conduction raises the overall heart rate Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The AV delay gets the timing between atria and ventricles right. The fast Purkinje network makes all the ventricular muscle contract together. 7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity? The key rule: the faster the phase 4 climb reaches threshold, the sooner the next beat. The nerves change heart rate by changing that climb. At rest, the vagus is already braking the heart. An SA node with no nerve input fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest, the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine all the time ( vagal tone), holding the rate down to about 60–80. The lecture's evidence: heart rate is controlled mainly by this constant vagal discharge, so cutting the vagus (vagotomy) or blocking it with atropine makes the heart rate rise. Sympathetic stimulation speeds the climb. Noradrenaline acts on β₁ receptors and raises cAMP inside the SA node cells. More funny current and more Ca²⁺ current flow, the phase 4 climb gets steeper, threshold comes sooner, and the heart rate rises. Conduction through the AV node also speeds up, so the PR interval shortens. Vagal stimulation slows the climb. Acetylcholine acts on M₂ receptors. It lowers cAMP, so there is less funny current. It also opens extra K⁺ channels, so the cell starts each climb lower (more negative) and climbs more slowly. Threshold comes later, and the heart rate falls. AV conduction slows, so the PR interval lengthens. How nerves set the heart rate What it shows: the SA node voltage over 3 seconds, with a dashed threshold line. Controls: four buttons: Strong vagal (starts lower, shallow climb, about 41 beats/min); Rest (vagal tone, about 75 beats/min); No nerve input (about 100 beats/min); Sympathetic (steep climb, about 150 beats/min). The trace redraws for each, the beats-per-minute readout updates, and a one-line explanation names what changed (the slope, the starting voltage, or both). Schematic. Two words for two effects: Chronotropy ( chrono = time): an effect on heart rate. Dromotropy ( dromo = running): an effect on conduction speed, especially through the AV node. Sympathetic stimulation is positively chronotropic and dromotropic; vagal stimulation is negatively chronotropic and dromotropic. Source note: The intrinsic SA node rate of about 100 beats/min is a standard textbook value. The lecture (Circulation 2) states that heart rate is controlled mainly by tonic vagal discharge, and that vagotomy and atropine raise it. 8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances Electrical problems come in two kinds: the pacemaker fails, or the signal is delayed or blocked on its way. The SA node fails. It fires too slowly or pauses. A backup pacemaker may take over with a slower escape rhythm ( §4). The signal is blocked at the AV node ( heart block). Think of it as three levels of trouble: First degree: every impulse gets through, but late. The PR interval is longer than 0.20 s. Second degree: some impulses don't get through. Some atrial beats are not followed by a ventricular beat; those beats are \"dropped\". Third degree (complete): no impulses get through. The atria keep beating at the SA node's rate, and the ventricles beat independently at a slow escape rate. In complete block, the atria sometimes contract while the tricuspid valve is shut. Blood is pushed back into the neck veins, giving the giant \"a\" waves the lecture describes in the JVP. ( → Pulses and JVP) Both kinds of problem reduce pumping: the heart rate may be too slow, and the atria and ventricles no longer work in step. Understanding Check 1. According to the lecture, atropine (which blocks vagal acetylcholine) raises the resting heart rate. What does this show? A. Atropine directly speeds up the funny current in the SA node B. Sympathetic nerves cannot act on the heart at rest C. At rest, the vagus nerve continuously slows the SA node D. The SA node needs acetylcholine before it can fire Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Removing the vagal brake lets the SA node rise towards its own rate of about 100 beats/min. So at rest, constant vagal tone is holding the rate down. 2. How does noradrenaline make the SA node fire faster? A. It makes the cell rest at −90 mV, closer to the Na⁺ threshold B. It steepens the slow climb of phase 4, so threshold comes sooner C. It adds a plateau, which shortens the time between beats D. It slows the AV node, so the SA node has less to wait for Show answer and explanation Answer: B. β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases the funny current and Ca²⁺ current. The climb gets steeper, so each beat starts sooner. 3. Vagal acetylcholine opens extra K⁺ channels in the SA node. Why does this slow the heart? A. K⁺ entering the cell makes phase 0 much larger and slower B. K⁺ blocks the gap junctions between the SA node and the atria C. The plateau lengthens, so the refractory period lasts longer D. The cell starts lower and climbs more slowly, so threshold comes later Show answer and explanation Answer: D. K⁺ leaving the cell pulls the voltage down, so each climb starts more negative. With less funny current the climb is also shallower, so it takes longer to reach threshold. 4. In complete heart block, the atria beat at 75 per minute and the ventricles at 35 per minute. Why? A. The ventricles follow their own slower escape pacemaker B. The SA node fires twice for every ventricular beat C. The AV node lets only every other impulse through D. The vagus nerve slows the ventricles but not the atria Show answer and explanation Answer: A. In complete block no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. A ventricular escape pacemaker takes over at its own slow rate, independent of the atria. Letting every other impulse through would be second-degree block. 9. Chapter Essentials Pathway: SA node → atria → AV node → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle. Automaticity: specialised cells fire on their own; the nerves only change the rate. The SA node sits where the superior vena cava joins the right atrium. It is the pacemaker because it reaches threshold first. The SA node action potential: phase 4 is a slow climb, mainly the funny current (Na⁺ in, through channels that open when the cell is most negative); phase 0 is Ca²⁺ in; phase 3 is K⁺ out. There is no plateau. The fastest pacemaker wins. If the SA node fails, the AV junction (about 40–60/min) or the ventricles (about 20–40/min) take over with an escape rhythm. The AV node sits just behind the tricuspid valve. It conducts slowly (about 0.05 m/s), so the atria finish contracting first. On the ECG this is the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s). Purkinje fibres conduct fast (2–4 m/s), so both ventricles contract together, within about 0.1 s (the QRS). Vagal tone: the SA node's own rate is about 100/min. At rest the vagus holds it at 60–80, which is why atropine or vagotomy raises the heart rate. Nerves change the phase 4 climb. Sympathetic (β₁, cAMP) makes it steeper and the rate faster. Vagal (M₂, more K⁺, less funny current) starts it lower and makes it shallower, so the rate is slower. Heart block: first degree, late; second degree, some dropped; third degree, none get through, with the atria and ventricles beating independently."},{"slug":"cardiac-cycle","title":"The Cardiac Cycle","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"11-21","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing","title":"1. One heartbeat: what the atria and ventricles are doing"},{"id":"2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows","title":"2. The one rule every valve follows"},{"id":"3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle","title":"3. Filling: how blood enters the relaxed ventricle"},{"id":"4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves","title":"4. Isovolumetric contraction: contracting, but no blood leaves"},{"id":"5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves","title":"5. Ejection: why blood finally leaves"},{"id":"6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation","title":"6. Closing the outflow valves and isovolumetric relaxation"},{"id":"7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump","title":"7. How much blood does the ventricle pump?"},{"id":"8-the-whole-cycle-on-one-map","title":"8. The whole cycle on one map"},{"id":"9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time","title":"9. Reading the Wiggers diagram one trace at a time"},{"id":"10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate","title":"10. Right versus left heart, S2 splitting and heart rate"},{"id":"11-understanding-check","title":"11. Understanding Check"},{"id":"12-chapter-essentials","title":"12. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>The Cardiac Cycle</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">1. One heartbeat: what the atria and ventricles are doing</h2>\n<p>The <strong>cardiac cycle</strong> is everything that happens in the heart during one heartbeat: the atria and ventricles each contract and relax once, in a fixed order. <strong>Systole</strong> means contraction and <strong>diastole</strong> means relaxation. On their own, the words are incomplete. Always ask <em>systole of which chambers?</em> The atria and ventricles are often doing different things, though for a large part of each beat they are both relaxed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why do the atria go first?</strong> Each beat starts with an electrical impulse from the <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> in the right atrium. The impulse spreads across the atria first, then pauses briefly at the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span> before reaching the ventricles. So the atria are activated, and contract, just before the ventricles. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Follow one beat:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole.</strong> At the end of ventricular diastole, the atria contract and push a final portion of blood into ventricles that are already nearly full.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole.</strong> The ventricles contract and eject blood. Meanwhile the atria relax and begin refilling with blood returning from the veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Both chambers in diastole.</strong> The ventricles now relax too, so both are relaxed together. For a brief moment the ventricles still cannot fill, because their pressure is higher than atrial pressure. As they relax further, their pressure falls below atrial pressure and blood flows in passively.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole again</strong>, and the next beat begins.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How long does each part take?</strong> At about 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 60 ÷ 75 = <strong>0.8 s</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Part of the cycle</th>\n  <th>Duration</th>\n  <th>Where it comes from</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular systole</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.3 s</td>\n  <td>Typical resting value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular diastole</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.5 s</td>\n  <td>0.8 − 0.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Atrial systole</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.1 s</td>\n  <td>Last part of ventricular diastole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Both chambers relaxed</td>\n  <td>≈ 0.4 s</td>\n  <td>0.5 − 0.1, half of every beat</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are rounded teaching values; the lecture gives no phase durations.</p>\n<p>That shared 0.4 s matters. After a short pause while ventricular pressure is still falling, it is when most ventricular filling happens. This is why atrial contraction is only a top-up. It is also why very fast heart rates, which shorten this period most, can limit filling (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Section 10</a>).</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"chamber-timeline\"><figcaption>One heartbeat at 75 beats/min: what the atria and ventricles are doing</figcaption><p>Two aligned bars across one 0.8 s cycle. Atria: systole for the first 0.1 s, then diastole. Ventricles: systole from 0.1 s to 0.4 s, then diastole. From 0.4 s to 0.8 s both are relaxed: first a short pause while the ventricle is still relaxing with no filling, then passive filling. Proportions approximate, at 75 beats/min.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>What drives each step?</strong> Every phase follows one chain:</p>\n<p><strong>Electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes</strong></p>\n<p>The electrical signal starts the chain but never moves blood or valves directly. Each link takes a moment, so the steps happen in sequence, not all at once. <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Section 2</a> explains the key link: how pressure moves the valves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">2. The one rule every valve follows</h2>\n<p>The heart has four valves, in two pairs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AV (atrioventricular) valves</strong> sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the <strong>mitral</strong> valve on the left and the <strong>tricuspid</strong> valve on the right.</li>\n<li><strong>Semilunar valves</strong> sit between each ventricle and its artery: the <strong>aortic</strong> valve on the left and the <strong>pulmonary</strong> valve on the right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All four obey one rule. A valve <strong>opens</strong> when pressure upstream (in the chamber blood is coming from) is higher than pressure downstream (where blood is going). It <strong>closes</strong> when that difference reverses.</p>\n<p><strong>How can pressure alone do this?</strong> Each valve is made of thin flaps called cusps, shaped to open in one direction only. Higher pressure upstream pushes the cusps apart, and blood flows through. When downstream pressure becomes higher, blood starts to move backwards and pushes the cusps together until they seal. No nerve or electrical signal is involved. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\">→ Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §3</a>: valves keep flow one-way)</em></p>\n<p><strong>What about the papillary muscles?</strong> The AV valve cusps are tethered by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles in the ventricular wall. These muscles contract with the ventricle, but they do not open or close the valve. They hold the cusps in place so that rising ventricular pressure cannot push them inside out into the atrium.</p>\n<p>Now apply the rule to the left heart: <strong>left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"pressure-rules\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Left-heart pressure rules\"><div><strong>Mitral valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LA</sub> &gt; P<sub>LV</sub></span><small>Blood enters the ventricle</small></div><div><strong>Aortic valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LV</sub> &gt; P<sub>aorta</sub></span><small>Blood leaves the ventricle</small></div></div>\n\n<p>Here P means pressure, LA is left atrium and LV is left ventricle. The two valves respond to <strong>different</strong> pressure differences, so there are moments when <strong>both are closed at once</strong>. Those moments explain two phases you will meet in Sections <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">4</a> and <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">6</a>, when the ventricle's pressure changes but its volume does not.</p>\n<p>One refinement comes later: moving blood carries momentum, so flow and valve closure can lag slightly behind a change in pressure (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">Section 6</a>).</p>\n<p>The right heart follows the same rule at lower pressures, through the tricuspid and pulmonary valves. We follow the left heart first and return to the right in <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Section 10</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">3. Filling: how blood enters the relaxed ventricle</h2>\n<p>Blood keeps returning to the heart all the time, even while the ventricles are contracting and their <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span> are shut. During that time the relaxed atria act as <strong>reservoirs</strong>, collecting the returning blood, so atrial pressure slowly rises.</p>\n<p>When the ventricle relaxes, its pressure falls. As soon as ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the <strong>mitral valve opens</strong> and the stored blood rushes in. No contraction is needed for this: blood simply moves down the pressure difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is filling fast at first, then slow?</strong> At first the difference is large: the atrium is full and pressurised, and the ventricle has only just relaxed. So filling is <strong>rapid</strong>. As blood enters, ventricular pressure rises only a little, because the relaxed ventricle stretches easily. It stays low, as the lecture notes, but it creeps closer to atrial pressure. With a smaller difference, inflow slows. This slower period is <strong>diastasis</strong>. Throughout passive filling, blood flows from the veins <em>through</em> the open atrium into the ventricle, so the atrium is acting as a <strong>conduit</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why do the atria contract at all?</strong> Near the end of diastole, the atria contract and push in a final volume, the <strong>atrial kick</strong>, with the atrium acting as a <strong>booster pump</strong>. The lecture describes filling as about <strong>70% passive and 30% atrial</strong>. These are teaching proportions: at rest the atrial share is often 10–30%. The kick matters more in two situations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A stiff ventricle</strong> resists passive stretching, so less blood enters passively and the active push makes up more of the total.</li>\n<li><strong>A fast heart rate</strong> shortens diastole, leaving less time for passive filling, so the final push contributes a larger share.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When filling ends, the ventricle holds its <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong>, about <strong>130 mL</strong> in the lecture's example. This is the volume it starts its next contraction with (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">Section 7</a>).</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"diastolic-plot\"><figcaption>Ventricular filling: why it is fast, then slow, then topped up</figcaption><p>Left atrial and left ventricular pressure across ventricular diastole, with ventricular volume beneath. When the mitral valve opens the pressure gap is large and volume rises steeply (rapid filling). The gap then narrows and volume rises slowly (diastasis). Atrial contraction makes the a wave and adds a final step of volume up to EDV. Ventricular pressure stays low throughout. Schematic; pressures not to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>What is happening electrically during filling?</strong> The ventricles repolarised near the end of their contraction (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a>), before filling began. During rapid filling and diastasis, no excitation wave is spreading through the working heart muscle, and none is needed: the pressure difference alone moves the blood. The <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> is not idle, though. Its pacemaker cells are slowly depolarising towards threshold. When they fire, the impulse spreads across the atria (the <span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span> on an ECG) and atrial contraction follows. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">→ Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 3</strong></p>\n<p>A patient's atria stop contracting effectively, but ventricular relaxation is normal. Why does most ventricular filling continue?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Venous blood is pushed through the closed AV valves by contracting veins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Ventricular contraction draws blood back from the atria during systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The SA node keeps triggering small atrial contractions during diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Atrial pressure exceeds relaxed ventricular pressure, so blood flows passively</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Filling needs only a pressure difference across an open AV valve. Atrial systole adds a final top-up. Losing it reduces filling but does not stop it.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">4. Isovolumetric contraction: contracting, but no blood leaves</h2>\n<p>We left the ventricle full, holding its <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span>, with the atria just finished contracting (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">Section 3</a>). After the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV nodal</span> pause (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">Section 1</a>), the impulse races through the <strong><span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">His–Purkinje system</span></strong>, activating the ventricular muscle almost all at once. On an ECG, this ventricular activation is the <strong><span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span></strong> (more in <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">Section 9</a>). <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why does force lag slightly behind activation?</strong> Electrical activation first has to release calcium inside each muscle cell; only then can the cell develop force. So ventricular pressure starts rising a moment after the QRS. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">→ Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why does the mitral valve close so quickly?</strong> Atrial pressure is only a few mmHg, so ventricular pressure overtakes it almost as soon as contraction begins. The pressure difference across the mitral and tricuspid valves reverses and they <strong>close</strong>. This closure contributes to the <strong>first heart sound (<span data-term=\"s1\">S1</span>)</strong>.</p>\n<p>But ventricular pressure is still far below aortic pressure, so the <strong>aortic valve stays closed</strong>. Now <strong>both valves are closed</strong>, and the ventricle is a sealed chamber.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does pressure climb so steeply?</strong> Blood cannot be compressed. With no way in or out, the volume cannot change, so all the force the muscle develops goes into raising pressure. The muscle fibres build tension but barely shorten, as the lecture notes. Pressure therefore climbs steeply while volume stays fixed at EDV. This is <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong> (<em>iso</em> = same, <em>volumetric</em> = volume). It is the start of <span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">ventricular systole</span>, not yet ejection.</p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↑</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = EDV</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Meanwhile, the atria</strong> have relaxed and are again collecting venous blood behind closed <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span>. As ventricular pressure rises, the closed AV valves are the only part of the sealed chamber that can give a little. As the lecture puts it, they bulge back into the atrium. This causes a small bump in atrial pressure, the <strong>c wave</strong>. The atrium is not contracting; it is being pushed on.</p>\n<p><strong>What ends this phase?</strong> Ventricular pressure must exceed pressure in its outflow artery. The lecture uses about <strong>80 mmHg</strong> for aortic diastolic pressure and about <strong>10 mmHg</strong> for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure. These are typical reference values, not fixed thresholds in every person. <strong>Contraction is not the same as ejection:</strong> the ventricle must first overcome the pressure already in the artery.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says closure of the AV valves \"initiates systole\". More precisely, ventricular contraction starts first, and the rising pressure then closes the AV valves. Valve closure marks the start of isovolumetric contraction rather than causing it.</aside>\n\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"valve-stepper\"><figcaption>Isovolumetric contraction in three steps</figcaption><p>Step 1: ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, so the mitral and tricuspid valves close. Step 2: all four valves are closed; ventricular pressure rises while volume stays the same. Step 3: ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, so the aortic and pulmonary valves open and ejection begins. The atria stay relaxed and keep filling throughout.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Just after the mitral valve closes, LV pressure climbs steeply but LV volume does not change. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed, so blood has no exit</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The ventricular muscle has not yet been depolarised by the impulse</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood leaves the ventricle as fast as it enters from the atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The aortic valve is open, but aortic pressure prevents any outflow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Contraction is already under way and raising pressure, but with both valves shut there is nowhere for blood to go.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A person's aortic diastolic pressure is higher than usual. Ventricular activation and contractility are unchanged. What happens to isovolumetric contraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It shortens, because the aortic valve opens at a lower LV pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It stays the same, because valve opening depends on electrical timing</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It lengthens, because LV pressure must climb higher to open the valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It disappears, because the mitral valve stays open until ejection</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The aortic valve opens only when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure. A higher starting aortic pressure means the ventricle must build more pressure first, so, all else equal, this phase lasts longer.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">5. Ejection: why blood finally leaves</h2>\n<p>As soon as LV pressure rises above aortic pressure, the <strong>aortic valve opens</strong> (on the right, the pulmonary valve opens the same way). Blood flows into the artery and <strong>ventricular volume begins to fall</strong>. <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">Isovolumetric contraction</span> is over; ejection has begun.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is ejection rapid at first?</strong> At the moment the valve opens, the muscle is contracting strongly and the pressure difference pushing blood out is at its largest. Blood accelerates out quickly: <strong>rapid ejection</strong>. Ventricular pressure keeps rising for a while, peaking at about <strong>120 mmHg</strong> in the lecture's example, even though volume is already falling. Pressure and volume measure different things and need not move together. On the volume curve, this is the steep part of the fall.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does it then slow down?</strong> Two things shrink the pressure difference at once:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The muscle weakens.</strong> As contraction passes its peak, ventricular force and pressure fall.</li>\n<li><strong>The aorta fills.</strong> Blood entering the aorta stretches its elastic wall and raises aortic pressure. With the valve open, LV and aortic pressures now rise and fall almost together. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/blood-pressure?section=2-why-doesn-t-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole\">→ Arterial Blood Pressure, §2</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>With a smaller difference, outflow slows: <strong>reduced ejection</strong>, the gentler part of the volume fall. Late in this phase, ventricular pressure can dip slightly below aortic pressure while blood still flows forward. Moving blood carries momentum and takes time to stop (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">Section 6</a>).</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture describes ejection as \"initially slow, then rapid\". Standard physiology, including the volume curve on every <span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span>, shows the reverse: rapid ejection first, then reduced ejection. We teach the standard order.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Meanwhile, the atria</strong> stay relaxed behind closed <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span>. Early in ejection, atrial pressure briefly dips, the <strong>x descent</strong>. The atrium is finishing its relaxation, and the contracting ventricle pulls the AV valve ring downward, enlarging the atrium. After that, returning venous blood fills the atrium steadily and its pressure rises again. So while the ventricles empty, the atria store the blood that will rush in once the ventricles relax.</p>\n<p><strong>Electrically,</strong> the ventricles were activated before ejection began, and no new impulse is needed to keep it going. During the later part of ejection the ventricular muscle <strong>repolarises</strong> (the <span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span> on an ECG), which sets up the relaxation that follows. Repolarisation does not switch contraction off instantly; force declines over the following moments.</p>\n<p>When ejection ends, some blood remains: the <strong>end-systolic volume (ESV)</strong>. <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">Section 7</a> shows how EDV and ESV give the stroke volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 5</strong></p>\n<p>During early ejection, ventricular volume is falling, yet ventricular pressure is still rising. How can both happen at once?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Volume falls because the aortic valve is open and blood is leaving. Pressure rises because the muscle is still increasing its force faster than blood escapes. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">6. Closing the outflow valves and isovolumetric relaxation</h2>\n<p><strong>Why does the aortic valve close?</strong> <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a> left blood still coasting forward on momentum, even though ventricular pressure had dipped below aortic pressure. That forward flow now slows to a stop and briefly reverses. The tiny backflow pushes the aortic cusps together and they seal (the rule from <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Section 2</a>). The lecture calls this short interval, from the end of forward ejection to <span data-term=\"semilunar-valves\">semilunar valve</span> closure, <strong>protodiastole</strong>.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture times protodiastole from the moment \"ventricular pressure starts falling\". In our account, pressure begins to fall during reduced ejection, while blood is still leaving. We therefore date protodiastole from the end of forward flow, as most physiology texts do. Either way, it ends when the semilunar valves close.</aside>\n\n<p>Aortic valve closure contributes the <strong>aortic component of the second heart sound (<span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span>)</strong>. The pulmonary valve closes the same way, slightly later (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Section 10</a>). On the aortic pressure trace, closure leaves a small dip and rebound, the <strong>dicrotic notch</strong>. The dip is the brief backflow; the rebound is the stretched aortic wall recoiling against the now-closed valve. It is not a second contraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Why doesn't filling restart straight away?</strong> The aortic valve is now shut, but ventricular pressure is still above atrial pressure, so the <strong>mitral valve is also shut</strong>. Both valves are closed again, and the ventricle is once more a sealed chamber.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does pressure fall so steeply?</strong> This is <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">Section 4</a> in reverse. The muscle relaxes as calcium is pumped out of the cytoplasm, so its tension drops. Volume cannot change, so the falling tension shows up entirely as falling pressure. Pressure drops steeply while volume stays at <span data-term=\"esv\">ESV</span>. This is <strong>isovolumetric relaxation</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax\">→ Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↓</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = ESV</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Meanwhile, the atria</strong> have kept filling behind closed <span data-term=\"av-valves\">AV valves</span> since the <span data-term=\"x-descent\">x descent</span> (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a>). Atrial pressure reaches its peak now: the <strong>v wave</strong>. No atrial contraction is involved; it is simply blood accumulating.</p>\n<p>When ventricular pressure finally falls below atrial pressure, the <strong>mitral valve opens</strong>. The stored atrial blood rushes in, atrial pressure drops (the <strong>y descent</strong>), and rapid filling begins. We are back at <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">Section 3</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Electrically,</strong> ventricular repolarisation (the <span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span>) happened during late ejection, before most of this relaxation. The T wave, aortic valve closure and mitral valve opening are three separate moments, in that order, not one event.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> The aortic valve has just closed. Ventricular pressure is falling quickly, yet the mitral valve is still shut. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Atrial pressure is falling faster than ventricular pressure at this moment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Ventricular pressure is still higher than left atrial pressure at this moment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The mitral valve opens only when the next P wave triggers atrial systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Ventricular repolarisation must finish before either valve is able to move</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The mitral valve opens only when LV pressure falls <em>below</em> LA pressure. Until then, the ventricle relaxes with both valves closed.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises steadily during ventricular systole. Which wave is this, and why does it happen?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The <strong>v wave</strong>. Venous blood keeps entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed, so blood accumulates and pressure rises. When the AV valve opens, that stored blood flows into the ventricle.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">7. How much blood does the ventricle pump?</h2>\n<p>A ventricle never empties completely. <strong><span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span></strong> is the volume just before contraction; <strong><span data-term=\"esv\">ESV</span></strong> is what remains after ejection. The difference is the <strong>stroke volume (SV)</strong>, the volume ejected in one beat.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Stroke volume</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"SV = EDV − ESV\">SV = EDV − ESV</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = <strong>80 mL</strong></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why is some blood always left behind?</strong> Ejection stops when the pressure difference and the blood's momentum run out (Sections <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">5</a> and <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">6</a>), not when the chamber is empty. The remaining ESV is a reserve: a stronger contraction can eject some of it.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ejection fraction (EF)</strong> is the share of EDV that is ejected:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ejection fraction</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"EF = (SV) / (EDV) × 100%\">EF = (SV) / (EDV) × 100%</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 ≈ <strong>61.5%</strong></span></div>\n\n<p>The lecture uses EDV ≈ 130 mL, SV ≈ 70–90 mL and EF ≈ 65% as typical values; small differences between rounded examples are not contradictions. EF is the fraction of <em>that ventricle's</em> end-diastolic blood ejected, not a fraction of total blood volume. Because it compares what is ejected with what was available, EF is widely used clinically as a measure of how well the ventricle contracts. A low EF suggests weakened contraction. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure?section=2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill\">→ Heart Failure Physiology</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Two ways to raise stroke volume.</strong> Since SV = EDV − ESV, the ventricle can eject more by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>filling more</strong> (a larger EDV), or</li>\n<li><strong>emptying more</strong> (a smaller ESV), for example through stronger contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chapter 7 explains what controls each. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=3-what-determines-stroke-volume\">→ Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Now trace the volume through one beat. It <strong>rises</strong> during filling and reaches EDV after atrial systole. It stays <strong>flat</strong> during isovolumetric contraction, then <strong>falls</strong> during ejection, steeply then gradually. It stays <strong>flat</strong> at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation, then <strong>rises</strong> again when the mitral valve opens.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"volume-bar\"><figcaption>Stroke volume and ejection fraction in one picture</figcaption><p>A ventricle filled to its end-diastolic volume of 130 mL. After ejection, 50 mL remains (end-systolic volume). The 80 mL that left is the stroke volume, which is 61.5% of 130 mL: the ejection fraction.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 7</strong></p>\n<p>EDV stays at 140 mL, but a drug lowers ESV from 70 mL to 50 mL. What happens to SV and EF?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Before: SV = 140 − 70 = 70 mL; EF = 70 ÷ 140 = <strong>50%</strong>. After: SV = 140 − 50 = 90 mL; EF = 90 ÷ 140 ≈ <strong>64.3%</strong>. Emptying more completely raises both SV and EF, even with the same filling.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-the-whole-cycle-on-one-map\">8. The whole cycle on one map</h2>\n<p>Use this after reading the explanation, to rebuild each step from its cause. Start with atrial systole, where the heartbeat begins.</p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget map-quiz\" data-widget=\"map-quiz\"></div>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>What changes?</th>\n  <th>Immediate consequence</th>\n  <th>Next phase</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>SA node fires; atria depolarise</td>\n  <td>Atria contract; final filling to EDV</td>\n  <td>Atrial systole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricles depolarise; ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure</td>\n  <td>Mitral valve closes (S1)</td>\n  <td>Isovolumetric contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure</td>\n  <td>Aortic valve opens</td>\n  <td>Rapid, then reduced ejection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Forward flow stops and briefly reverses (<span data-term=\"protodiastole\">protodiastole</span>)</td>\n  <td>Aortic valve closes (S2)</td>\n  <td>Isovolumetric relaxation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</td>\n  <td>Mitral valve opens</td>\n  <td>Rapid filling, then diastasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>SA node fires again</td>\n  <td>Next atrial systole</td>\n  <td>Next cycle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Compare the two isovolumetric phases</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th>Isovolumetric contraction</th>\n  <th>Isovolumetric relaxation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Muscle</td>\n  <td>Developing force</td>\n  <td>Relaxing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Mitral and aortic valves</td>\n  <td>Both closed</td>\n  <td>Both closed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular pressure</td>\n  <td>Rising</td>\n  <td>Falling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricular volume</td>\n  <td>Constant at EDV</td>\n  <td>Constant at ESV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Atria</td>\n  <td>Relaxed, filling; c wave</td>\n  <td>Relaxed, full; v wave peaks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ends when</td>\n  <td>Aortic valve opens</td>\n  <td>Mitral valve opens</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Volume is constant in both because neither valve is open. Pressure moves in opposite directions because the muscle is building force in one and relaxing in the other.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">9. Reading the Wiggers diagram one trace at a time</h2>\n<p>A <strong>Wiggers diagram</strong> lines up everything you have just learned on one time axis: the ECG, atrial, ventricular and aortic pressures, ventricular volume and heart sounds. It looks crowded, but every curve follows the events above. We will build it one trace at a time for the left heart.</p>\n<p><strong>How to read it.</strong> Time runs from left to right, and every trace shares the same clock. A vertical line drawn anywhere cuts all the traces at the same instant: it tells you what the ECG, pressures, volume and sounds are doing at that moment.</p>\n<p><strong>The key insight: valves move where pressure curves cross.</strong> By the rule in <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Section 2</a>, a valve opens or closes exactly when the pressures on its two sides swap order. So on the diagram:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>mitral valve</strong> closes and opens where the <strong>ventricular and atrial</strong> pressure curves cross.</li>\n<li>The <strong>aortic valve</strong> opens and closes where the <strong>ventricular and aortic</strong> pressure curves cross.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Find those four crossings and you have found the four valve events. Between them, the volume curve tells you whether blood is entering, leaving or neither.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget wiggers-figure\" data-widget=\"wiggers\"><figcaption>The Wiggers diagram, built one step at a time (left heart)</figcaption><p>One heartbeat plus the start of the next, with the ECG, left atrial, left ventricular and aortic pressures, left ventricular volume and heart sounds on one time axis. The four valve events are where pressure curves cross: mitral closure and opening where the ventricular and atrial curves cross, aortic opening and closure where the ventricular and aortic curves cross. Labels show the P, QRS and T waves; the a, c and v waves and x and y descents; the dicrotic notch; EDV and ESV; and S1 and S2. Schematic; values approximate.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1: Atrial systole.</strong> The SA node fires and the atria depolarise: the <strong><span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span></strong>. Shortly afterwards the atria contract, producing the <strong>a wave</strong> on the atrial pressure trace and topping up the ventricle to <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span>. <em>P wave → atrial contraction → a wave → EDV</em>: related, but in sequence, not simultaneous. (In a stiff ventricle, this final push can produce an extra sound, <strong>S4</strong>.)</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Ventricular contraction begins.</strong> After the AV nodal delay, ventricular activation gives the <strong><span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span></strong>. Ventricular pressure then rises and crosses atrial pressure: the mitral valve closes (<strong><span data-term=\"s1\">S1</span></strong>), and the bulging valve makes the <strong><span data-term=\"c-wave\">c wave</span></strong>. With both valves shut, the volume trace is flat: <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Ejection.</strong> Ventricular pressure crosses aortic pressure and the aortic valve opens. From here the LV and aortic pressure traces run almost together, because they are connected through the open valve. The volume trace falls steeply (<span data-term=\"rapid-reduced-ejection\">rapid ejection</span>), then more gently (reduced ejection). Meanwhile atrial pressure dips in the <strong><span data-term=\"x-descent\">x descent</span></strong> (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=5-ejection-why-blood-finally-leaves\">Section 5</a>), then begins to climb.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Relaxation.</strong> The <strong><span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span></strong> appears during reduced ejection as the ventricles repolarise. Forward flow stops and briefly reverses, the aortic valve closes (<strong><span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span></strong>), and the <strong><span data-term=\"dicrotic-notch\">dicrotic notch</span></strong> marks the aortic trace. Ventricular pressure drops steeply while volume stays flat at <span data-term=\"esv\">ESV</span>; atrial pressure reaches the <strong><span data-term=\"v-wave\">v wave</span></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Filling resumes.</strong> Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, atrial pressure falls (<strong><span data-term=\"y-descent\">y descent</span></strong>) and the volume trace rises again. (Rapid inflow can sometimes produce an extra sound, <strong>S3</strong>.)</p>\n<h3>Read it in four passes</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Find QRS and mitral closure.</strong> QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Find aortic opening.</strong> Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now <strong>falls</strong> as ejection begins.</li>\n<li><strong>Find aortic closure.</strong> Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and <strong>S2</strong>. A small <strong>dicrotic notch</strong> appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation.</li>\n<li><strong>Find mitral reopening.</strong> Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve <strong>rises</strong> as filling resumes.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Wave</th>\n  <th>Cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>a wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>c wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>x descent</strong></td>\n  <td>Atrial relaxation, then the AV valve ring pulled down during ejection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>v wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>y descent</strong></td>\n  <td>AV valve opens and stored atrial blood empties into the ventricle.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> full JVP waves and descents → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents\">Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse, §4</a> · ECG timing → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/ecg?section=5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events\">Electrocardiography, §5</a> · S1 and S2 → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §2</a> · S3 and S4 → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=4-what-causes-s3-and-s4\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §4</a></p>\n\n<h3>Watch the mechanism</h3>\n<div class=\"media-resource animation-resource\" data-animation-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" data-media-alt=\"Animated heart with valve motion and cardiac cycle pressure curves.\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL</span><h3>Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces</h3><p>Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-animation\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play animation</button><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Loaded only when selected.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"IS9TD9fHFv0\" data-media-title=\"The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IS9TD9fHFv0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 9</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> On a Wiggers diagram, ventricular pressure rises sharply while ventricular volume stays flat. Which event ends this phase?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The mitral valve opens as ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The T wave appears and the ventricle begins its electrical recovery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The aortic valve closes, leaving the dicrotic notch on aortic pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The aortic valve opens as ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The flat volume with rising pressure is isovolumetric contraction. It ends when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure and blood can leave.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The T wave has begun, yet the aortic valve is still open and blood is still leaving. Is this possible?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Yes.</strong> Repolarisation happens during reduced ejection. Electrical recovery starts the decline in force, but force and forward flow do not stop instantly. The aortic valve closes only when flow reverses at the end of ejection.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">10. Right versus left heart, S2 splitting and heart rate</h2>\n<p><strong>Same rules, lower pressures.</strong> Both ventricles are activated in the same beat and follow the same valve rule. The right ventricle pumps into the low-resistance pulmonary circulation, reaching about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>; the left reaches about <strong>120 mmHg</strong>. Even so, over time both pump almost exactly the same volume per minute. The two circuits are connected in series, so whatever the right ventricle sends through the lungs must come back to the left. Higher pressure does not mean more blood. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">→ Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why the right ventricle ejects first.</strong> The lecture notes that the left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet right ventricular ejection begins first. Both points are correct. The right ventricle only has to exceed about <strong>10 mmHg</strong> in the pulmonary artery, versus about <strong>80 mmHg</strong> in the aorta, so its <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric phase</span> (contracting with all valves shut, before ejection) is much shorter and the pulmonary valve opens first. The <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> does not send separate beats to each side.</p>\n<p><strong>Why S2 has two parts.</strong> <span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span> is made of <strong>A2</strong> (aortic closure) and <strong>P2</strong> (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During <strong>expiration</strong> they are close together and often sound single. During <strong>inspiration</strong>, P2 moves later and the gap widens: <strong>physiological splitting of S2</strong>. Two effects delay P2:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>More right-heart filling.</strong> Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower pulmonary impedance.</strong> The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"s2-timing\"><figcaption>Why S2 splits on inspiration</figcaption><p>Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says the valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the inspiratory widening. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it.</aside>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> listening for splitting → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §3</a> · breathing and venous return → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return\">Veins and Venous Return, §5</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>What happens when heart rate rises?</strong> At <strong>75 beats/min</strong>, one cycle lasts <strong>0.8 s</strong>; at <strong>150 beats/min</strong>, only <strong>0.4 s</strong>. Both phases shorten, but not equally.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does diastole lose most of the time?</strong> How long systole lasts is tied to how long the ventricular action potential lasts. Faster contraction and repolarisation shorten it a little, giving quicker ejection, as the lecture notes. But diastole is simply whatever time is left before the next beat, so it absorbs most of the cut. That matters, because diastole is when the ventricles fill.</p>\n<p>At very high rates, or when the ventricle relaxes poorly, there may not be enough time to fill. <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span> then falls, and <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span> falls with it. A faster heart rate does <strong>not automatically</strong> mean less stroke volume, though. During exercise, greater venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can maintain or even raise it. The heart also cannot be driven into a sustained, tetanic contraction, because its long refractory period forces relaxation between beats.</p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget hr-slider\" data-widget=\"hr-slider\"><p>Cycle length = 60 ÷ heart rate. At 75 beats/min it is 0.80 s (800 ms). This tool calculates total cycle length only; it does not model the exact split between systole and diastole.</p></div>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> cardiac output and rate → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §2</a> · refractory period → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=6-why-can-t-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §6</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Coronary blood flow.</strong> The left ventricle's own muscle receives most of its blood flow during <strong>diastole</strong>. During systole the contracting muscle squeezes the vessels running through it, especially the deeper ones. So marked tachycardia hits twice: it shortens the time for coronary perfusion just as the faster-working heart needs more oxygen. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole\">→ Coronary Circulation, §3</a> and <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary?section=5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia\">§5</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check — Section 10</strong></p>\n<p>During inspiration, P2 occurs later than during expiration. Which explanation is best?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. More right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance prolong RV ejection</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Inspiration slows conduction in the right bundle, delaying RV activation</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pulmonary arterial pressure rises on inspiration, so RV ejection starts late</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Less blood returns to the right atrium, so the tricuspid valve closes later</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Both inspiratory effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so the pulmonary valve closes later and the A2–P2 gap widens.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"11-understanding-check\">11. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>Predict first, then reveal.</strong> Each scenario changes one thing. Work out what happens in the rest of the heart before opening the answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario 1: Contracting, but no blood leaving.</strong> The left ventricle has just started contracting. Its pressure is rising, but its volume is not changing. What are the valves and the left atrium doing?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>This is <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>. The mitral valve has just shut, and the aortic valve has not opened yet, because ventricular pressure is still below aortic pressure. With both shut, blood can't get in or out. Meanwhile the left atrium is relaxed and filling with blood from the lungs. Contracting and ejecting are not the same thing.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Scenario 2: Relaxing, but no blood entering.</strong> Ejection has ended and the aortic valve has shut. Ventricular pressure is falling, but volume is not changing. Why is no blood coming in?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>This is isovolumetric relaxation. Ventricular pressure is still higher than atrial pressure, so the mitral valve stays shut. The atrium is full, at its <span data-term=\"v-wave\">v wave</span>. Once ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and blood rushes in.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Scenario 3: The atria stop contracting properly.</strong> Does ventricular filling stop?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>No. Most filling is passive: blood flows in whenever atrial pressure is higher than ventricular pressure. Only the final top-up from atrial contraction is lost. That loss matters most when the ventricle is stiff or the heart rate is fast.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Scenario 4: Heart rate doubles.</strong> The rate rises from 75 to 150 beats/min. What may happen to filling, stroke volume and blood flow to the heart muscle?</p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Each beat now lasts 0.4 s instead of 0.8 s, and most of the lost time comes out of diastole. So there is less time to fill the ventricle and less time for blood to flow into the left ventricle's own muscle. Stroke volume may fall, but not always: during exercise, more venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can protect it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Final questions</strong> (answer and explanation shown straight after each response)</p>\n<p><strong>Q1.</strong> A patient's ventricle becomes stiff and relaxes poorly. Atrial contraction is normal. What happens to ventricular filling?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Passive filling rises, so the atrial kick becomes unnecessary for filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Filling stops completely, because the mitral valve can no longer open</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Passive filling falls, so the atrial kick supplies a larger share of filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Filling is unchanged, because filling depends only on the SA node firing</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A stiff ventricle doesn't stretch easily. As blood enters, its pressure rises quickly and soon matches atrial pressure, so passive inflow stops early. The atria's final push then makes up a bigger part of the filling. This is why losing atrial contraction hurts these patients most.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q2.</strong> During <span data-term=\"diastasis\">diastasis</span>, before the next P wave, what is happening electrically?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. SA nodal cells are slowly depolarising towards their next threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A slow excitation wave is spreading through the ventricular muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The AV node is firing repeatedly to sustain passive ventricular filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Purkinje fibres are repolarising the atria in preparation for systole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Passive filling needs no electrical impulse; the pressure difference moves the blood. Meanwhile the SA node's pacemaker cells slowly drift towards threshold. When they fire, the P wave and atrial contraction follow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q3.</strong> The left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet the pulmonary valve opens before the aortic valve. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The right ventricle contracts more strongly than the left ventricle does</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The impulse reaches the right ventricle first by a faster route</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The pulmonary valve has thinner cusps that open at any pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The right ventricle only has to exceed a much lower arterial pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The right ventricle only needs to beat about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery; the left must beat about 80 mmHg in the aorta. The right ventricle reaches its target sooner, so its valve opens first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q4.</strong> Ventricles X and Y eject the same stroke volume, but X starts with a larger <span data-term=\"edv\">end-diastolic volume</span>. Which conclusion follows?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both have the same ejection fraction, because their stroke volumes match</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. X has the lower ejection fraction, because SV is a smaller share of its EDV</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. X has the lower end-systolic volume, because it started with more blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Both have the same end-systolic volume, because they ejected equal volumes</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> EF = SV ÷ EDV. If SV is the same but EDV is bigger, the fraction is smaller. X also has the <em>larger</em> ESV, since ESV = EDV − SV.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q5.</strong> Late in ejection, left ventricular pressure falls slightly below aortic pressure, yet blood keeps leaving for a moment. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The left atrium contracts and pushes blood on through the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The aortic cusps actively propel blood forward as they begin closing</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood already moving forward carries momentum that takes time to stop</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The mitral valve opens and adds atrial pressure behind the outflow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Moving blood can't stop instantly. It slows down, then briefly flows backwards, and that backflow is what shuts the aortic valve.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q6.</strong> An arrhythmia raises a resting patient's heart rate from 75 to 150 beats/min. Which change is most likely?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Systole and diastole both halve, so filling time is proportionally kept</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Systole shortens most, so ejection is incomplete but filling is protected</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Coronary perfusion time rises, because the heart relaxes more often</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Diastole shortens most, so filling and coronary perfusion time both fall</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Systole can only shorten a little, so diastole loses most of the time. At rest, without the boosts that exercise brings, less filling time can lower EDV and stroke volume. The left ventricle's muscle also gets less time to receive blood.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q7.</strong> Which pairing of an atrial pressure wave with its cause is correct?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. c wave: the closed AV valve bulging back towards the atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. v wave: the atrium contracting against a closed AV valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. a wave: venous blood filling the atrium while the AV valve is shut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. y descent: the atrium relaxing while the AV valve stays closed</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The c wave comes from the contracting ventricle pushing the shut valve back. The a wave is the atrium contracting, the v wave is the atrium filling up, and the y descent happens when the AV valve opens and the atrium empties.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>Q8.</strong> End-diastolic volume is 150 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. What are the stroke volume and ejection fraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 90 mL and 40%</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 90 mL and 60%</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 60 mL and 60%</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 60 mL and 40%</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> SV = 150 − 60 = 90 mL. EF = 90 ÷ 150 × 100 = 60%.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"12-chapter-essentials\">12. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The atria and ventricles take turns.</strong> The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. While the ventricles contract, the atria relax and refill. For about 0.4 s of a 0.8 s beat, both are relaxed together.</li>\n<li><strong>One chain drives every phase:</strong> electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Valves are passive.</strong> Each opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front, and shuts when that reverses. Papillary muscles stop the AV valves turning inside out; they do not open them.</li>\n<li><strong>Filling is mostly passive:</strong> rapid filling, then slower diastasis, then the atrial kick (lecture: 70% passive, 30% atrial; at rest often 10–30% atrial). The kick matters more with a stiff ventricle or a fast heart rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> all four valves are shut, so the contracting ventricle's pressure shoots up while its volume stays at EDV. Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves contributes to S1.</li>\n<li><strong>Ejection</strong> starts only when ventricular pressure beats arterial pressure: about 80 mmHg in the aorta, 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery. It is rapid first, then reduced, as the muscle weakens and the aorta fills.</li>\n<li><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> forward flow briefly reverses and shuts the aortic and pulmonary valves (S2, dicrotic notch). With all valves shut, pressure falls while volume stays at ESV. It ends when the mitral and tricuspid valves open.</li>\n<li><strong>Volumes:</strong> SV = EDV − ESV and EF = SV ÷ EDV × 100%. Stroke volume rises by filling more (bigger EDV) or emptying more (smaller ESV).</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial pressure waves:</strong> a = atria contract; c = shut AV valves bulge back; x = atria relax and the valve ring is pulled down; v = atria fill behind shut valves; y = AV valves open and the atria empty.</li>\n<li><strong>Electrical comes before mechanical:</strong> the P wave before atrial contraction, the QRS before ventricular contraction, and the T wave during reduced ejection, before the outflow valves shut.</li>\n<li><strong>On a Wiggers diagram, every valve event is a point where two pressure curves cross.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right heart and heart rate:</strong> the right heart follows the same rules at lower pressures, so the right ventricle ejects first; on inspiration P2 is delayed and S2 splitting widens. At faster heart rates diastole loses most of the time, so very high rates cut filling and coronary perfusion time.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"The Cardiac Cycle 1. One heartbeat: what the atria and ventricles are doing The cardiac cycle is everything that happens in the heart during one heartbeat: the atria and ventricles each contract and relax once, in a fixed order. Systole means contraction and diastole means relaxation. On their own, the words are incomplete. Always ask systole of which chambers? The atria and ventricles are often doing different things, though for a large part of each beat they are both relaxed. Why do the atria go first? Each beat starts with an electrical impulse from the SA node in the right atrium. The impulse spreads across the atria first, then pauses briefly at the AV node before reaching the ventricles. So the atria are activated, and contract, just before the ventricles. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §5) Follow one beat: Atrial systole. At the end of ventricular diastole, the atria contract and push a final portion of blood into ventricles that are already nearly full. Ventricular systole. The ventricles contract and eject blood. Meanwhile the atria relax and begin refilling with blood returning from the veins. Both chambers in diastole. The ventricles now relax too, so both are relaxed together. For a brief moment the ventricles still cannot fill, because their pressure is higher than atrial pressure. As they relax further, their pressure falls below atrial pressure and blood flows in passively. Atrial systole again, and the next beat begins. How long does each part take? At about 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 60 ÷ 75 = 0.8 s. Part of the cycle Duration Where it comes from Ventricular systole ≈ 0.3 s Typical resting value Ventricular diastole ≈ 0.5 s 0.8 − 0.3 Atrial systole ≈ 0.1 s Last part of ventricular diastole Both chambers relaxed ≈ 0.4 s 0.5 − 0.1, half of every beat These are rounded teaching values; the lecture gives no phase durations. That shared 0.4 s matters. After a short pause while ventricular pressure is still falling, it is when most ventricular filling happens. This is why atrial contraction is only a top-up. It is also why very fast heart rates, which shorten this period most, can limit filling ( Section 10). One heartbeat at 75 beats/min: what the atria and ventricles are doing Two aligned bars across one 0.8 s cycle. Atria: systole for the first 0.1 s, then diastole. Ventricles: systole from 0.1 s to 0.4 s, then diastole. From 0.4 s to 0.8 s both are relaxed: first a short pause while the ventricle is still relaxing with no filling, then passive filling. Proportions approximate, at 75 beats/min. What drives each step? Every phase follows one chain: Electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes The electrical signal starts the chain but never moves blood or valves directly. Each link takes a moment, so the steps happen in sequence, not all at once. Section 2 explains the key link: how pressure moves the valves. 2. The one rule every valve follows The heart has four valves, in two pairs: AV (atrioventricular) valves sit between each atrium and its ventricle: the mitral valve on the left and the tricuspid valve on the right. Semilunar valves sit between each ventricle and its artery: the aortic valve on the left and the pulmonary valve on the right. All four obey one rule. A valve opens when pressure upstream (in the chamber blood is coming from) is higher than pressure downstream (where blood is going). It closes when that difference reverses. How can pressure alone do this? Each valve is made of thin flaps called cusps, shaped to open in one direction only. Higher pressure upstream pushes the cusps apart, and blood flows through. When downstream pressure becomes higher, blood starts to move backwards and pushes the cusps together until they seal. No nerve or electrical signal is involved. ( → Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §3: valves keep flow one-way) What about the papillary muscles? The AV valve cusps are tethered by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles in the ventricular wall. These muscles contract with the ventricle, but they do not open or close the valve. They hold the cusps in place so that rising ventricular pressure cannot push them inside out into the atrium. Now apply the rule to the left heart: left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta. Mitral valve opens P LA > P LV Blood enters the ventricle Aortic valve opens P LV > P aorta Blood leaves the ventricle Here P means pressure, LA is left atrium and LV is left ventricle. The two valves respond to different pressure differences, so there are moments when both are closed at once. Those moments explain two phases you will meet in Sections 4 and 6, when the ventricle's pressure changes but its volume does not. One refinement comes later: moving blood carries momentum, so flow and valve closure can lag slightly behind a change in pressure ( Section 6). The right heart follows the same rule at lower pressures, through the tricuspid and pulmonary valves. We follow the left heart first and return to the right in Section 10. 3. Filling: how blood enters the relaxed ventricle Blood keeps returning to the heart all the time, even while the ventricles are contracting and their AV valves are shut. During that time the relaxed atria act as reservoirs, collecting the returning blood, so atrial pressure slowly rises. When the ventricle relaxes, its pressure falls. As soon as ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and the stored blood rushes in. No contraction is needed for this: blood simply moves down the pressure difference. Why is filling fast at first, then slow? At first the difference is large: the atrium is full and pressurised, and the ventricle has only just relaxed. So filling is rapid. As blood enters, ventricular pressure rises only a little, because the relaxed ventricle stretches easily. It stays low, as the lecture notes, but it creeps closer to atrial pressure. With a smaller difference, inflow slows. This slower period is diastasis. Throughout passive filling, blood flows from the veins through the open atrium into the ventricle, so the atrium is acting as a conduit. Why do the atria contract at all? Near the end of diastole, the atria contract and push in a final volume, the atrial kick, with the atrium acting as a booster pump. The lecture describes filling as about 70% passive and 30% atrial. These are teaching proportions: at rest the atrial share is often 10–30%. The kick matters more in two situations: A stiff ventricle resists passive stretching, so less blood enters passively and the active push makes up more of the total. A fast heart rate shortens diastole, leaving less time for passive filling, so the final push contributes a larger share. When filling ends, the ventricle holds its end-diastolic volume (EDV), about 130 mL in the lecture's example. This is the volume it starts its next contraction with ( Section 7). Ventricular filling: why it is fast, then slow, then topped up Left atrial and left ventricular pressure across ventricular diastole, with ventricular volume beneath. When the mitral valve opens the pressure gap is large and volume rises steeply (rapid filling). The gap then narrows and volume rises slowly (diastasis). Atrial contraction makes the a wave and adds a final step of volume up to EDV. Ventricular pressure stays low throughout. Schematic; pressures not to scale. What is happening electrically during filling? The ventricles repolarised near the end of their contraction ( Section 5), before filling began. During rapid filling and diastasis, no excitation wave is spreading through the working heart muscle, and none is needed: the pressure difference alone moves the blood. The SA node is not idle, though. Its pacemaker cells are slowly depolarising towards threshold. When they fire, the impulse spreads across the atria (the P wave on an ECG) and atrial contraction follows. ( → Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity, §3) Understanding Check — Section 3 A patient's atria stop contracting effectively, but ventricular relaxation is normal. Why does most ventricular filling continue? A. Venous blood is pushed through the closed AV valves by contracting veins B. Ventricular contraction draws blood back from the atria during systole C. The SA node keeps triggering small atrial contractions during diastole D. Atrial pressure exceeds relaxed ventricular pressure, so blood flows passively Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Filling needs only a pressure difference across an open AV valve. Atrial systole adds a final top-up. Losing it reduces filling but does not stop it. 4. Isovolumetric contraction: contracting, but no blood leaves We left the ventricle full, holding its EDV, with the atria just finished contracting ( Section 3). After the AV nodal pause ( Section 1), the impulse races through the His–Purkinje system, activating the ventricular muscle almost all at once. On an ECG, this ventricular activation is the QRS complex (more in Section 9). ( → Cardiac Conduction, §6) Why does force lag slightly behind activation? Electrical activation first has to release calcium inside each muscle cell; only then can the cell develop force. So ventricular pressure starts rising a moment after the QRS. ( → Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §4) Why does the mitral valve close so quickly? Atrial pressure is only a few mmHg, so ventricular pressure overtakes it almost as soon as contraction begins. The pressure difference across the mitral and tricuspid valves reverses and they close. This closure contributes to the first heart sound ( S1). But ventricular pressure is still far below aortic pressure, so the aortic valve stays closed. Now both valves are closed, and the ventricle is a sealed chamber. Why does pressure climb so steeply? Blood cannot be compressed. With no way in or out, the volume cannot change, so all the force the muscle develops goes into raising pressure. The muscle fibres build tension but barely shorten, as the lecture notes. Pressure therefore climbs steeply while volume stays fixed at EDV. This is isovolumetric contraction ( iso = same, volumetric = volume). It is the start of ventricular systole, not yet ejection. Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↑ Volume = EDV Meanwhile, the atria have relaxed and are again collecting venous blood behind closed AV valves. As ventricular pressure rises, the closed AV valves are the only part of the sealed chamber that can give a little. As the lecture puts it, they bulge back into the atrium. This causes a small bump in atrial pressure, the c wave. The atrium is not contracting; it is being pushed on. What ends this phase? Ventricular pressure must exceed pressure in its outflow artery. The lecture uses about 80 mmHg for aortic diastolic pressure and about 10 mmHg for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure. These are typical reference values, not fixed thresholds in every person. Contraction is not the same as ejection: the ventricle must first overcome the pressure already in the artery. Source note: The lecture says closure of the AV valves \"initiates systole\". More precisely, ventricular contraction starts first, and the rising pressure then closes the AV valves. Valve closure marks the start of isovolumetric contraction rather than causing it. Isovolumetric contraction in three steps Step 1: ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, so the mitral and tricuspid valves close. Step 2: all four valves are closed; ventricular pressure rises while volume stays the same. Step 3: ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure, so the aortic and pulmonary valves open and ejection begins. The atria stay relaxed and keep filling throughout. Understanding Check — Section 4 1. Just after the mitral valve closes, LV pressure climbs steeply but LV volume does not change. Why? A. Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed, so blood has no exit B. The ventricular muscle has not yet been depolarised by the impulse C. Blood leaves the ventricle as fast as it enters from the atrium D. The aortic valve is open, but aortic pressure prevents any outflow Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Contraction is already under way and raising pressure, but with both valves shut there is nowhere for blood to go. 2. A person's aortic diastolic pressure is higher than usual. Ventricular activation and contractility are unchanged. What happens to isovolumetric contraction? A. It shortens, because the aortic valve opens at a lower LV pressure B. It stays the same, because valve opening depends on electrical timing C. It lengthens, because LV pressure must climb higher to open the valve D. It disappears, because the mitral valve stays open until ejection Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The aortic valve opens only when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure. A higher starting aortic pressure means the ventricle must build more pressure first, so, all else equal, this phase lasts longer. 5. Ejection: why blood finally leaves As soon as LV pressure rises above aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens (on the right, the pulmonary valve opens the same way). Blood flows into the artery and ventricular volume begins to fall. Isovolumetric contraction is over; ejection has begun. Why is ejection rapid at first? At the moment the valve opens, the muscle is contracting strongly and the pressure difference pushing blood out is at its largest. Blood accelerates out quickly: rapid ejection. Ventricular pressure keeps rising for a while, peaking at about 120 mmHg in the lecture's example, even though volume is already falling. Pressure and volume measure different things and need not move together. On the volume curve, this is the steep part of the fall. Why does it then slow down? Two things shrink the pressure difference at once: The muscle weakens. As contraction passes its peak, ventricular force and pressure fall. The aorta fills. Blood entering the aorta stretches its elastic wall and raises aortic pressure. With the valve open, LV and aortic pressures now rise and fall almost together. ( → Arterial Blood Pressure, §2) With a smaller difference, outflow slows: reduced ejection, the gentler part of the volume fall. Late in this phase, ventricular pressure can dip slightly below aortic pressure while blood still flows forward. Moving blood carries momentum and takes time to stop ( Section 6). Source note: The lecture describes ejection as \"initially slow, then rapid\". Standard physiology, including the volume curve on every Wiggers diagram, shows the reverse: rapid ejection first, then reduced ejection. We teach the standard order. Meanwhile, the atria stay relaxed behind closed AV valves. Early in ejection, atrial pressure briefly dips, the x descent. The atrium is finishing its relaxation, and the contracting ventricle pulls the AV valve ring downward, enlarging the atrium. After that, returning venous blood fills the atrium steadily and its pressure rises again. So while the ventricles empty, the atria store the blood that will rush in once the ventricles relax. Electrically, the ventricles were activated before ejection began, and no new impulse is needed to keep it going. During the later part of ejection the ventricular muscle repolarises (the T wave on an ECG), which sets up the relaxation that follows. Repolarisation does not switch contraction off instantly; force declines over the following moments. When ejection ends, some blood remains: the end-systolic volume (ESV). Section 7 shows how EDV and ESV give the stroke volume. Understanding Check — Section 5 During early ejection, ventricular volume is falling, yet ventricular pressure is still rising. How can both happen at once? Reveal explanation Volume falls because the aortic valve is open and blood is leaving. Pressure rises because the muscle is still increasing its force faster than blood escapes. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling. 6. Closing the outflow valves and isovolumetric relaxation Why does the aortic valve close? Section 5 left blood still coasting forward on momentum, even though ventricular pressure had dipped below aortic pressure. That forward flow now slows to a stop and briefly reverses. The tiny backflow pushes the aortic cusps together and they seal (the rule from Section 2). The lecture calls this short interval, from the end of forward ejection to semilunar valve closure, protodiastole. Source note: The lecture times protodiastole from the moment \"ventricular pressure starts falling\". In our account, pressure begins to fall during reduced ejection, while blood is still leaving. We therefore date protodiastole from the end of forward flow, as most physiology texts do. Either way, it ends when the semilunar valves close. Aortic valve closure contributes the aortic component of the second heart sound ( S2). The pulmonary valve closes the same way, slightly later ( Section 10). On the aortic pressure trace, closure leaves a small dip and rebound, the dicrotic notch. The dip is the brief backflow; the rebound is the stretched aortic wall recoiling against the now-closed valve. It is not a second contraction. Why doesn't filling restart straight away? The aortic valve is now shut, but ventricular pressure is still above atrial pressure, so the mitral valve is also shut. Both valves are closed again, and the ventricle is once more a sealed chamber. Why does pressure fall so steeply? This is Section 4 in reverse. The muscle relaxes as calcium is pumped out of the cytoplasm, so its tension drops. Volume cannot change, so the falling tension shows up entirely as falling pressure. Pressure drops steeply while volume stays at ESV. This is isovolumetric relaxation. ( → Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §5) Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↓ Volume = ESV Meanwhile, the atria have kept filling behind closed AV valves since the x descent ( Section 5). Atrial pressure reaches its peak now: the v wave. No atrial contraction is involved; it is simply blood accumulating. When ventricular pressure finally falls below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens. The stored atrial blood rushes in, atrial pressure drops (the y descent), and rapid filling begins. We are back at Section 3. Electrically, ventricular repolarisation (the T wave) happened during late ejection, before most of this relaxation. The T wave, aortic valve closure and mitral valve opening are three separate moments, in that order, not one event. Understanding Check — Section 6 1. The aortic valve has just closed. Ventricular pressure is falling quickly, yet the mitral valve is still shut. Why? A. Atrial pressure is falling faster than ventricular pressure at this moment B. Ventricular pressure is still higher than left atrial pressure at this moment C. The mitral valve opens only when the next P wave triggers atrial systole D. Ventricular repolarisation must finish before either valve is able to move Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The mitral valve opens only when LV pressure falls below LA pressure. Until then, the ventricle relaxes with both valves closed. 2. The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises steadily during ventricular systole. Which wave is this, and why does it happen? Reveal explanation The v wave. Venous blood keeps entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed, so blood accumulates and pressure rises. When the AV valve opens, that stored blood flows into the ventricle. 7. How much blood does the ventricle pump? A ventricle never empties completely. EDV is the volume just before contraction; ESV is what remains after ejection. The difference is the stroke volume (SV), the volume ejected in one beat. Stroke volume SV = EDV − ESV Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = 80 mL Why is some blood always left behind? Ejection stops when the pressure difference and the blood's momentum run out (Sections 5 and 6), not when the chamber is empty. The remaining ESV is a reserve: a stronger contraction can eject some of it. The ejection fraction (EF) is the share of EDV that is ejected: Ejection fraction EF = (SV) / (EDV) × 100% Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 ≈ 61.5% The lecture uses EDV ≈ 130 mL, SV ≈ 70–90 mL and EF ≈ 65% as typical values; small differences between rounded examples are not contradictions. EF is the fraction of that ventricle's end-diastolic blood ejected, not a fraction of total blood volume. Because it compares what is ejected with what was available, EF is widely used clinically as a measure of how well the ventricle contracts. A low EF suggests weakened contraction. ( → Heart Failure Physiology) Two ways to raise stroke volume. Since SV = EDV − ESV, the ventricle can eject more by: filling more (a larger EDV), or emptying more (a smaller ESV), for example through stronger contraction. Chapter 7 explains what controls each. ( → Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §3) Now trace the volume through one beat. It rises during filling and reaches EDV after atrial systole. It stays flat during isovolumetric contraction, then falls during ejection, steeply then gradually. It stays flat at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation, then rises again when the mitral valve opens. Stroke volume and ejection fraction in one picture A ventricle filled to its end-diastolic volume of 130 mL. After ejection, 50 mL remains (end-systolic volume). The 80 mL that left is the stroke volume, which is 61.5% of 130 mL: the ejection fraction. Understanding Check — Section 7 EDV stays at 140 mL, but a drug lowers ESV from 70 mL to 50 mL. What happens to SV and EF? Reveal explanation Before: SV = 140 − 70 = 70 mL; EF = 70 ÷ 140 = 50%. After: SV = 140 − 50 = 90 mL; EF = 90 ÷ 140 ≈ 64.3%. Emptying more completely raises both SV and EF, even with the same filling. 8. The whole cycle on one map Use this after reading the explanation, to rebuild each step from its cause. Start with atrial systole, where the heartbeat begins. What changes? Immediate consequence Next phase SA node fires; atria depolarise Atria contract; final filling to EDV Atrial systole Ventricles depolarise; ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure Mitral valve closes (S1) Isovolumetric contraction Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Aortic valve opens Rapid, then reduced ejection Forward flow stops and briefly reverses ( protodiastole) Aortic valve closes (S2) Isovolumetric relaxation Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure Mitral valve opens Rapid filling, then diastasis SA node fires again Next atrial systole Next cycle Compare the two isovolumetric phases Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Muscle Developing force Relaxing Mitral and aortic valves Both closed Both closed Ventricular pressure Rising Falling Ventricular volume Constant at EDV Constant at ESV Atria Relaxed, filling; c wave Relaxed, full; v wave peaks Ends when Aortic valve opens Mitral valve opens Volume is constant in both because neither valve is open. Pressure moves in opposite directions because the muscle is building force in one and relaxing in the other. 9. Reading the Wiggers diagram one trace at a time A Wiggers diagram lines up everything you have just learned on one time axis: the ECG, atrial, ventricular and aortic pressures, ventricular volume and heart sounds. It looks crowded, but every curve follows the events above. We will build it one trace at a time for the left heart. How to read it. Time runs from left to right, and every trace shares the same clock. A vertical line drawn anywhere cuts all the traces at the same instant: it tells you what the ECG, pressures, volume and sounds are doing at that moment. The key insight: valves move where pressure curves cross. By the rule in Section 2, a valve opens or closes exactly when the pressures on its two sides swap order. So on the diagram: The mitral valve closes and opens where the ventricular and atrial pressure curves cross. The aortic valve opens and closes where the ventricular and aortic pressure curves cross. Find those four crossings and you have found the four valve events. Between them, the volume curve tells you whether blood is entering, leaving or neither. The Wiggers diagram, built one step at a time (left heart) One heartbeat plus the start of the next, with the ECG, left atrial, left ventricular and aortic pressures, left ventricular volume and heart sounds on one time axis. The four valve events are where pressure curves cross: mitral closure and opening where the ventricular and atrial curves cross, aortic opening and closure where the ventricular and aortic curves cross. Labels show the P, QRS and T waves; the a, c and v waves and x and y descents; the dicrotic notch; EDV and ESV; and S1 and S2. Schematic; values approximate. Step 1: Atrial systole. The SA node fires and the atria depolarise: the P wave. Shortly afterwards the atria contract, producing the a wave on the atrial pressure trace and topping up the ventricle to EDV. P wave → atrial contraction → a wave → EDV: related, but in sequence, not simultaneous. (In a stiff ventricle, this final push can produce an extra sound, S4.) Step 2: Ventricular contraction begins. After the AV nodal delay, ventricular activation gives the QRS complex. Ventricular pressure then rises and crosses atrial pressure: the mitral valve closes ( S1), and the bulging valve makes the c wave. With both valves shut, the volume trace is flat: isovolumetric contraction. Step 3: Ejection. Ventricular pressure crosses aortic pressure and the aortic valve opens. From here the LV and aortic pressure traces run almost together, because they are connected through the open valve. The volume trace falls steeply ( rapid ejection), then more gently (reduced ejection). Meanwhile atrial pressure dips in the x descent ( Section 5), then begins to climb. Step 4: Relaxation. The T wave appears during reduced ejection as the ventricles repolarise. Forward flow stops and briefly reverses, the aortic valve closes ( S2), and the dicrotic notch marks the aortic trace. Ventricular pressure drops steeply while volume stays flat at ESV; atrial pressure reaches the v wave. Step 5: Filling resumes. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, atrial pressure falls ( y descent) and the volume trace rises again. (Rapid inflow can sometimes produce an extra sound, S3.) Read it in four passes Find QRS and mitral closure. QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: isovolumetric contraction. Find aortic opening. Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now falls as ejection begins. Find aortic closure. Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and S2. A small dicrotic notch appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation. Find mitral reopening. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve rises as filling resumes. Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure? Wave Cause a wave Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure. c wave Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium. x descent Atrial relaxation, then the AV valve ring pulled down during ejection. v wave Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed. y descent AV valve opens and stored atrial blood empties into the ventricle. Related: full JVP waves and descents → Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse, §4 · ECG timing → Electrocardiography, §5 · S1 and S2 → Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §2 · S3 and S4 → Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §4 Watch the mechanism SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts. Play animation DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0. Loaded only when selected. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted. Understanding Check — Section 9 1. On a Wiggers diagram, ventricular pressure rises sharply while ventricular volume stays flat. Which event ends this phase? A. The mitral valve opens as ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure B. The T wave appears and the ventricle begins its electrical recovery C. The aortic valve closes, leaving the dicrotic notch on aortic pressure D. The aortic valve opens as ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The flat volume with rising pressure is isovolumetric contraction. It ends when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure and blood can leave. 2. The T wave has begun, yet the aortic valve is still open and blood is still leaving. Is this possible? Reveal explanation Yes. Repolarisation happens during reduced ejection. Electrical recovery starts the decline in force, but force and forward flow do not stop instantly. The aortic valve closes only when flow reverses at the end of ejection. 10. Right versus left heart, S2 splitting and heart rate Same rules, lower pressures. Both ventricles are activated in the same beat and follow the same valve rule. The right ventricle pumps into the low-resistance pulmonary circulation, reaching about 25 mmHg; the left reaches about 120 mmHg. Even so, over time both pump almost exactly the same volume per minute. The two circuits are connected in series, so whatever the right ventricle sends through the lungs must come back to the left. Higher pressure does not mean more blood. ( → Introduction to the Heart and Circulation, §2) Why the right ventricle ejects first. The lecture notes that the left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet right ventricular ejection begins first. Both points are correct. The right ventricle only has to exceed about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery, versus about 80 mmHg in the aorta, so its isovolumetric phase (contracting with all valves shut, before ejection) is much shorter and the pulmonary valve opens first. The SA node does not send separate beats to each side. Why S2 has two parts. S2 is made of A2 (aortic closure) and P2 (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During expiration they are close together and often sound single. During inspiration, P2 moves later and the gap widens: physiological splitting of S2. Two effects delay P2: More right-heart filling. Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject. Lower pulmonary impedance. The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later. A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly. Why S2 splits on inspiration Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale. Source note: The lecture says the valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the inspiratory widening. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it. Related: listening for splitting → Heart Sounds and Murmurs, §3 · breathing and venous return → Veins and Venous Return, §5 What happens when heart rate rises? At 75 beats/min, one cycle lasts 0.8 s; at 150 beats/min, only 0.4 s. Both phases shorten, but not equally. Why does diastole lose most of the time? How long systole lasts is tied to how long the ventricular action potential lasts. Faster contraction and repolarisation shorten it a little, giving quicker ejection, as the lecture notes. But diastole is simply whatever time is left before the next beat, so it absorbs most of the cut. That matters, because diastole is when the ventricles fill. At very high rates, or when the ventricle relaxes poorly, there may not be enough time to fill. EDV then falls, and stroke volume falls with it. A faster heart rate does not automatically mean less stroke volume, though. During exercise, greater venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can maintain or even raise it. The heart also cannot be driven into a sustained, tetanic contraction, because its long refractory period forces relaxation between beats. Cycle length = 60 ÷ heart rate. At 75 beats/min it is 0.80 s (800 ms). This tool calculates total cycle length only; it does not model the exact split between systole and diastole. Related: cardiac output and rate → Cardiac Output and Frank–Starling Law, §2 · refractory period → Cardiac Muscle Physiology, §6 Coronary blood flow. The left ventricle's own muscle receives most of its blood flow during diastole. During systole the contracting muscle squeezes the vessels running through it, especially the deeper ones. So marked tachycardia hits twice: it shortens the time for coronary perfusion just as the faster-working heart needs more oxygen. ( → Coronary Circulation, §3 and §5) Understanding Check — Section 10 During inspiration, P2 occurs later than during expiration. Which explanation is best? A. More right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance prolong RV ejection B. Inspiration slows conduction in the right bundle, delaying RV activation C. Pulmonary arterial pressure rises on inspiration, so RV ejection starts late D. Less blood returns to the right atrium, so the tricuspid valve closes later Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Both inspiratory effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so the pulmonary valve closes later and the A2–P2 gap widens. 11. Understanding Check Predict first, then reveal. Each scenario changes one thing. Work out what happens in the rest of the heart before opening the answer. Scenario 1: Contracting, but no blood leaving. The left ventricle has just started contracting. Its pressure is rising, but its volume is not changing. What are the valves and the left atrium doing? Reveal explanation This is isovolumetric contraction. The mitral valve has just shut, and the aortic valve has not opened yet, because ventricular pressure is still below aortic pressure. With both shut, blood can't get in or out. Meanwhile the left atrium is relaxed and filling with blood from the lungs. Contracting and ejecting are not the same thing. Scenario 2: Relaxing, but no blood entering. Ejection has ended and the aortic valve has shut. Ventricular pressure is falling, but volume is not changing. Why is no blood coming in? Reveal explanation This is isovolumetric relaxation. Ventricular pressure is still higher than atrial pressure, so the mitral valve stays shut. The atrium is full, at its v wave. Once ventricular pressure drops below atrial pressure, the mitral valve opens and blood rushes in. Scenario 3: The atria stop contracting properly. Does ventricular filling stop? Reveal explanation No. Most filling is passive: blood flows in whenever atrial pressure is higher than ventricular pressure. Only the final top-up from atrial contraction is lost. That loss matters most when the ventricle is stiff or the heart rate is fast. Scenario 4: Heart rate doubles. The rate rises from 75 to 150 beats/min. What may happen to filling, stroke volume and blood flow to the heart muscle? Reveal explanation Each beat now lasts 0.4 s instead of 0.8 s, and most of the lost time comes out of diastole. So there is less time to fill the ventricle and less time for blood to flow into the left ventricle's own muscle. Stroke volume may fall, but not always: during exercise, more venous return, faster relaxation and stronger contraction can protect it. Final questions (answer and explanation shown straight after each response) Q1. A patient's ventricle becomes stiff and relaxes poorly. Atrial contraction is normal. What happens to ventricular filling? A. Passive filling rises, so the atrial kick becomes unnecessary for filling B. Filling stops completely, because the mitral valve can no longer open C. Passive filling falls, so the atrial kick supplies a larger share of filling D. Filling is unchanged, because filling depends only on the SA node firing Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A stiff ventricle doesn't stretch easily. As blood enters, its pressure rises quickly and soon matches atrial pressure, so passive inflow stops early. The atria's final push then makes up a bigger part of the filling. This is why losing atrial contraction hurts these patients most. Q2. During diastasis, before the next P wave, what is happening electrically? A. SA nodal cells are slowly depolarising towards their next threshold B. A slow excitation wave is spreading through the ventricular muscle C. The AV node is firing repeatedly to sustain passive ventricular filling D. Purkinje fibres are repolarising the atria in preparation for systole Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Passive filling needs no electrical impulse; the pressure difference moves the blood. Meanwhile the SA node's pacemaker cells slowly drift towards threshold. When they fire, the P wave and atrial contraction follow. Q3. The left ventricle starts contracting slightly before the right, yet the pulmonary valve opens before the aortic valve. Why? A. The right ventricle contracts more strongly than the left ventricle does B. The impulse reaches the right ventricle first by a faster route C. The pulmonary valve has thinner cusps that open at any pressure D. The right ventricle only has to exceed a much lower arterial pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The right ventricle only needs to beat about 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery; the left must beat about 80 mmHg in the aorta. The right ventricle reaches its target sooner, so its valve opens first. Q4. Ventricles X and Y eject the same stroke volume, but X starts with a larger end-diastolic volume. Which conclusion follows? A. Both have the same ejection fraction, because their stroke volumes match B. X has the lower ejection fraction, because SV is a smaller share of its EDV C. X has the lower end-systolic volume, because it started with more blood D. Both have the same end-systolic volume, because they ejected equal volumes Show answer and explanation Answer: B. EF = SV ÷ EDV. If SV is the same but EDV is bigger, the fraction is smaller. X also has the larger ESV, since ESV = EDV − SV. Q5. Late in ejection, left ventricular pressure falls slightly below aortic pressure, yet blood keeps leaving for a moment. Why? A. The left atrium contracts and pushes blood on through the ventricle B. The aortic cusps actively propel blood forward as they begin closing C. Blood already moving forward carries momentum that takes time to stop D. The mitral valve opens and adds atrial pressure behind the outflow Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Moving blood can't stop instantly. It slows down, then briefly flows backwards, and that backflow is what shuts the aortic valve. Q6. An arrhythmia raises a resting patient's heart rate from 75 to 150 beats/min. Which change is most likely? A. Systole and diastole both halve, so filling time is proportionally kept B. Systole shortens most, so ejection is incomplete but filling is protected C. Coronary perfusion time rises, because the heart relaxes more often D. Diastole shortens most, so filling and coronary perfusion time both fall Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Systole can only shorten a little, so diastole loses most of the time. At rest, without the boosts that exercise brings, less filling time can lower EDV and stroke volume. The left ventricle's muscle also gets less time to receive blood. Q7. Which pairing of an atrial pressure wave with its cause is correct? A. c wave: the closed AV valve bulging back towards the atrium B. v wave: the atrium contracting against a closed AV valve C. a wave: venous blood filling the atrium while the AV valve is shut D. y descent: the atrium relaxing while the AV valve stays closed Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The c wave comes from the contracting ventricle pushing the shut valve back. The a wave is the atrium contracting, the v wave is the atrium filling up, and the y descent happens when the AV valve opens and the atrium empties. Q8. End-diastolic volume is 150 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. What are the stroke volume and ejection fraction? A. 90 mL and 40% B. 90 mL and 60% C. 60 mL and 60% D. 60 mL and 40% Show answer and explanation Answer: B. SV = 150 − 60 = 90 mL. EF = 90 ÷ 150 × 100 = 60%. 12. Chapter Essentials The atria and ventricles take turns. The atria contract at the end of ventricular diastole. While the ventricles contract, the atria relax and refill. For about 0.4 s of a 0.8 s beat, both are relaxed together. One chain drives every phase: electrical signal → contraction or relaxation → pressure change → valves open or close → blood moves and volume changes. Valves are passive. Each opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front, and shuts when that reverses. Papillary muscles stop the AV valves turning inside out; they do not open them. Filling is mostly passive: rapid filling, then slower diastasis, then the atrial kick (lecture: 70% passive, 30% atrial; at rest often 10–30% atrial). The kick matters more with a stiff ventricle or a fast heart rate. Isovolumetric contraction: all four valves are shut, so the contracting ventricle's pressure shoots up while its volume stays at EDV. Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves contributes to S1. Ejection starts only when ventricular pressure beats arterial pressure: about 80 mmHg in the aorta, 10 mmHg in the pulmonary artery. It is rapid first, then reduced, as the muscle weakens and the aorta fills. Isovolumetric relaxation: forward flow briefly reverses and shuts the aortic and pulmonary valves (S2, dicrotic notch). With all valves shut, pressure falls while volume stays at ESV. It ends when the mitral and tricuspid valves open. Volumes: SV = EDV − ESV and EF = SV ÷ EDV × 100%. Stroke volume rises by filling more (bigger EDV) or emptying more (smaller ESV). Atrial pressure waves: a = atria contract; c = shut AV valves bulge back; x = atria relax and the valve ring is pulled down; v = atria fill behind shut valves; y = AV valves open and the atria empty. Electrical comes before mechanical: the P wave before atrial contraction, the QRS before ventricular contraction, and the T wave during reduced ejection, before the outflow valves shut. On a Wiggers diagram, every valve event is a point where two pressure curves cross. Right heart and heart rate: the right heart follows the same rules at lower pressures, so the right ventricle ejects first; on inspiration P2 is delayed and S2 splitting widens. At faster heart rates diastole loses most of the time, so very high rates cut filling and coronary perfusion time."},{"slug":"heart-sounds","title":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"18-19 and 27-34","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds","title":"1. Why does the heart produce sounds?"},{"id":"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2","title":"2. Why does S1 occur before S2?"},{"id":"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration","title":"3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?"},{"id":"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4","title":"4. What causes S3 and S4?"},{"id":"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-loudness","title":"5. Why do heart sounds change in loudness?"},{"id":"6-what-makes-a-murmur","title":"6. What makes a murmur?"},{"id":"7-predict-a-murmur-s-timing-without-memorising-a-list","title":"7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list"},{"id":"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve","title":"8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?"},{"id":"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere","title":"9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?"},{"id":"10-understanding-check","title":"10. Understanding Check"},{"id":"11-chapter-essentials","title":"11. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Sounds and Murmurs</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds\">1. Why does the heart produce sounds?</h2>\n<p>With each beat, a healthy heart makes two main sounds, heard through a stethoscope as <strong>&quot;lub-dub&quot;</strong>. Each one is made by a pair of valves shutting.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Sound</th>\n  <th>Valves that shut</th>\n  <th>When it happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"s1\">S1</span>, &quot;lub&quot;</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Mitral</strong> and <strong>tricuspid</strong>, the inlet valves between the atria and the ventricles</td>\n  <td>As the ventricles start to contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span>, &quot;dub&quot;</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Aortic</strong> and <strong>pulmonary</strong>, the outlet valves into the aorta and the pulmonary artery</td>\n  <td>As the ventricles stop ejecting and start to relax</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does a shutting valve make a sound?</strong> The lecture puts it simply: the sounds are made by the valves closing. But what you hear is not the leaflets clapping together. When a valve snaps shut, the blood moving against it stops suddenly. That sudden stop makes the blood, the tightened valve leaflets and the nearby heart and artery walls vibrate. It's the same reason a water pipe bangs when a tap is turned off quickly. The vibrations travel through the chest wall to the stethoscope.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is there no sound when a valve opens?</strong> A normal valve opens gently as pressure builds behind it, and blood simply starts to flow smoothly. Nothing is stopped suddenly, so there is nothing to hear.</p>\n<p><strong>What the sounds tell you about timing.</strong> S1 marks the start of ventricular contraction, and S2 marks its end. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S1 → S2</strong> is <strong><span data-term=\"systole-diastole\">ventricular systole</span></strong>: the ventricles contract and eject.</li>\n<li><strong>S2 → next S1</strong> is <strong>ventricular diastole</strong>: the ventricles relax and fill.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At a resting heart rate, diastole is the longer phase. So the silence after &quot;dub&quot; is longer than the gap between &quot;lub&quot; and &quot;dub&quot;. That is how you tell S1 from S2 when listening. At fast heart rates the two gaps become similar, so clinicians feel the carotid pulse while listening: the pulse comes just after S1. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=1-one-heartbeat-what-the-atria-and-ventricles-are-doing\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"heart-sound-timeline\"><figcaption>Watch and listen to one heartbeat</figcaption><p>A marker moves across one heartbeat. At S1 the mitral and tricuspid valves light up as they shut; at S2 the aortic and pulmonary valves light up. The span from S1 to S2 is ventricular systole; the longer span after S2 is ventricular diastole. Play gives a lub-dub in time with the marker, and a heart-rate control (60–150 beats/min) shows diastole shrinking much more than systole as the rate rises. Schematic sounds and timings, not a recording.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Hear a real heart.</strong> Listen for lub, dub, then the longer pause.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource audio-resource\" data-audio-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/HROgg_Louder.ogg\" data-audio-mp3=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d3/HROgg_Louder.ogg/HROgg_Louder.ogg.mp3\" data-media-title=\"Amplified normal heart sounds at approximately 70 beats per minute\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">REAL HEART · AMPLIFIED RECORDING · ABOUT 19 SECONDS</span><h3>Normal S1–S2 sequence</h3><p>A real chest, recorded through a stethoscope. Headphones work best.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-audio\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Listen</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HROgg_Louder.ogg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on Wikimedia Commons</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">Original recording: James Heilman, MD (<a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HROgg.ogg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC0</a>); amplified version: Natural Philo (<a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>). Audio loads only when selected.</p></div>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the valve rule → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">Cardiac Cycle, §2</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2\">2. Why does S1 occur before S2?</h2>\n<p>Both sounds follow the valve rule from Chapter 4. A valve <strong>opens</strong> when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it. It <strong>shuts</strong> the moment that difference reverses. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=2-the-one-rule-every-valve-follows\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §2</a>)</em> Follow one beat. The left and right sides do the same thing at the same time; the right side just works at lower pressures.</p>\n<p><strong>S1: the inlet valves shut as contraction begins.</strong> The electrical signal spreads through the ventricles (the <span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span> on the ECG), and the ventricles start to contract. Within moments, pressure in each ventricle rises above the pressure in its atrium. Blood starts to push back towards the atria, and that push snaps the <strong>mitral</strong> and <strong>tricuspid</strong> valves shut: <strong>S1</strong>. The outlet valves are still shut as well, so for a moment the ventricles squeeze with every valve closed. This is <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>. S1 therefore marks the <strong>start of ventricular systole</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=4-isovolumetric-contraction-contracting-but-no-blood-leaves\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>S2: the outlet valves shut as ejection ends.</strong> The ventricles keep ejecting until their force fades. Their pressure then falls below the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. Blood briefly starts to flow back towards the ventricles, and this snaps the <strong>aortic</strong> and <strong>pulmonary</strong> valves shut: <strong>S2</strong>. The ventricles now relax with every valve closed again. This is isovolumetric relaxation. As the lecture puts it, S2 marks the <strong>end of ventricular systole, which is the beginning of diastole</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=6-closing-the-outflow-valves-and-isovolumetric-relaxation\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p>In one line: <strong>S1 is when the ventricle overtakes the atrium; S2 is when the artery overtakes the ventricle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How long are they, and why do they sound different?</strong> The lecture gives about <strong>0.15 s for S1</strong> and <strong>0.12 s for S2</strong>. S1 is the longer, lower &quot;lub&quot;. S2 is the shorter, sharper &quot;dub&quot;: the aortic and pulmonary valves, and the stretched artery walls behind them, are tighter, so they vibrate at a higher pitch.</p>\n<p><strong>Do both valves in a pair shut at exactly the same moment?</strong> Almost, but not quite. The lecture says they close at about the same time. For S1 the gap is too small to hear, so S1 sounds single. For S2 the gap can widen enough to hear as two parts. That is S2 splitting, the subject of the next section.</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> where S1 and S2 sit on the full timeline → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">Cardiac Cycle, §9</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration\">3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?</h2>\n<p><strong>Why S2 has two parts.</strong> S2 is made of <strong>A2</strong> (aortic closure) and <strong>P2</strong> (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During <strong>expiration</strong> they are close together and often sound single. During <strong>inspiration</strong>, P2 moves later and the gap widens: <strong><span data-term=\"s2-splitting\">physiological splitting of S2</span></strong>. Two effects delay P2:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>More right-heart filling.</strong> Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower pulmonary impedance.</strong> The expanding lungs lower the <span data-term=\"pulmonary-impedance\">impedance</span> (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly.</p>\n<p>These two effects are what the lecture means by &quot;venous return&quot; and &quot;reduces pulmonary resistance… thus longer time for blood flow&quot;.</p>\n<p><strong>Why &quot;physiological&quot;?</strong> Because it is normal. It happens in healthy people with every breath in, and it fades with every breath out. What matters is the <strong>change with breathing</strong>: the gap widens on inspiration and narrows on expiration. A split that stays wide during expiration as well is not normal. That is clinical material beyond this lecture.</p>\n<p><strong>Where and how to hear it.</strong> P2 is a soft sound, so listen at the <strong>pulmonary area</strong>: the second left intercostal space, beside the sternum (see <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve\">§8</a>). Ask the person to breathe slowly. On a breath in, the single &quot;dub&quot; becomes a quick &quot;da-dub&quot;. On a breath out, it merges back into one.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"s2-timing\"><figcaption>Why S2 splits on inspiration</figcaption><p>Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Hear it.</strong> A simulator recording at the pulmonary area. The simulator breathes quickly, so S2 changes from one sound to two and back every few beats. Listen for the &quot;dub&quot; becoming a short &quot;da-dub&quot;. <em>(Recording: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, &quot;split S2, transient&quot;, CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended.)</em></p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget sound-clips\" data-widget=\"sound-clips\" data-clips=\"split-s2\"><p>Recording: split S2 that comes and goes with breathing, at the pulmonary area.</p></div>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says the aortic and pulmonary valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the widening on inspiration. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it.</aside>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the full timing picture → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Cardiac Cycle, §10</a> · breathing and venous return → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return\">Veins and Venous Return, §5</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Listening at the second left intercostal space, you hear S2 as two sounds when the person breathes in and as one sound when they breathe out. What does this most likely show?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Delayed closure of the mitral valve because more blood fills the left heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Early closure of the pulmonary valve because less blood returns to the right heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Normal widening of the A2–P2 gap as the right ventricle ejects for longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A stiff right ventricle that delays the start of right ventricular ejection</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Inspiration fills the right ventricle more and lowers the opposition to flow in the lungs. Both effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so P2 comes later. The mitral valve has nothing to do with S2, and the gap closing again on expiration shows this is normal, not disease.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> At the instant S2 is heard over the aortic area, which pressure change has just happened?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above left atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below left atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above aortic pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below aortic pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The aortic valve shuts when the artery overtakes the ventricle. A is the moment of S1 (the mitral valve shuts). C is the aortic valve opening, which makes no sound. B is the mitral valve opening at the end of isovolumetric relaxation, which is also silent.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> During inspiration, why does blood keep flowing into the pulmonary artery after right ventricular pressure has peaked?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The tricuspid valve stays open later during inspiration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The expanded lung vessels offer less opposition to flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The pulmonary valve has fewer cusps than the aortic valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Right ventricular contraction starts later on inspiration</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Expanded lungs lower the impedance of the pulmonary vessels, so forward flow continues for longer and the pulmonary valve shuts later. The tricuspid valve must be shut while the right ventricle ejects. Both outflow valves have three cusps. Inspiration does not delay the electrical activation of the right ventricle.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4\">4. What causes S3 and S4?</h2>\n<p>S1 and S2 are valves shutting. S3 and S4 are different: no valve shuts. They are <strong>filling sounds</strong>, made in diastole as blood rushes into the ventricle and is then suddenly slowed. The lecture says the same: both are due to ventricular filling. What separates them is <strong>what drives the rush</strong>, and that puts them at opposite ends of diastole.</p>\n<p>Recall how a ventricle fills <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §3</a>)</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rapid filling.</strong> The mitral and tricuspid valves open, and blood stored in the atria pours into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"diastasis\">Diastasis</span>.</strong> Filling slows to a trickle.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"atrial-kick\">Atrial kick</span>.</strong> The atria contract and push in the last portion of blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"s3\">S3</span>: at the end of rapid filling, early in diastole.</strong> About 0.1–0.2 s after S2, the rush of early filling reaches the limit of how far the ventricle wall will easily stretch. The inflowing blood is suddenly slowed, and the ventricle wall and blood vibrate. The result is a low, dull sound just after S2: <strong>&quot;lub-dub-dum&quot;</strong>. Many students remember the rhythm as <em>Ken-TUC-ky</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When it is normal:</strong> in children, young adults, athletes and pregnant women. Their ventricles relax quickly and fill very fast.</li>\n<li><strong>When it suggests a problem:</strong> with age, early filling normally slows and S3 disappears. In an older adult, an S3 usually means the ventricle is being overfilled or is enlarged and failing. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure\">→ Heart Failure</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"s4\">S4</span>: during the atrial kick, late in diastole.</strong> Just before S1, the atria contract and push their blood into the ventricles. If the ventricle is <strong>stiff</strong>, for example thickened, it resists this final push. The blood is stopped abruptly and the wall vibrates. The result is a low sound just before S1: <strong>&quot;da-lub-dub&quot;</strong>, or <em>TEN-nes-see</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It depends entirely on atrial contraction.</strong> It comes just after the <span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span> and goes with the <span data-term=\"a-wave\">a wave</span> in the atria. So it is <strong>absent in atrial fibrillation</strong>, where the atria quiver instead of contracting together.</li>\n<li><strong>When it suggests a problem:</strong> an S4 you can actually hear with a stethoscope in an adult usually means a stiff ventricle.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>S3</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>S4</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>When</td>\n  <td>Early diastole, just after S2</td>\n  <td>Late diastole, just before S1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>What drives the rush</td>\n  <td>Passive rapid filling</td>\n  <td>Atrial contraction (the atrial kick)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Rhythm</td>\n  <td>lub-dub-<strong>dum</strong> (<em>Ken-TUC-ky</em>)</td>\n  <td><strong>da</strong>-lub-dub (<em>TEN-nes-see</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Heard in healthy people?</td>\n  <td>Often, in the young</td>\n  <td>Rarely heard with a stethoscope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>In an older adult it usually means</td>\n  <td>An overfilled or failing ventricle</td>\n  <td>A stiff ventricle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Needs atrial contraction?</td>\n  <td>No</td>\n  <td>Yes, so it is lost in atrial fibrillation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>How to listen.</strong> Both are very low-pitched. Use the <strong>bell</strong> of the stethoscope, pressed lightly, at the <strong>apex</strong>, with the person turned onto their <strong>left side</strong>. This brings the apex closer to the chest wall.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"filling-sounds\"><figcaption>Where S3 and S4 sit in filling</figcaption><p>Ventricular volume through one diastole: a steep rise (rapid filling), a flat stretch (diastasis), then a small final rise (atrial kick). S3 is marked where the steep rise suddenly levels off; S4 is marked on the atrial kick; S2 and the next S1 are marked at either end. Hear S3 and Hear S4 play the matching recording while the marker lights up. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture lists both S3 and S4 as normal sounds due to rapid ventricular filling. We agree that both are filling sounds. Standard physiology texts (e.g. Guyton and Hall) separate them by cause: S3 comes from rapid passive filling, S4 from atrial contraction. They also note that S3 is often normal in young people, while an audible S4 in an adult usually points to a stiff ventricle. We teach that distinction because it explains why S4 disappears in atrial fibrillation.</aside>\n\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the filling phases → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=3-filling-how-blood-enters-the-relaxed-ventricle\">Cardiac Cycle, §3</a> · S3 and S4 on the Wiggers diagram → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">Cardiac Cycle, §9</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-loudness\">5. Why do heart sounds change in loudness?</h2>\n<p>Each sound is made by a valve snapping shut. So anything that makes the snap <strong>harder</strong> makes the sound <strong>louder</strong>. For S1 and S2, two different things control how hard the snap is.</p>\n<p><strong>S1 depends on how wide open the mitral and tricuspid valves are when contraction starts.</strong> The lecture links S1's loudness to how the ventricle fills. Think of a door: one that is already nearly shut closes quietly, but one flung shut from wide open bangs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slow heart rate → soft S1.</strong> Diastole is long, so the ventricles finish filling well before they contract. As the inflow slows, the cusps of the mitral and tricuspid valves drift up towards each other. The lecture calls these &quot;floating valves&quot;. When the ventricles finally contract, the cusps only have a short way to go, so they close gently.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast heart rate → loud S1.</strong> Diastole is short, so the ventricles contract while blood is still rushing in and the cusps are still wide apart. Rising ventricular pressure slams them shut from fully open.</li>\n<li><strong>A stronger contraction also gives a louder S1</strong>, for example during exercise. Ventricular pressure rises faster, so the cusps are driven shut harder.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"s1-loudness\"><figcaption>Why a slow heart has a soft S1</figcaption><p>Two panels of the mitral valve at the instant the ventricle starts to contract. Slow heart: the cusps are already close together and travel a short distance (soft S1). Fast heart: the cusps are still wide open and travel a long distance (loud S1). Play animates both closing at the same moment, with a quiet and a loud lub. Schematic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>S2 depends on the pressure in the artery behind the valve.</strong> The aortic and pulmonary valves are pushed shut by the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. The higher that pressure, the harder and quicker the valve is driven shut. This is the lecture's &quot;brisk closure&quot;.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High blood pressure in the aorta</strong> (systemic hypertension) → loud <strong>A2</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>High pressure in the pulmonary artery</strong> (pulmonary hypertension) → loud <strong>P2</strong>. It is heard best at the pulmonary area, the second left intercostal space.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What a stethoscope picks up also depends on the route to the chest wall.</strong> Sounds are quieter through a thick chest wall, fluid around the heart, or over-inflated lungs. A badly damaged, stiff valve may also barely move, so its closing sound becomes softer even though the disease is severe. So <strong>loudness alone never tells you how serious a problem is</strong>. It is one clue to put alongside timing and where the sound is heard.</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> heart rate and the length of diastole → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=10-right-versus-left-heart-s2-splitting-and-heart-rate\">Cardiac Cycle, §10</a> · contractility → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=7-what-changes-contractility\">Cardiac Output, §7</a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A patient's S4 disappears when they develop atrial fibrillation. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricle becomes less stiff once the heart rate rises</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The mitral valve no longer opens fully during diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Rapid filling ends before the atria have time to empty</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The atria no longer contract together to push the last blood in</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> S4 is made when the atrial kick pushes blood into a stiff ventricle. In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver instead of contracting together, so there is no kick and no S4. The ventricle is just as stiff as before.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> An S3 is heard in a healthy 19-year-old athlete. Which explanation fits best?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Atrial contraction is pushing blood into a stiff left ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fast early filling of a ventricle that relaxes quickly is suddenly slowed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The aortic and pulmonary valves are closing at clearly different times</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The mitral valve is closing late because the heart rate is very slow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> S3 comes at the end of rapid filling. Young, quickly relaxing ventricles fill very fast, so the sudden slowing can be heard, and that is normal. A describes S4. C describes split S2. D mixes up S3 with S1.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In a person with a slow resting heart rate, S1 is soft. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricle contracts less forcefully because it is overfilled</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The aortic valve shuts gently because aortic pressure is low</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The mitral and tricuspid cusps have nearly floated shut already</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The atria contract weakly, so less blood crosses the valves</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A long diastole lets filling finish, and the cusps drift close together before contraction starts. They only have a short way to travel, so they close softly. A fuller ventricle actually contracts more strongly. The aortic valve makes S2, not S1.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A loud P2 at the second left intercostal space most likely points to:<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised pressure in the pulmonary artery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Raised pressure in the right atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Faster flow through the tricuspid valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Lower resistance in the lung vessels</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The higher the pressure behind the pulmonary valve, the harder it is driven shut, so P2 gets louder. D is what happens on inspiration: it delays P2 rather than making it louder.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-what-makes-a-murmur\">6. What makes a murmur?</h2>\n<p><strong>Normal blood flow is silent.</strong> Blood normally moves in smooth layers, sliding past one another. This is <strong><span data-term=\"laminar-flow\">laminar flow</span></strong>, the lecture's &quot;streamlined&quot; flow. Nothing tumbles or collides, so there is nothing to hear.</p>\n<p>A <strong><span data-term=\"murmur\">murmur</span></strong> is the sound of blood flow that has become <strong><span data-term=\"turbulent-flow\">turbulent</span></strong>: swirling and tumbling instead of sliding in layers. The swirls shake the heart and vessel walls for as long as the turbulence lasts. So a murmur is a longer <strong>whooshing</strong> sound, not a short thud like S1 or S2.</p>\n<p><strong>What makes flow turbulent?</strong> Flow tips into turbulence when it is <strong>fast</strong>, when the blood is <strong>thin</strong> (low viscosity), or when it is forced through a <strong>sudden narrowing</strong>. The lecture sums this up with the Reynolds number: the higher it is, the more likely turbulence becomes. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">→ Haemodynamics, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>The heart can make flow turbulent in three ways.</strong> These follow the lecture's list.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A narrowed valve (<span data-term=\"stenosis\">stenosis</span>).</strong> When the valve should be open, it opens only partly. Blood is squeezed through the small opening as a fast jet, which becomes turbulent beyond the valve. This murmur is made by <strong>forward</strong> flow.</li>\n<li><strong>A leaking valve (<span data-term=\"regurgitation\">regurgitation</span>, the lecture's &quot;incompetence&quot;).</strong> When the valve should be shut, it does not close fully. Blood is driven <strong>backwards</strong> through the gap as a fast jet. This is the lecture's &quot;backflow&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast flow through normal valves (a &quot;hyperdynamic&quot; circulation).</strong> The valves are healthy, but blood moves fast enough, or is thin enough, to become turbulent anyway. The lecture's example is <strong>anaemia</strong>, where thin blood and a raised cardiac output combine. Exercise, fever and pregnancy can do the same. These <strong><span data-term=\"flow-murmur\">flow murmurs</span></strong> do not mean the valves are damaged.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"murmur-figure\"><figcaption>Three ways to make a murmur</figcaption><p>Four panels. Normal: a fully open valve with smooth, parallel arrows (laminar: silent). Stenosis: a narrowed opening with a fast jet that breaks into swirls beyond it (forward jet). Regurgitation: a valve that should be shut, with a thin jet leaking backwards into swirls (backward jet). High flow: a normal open valve with many long, fast arrows becoming swirls (normal valve, fast flow). In every murmur there is turbulence somewhere; the questions are where it is and which way the blood is going.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The key question for any murmur:</strong> which way is the abnormal blood flowing, and is that happening in systole or diastole? That decides when you hear it, which is the subject of <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=7-predict-a-murmur-s-timing-without-memorising-a-list\">§7</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Murmur or bruit?</strong> Both come from the same mechanism. A murmur is heard over the heart; a bruit is heard over a blood vessel (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere\">§9</a>).</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> laminar flow, turbulence and the Reynolds number → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">Haemodynamics, §5</a> · anaemia and viscosity → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells\">Haemodynamics, §7</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-predict-a-murmur-s-timing-without-memorising-a-list\">7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list</h2>\n<p>The lecture's tip is to <strong>group the valves that close together</strong>. The aortic and pulmonary valves behave as one pair, and the mitral and tricuspid valves as the other. Then you only need to know what each pair should be doing in each phase:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>During</th>\n  <th>Mitral and tricuspid (inlet valves)</th>\n  <th>Aortic and pulmonary (outlet valves)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ventricular systole</strong> (S1 → S2)</td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>shut</strong></td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>open</strong>, for ejection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ventricular diastole</strong> (S2 → S1)</td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>open</strong>, for filling</td>\n  <td>Should be <strong>shut</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For any faulty valve, ask one question: <strong>when is the faulty valve supposed to be open (for stenosis) or shut (for a leak)?</strong> That phase is when the abnormal flow happens, so that is when you hear the murmur.</p>\n<p><strong>Systolic murmurs (between S1 and S2)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aortic or pulmonary stenosis.</strong> These valves should be open while the ventricles eject. A narrowed opening squeezes forward flow into a fast jet, so the murmur is <strong>systolic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation.</strong> These valves should be shut while the ventricles contract. If they leak, the contracting ventricle drives blood back into the atrium. The leak starts as soon as contraction starts and lasts until relaxation, so the murmur can fill <strong>all of systole</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diastolic murmurs (between S2 and the next S1)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mitral or tricuspid stenosis.</strong> These valves should be open while the ventricles fill. A narrowed opening obstructs filling, so the murmur is <strong>diastolic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation.</strong> These valves should be shut while the ventricles relax. The high pressure in the artery drives blood back into the ventricle, starting <strong>right after S2</strong>, so the murmur is <strong>diastolic</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>At a glance</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Valve pair</th>\n  <th>Stenosis (forward jet)</th>\n  <th>Regurgitation (backward jet)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aortic / pulmonary</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Systolic</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Diastolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Mitral / tricuspid</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Diastolic</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Systolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The pattern is a cross: each lesion falls in the opposite phase to its partner. You never need to memorise it. If you forget, ask the question again.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"murmur-predictor\"><figcaption>Murmur predictor</figcaption><p>Pick a valve (aortic, pulmonary, mitral, tricuspid) and a fault (narrowed or leaking). The tool then shows, one step at a time, when that valve should be open or shut, which way the abnormal jet flows, the phase on an S1–S2–S1 timeline, and the answer: systolic or diastolic. Hear an example plays a recording typical of that lesion where one exists. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0.</p></figure>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"dBwr2GZCmQM\" data-media-title=\"Heart Sounds and Heart Murmurs — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · ABOUT 4 MINUTES</span><h3>Watch it all together</h3><p>Alila Medical Media, <em>Heart Sounds and Heart Murmurs</em>. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dBwr2GZCmQM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. When is the murmur heard?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. During systole, between S1 and S2, as the ventricle ejects</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. During diastole, starting just after S2, as the ventricle relaxes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Late in diastole only, when the atria push blood into the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Throughout the cycle, because aortic pressure is always higher</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The aortic valve should be shut while the ventricle relaxes. Aortic pressure is far above ventricular pressure then, so blood leaks back from the moment the valve closes (S2). In systole the valve is open anyway, so there is no backflow to hear.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur, while mitral regurgitation causes a systolic one?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Stenosis raises aortic pressure in diastole; a leak lowers left atrial pressure in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Stenosis is heard at the apex only in diastole; a leak is heard at the base only in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Stenosis blocks filling when the valve should be open; a leak lets blood back when it should be shut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Stenosis delays mitral closure into diastole; a leak moves aortic closure earlier into systole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The mitral valve should be open during filling (diastole), so a narrowed one causes a diastolic murmur. It should be shut during contraction (systole), so a leaking one causes a systolic murmur.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient with severe anaemia has a soft systolic murmur, but all four valves are normal on a scan. What is the most likely cause?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The mitral valve leaks because the left ventricle is overfilled</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The aortic valve narrows because red cells clump on its cusps</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Low blood viscosity delays closure of the pulmonary valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Thin blood and a high cardiac output make ejection flow turbulent</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> This is a flow murmur: normal valves, but blood that is thin and fast enough to become turbulent during ejection. It does not mean the valves are damaged.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve\">8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?</h2>\n<p>The lecture says each valve is best heard over its <strong>surface marking</strong>: the spot on the chest wall where that valve's sound comes through most clearly. These four spots are the <strong><span data-term=\"auscultation-areas\">auscultation areas</span></strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Area</th>\n  <th>Where on the chest</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aortic</strong></td>\n  <td>Second <strong>right</strong> intercostal space, beside the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pulmonary</strong></td>\n  <td>Second <strong>left</strong> intercostal space, beside the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Tricuspid</strong></td>\n  <td>Lower <strong>left</strong> border of the sternum (fourth or fifth intercostal space)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Mitral</strong></td>\n  <td>The <strong>apex</strong>: fifth left intercostal space, in line with the middle of the clavicle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration teaching-illustration-portrait\" id=\"cvs05-auscultation\">\n<img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/chapter05/auscultation-areas.jpg\" alt=\"Front view of the rib cage and sternum with four labelled listening points: aortic at the second right intercostal space beside the sternum, pulmonary at the second left, tricuspid at the lower left sternal border, and mitral at the apex in the fifth left intercostal space.\" width=\"850\" height=\"793\">\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Where to place the stethoscope for each valve.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Rib cage drawing from Gray's <em>Anatomy of the Human Body</em> (1918), public domain; listening-point labels added. Supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why aren't these spots directly over the valves?</strong> The four valves sit close together behind the sternum, packed into a small area. Each valve's sound travels <strong>in the direction its blood flows</strong>, and comes out where that chamber or vessel lies closest to the chest wall:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The aortic valve sits behind the left side of the sternum. Its sound is carried up the aorta, which comes nearest the surface at the <strong>right</strong> second intercostal space.</li>\n<li>The mitral valve also sits behind the sternum. Its sound is carried down through the left ventricle to the <strong>apex</strong>, where the ventricle touches the chest wall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A practical tip for telling S1 from S2:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S1 is loudest at the apex</strong>, where the mitral sound comes out.</li>\n<li><strong>S2 is loudest at the base</strong>, the aortic and pulmonary areas at the top of the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Moving the stethoscope between the two and hearing which sound gets louder tells you which is which.</p>\n<div class=\"teaching-widget sound-clips\" data-widget=\"sound-clips\" data-clips=\"normal-apex,normal-base\"><p>Two recordings of the same normal heart sounds: at the apex, where S1 is louder, and at the base, where S2 is louder.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Bell or diaphragm?</strong> The <strong>diaphragm</strong>, pressed firmly, picks up higher-pitched sounds: S1, S2 and most murmurs. The <strong>bell</strong>, resting lightly, picks up low-pitched sounds such as S3, S4 and the rumble of mitral stenosis.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture slide gives \"AT PM\" beside the listening areas. We could not confirm what this abbreviation means, so we present the four standard areas without it. Valve surface positions follow standard anatomy texts.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere\">9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?</h2>\n<p>Turbulence is not limited to the heart valves. Wherever blood is forced through a narrowing, takes a short cut, or flows unusually fast, it can become turbulent and make a sound (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=6-what-makes-a-murmur\">§6</a>). Heard over a vessel, this sound is called a <strong><span data-term=\"bruit\">bruit</span></strong>. The lecture lists these examples:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Condition</th>\n  <th>Why the flow becomes turbulent</th>\n  <th>When it is heard</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</strong>: the fetal channel between the aorta and pulmonary artery stays open after birth</td>\n  <td>Aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure in <strong>both</strong> systole and diastole, so blood crosses all the time</td>\n  <td><strong>Continuous</strong>, through S2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</strong>: a hole between the ventricles</td>\n  <td>When the ventricles contract, left ventricular pressure far exceeds right, so blood jets through the hole</td>\n  <td><strong>Systolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arteriovenous (AV) fistula</strong>: a direct channel from an artery to a vein</td>\n  <td>High-pressure arterial blood rushes into a low-pressure vein, skipping the capillaries</td>\n  <td>Continuous bruit over the site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aneurysm</strong>: a ballooned section of artery</td>\n  <td>The sudden widening disturbs smooth flow (not every aneurysm makes a sound)</td>\n  <td>A bruit over the aneurysm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Goitre</strong> (enlarged thyroid) with high blood flow</td>\n  <td>Increased blood flow through the gland</td>\n  <td>A bruit over the thyroid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Anaemia</strong></td>\n  <td>Thin, fast blood (see §6)</td>\n  <td>A soft systolic flow murmur</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The rule is the same everywhere: <strong>find the pressure difference that drives the abnormal flow, and when it exists.</strong> That tells you when the sound is heard. The PDA murmur is continuous for exactly this reason: the pressure difference between the aorta and pulmonary artery never reverses.</p>\n<p class=\"related-links\"><strong>Related:</strong> the ductus arteriosus and why it should close after birth → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/fetal?section=4-what-closes-after-delivery\">Fetal Circulation, \"What closes after delivery?\"</a> · bruits over narrowed arteries → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">Haemodynamics, §5</a> · Korotkoff sounds in blood-pressure measurement → <a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/blood-pressure?section=7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure\">Blood Pressure, §7</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-understanding-check\">10. Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><em>Questions on the whole chapter. The earlier checks cover Sections 1–7 in detail.</em></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A patient has a patent ductus arteriosus, an open channel joining the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Why is its murmur continuous through systole and diastole?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ductus closes in systole and reopens in diastole each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure through the whole cycle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pulmonary pressure stays above aortic pressure through the whole cycle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Both the aortic and pulmonary valves leak during every diastole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Flow crosses the ductus whenever aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure, and in this patient that is true throughout the cycle. Because the driving difference never reverses, the turbulence and the sound never stop.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is the mitral valve best heard at the apex rather than directly over the valve?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The mitral valve actually lies beneath the fifth left intercostal space</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The sternum blocks all sound, so no valve can be heard over it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its sound is carried to where the left ventricle meets the chest wall</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The apex is the only area where the bell can be applied lightly</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The mitral valve sits behind the sternum, but its sound travels with the blood into the left ventricle and comes out at the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A 70-year-old has a stiff, thickened left ventricle. Which extra sound would you most expect, and why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. An S4 just before S1, from the atrial kick</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. An S3 just after S2, from rapid early filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A loud S1 at the apex, from floating cusps</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A split S2 on expiration, from late P2</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A stiff ventricle resists the final push of blood from the atria, which makes S4. An S3 points to an overfilled or failing ventricle. Floating cusps make S1 <em>soft</em>, not loud. A split that persists on expiration is not explained by stiffness.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Listening at the apex, you hear \"lub-dub\" with a long pause after \"dub\". Then you move to the second right intercostal space, and the second sound becomes the louder one. What have you confirmed?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The second sound is S1, because S1 is loudest over the aortic area</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The first sound is S1, because S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The patient has aortic regurgitation, because S2 is loud at the base</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The second sound is split, because it changes when you move position</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> S1 is loudest at the apex and S2 at the base. Together with the long pause after S2 (diastole), this confirms which sound is which. A louder S2 at the base is normal, not a sign of disease.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"11-chapter-essentials\">11. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>S1 (&quot;lub&quot;)</strong>: the mitral and tricuspid valves shutting as the ventricles start to contract. It marks the start of ventricular systole.</li>\n<li><strong>S2 (&quot;dub&quot;)</strong>: the aortic and pulmonary valves shutting as ejection ends. It marks the start of ventricular diastole.</li>\n<li><strong>The sound is not the leaflets clapping</strong>: it is the vibration caused when moving blood is suddenly stopped. Normal valves open silently.</li>\n<li><strong>S1 → S2 is systole; S2 → S1 is diastole.</strong> At rest the pause after S2 is longer. S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiological splitting of S2</strong>: on inspiration, more right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance delay P2, so A2 and P2 separate. They merge again on expiration.</li>\n<li><strong>S3</strong>: early diastole, at the end of rapid filling. Often normal in the young; in older adults it suggests an overfilled or failing ventricle.</li>\n<li><strong>S4</strong>: late diastole, from the atrial kick into a stiff ventricle. It needs atrial contraction, so it is absent in atrial fibrillation.</li>\n<li><strong>Loudness</strong>: a slow heart gives a soft S1 (the cusps have floated nearly shut). High aortic or pulmonary pressure gives a loud A2 or P2. Loudness alone does not measure severity.</li>\n<li><strong>Murmurs are turbulent flow</strong>: from a narrowed valve (forward jet), a leaking valve (backward jet), or fast, thin blood through normal valves (flow murmur, e.g. anaemia).</li>\n<li><strong>Timing rule</strong>: ask when the faulty valve should be open (stenosis) or shut (leak). Aortic/pulmonary stenosis and mitral/tricuspid regurgitation are <strong>systolic</strong>. Mitral/tricuspid stenosis and aortic/pulmonary regurgitation are <strong>diastolic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Auscultation areas</strong>: aortic at the second right intercostal space; pulmonary at the second left; tricuspid at the lower left sternal border; mitral at the apex. These are where each valve's sound comes out, not where the valves sit.</li>\n<li><strong>Bruits</strong> follow the same rule as murmurs: find the pressure difference driving the abnormal flow, and when it exists. A PDA is continuous because aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure all the time.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs 1. Why does the heart produce sounds? With each beat, a healthy heart makes two main sounds, heard through a stethoscope as \"lub-dub\". Each one is made by a pair of valves shutting. Sound Valves that shut When it happens S1, \"lub\" Mitral and tricuspid, the inlet valves between the atria and the ventricles As the ventricles start to contract S2, \"dub\" Aortic and pulmonary, the outlet valves into the aorta and the pulmonary artery As the ventricles stop ejecting and start to relax Why does a shutting valve make a sound? The lecture puts it simply: the sounds are made by the valves closing. But what you hear is not the leaflets clapping together. When a valve snaps shut, the blood moving against it stops suddenly. That sudden stop makes the blood, the tightened valve leaflets and the nearby heart and artery walls vibrate. It's the same reason a water pipe bangs when a tap is turned off quickly. The vibrations travel through the chest wall to the stethoscope. Why is there no sound when a valve opens? A normal valve opens gently as pressure builds behind it, and blood simply starts to flow smoothly. Nothing is stopped suddenly, so there is nothing to hear. What the sounds tell you about timing. S1 marks the start of ventricular contraction, and S2 marks its end. So: S1 → S2 is ventricular systole: the ventricles contract and eject. S2 → next S1 is ventricular diastole: the ventricles relax and fill. At a resting heart rate, diastole is the longer phase. So the silence after \"dub\" is longer than the gap between \"lub\" and \"dub\". That is how you tell S1 from S2 when listening. At fast heart rates the two gaps become similar, so clinicians feel the carotid pulse while listening: the pulse comes just after S1. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §1) Watch and listen to one heartbeat A marker moves across one heartbeat. At S1 the mitral and tricuspid valves light up as they shut; at S2 the aortic and pulmonary valves light up. The span from S1 to S2 is ventricular systole; the longer span after S2 is ventricular diastole. Play gives a lub-dub in time with the marker, and a heart-rate control (60–150 beats/min) shows diastole shrinking much more than systole as the rate rises. Schematic sounds and timings, not a recording. Hear a real heart. Listen for lub, dub, then the longer pause. REAL HEART · AMPLIFIED RECORDING · ABOUT 19 SECONDS Normal S1–S2 sequence A real chest, recorded through a stethoscope. Headphones work best. Listen Open on Wikimedia Commons Original recording: James Heilman, MD ( CC0); amplified version: Natural Philo ( CC BY-SA 3.0). Audio loads only when selected. Related: the valve rule → Cardiac Cycle, §2 2. Why does S1 occur before S2? Both sounds follow the valve rule from Chapter 4. A valve opens when the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure in front of it. It shuts the moment that difference reverses. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §2) Follow one beat. The left and right sides do the same thing at the same time; the right side just works at lower pressures. S1: the inlet valves shut as contraction begins. The electrical signal spreads through the ventricles (the QRS complex on the ECG), and the ventricles start to contract. Within moments, pressure in each ventricle rises above the pressure in its atrium. Blood starts to push back towards the atria, and that push snaps the mitral and tricuspid valves shut: S1. The outlet valves are still shut as well, so for a moment the ventricles squeeze with every valve closed. This is isovolumetric contraction. S1 therefore marks the start of ventricular systole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §4) S2: the outlet valves shut as ejection ends. The ventricles keep ejecting until their force fades. Their pressure then falls below the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. Blood briefly starts to flow back towards the ventricles, and this snaps the aortic and pulmonary valves shut: S2. The ventricles now relax with every valve closed again. This is isovolumetric relaxation. As the lecture puts it, S2 marks the end of ventricular systole, which is the beginning of diastole. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §6) In one line: S1 is when the ventricle overtakes the atrium; S2 is when the artery overtakes the ventricle. How long are they, and why do they sound different? The lecture gives about 0.15 s for S1 and 0.12 s for S2. S1 is the longer, lower \"lub\". S2 is the shorter, sharper \"dub\": the aortic and pulmonary valves, and the stretched artery walls behind them, are tighter, so they vibrate at a higher pitch. Do both valves in a pair shut at exactly the same moment? Almost, but not quite. The lecture says they close at about the same time. For S1 the gap is too small to hear, so S1 sounds single. For S2 the gap can widen enough to hear as two parts. That is S2 splitting, the subject of the next section. Related: where S1 and S2 sit on the full timeline → Cardiac Cycle, §9 3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration? Why S2 has two parts. S2 is made of A2 (aortic closure) and P2 (pulmonary closure). A2 normally comes first. During expiration they are close together and often sound single. During inspiration, P2 moves later and the gap widens: physiological splitting of S2. Two effects delay P2: More right-heart filling. Inspiration makes chest pressure more negative, drawing more venous blood into the right heart. The right ventricle fills more and takes longer to eject. Lower pulmonary impedance. The expanding lungs lower the impedance (opposition to flow) of the pulmonary vessels. Blood keeps flowing forward into the pulmonary artery for longer after right ventricular pressure peaks, so the pulmonary valve closes later. A2 may also move slightly earlier. The expanded lung vessels hold more blood for a few beats, so left ventricular filling dips briefly. These two effects are what the lecture means by \"venous return\" and \"reduces pulmonary resistance… thus longer time for blood flow\". Why \"physiological\"? Because it is normal. It happens in healthy people with every breath in, and it fades with every breath out. What matters is the change with breathing: the gap widens on inspiration and narrows on expiration. A split that stays wide during expiration as well is not normal. That is clinical material beyond this lecture. Where and how to hear it. P2 is a soft sound, so listen at the pulmonary area: the second left intercostal space, beside the sternum (see §8). Ask the person to breathe slowly. On a breath in, the single \"dub\" becomes a quick \"da-dub\". On a breath out, it merges back into one. Why S2 splits on inspiration Two timelines. In expiration, A2 (aortic valve closing) and P2 (pulmonary valve closing) are almost together. In inspiration, A2 comes slightly earlier and P2 clearly later, so the gap widens. Schematic, not to scale. Hear it. A simulator recording at the pulmonary area. The simulator breathes quickly, so S2 changes from one sound to two and back every few beats. Listen for the \"dub\" becoming a short \"da-dub\". (Recording: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, \"split S2, transient\", CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended.) Recording: split S2 that comes and goes with breathing, at the pulmonary area. Source note: The lecture says the aortic and pulmonary valves close \"at the same time\" in expiration. In practice a small gap remains but is usually too short to hear; the teaching point is the widening on inspiration. Mechanism 2 corresponds to the lecture's \"decrease in the impedance of the pulmonary vasculature\". The lecture also lists \"contracting lungs exert a force on the vessels\"; this is not a standard explanation of splitting, so we have not taught it. Related: the full timing picture → Cardiac Cycle, §10 · breathing and venous return → Veins and Venous Return, §5 Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Listening at the second left intercostal space, you hear S2 as two sounds when the person breathes in and as one sound when they breathe out. What does this most likely show? A. Delayed closure of the mitral valve because more blood fills the left heart B. Early closure of the pulmonary valve because less blood returns to the right heart C. Normal widening of the A2–P2 gap as the right ventricle ejects for longer D. A stiff right ventricle that delays the start of right ventricular ejection Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inspiration fills the right ventricle more and lowers the opposition to flow in the lungs. Both effects let the right ventricle keep ejecting for longer, so P2 comes later. The mitral valve has nothing to do with S2, and the gap closing again on expiration shows this is normal, not disease. 2. At the instant S2 is heard over the aortic area, which pressure change has just happened? A. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above left atrial pressure B. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below left atrial pressure C. Left ventricular pressure has just risen above aortic pressure D. Left ventricular pressure has just fallen below aortic pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. The aortic valve shuts when the artery overtakes the ventricle. A is the moment of S1 (the mitral valve shuts). C is the aortic valve opening, which makes no sound. B is the mitral valve opening at the end of isovolumetric relaxation, which is also silent. 3. During inspiration, why does blood keep flowing into the pulmonary artery after right ventricular pressure has peaked? A. The tricuspid valve stays open later during inspiration B. The expanded lung vessels offer less opposition to flow C. The pulmonary valve has fewer cusps than the aortic valve D. Right ventricular contraction starts later on inspiration Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Expanded lungs lower the impedance of the pulmonary vessels, so forward flow continues for longer and the pulmonary valve shuts later. The tricuspid valve must be shut while the right ventricle ejects. Both outflow valves have three cusps. Inspiration does not delay the electrical activation of the right ventricle. 4. What causes S3 and S4? S1 and S2 are valves shutting. S3 and S4 are different: no valve shuts. They are filling sounds, made in diastole as blood rushes into the ventricle and is then suddenly slowed. The lecture says the same: both are due to ventricular filling. What separates them is what drives the rush, and that puts them at opposite ends of diastole. Recall how a ventricle fills ( → Cardiac Cycle, §3): Rapid filling. The mitral and tricuspid valves open, and blood stored in the atria pours into the ventricles. Diastasis. Filling slows to a trickle. Atrial kick. The atria contract and push in the last portion of blood. S3: at the end of rapid filling, early in diastole. About 0.1–0.2 s after S2, the rush of early filling reaches the limit of how far the ventricle wall will easily stretch. The inflowing blood is suddenly slowed, and the ventricle wall and blood vibrate. The result is a low, dull sound just after S2: \"lub-dub-dum\". Many students remember the rhythm as Ken-TUC-ky. When it is normal: in children, young adults, athletes and pregnant women. Their ventricles relax quickly and fill very fast. When it suggests a problem: with age, early filling normally slows and S3 disappears. In an older adult, an S3 usually means the ventricle is being overfilled or is enlarged and failing. ( → Heart Failure) S4: during the atrial kick, late in diastole. Just before S1, the atria contract and push their blood into the ventricles. If the ventricle is stiff, for example thickened, it resists this final push. The blood is stopped abruptly and the wall vibrates. The result is a low sound just before S1: \"da-lub-dub\", or TEN-nes-see. It depends entirely on atrial contraction. It comes just after the P wave and goes with the a wave in the atria. So it is absent in atrial fibrillation, where the atria quiver instead of contracting together. When it suggests a problem: an S4 you can actually hear with a stethoscope in an adult usually means a stiff ventricle. S3 S4 When Early diastole, just after S2 Late diastole, just before S1 What drives the rush Passive rapid filling Atrial contraction (the atrial kick) Rhythm lub-dub- dum ( Ken-TUC-ky) da -lub-dub ( TEN-nes-see) Heard in healthy people? Often, in the young Rarely heard with a stethoscope In an older adult it usually means An overfilled or failing ventricle A stiff ventricle Needs atrial contraction? No Yes, so it is lost in atrial fibrillation How to listen. Both are very low-pitched. Use the bell of the stethoscope, pressed lightly, at the apex, with the person turned onto their left side. This brings the apex closer to the chest wall. Where S3 and S4 sit in filling Ventricular volume through one diastole: a steep rise (rapid filling), a flat stretch (diastasis), then a small final rise (atrial kick). S3 is marked where the steep rise suddenly levels off; S4 is marked on the atrial kick; S2 and the next S1 are marked at either end. Hear S3 and Hear S4 play the matching recording while the marker lights up. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. Headphones recommended. Source note: The lecture lists both S3 and S4 as normal sounds due to rapid ventricular filling. We agree that both are filling sounds. Standard physiology texts (e.g. Guyton and Hall) separate them by cause: S3 comes from rapid passive filling, S4 from atrial contraction. They also note that S3 is often normal in young people, while an audible S4 in an adult usually points to a stiff ventricle. We teach that distinction because it explains why S4 disappears in atrial fibrillation. Related: the filling phases → Cardiac Cycle, §3 · S3 and S4 on the Wiggers diagram → Cardiac Cycle, §9 5. Why do heart sounds change in loudness? Each sound is made by a valve snapping shut. So anything that makes the snap harder makes the sound louder. For S1 and S2, two different things control how hard the snap is. S1 depends on how wide open the mitral and tricuspid valves are when contraction starts. The lecture links S1's loudness to how the ventricle fills. Think of a door: one that is already nearly shut closes quietly, but one flung shut from wide open bangs. Slow heart rate → soft S1. Diastole is long, so the ventricles finish filling well before they contract. As the inflow slows, the cusps of the mitral and tricuspid valves drift up towards each other. The lecture calls these \"floating valves\". When the ventricles finally contract, the cusps only have a short way to go, so they close gently. Fast heart rate → loud S1. Diastole is short, so the ventricles contract while blood is still rushing in and the cusps are still wide apart. Rising ventricular pressure slams them shut from fully open. A stronger contraction also gives a louder S1, for example during exercise. Ventricular pressure rises faster, so the cusps are driven shut harder. Why a slow heart has a soft S1 Two panels of the mitral valve at the instant the ventricle starts to contract. Slow heart: the cusps are already close together and travel a short distance (soft S1). Fast heart: the cusps are still wide open and travel a long distance (loud S1). Play animates both closing at the same moment, with a quiet and a loud lub. Schematic. S2 depends on the pressure in the artery behind the valve. The aortic and pulmonary valves are pushed shut by the pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery. The higher that pressure, the harder and quicker the valve is driven shut. This is the lecture's \"brisk closure\". High blood pressure in the aorta (systemic hypertension) → loud A2. High pressure in the pulmonary artery (pulmonary hypertension) → loud P2. It is heard best at the pulmonary area, the second left intercostal space. What a stethoscope picks up also depends on the route to the chest wall. Sounds are quieter through a thick chest wall, fluid around the heart, or over-inflated lungs. A badly damaged, stiff valve may also barely move, so its closing sound becomes softer even though the disease is severe. So loudness alone never tells you how serious a problem is. It is one clue to put alongside timing and where the sound is heard. Related: heart rate and the length of diastole → Cardiac Cycle, §10 · contractility → Cardiac Output, §7 Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. A patient's S4 disappears when they develop atrial fibrillation. What is the best explanation? A. The ventricle becomes less stiff once the heart rate rises B. The mitral valve no longer opens fully during diastole C. Rapid filling ends before the atria have time to empty D. The atria no longer contract together to push the last blood in Show answer and explanation Answer: D. S4 is made when the atrial kick pushes blood into a stiff ventricle. In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver instead of contracting together, so there is no kick and no S4. The ventricle is just as stiff as before. 2. An S3 is heard in a healthy 19-year-old athlete. Which explanation fits best? A. Atrial contraction is pushing blood into a stiff left ventricle B. Fast early filling of a ventricle that relaxes quickly is suddenly slowed C. The aortic and pulmonary valves are closing at clearly different times D. The mitral valve is closing late because the heart rate is very slow Show answer and explanation Answer: B. S3 comes at the end of rapid filling. Young, quickly relaxing ventricles fill very fast, so the sudden slowing can be heard, and that is normal. A describes S4. C describes split S2. D mixes up S3 with S1. 3. In a person with a slow resting heart rate, S1 is soft. What is the best explanation? A. The ventricle contracts less forcefully because it is overfilled B. The aortic valve shuts gently because aortic pressure is low C. The mitral and tricuspid cusps have nearly floated shut already D. The atria contract weakly, so less blood crosses the valves Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A long diastole lets filling finish, and the cusps drift close together before contraction starts. They only have a short way to travel, so they close softly. A fuller ventricle actually contracts more strongly. The aortic valve makes S2, not S1. 4. A loud P2 at the second left intercostal space most likely points to: A. Raised pressure in the pulmonary artery B. Raised pressure in the right atrium C. Faster flow through the tricuspid valve D. Lower resistance in the lung vessels Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The higher the pressure behind the pulmonary valve, the harder it is driven shut, so P2 gets louder. D is what happens on inspiration: it delays P2 rather than making it louder. 6. What makes a murmur? Normal blood flow is silent. Blood normally moves in smooth layers, sliding past one another. This is laminar flow, the lecture's \"streamlined\" flow. Nothing tumbles or collides, so there is nothing to hear. A murmur is the sound of blood flow that has become turbulent: swirling and tumbling instead of sliding in layers. The swirls shake the heart and vessel walls for as long as the turbulence lasts. So a murmur is a longer whooshing sound, not a short thud like S1 or S2. What makes flow turbulent? Flow tips into turbulence when it is fast, when the blood is thin (low viscosity), or when it is forced through a sudden narrowing. The lecture sums this up with the Reynolds number: the higher it is, the more likely turbulence becomes. ( → Haemodynamics, §5) The heart can make flow turbulent in three ways. These follow the lecture's list. A narrowed valve ( stenosis). When the valve should be open, it opens only partly. Blood is squeezed through the small opening as a fast jet, which becomes turbulent beyond the valve. This murmur is made by forward flow. A leaking valve ( regurgitation, the lecture's \"incompetence\"). When the valve should be shut, it does not close fully. Blood is driven backwards through the gap as a fast jet. This is the lecture's \"backflow\". Fast flow through normal valves (a \"hyperdynamic\" circulation). The valves are healthy, but blood moves fast enough, or is thin enough, to become turbulent anyway. The lecture's example is anaemia, where thin blood and a raised cardiac output combine. Exercise, fever and pregnancy can do the same. These flow murmurs do not mean the valves are damaged. Three ways to make a murmur Four panels. Normal: a fully open valve with smooth, parallel arrows (laminar: silent). Stenosis: a narrowed opening with a fast jet that breaks into swirls beyond it (forward jet). Regurgitation: a valve that should be shut, with a thin jet leaking backwards into swirls (backward jet). High flow: a normal open valve with many long, fast arrows becoming swirls (normal valve, fast flow). In every murmur there is turbulence somewhere; the questions are where it is and which way the blood is going. The key question for any murmur: which way is the abnormal blood flowing, and is that happening in systole or diastole? That decides when you hear it, which is the subject of §7. Murmur or bruit? Both come from the same mechanism. A murmur is heard over the heart; a bruit is heard over a blood vessel ( §9). Related: laminar flow, turbulence and the Reynolds number → Haemodynamics, §5 · anaemia and viscosity → Haemodynamics, §7 7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list The lecture's tip is to group the valves that close together. The aortic and pulmonary valves behave as one pair, and the mitral and tricuspid valves as the other. Then you only need to know what each pair should be doing in each phase: During Mitral and tricuspid (inlet valves) Aortic and pulmonary (outlet valves) Ventricular systole (S1 → S2) Should be shut Should be open, for ejection Ventricular diastole (S2 → S1) Should be open, for filling Should be shut For any faulty valve, ask one question: when is the faulty valve supposed to be open (for stenosis) or shut (for a leak)? That phase is when the abnormal flow happens, so that is when you hear the murmur. Systolic murmurs (between S1 and S2) Aortic or pulmonary stenosis. These valves should be open while the ventricles eject. A narrowed opening squeezes forward flow into a fast jet, so the murmur is systolic. Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation. These valves should be shut while the ventricles contract. If they leak, the contracting ventricle drives blood back into the atrium. The leak starts as soon as contraction starts and lasts until relaxation, so the murmur can fill all of systole. Diastolic murmurs (between S2 and the next S1) Mitral or tricuspid stenosis. These valves should be open while the ventricles fill. A narrowed opening obstructs filling, so the murmur is diastolic. Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation. These valves should be shut while the ventricles relax. The high pressure in the artery drives blood back into the ventricle, starting right after S2, so the murmur is diastolic. At a glance Valve pair Stenosis (forward jet) Regurgitation (backward jet) Aortic / pulmonary Systolic Diastolic Mitral / tricuspid Diastolic Systolic The pattern is a cross: each lesion falls in the opposite phase to its partner. You never need to memorise it. If you forget, ask the question again. Murmur predictor Pick a valve (aortic, pulmonary, mitral, tricuspid) and a fault (narrowed or leaking). The tool then shows, one step at a time, when that valve should be open or shut, which way the abnormal jet flows, the phase on an S1–S2–S1 timeline, and the answer: systolic or diastolic. Hear an example plays a recording typical of that lesion where one exists. Recordings: University of Michigan Heart Sound and Murmur Library, CC BY-SA 4.0. OPTIONAL VIDEO · ABOUT 4 MINUTES Watch it all together Alila Medical Media, Heart Sounds and Heart Murmurs. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. Understanding Check: Sections 6–7 1. A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. When is the murmur heard? A. During systole, between S1 and S2, as the ventricle ejects B. During diastole, starting just after S2, as the ventricle relaxes C. Late in diastole only, when the atria push blood into the ventricle D. Throughout the cycle, because aortic pressure is always higher Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The aortic valve should be shut while the ventricle relaxes. Aortic pressure is far above ventricular pressure then, so blood leaks back from the moment the valve closes (S2). In systole the valve is open anyway, so there is no backflow to hear. 2. Why does mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur, while mitral regurgitation causes a systolic one? A. Stenosis raises aortic pressure in diastole; a leak lowers left atrial pressure in systole B. Stenosis is heard at the apex only in diastole; a leak is heard at the base only in systole C. Stenosis blocks filling when the valve should be open; a leak lets blood back when it should be shut D. Stenosis delays mitral closure into diastole; a leak moves aortic closure earlier into systole Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The mitral valve should be open during filling (diastole), so a narrowed one causes a diastolic murmur. It should be shut during contraction (systole), so a leaking one causes a systolic murmur. 3. A patient with severe anaemia has a soft systolic murmur, but all four valves are normal on a scan. What is the most likely cause? A. The mitral valve leaks because the left ventricle is overfilled B. The aortic valve narrows because red cells clump on its cusps C. Low blood viscosity delays closure of the pulmonary valve D. Thin blood and a high cardiac output make ejection flow turbulent Show answer and explanation Answer: D. This is a flow murmur: normal valves, but blood that is thin and fast enough to become turbulent during ejection. It does not mean the valves are damaged. 8. Where do we listen to each heart valve? The lecture says each valve is best heard over its surface marking: the spot on the chest wall where that valve's sound comes through most clearly. These four spots are the auscultation areas: Area Where on the chest Aortic Second right intercostal space, beside the sternum Pulmonary Second left intercostal space, beside the sternum Tricuspid Lower left border of the sternum (fourth or fifth intercostal space) Mitral The apex: fifth left intercostal space, in line with the middle of the clavicle Figure 01. Where to place the stethoscope for each valve. Image credit Rib cage drawing from Gray's Anatomy of the Human Body (1918), public domain; listening-point labels added. Supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project. Why aren't these spots directly over the valves? The four valves sit close together behind the sternum, packed into a small area. Each valve's sound travels in the direction its blood flows, and comes out where that chamber or vessel lies closest to the chest wall: The aortic valve sits behind the left side of the sternum. Its sound is carried up the aorta, which comes nearest the surface at the right second intercostal space. The mitral valve also sits behind the sternum. Its sound is carried down through the left ventricle to the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall. A practical tip for telling S1 from S2: S1 is loudest at the apex, where the mitral sound comes out. S2 is loudest at the base, the aortic and pulmonary areas at the top of the heart. Moving the stethoscope between the two and hearing which sound gets louder tells you which is which. Two recordings of the same normal heart sounds: at the apex, where S1 is louder, and at the base, where S2 is louder. Bell or diaphragm? The diaphragm, pressed firmly, picks up higher-pitched sounds: S1, S2 and most murmurs. The bell, resting lightly, picks up low-pitched sounds such as S3, S4 and the rumble of mitral stenosis. Source note: The lecture slide gives \"AT PM\" beside the listening areas. We could not confirm what this abbreviation means, so we present the four standard areas without it. Valve surface positions follow standard anatomy texts. 9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere? Turbulence is not limited to the heart valves. Wherever blood is forced through a narrowing, takes a short cut, or flows unusually fast, it can become turbulent and make a sound ( §6). Heard over a vessel, this sound is called a bruit. The lecture lists these examples: Condition Why the flow becomes turbulent When it is heard Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA): the fetal channel between the aorta and pulmonary artery stays open after birth Aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure in both systole and diastole, so blood crosses all the time Continuous, through S2 Ventricular septal defect (VSD): a hole between the ventricles When the ventricles contract, left ventricular pressure far exceeds right, so blood jets through the hole Systolic Arteriovenous (AV) fistula: a direct channel from an artery to a vein High-pressure arterial blood rushes into a low-pressure vein, skipping the capillaries Continuous bruit over the site Aneurysm: a ballooned section of artery The sudden widening disturbs smooth flow (not every aneurysm makes a sound) A bruit over the aneurysm Goitre (enlarged thyroid) with high blood flow Increased blood flow through the gland A bruit over the thyroid Anaemia Thin, fast blood (see §6) A soft systolic flow murmur The rule is the same everywhere: find the pressure difference that drives the abnormal flow, and when it exists. That tells you when the sound is heard. The PDA murmur is continuous for exactly this reason: the pressure difference between the aorta and pulmonary artery never reverses. Related: the ductus arteriosus and why it should close after birth → Fetal Circulation, \"What closes after delivery?\" · bruits over narrowed arteries → Haemodynamics, §5 · Korotkoff sounds in blood-pressure measurement → Blood Pressure, §7 10. Understanding Check Questions on the whole chapter. The earlier checks cover Sections 1–7 in detail. 1. A patient has a patent ductus arteriosus, an open channel joining the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Why is its murmur continuous through systole and diastole? A. The ductus closes in systole and reopens in diastole each beat B. Aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure through the whole cycle C. Pulmonary pressure stays above aortic pressure through the whole cycle D. Both the aortic and pulmonary valves leak during every diastole Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Flow crosses the ductus whenever aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary pressure, and in this patient that is true throughout the cycle. Because the driving difference never reverses, the turbulence and the sound never stop. 2. Why is the mitral valve best heard at the apex rather than directly over the valve? A. The mitral valve actually lies beneath the fifth left intercostal space B. The sternum blocks all sound, so no valve can be heard over it C. Its sound is carried to where the left ventricle meets the chest wall D. The apex is the only area where the bell can be applied lightly Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The mitral valve sits behind the sternum, but its sound travels with the blood into the left ventricle and comes out at the apex, where the ventricle touches the chest wall. 3. A 70-year-old has a stiff, thickened left ventricle. Which extra sound would you most expect, and why? A. An S4 just before S1, from the atrial kick B. An S3 just after S2, from rapid early filling C. A loud S1 at the apex, from floating cusps D. A split S2 on expiration, from late P2 Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A stiff ventricle resists the final push of blood from the atria, which makes S4. An S3 points to an overfilled or failing ventricle. Floating cusps make S1 soft, not loud. A split that persists on expiration is not explained by stiffness. 4. Listening at the apex, you hear \"lub-dub\" with a long pause after \"dub\". Then you move to the second right intercostal space, and the second sound becomes the louder one. What have you confirmed? A. The second sound is S1, because S1 is loudest over the aortic area B. The first sound is S1, because S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base C. The patient has aortic regurgitation, because S2 is loud at the base D. The second sound is split, because it changes when you move position Show answer and explanation Answer: B. S1 is loudest at the apex and S2 at the base. Together with the long pause after S2 (diastole), this confirms which sound is which. A louder S2 at the base is normal, not a sign of disease. 11. Chapter Essentials S1 (\"lub\"): the mitral and tricuspid valves shutting as the ventricles start to contract. It marks the start of ventricular systole. S2 (\"dub\"): the aortic and pulmonary valves shutting as ejection ends. It marks the start of ventricular diastole. The sound is not the leaflets clapping: it is the vibration caused when moving blood is suddenly stopped. Normal valves open silently. S1 → S2 is systole; S2 → S1 is diastole. At rest the pause after S2 is longer. S1 is loudest at the apex, S2 at the base. Physiological splitting of S2: on inspiration, more right-heart filling and lower pulmonary impedance delay P2, so A2 and P2 separate. They merge again on expiration. S3: early diastole, at the end of rapid filling. Often normal in the young; in older adults it suggests an overfilled or failing ventricle. S4: late diastole, from the atrial kick into a stiff ventricle. It needs atrial contraction, so it is absent in atrial fibrillation. Loudness: a slow heart gives a soft S1 (the cusps have floated nearly shut). High aortic or pulmonary pressure gives a loud A2 or P2. Loudness alone does not measure severity. Murmurs are turbulent flow: from a narrowed valve (forward jet), a leaking valve (backward jet), or fast, thin blood through normal valves (flow murmur, e.g. anaemia). Timing rule: ask when the faulty valve should be open (stenosis) or shut (leak). Aortic/pulmonary stenosis and mitral/tricuspid regurgitation are systolic. Mitral/tricuspid stenosis and aortic/pulmonary regurgitation are diastolic. Auscultation areas: aortic at the second right intercostal space; pulmonary at the second left; tricuspid at the lower left sternal border; mitral at the apex. These are where each valve's sound comes out, not where the valves sit. Bruits follow the same rule as murmurs: find the pressure difference driving the abnormal flow, and when it exists. A PDA is continuous because aortic pressure stays above pulmonary pressure all the time."},{"slug":"ecg","title":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms","group":"Heart","source":"Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf","pages":"1-14; extra diagrams Circulation 3 35-38","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record","title":"1. What does an ECG actually record?"},{"id":"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards","title":"2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?"},{"id":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","title":"3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?"},{"id":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","title":"4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?"},{"id":"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events","title":"5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events"},{"id":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","title":"6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?"},{"id":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","title":"7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing"},{"id":"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary","title":"8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)"},{"id":"9-chapter-essentials","title":"9. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record\">1. What does an ECG actually record?</h2>\n<p>Each heartbeat starts with an electrical signal that spreads through the heart muscle. As the muscle cells depolarise (switch on) and then repolarise (reset), they create small voltage differences. These spread through the body's tissues, all the way to the skin. Electrodes on the skin pick them up, and the machine draws them over time. That drawing is the <strong><span data-term=\"ecg\">electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG)</span></strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-concept-line\" aria-label=\"Electrical and mechanical sequence\"><span>Electrical activation</span><b>→</b><span>Contraction</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Blood movement</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>An ECG records electricity, not pumping.</strong> It does not measure how strongly the heart contracts, the pressure it makes or how much blood it ejects. A QRS complex means the ventricles have been <strong>switched on</strong> electrically, not that they have pumped blood. Usually contraction follows. But the electrical signal can carry on even when the heart muscle pumps little or no blood. So a normal-looking ECG does not prove the heart is pumping well.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"HQqPYH7pdPM\" data-media-title=\"(ECG or EKG) Rhythm Strips part 1 — BioVFX\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 1 OF 3</span><h3>(ECG or EKG) Rhythm Strips part 1</h3><p>BioVFX. An introduction to ECG recordings, ECG paper and rhythm strips, and how to measure time using the boxes on the paper. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HQqPYH7pdPM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards\">2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?</h2>\n<p>First, two words that are easy to mix up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>electrode</strong> is the sticker on the skin.</li>\n<li>A <strong>lead</strong> is one view of the heart's electrical activity, worked out from the voltage between electrodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Think of each lead as a camera</strong> pointed at the heart from one direction. Every lead watches the same heartbeat, but from a different angle, so each draws a slightly different shape.</p>\n<p>At any moment, the electrical wave spreading through the heart has an overall <strong>direction</strong> and <strong>size</strong>. We draw it as an arrow, the electrical <strong><span data-term=\"ecg-vector\">vector</span></strong>. What a lead records depends on which way that arrow points compared with the lead:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>The wave of depolarisation travels…</th>\n  <th>The lead draws…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Towards</strong> the lead's positive end</td>\n  <td>An upward deflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Away from</strong> it</td>\n  <td>A downward deflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Across</strong> it, at right angles</td>\n  <td>A small or up-and-down (biphasic) deflection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> in a normal heart, the ventricles depolarise mostly downwards and to the patient's left. Lead II looks at the heart from that direction, so its QRS is usually upright. aVR looks from the right shoulder, so the same wave travels away from it, and its QRS points down.</p>\n<p><strong>The up and down are electrical, not movement.</strong> An upward wave does not mean the heart moved up. It means the electrical wave was heading towards that lead.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"lead-view\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Lead view</figcaption><p>A heart outline with one lead's positive end marked. Drag or tap to turn the depolarisation arrow. A small trace beside it updates live: upward when the arrow points towards the lead, downward when it points away, and a small up-and-down deflection when it points across. Buttons switch between Lead I, Lead II and aVR. Default: Lead II with the normal ventricular direction (upright QRS).</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why is the normal T wave upright, if it is &quot;the opposite&quot; of depolarisation?</strong> The rule in the table is for depolarisation. Repolarisation (the cells resetting) gives the opposite sign: a resetting wave travelling <em>towards</em> a lead draws a <em>downward</em> deflection. But in the ventricles, the last cells to switch on (the outer layer) are the first to reset. So repolarisation travels in the <strong>reverse direction</strong>, from outside to inside. Opposite sign plus opposite direction cancel out, so the T wave usually points the same way as the QRS.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture does not explain T-wave direction. The outer-layer-first order of ventricular repolarisation is standard textbook physiology (Guyton and Hall).</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What is the difference between an ECG electrode and an ECG lead?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. An electrode is a skin contact; a lead is a view worked out from electrodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. An electrode records voltage; a lead records the strength of contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. An electrode is placed on the chest; a lead is always placed on a limb</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. An electrode is one view of the heart; a lead is the wire to the machine</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Electrodes are the physical contacts. Leads are views calculated from the voltages between them, so ten electrodes can give twelve leads.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> In one lead, the wave of ventricular depolarisation travels away from the positive end. What does that lead draw?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. An upward QRS, because the ventricles are contracting</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A downward QRS, because the wave is moving away</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A flat line, because no wave is heading towards it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A taller T wave, because repolarisation comes first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Depolarisation moving towards a lead draws an upward deflection, and moving away draws a downward one. The deflection shows direction, not contraction.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient's ECG shows regular, normal-looking QRS complexes, but no pulse can be felt. How is this possible?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The QRS complexes must be interference from the electrodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The ECG measures blood flow, so a pulse must be present</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ECG records electrical activity, which can occur without effective pumping</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The pulse is always delayed by several seconds after each QRS</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The ECG shows the electrical signal only. The muscle can fail to pump effectively even while that signal continues, so you must also check the pulse and blood pressure.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads\">3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</h2>\n<p>A standard <strong>12-lead ECG</strong> uses <strong>10 electrodes</strong>: one on each limb and six on the chest. The machine combines their voltages to give twelve views:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lead group</th>\n  <th style=\"text-align:right\">How many</th>\n  <th>Names</th>\n  <th>Viewing plane</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Standard limb leads (bipolar)</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">3</td>\n  <td>I, II, III</td>\n  <td>Frontal (front-on)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Augmented limb leads</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">3</td>\n  <td>aVR, aVL, aVF</td>\n  <td>Frontal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Chest leads</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">6</td>\n  <td>V1–V6</td>\n  <td>Horizontal (a slice across the chest)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>right-leg electrode</strong> is not used to make a view. It works as an earth that reduces electrical interference. That is why there are ten electrodes but only nine are used to make the twelve views.</p>\n<h3>The standard limb leads: I, II and III</h3>\n<p>Each one measures the voltage <strong>between two limb electrodes</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lead</th>\n  <th>Negative end</th>\n  <th>Positive end</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>I</strong></td>\n  <td>Right arm</td>\n  <td>Left arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>II</strong></td>\n  <td>Right arm</td>\n  <td>Left leg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>III</strong></td>\n  <td>Left arm</td>\n  <td>Left leg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Together they form a triangle around the heart, <strong><span data-term=\"einthoven\">Einthoven's triangle</span></strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-einthoven\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" alt=\"Diagram of Einthoven triangle connecting arm and leg lead positions with bipolar Leads I, II and III.\" width=\"992\" height=\"744\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Kychot · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>Because the three leads share the same three electrodes, their voltages at any instant are linked by <strong>Einthoven's law</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Einthoven's law</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"V_II = V_I + V_III\">V_II = V_I + V_III</span></div>\n\n<p>So if Lead I reads +0.5 mV and Lead III reads +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II must read <strong>+0.8 mV</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The augmented limb leads: aVR, aVL and aVF</h3>\n<p>Each uses <strong>one limb electrode as its positive end</strong>, compared with an average of the other two limb electrodes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>aVR</strong> looks from the right arm.</li>\n<li><strong>aVL</strong> looks from the left arm.</li>\n<li><strong>aVF</strong> looks from the left foot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture calls these leads <strong><span data-term=\"unipolar-lead\">unipolar</span></strong>, because only one electrode forms the positive end. Normal ventricular depolarisation travels away from the right shoulder, so the QRS in <strong>aVR is usually negative</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The chest leads: V1–V6</h3>\n<p>Each chest electrode is a positive end, compared with an average of the limb electrodes. Together they wrap around the heart from right to left.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Electrode</th>\n  <th>Position</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V1</strong></td>\n  <td>4th intercostal space, right edge of the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V2</strong></td>\n  <td>4th intercostal space, left edge of the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V3</strong></td>\n  <td>Midway between V2 and V4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V4</strong></td>\n  <td>5th intercostal space, midclavicular line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V5</strong></td>\n  <td>Anterior axillary line, at the level of V4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V6</strong></td>\n  <td>Midaxillary line, at the level of V4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Place <strong>V4 before V3</strong>, because V3 sits midway between V2 and V4. A misplaced electrode changes the shapes and can make a normal ECG look abnormal.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-precordial\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" alt=\"Anterior torso and rib cage with six labelled ECG chest electrodes V1 to V6 in their correct anatomical positions.\" width=\"287\" height=\"252\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture names the three lead groups (bipolar, augmented unipolar, chest unipolar). Electrode positions and the right-leg electrode's role are standard clinical teaching.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?</h2>\n<p>The ECG trace is made of three kinds of parts. The words are not interchangeable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wave:</strong> a bump up or down from the baseline (P, QRS, T).</li>\n<li><strong>Segment:</strong> the flat stretch <strong>between</strong> two waves (PR segment, ST segment).</li>\n<li><strong>Interval:</strong> a span that <strong>includes at least one wave</strong> (<span data-term=\"pr-interval\">PR interval</span>, <span data-term=\"qt-interval\">QT interval</span>).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-signal\" id=\"ecg-normal-wave\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" alt=\"Labeled normal ECG with P wave, QRS complex, T wave, PR segment and interval, ST segment and QT interval.\" width=\"512\" height=\"512\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"OKJvR83fIrc\" data-media-title=\"(ECG or EKG) Wave patterns. Part 2 — BioVFX\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 2 OF 3</span><h3>(ECG or EKG) Wave patterns. Part 2</h3><p>BioVFX. Upward and downward deflections, and how to identify the P, Q, R, S and T waves. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OKJvR83fIrc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>P wave: the atria are switched on.</strong> The signal starts in the SA node and spreads across both atria. The P wave is that spread through the atrial muscle; the SA node itself is too small to show. The atria contract just after it. Normal duration: <strong>0.08–0.10 s</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>PR interval: from the atria switching on to the ventricles switching on.</strong> It runs from the <strong>start of the P wave</strong> to the <strong>start of the QRS</strong>. Normal: <strong>0.12–0.20 s</strong>. It includes the spread across the atria, the hold-up in the AV node and the run down the His–Purkinje fibres. So the PR interval is <strong>not just the AV delay</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The <strong>PR segment</strong> is shorter. It runs from the <strong>end</strong> of the P wave to the start of the QRS, when the signal is passing through the AV node and His–Purkinje fibres.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"MOD3NthLAB0\" data-media-title=\"(ECG or EKG) PR interval part 3 — BioVFX\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 3 OF 3</span><h3>(ECG or EKG) PR interval part 3</h3><p>BioVFX. The PR interval, measured from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex, normally about 120–200 ms. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MOD3NthLAB0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>QRS complex: the ventricles are switched on.</strong> The ventricles have far more muscle than the atria, so the QRS is much bigger than the P wave. Normal duration: <strong>0.06–0.10 s</strong> in the lecture; clinically, anything under 0.12 s is accepted as normal.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q:</strong> a downward deflection before the first upward one</li>\n<li><strong>R:</strong> the upward deflection</li>\n<li><strong>S:</strong> a downward deflection after the R</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not every lead shows all three.</p>\n<p><strong>Where is the atria resetting?</strong> It happens at the same time as the QRS, and its small signal is hidden inside the much bigger one.</p>\n<p><strong>ST segment: the whole ventricle is switched on.</strong> It runs from the end of the QRS (the <strong><span data-term=\"j-point\">J point</span></strong>) to the start of the T wave. All the ventricular cells are in the plateau of their action potential at the same voltage, so there is no voltage difference for the electrodes to pick up, and the line is flat. <strong>Flat does not mean resting:</strong> this is when the ventricles are contracting hardest. The lecture stresses that the ST segment can rise or fall when the heart muscle is short of oxygen (ischaemia).</p>\n<p><strong>T wave: the ventricles reset.</strong> Resetting spreads more slowly than switching on, so the T wave is longer and lower than the QRS.</p>\n<p><strong>U wave: sometimes seen.</strong> A small wave after the T. The lecture describes it as the last remnants of ventricular repolarisation; its exact source is still debated.</p>\n<p><strong>QT interval: the ventricles switch on and reset.</strong> It runs from the <strong>start of the QRS</strong> to the <strong>end of the T wave</strong>, roughly one ventricular action potential. It is <strong>0.2–0.4 s</strong>, and it <strong>shortens as heart rate rises</strong>, so there is no single normal QT value.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Part</th>\n  <th>What happens</th>\n  <th>Normal (lecture)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>P wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Atria switch on</td>\n  <td>0.08–0.10 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>PR interval</strong></td>\n  <td>Start of P to start of QRS</td>\n  <td>0.12–0.20 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>QRS complex</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles switch on</td>\n  <td>0.06–0.10 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>ST segment</strong></td>\n  <td>Whole ventricle switched on (plateau)</td>\n  <td>Flat at the baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>T wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles reset</td>\n  <td>Longer than QRS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>QT interval</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles switch on and reset</td>\n  <td>0.2–0.4 s, depending on heart rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Values are the lecture's. \"Under 0.12 s\" for the QRS is the usual clinical cut-off. Doctors correct the QT for heart rate (the QTc, often with Bazett's formula, QT ÷ √RR); that is beyond this lecture.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events\">5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events</h2>\n<p>Each electrical event comes <strong>just before</strong> the mechanical event it triggers:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>ECG</th>\n  <th>Electrical event</th>\n  <th>What follows mechanically</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>P wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Atria switch on</td>\n  <td>Atria contract and top up the ventricles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>PR interval</strong></td>\n  <td>The signal is held in the AV node</td>\n  <td>The atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>QRS</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles switch on</td>\n  <td>Ventricles contract; S1 is heard soon after</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>ST segment</strong></td>\n  <td>Whole ventricle switched on</td>\n  <td>Ventricles eject blood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>T wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles reset</td>\n  <td>Ventricles relax; S2 comes near the end of the T wave</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span> puts these electrical events above the pressure and volume curves of the same beat. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §9</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 3–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> At the same instant, Lead I reads +0.6 mV and Lead III reads +0.4 mV. What does Lead II read?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. +0.2 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I minus Lead III</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. +1.0 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I plus Lead III</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. +0.5 mV, because Lead II is the average of the other two</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. +2.4 mV, because Lead II is the product of the other two</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Einthoven's law: Lead II = Lead I + Lead III, compared at the same instant.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A student measures from the end of the P wave to the start of the QRS and calls it the PR interval. What has the student left out?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The QRS complex, which the interval also includes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The AV delay, which happens after the QRS starts</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The P wave, which the interval includes from its start</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The T wave, which ends the interval</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The PR interval starts at the <strong>beginning</strong> of the P wave. What the student measured is the PR segment.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why is the ST segment flat, even though the ventricles are contracting hard?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles are electrically silent and fully at rest</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The atria are resetting and cancel out the ventricles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. All ventricular cells are at the same voltage, so no difference is picked up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The electrodes stop recording while blood is ejected</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> During the plateau, every ventricular cell sits at about the same voltage. With no difference between regions, the lead draws a flat line.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why is the QRS complex much bigger than the P wave?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles have far more muscle to switch on</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The QRS includes the atria resetting as well</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ventricles are closer to the electrodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The AV node amplifies the signal</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> More muscle switching on gives a bigger total signal. The atria resetting is hidden inside the QRS, but it is tiny and not the reason.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage\">6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?</h2>\n<p>ECG paper is a grid of <strong>small squares</strong> (1 mm) grouped into <strong>large squares</strong> (5 small squares, outlined in bold). In the lecture's standard settings, the paper moves at <strong>25 mm/s</strong>, and <strong>10 mm of height = 1 mV</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-calibration-mini\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"ECG paper calibration\"><div class=\"ecg-paper-sample\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><div><strong>At 25 mm/s</strong><p>1 small 1-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.04 s</strong><br>1 large 5-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.20 s</strong></p><p><strong>At 10 mm/mV:</strong> 1 small vertical square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong>; 1 large vertical square = <strong>0.5 mV</strong>.</p></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Across the paper is time.</strong> The paper moves 25 small squares every second. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 small square = 1 ÷ 25 = <strong>0.04 s</strong></li>\n<li>1 large square = 5 × 0.04 = <strong>0.20 s</strong></li>\n<li>5 large squares = <strong>1 second</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Up the paper is voltage.</strong> 10 small squares = 1 mV. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 small square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong></li>\n<li>1 large square = <strong>0.5 mV</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Don't mix the two.</strong> A taller QRS is a bigger voltage, not a longer one. To measure how long something lasts, count squares <strong>across</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Try it:</strong> a PR interval spans 4 small squares. How long is it? 4 × 0.04 = <strong>0.16 s</strong>, which is normal (0.12–0.20 s). In large squares, a normal PR interval is <strong>3 to 5 small squares</strong> (0.12–0.20 s), and a normal QRS is <strong>under 3 small squares</strong> (under 0.12 s).</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing\">7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing</h2>\n<p><strong>The idea in one sentence:</strong> work out how long one beat takes, then see how many of those beats fit into a minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: count the small squares between two R waves.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: change squares to seconds.</strong> Multiply by 0.04. This is the <span data-term=\"rr-interval\">RR interval</span>, the time for one beat.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: change to beats per minute.</strong> Divide 60 by the RR interval.</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture's example: 22 small squares.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Step 1: 22 small squares</li>\n<li>Step 2: 22 × 0.04 = <strong>0.88 s</strong> per beat</li>\n<li>Step 3: 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ <strong>68 beats/min</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The quicker way, and where 1500 comes from.</strong> In one minute the paper moves 25 mm/s × 60 s = <strong>1500 mm</strong>, which is <strong>1500 small squares</strong>. If one beat takes 22 squares, the number of beats in a minute is:</p>\n<p><strong>1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68 beats/min.</strong> Same answer, one step.</p>\n<p>Counting large squares works the same way: 1500 small squares = <strong>300 large squares</strong>, so 300 ÷ 4.4 large squares ≈ 68.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">General relationship</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"HR = (60) / (RRin seconds)\">HR = (60) / (RRin seconds)</span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">25 mm/s · regular rhythm</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"HR = (1500) / (N_small squares)\">HR = (1500) / (N_small squares)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>No calculator? Use the countdown.</strong> Find an R wave that sits on a thick line. For each thick line after it, say the next number in the countdown. Stop where the next R wave lands:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Next R wave lands after…</th>\n  <th>1 large square</th>\n  <th>2</th>\n  <th>3</th>\n  <th>4</th>\n  <th>5</th>\n  <th>6</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Heart rate (beats/min)</strong></td>\n  <td>300</td>\n  <td>150</td>\n  <td>100</td>\n  <td>75</td>\n  <td>60</td>\n  <td>50</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Each number is just 300 ÷ the number of large squares. If the R wave falls between two lines, the rate lies between those two numbers. For the lecture's strip (4.4 large squares), it lands between 75 and 60, nearer 75: about 68.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"rate-ruler\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Rate ruler</figcaption><p>A strip of ECG paper with two R waves. A slider sets the gap between them, from 10 to 40 small squares. Underneath, the three steps update live: squares → seconds (× 0.04) → beats/min (60 ÷ RR), with the 1500 shortcut and the countdown range beside them. It starts at the lecture's 22 squares, 68 beats/min.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>A second example:</strong> 15 small squares. 15 × 0.04 = 0.6 s; 60 ÷ 0.6 = <strong>100 beats/min</strong>. Check: 1500 ÷ 15 = 100, or 3 large squares on the countdown = 100. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>If the rhythm is irregular,</strong> the gaps between R waves vary, so one gap gives the rate for one beat only. Instead, count the QRS complexes in a <strong>6-second strip</strong> (30 large squares) and multiply by <strong>10</strong>, because 6 s is one tenth of a minute. Eight complexes in 6 s ≈ <strong>80 beats/min</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Three common mistakes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dividing 1500 by large squares.</strong> Use 1500 for small squares and 300 for large squares.</li>\n<li><strong>Using the 1500 rule on an irregular rhythm.</strong> Count complexes in 6 s × 10 instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting the paper speed.</strong> At 50 mm/s each small square is 0.02 s, so the rules become 3000 ÷ small squares and 600 ÷ large squares.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The 1500 and 300 rules and the 22-square example are from the lecture. The countdown, the 6-second method and the 50 mm/s adjustment are standard supplementary teaching.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary\">8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)</h2>\n<p>The lecture focuses on ECG basics, not rhythm diagnosis. But four questions turn the trace into a first look at the rhythm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Rate:</strong> is it between about 60 and 100 beats/min at rest?</li>\n<li><strong>Regularity:</strong> are the RR intervals equal?</li>\n<li><strong>P waves:</strong> is there one before every QRS, and do they all look the same?</li>\n<li><strong>Intervals:</strong> is the PR interval 0.12–0.20 s, and the QRS narrow?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Normal sinus rhythm</strong> answers yes to all four.</p>\n<p>The PR interval also shows whether the signal is getting through the AV node:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>What you see</th>\n  <th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>PR longer than 0.20 s, but every P is followed by a QRS</td>\n  <td><strong>First-degree</strong> <span data-term=\"heart-block\">heart block</span>: delayed, but always gets through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Some P waves are not followed by a QRS</td>\n  <td><strong>Second-degree</strong> heart block: some signals are dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>P waves and QRS complexes at their own separate rates</td>\n  <td><strong>Third-degree (complete)</strong> heart block: nothing gets through</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The mechanisms are explained in Cardiac Conduction. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §8</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–8</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> At 25 mm/s, two R waves are 20 small squares apart. What is the heart rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 50 beats/min, because 1000 ÷ 20 = 50</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 60 beats/min, because 1200 ÷ 20 = 60</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 75 beats/min, because 1500 ÷ 20 = 75</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 80 beats/min, because 1600 ÷ 20 = 80</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> At 25 mm/s, 1500 small squares pass in a minute, so 1500 ÷ 20 = 75. Check: 20 × 0.04 = 0.8 s, and 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> On a regular strip, each R wave lands 3 large squares after the one before. What is the heart rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. About 75 beats/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. About 100 beats/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. About 150 beats/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. About 300 beats/min</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The countdown runs 300, 150, 100, so 3 large squares is 100 beats/min (300 ÷ 3).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Two QRS complexes look identical, but one is twice as tall. What does the extra height tell you?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. That beat lasted twice as long</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. That beat had a bigger voltage</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. That beat came at twice the rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. That beat had a longer PR interval</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Height is voltage; width is time. A taller complex is a bigger signal, not a longer one.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> An irregular rhythm shows 9 QRS complexes in a 6-second strip. What is the approximate rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. About 54 beats/min, from 9 × 6</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. About 75 beats/min, from 1500 ÷ 20 squares</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. About 90 beats/min, from 9 × 10</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. About 150 beats/min, from 9 ÷ 6 × 100</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A 6-second strip is one tenth of a minute, so multiply by 10. For an irregular rhythm this is more reliable than measuring one RR interval.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Every P wave is followed by a QRS, but the PR interval is 0.28 s. What does this show?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Complete heart block: no signals get through</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Second-degree heart block: some signals are dropped</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. First-degree heart block: signals are delayed but all get through</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A normal rhythm, because every P has a QRS</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A PR interval over 0.20 s means the signal is slow through the AV node, but since every P is followed by a QRS, none are dropped.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\">9. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The ECG records electricity, not pumping.</strong> A normal trace does not prove the heart is ejecting blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Electrode = skin contact; lead = a view.</strong> Ten electrodes give twelve leads; the right-leg electrode is an earth.</li>\n<li><strong>Direction rule:</strong> depolarisation towards a lead draws an upward deflection; away draws a downward one; across draws a small up-and-down one.</li>\n<li><strong>The T wave is usually upright</strong>, because ventricles reset in the reverse order to how they switched on.</li>\n<li><strong>Leads:</strong> I, II and III (bipolar, Einthoven's triangle, II = I + III); aVR, aVL and aVF; V1–V6 across the chest.</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong> = atria switch on (0.08–0.10 s); <strong>QRS</strong> = ventricles switch on (0.06–0.10 s); <strong>T</strong> = ventricles reset.</li>\n<li><strong>PR interval</strong> = start of P to start of QRS (0.12–0.20 s), including the AV delay. The <strong>PR segment</strong> starts at the end of P.</li>\n<li><strong>ST segment</strong> = whole ventricle switched on (plateau): flat but active. It shifts in ischaemia.</li>\n<li><strong>QT interval</strong> = start of QRS to end of T (0.2–0.4 s), shorter at higher heart rates.</li>\n<li><strong>Paper:</strong> 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV. Small square = 0.04 s and 0.1 mV; large square = 0.20 s.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate (regular rhythm):</strong> 60 ÷ RR (s), 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares. Irregular: QRS complexes in 6 s × 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart block:</strong> first degree = long PR, all conducted; second degree = some P waves dropped; third degree = P and QRS independent.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms 1. What does an ECG actually record? Each heartbeat starts with an electrical signal that spreads through the heart muscle. As the muscle cells depolarise (switch on) and then repolarise (reset), they create small voltage differences. These spread through the body's tissues, all the way to the skin. Electrodes on the skin pick them up, and the machine draws them over time. That drawing is the electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG). Electrical activation → Contraction → Pressure changes → Blood movement An ECG records electricity, not pumping. It does not measure how strongly the heart contracts, the pressure it makes or how much blood it ejects. A QRS complex means the ventricles have been switched on electrically, not that they have pumped blood. Usually contraction follows. But the electrical signal can carry on even when the heart muscle pumps little or no blood. So a normal-looking ECG does not prove the heart is pumping well. OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 1 OF 3 (ECG or EKG) Rhythm Strips part 1 BioVFX. An introduction to ECG recordings, ECG paper and rhythm strips, and how to measure time using the boxes on the paper. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video. 2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards? First, two words that are easy to mix up: An electrode is the sticker on the skin. A lead is one view of the heart's electrical activity, worked out from the voltage between electrodes. Think of each lead as a camera pointed at the heart from one direction. Every lead watches the same heartbeat, but from a different angle, so each draws a slightly different shape. At any moment, the electrical wave spreading through the heart has an overall direction and size. We draw it as an arrow, the electrical vector. What a lead records depends on which way that arrow points compared with the lead: The wave of depolarisation travels… The lead draws… Towards the lead's positive end An upward deflection Away from it A downward deflection Across it, at right angles A small or up-and-down (biphasic) deflection Example: in a normal heart, the ventricles depolarise mostly downwards and to the patient's left. Lead II looks at the heart from that direction, so its QRS is usually upright. aVR looks from the right shoulder, so the same wave travels away from it, and its QRS points down. The up and down are electrical, not movement. An upward wave does not mean the heart moved up. It means the electrical wave was heading towards that lead. Lead view A heart outline with one lead's positive end marked. Drag or tap to turn the depolarisation arrow. A small trace beside it updates live: upward when the arrow points towards the lead, downward when it points away, and a small up-and-down deflection when it points across. Buttons switch between Lead I, Lead II and aVR. Default: Lead II with the normal ventricular direction (upright QRS). Why is the normal T wave upright, if it is \"the opposite\" of depolarisation? The rule in the table is for depolarisation. Repolarisation (the cells resetting) gives the opposite sign: a resetting wave travelling towards a lead draws a downward deflection. But in the ventricles, the last cells to switch on (the outer layer) are the first to reset. So repolarisation travels in the reverse direction, from outside to inside. Opposite sign plus opposite direction cancel out, so the T wave usually points the same way as the QRS. Source note: The lecture does not explain T-wave direction. The outer-layer-first order of ventricular repolarisation is standard textbook physiology (Guyton and Hall). Understanding Check: Sections 1–2 1. What is the difference between an ECG electrode and an ECG lead? A. An electrode is a skin contact; a lead is a view worked out from electrodes B. An electrode records voltage; a lead records the strength of contraction C. An electrode is placed on the chest; a lead is always placed on a limb D. An electrode is one view of the heart; a lead is the wire to the machine Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Electrodes are the physical contacts. Leads are views calculated from the voltages between them, so ten electrodes can give twelve leads. 2. In one lead, the wave of ventricular depolarisation travels away from the positive end. What does that lead draw? A. An upward QRS, because the ventricles are contracting B. A downward QRS, because the wave is moving away C. A flat line, because no wave is heading towards it D. A taller T wave, because repolarisation comes first Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Depolarisation moving towards a lead draws an upward deflection, and moving away draws a downward one. The deflection shows direction, not contraction. 3. A patient's ECG shows regular, normal-looking QRS complexes, but no pulse can be felt. How is this possible? A. The QRS complexes must be interference from the electrodes B. The ECG measures blood flow, so a pulse must be present C. The ECG records electrical activity, which can occur without effective pumping D. The pulse is always delayed by several seconds after each QRS Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The ECG shows the electrical signal only. The muscle can fail to pump effectively even while that signal continues, so you must also check the pulse and blood pressure. 3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? A standard 12-lead ECG uses 10 electrodes: one on each limb and six on the chest. The machine combines their voltages to give twelve views: Lead group How many Names Viewing plane Standard limb leads (bipolar) 3 I, II, III Frontal (front-on) Augmented limb leads 3 aVR, aVL, aVF Frontal Chest leads 6 V1–V6 Horizontal (a slice across the chest) The right-leg electrode is not used to make a view. It works as an earth that reduces electrical interference. That is why there are ten electrodes but only nine are used to make the twelve views. The standard limb leads: I, II and III Each one measures the voltage between two limb electrodes: Lead Negative end Positive end I Right arm Left arm II Right arm Left leg III Left arm Left leg Together they form a triangle around the heart, Einthoven's triangle. Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions. Image credit Kychot · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. Because the three leads share the same three electrodes, their voltages at any instant are linked by Einthoven's law: Einthoven's law V_II = V_I + V_III So if Lead I reads +0.5 mV and Lead III reads +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II must read +0.8 mV. The augmented limb leads: aVR, aVL and aVF Each uses one limb electrode as its positive end, compared with an average of the other two limb electrodes: aVR looks from the right arm. aVL looks from the left arm. aVF looks from the left foot. The lecture calls these leads unipolar, because only one electrode forms the positive end. Normal ventricular depolarisation travels away from the right shoulder, so the QRS in aVR is usually negative. The chest leads: V1–V6 Each chest electrode is a positive end, compared with an average of the limb electrodes. Together they wrap around the heart from right to left. Electrode Position V1 4th intercostal space, right edge of the sternum V2 4th intercostal space, left edge of the sternum V3 Midway between V2 and V4 V4 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line V5 Anterior axillary line, at the level of V4 V6 Midaxillary line, at the level of V4 Place V4 before V3, because V3 sits midway between V2 and V4. A misplaced electrode changes the shapes and can make a normal ECG look abnormal. Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions. Image credit Jmarchn · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. Source note: The lecture names the three lead groups (bipolar, augmented unipolar, chest unipolar). Electrode positions and the right-leg electrode's role are standard clinical teaching. 4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference? The ECG trace is made of three kinds of parts. The words are not interchangeable: Wave: a bump up or down from the baseline (P, QRS, T). Segment: the flat stretch between two waves (PR segment, ST segment). Interval: a span that includes at least one wave ( PR interval, QT interval). Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals. Image credit Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · Wikimedia Commons · public domain. OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 2 OF 3 (ECG or EKG) Wave patterns. Part 2 BioVFX. Upward and downward deflections, and how to identify the P, Q, R, S and T waves. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video. P wave: the atria are switched on. The signal starts in the SA node and spreads across both atria. The P wave is that spread through the atrial muscle; the SA node itself is too small to show. The atria contract just after it. Normal duration: 0.08–0.10 s. PR interval: from the atria switching on to the ventricles switching on. It runs from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS. Normal: 0.12–0.20 s. It includes the spread across the atria, the hold-up in the AV node and the run down the His–Purkinje fibres. So the PR interval is not just the AV delay. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §6) The PR segment is shorter. It runs from the end of the P wave to the start of the QRS, when the signal is passing through the AV node and His–Purkinje fibres. OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 3 OF 3 (ECG or EKG) PR interval part 3 BioVFX. The PR interval, measured from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex, normally about 120–200 ms. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video. QRS complex: the ventricles are switched on. The ventricles have far more muscle than the atria, so the QRS is much bigger than the P wave. Normal duration: 0.06–0.10 s in the lecture; clinically, anything under 0.12 s is accepted as normal. Q: a downward deflection before the first upward one R: the upward deflection S: a downward deflection after the R Not every lead shows all three. Where is the atria resetting? It happens at the same time as the QRS, and its small signal is hidden inside the much bigger one. ST segment: the whole ventricle is switched on. It runs from the end of the QRS (the J point) to the start of the T wave. All the ventricular cells are in the plateau of their action potential at the same voltage, so there is no voltage difference for the electrodes to pick up, and the line is flat. Flat does not mean resting: this is when the ventricles are contracting hardest. The lecture stresses that the ST segment can rise or fall when the heart muscle is short of oxygen (ischaemia). T wave: the ventricles reset. Resetting spreads more slowly than switching on, so the T wave is longer and lower than the QRS. U wave: sometimes seen. A small wave after the T. The lecture describes it as the last remnants of ventricular repolarisation; its exact source is still debated. QT interval: the ventricles switch on and reset. It runs from the start of the QRS to the end of the T wave, roughly one ventricular action potential. It is 0.2–0.4 s, and it shortens as heart rate rises, so there is no single normal QT value. Part What happens Normal (lecture) P wave Atria switch on 0.08–0.10 s PR interval Start of P to start of QRS 0.12–0.20 s QRS complex Ventricles switch on 0.06–0.10 s ST segment Whole ventricle switched on (plateau) Flat at the baseline T wave Ventricles reset Longer than QRS QT interval Ventricles switch on and reset 0.2–0.4 s, depending on heart rate Source note: Values are the lecture's. \"Under 0.12 s\" for the QRS is the usual clinical cut-off. Doctors correct the QT for heart rate (the QTc, often with Bazett's formula, QT ÷ √RR); that is beyond this lecture. 5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events Each electrical event comes just before the mechanical event it triggers: ECG Electrical event What follows mechanically P wave Atria switch on Atria contract and top up the ventricles PR interval The signal is held in the AV node The atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract QRS Ventricles switch on Ventricles contract; S1 is heard soon after ST segment Whole ventricle switched on Ventricles eject blood T wave Ventricles reset Ventricles relax; S2 comes near the end of the T wave The Wiggers diagram puts these electrical events above the pressure and volume curves of the same beat. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §9) Understanding Check: Sections 3–5 1. At the same instant, Lead I reads +0.6 mV and Lead III reads +0.4 mV. What does Lead II read? A. +0.2 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I minus Lead III B. +1.0 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I plus Lead III C. +0.5 mV, because Lead II is the average of the other two D. +2.4 mV, because Lead II is the product of the other two Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Einthoven's law: Lead II = Lead I + Lead III, compared at the same instant. 2. A student measures from the end of the P wave to the start of the QRS and calls it the PR interval. What has the student left out? A. The QRS complex, which the interval also includes B. The AV delay, which happens after the QRS starts C. The P wave, which the interval includes from its start D. The T wave, which ends the interval Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The PR interval starts at the beginning of the P wave. What the student measured is the PR segment. 3. Why is the ST segment flat, even though the ventricles are contracting hard? A. The ventricles are electrically silent and fully at rest B. The atria are resetting and cancel out the ventricles C. All ventricular cells are at the same voltage, so no difference is picked up D. The electrodes stop recording while blood is ejected Show answer and explanation Answer: C. During the plateau, every ventricular cell sits at about the same voltage. With no difference between regions, the lead draws a flat line. 4. Why is the QRS complex much bigger than the P wave? A. The ventricles have far more muscle to switch on B. The QRS includes the atria resetting as well C. The ventricles are closer to the electrodes D. The AV node amplifies the signal Show answer and explanation Answer: A. More muscle switching on gives a bigger total signal. The atria resetting is hidden inside the QRS, but it is tiny and not the reason. 6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage? ECG paper is a grid of small squares (1 mm) grouped into large squares (5 small squares, outlined in bold). In the lecture's standard settings, the paper moves at 25 mm/s, and 10 mm of height = 1 mV. At 25 mm/s 1 small 1-mm horizontal square = 0.04 s 1 large 5-mm horizontal square = 0.20 s At 10 mm/mV: 1 small vertical square = 0.1 mV; 1 large vertical square = 0.5 mV. Across the paper is time. The paper moves 25 small squares every second. So: 1 small square = 1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s 1 large square = 5 × 0.04 = 0.20 s 5 large squares = 1 second Up the paper is voltage. 10 small squares = 1 mV. So: 1 small square = 0.1 mV 1 large square = 0.5 mV Don't mix the two. A taller QRS is a bigger voltage, not a longer one. To measure how long something lasts, count squares across. Try it: a PR interval spans 4 small squares. How long is it? 4 × 0.04 = 0.16 s, which is normal (0.12–0.20 s). In large squares, a normal PR interval is 3 to 5 small squares (0.12–0.20 s), and a normal QRS is under 3 small squares (under 0.12 s). 7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing The idea in one sentence: work out how long one beat takes, then see how many of those beats fit into a minute. Step 1: count the small squares between two R waves. Step 2: change squares to seconds. Multiply by 0.04. This is the RR interval, the time for one beat. Step 3: change to beats per minute. Divide 60 by the RR interval. The lecture's example: 22 small squares. Step 1: 22 small squares Step 2: 22 × 0.04 = 0.88 s per beat Step 3: 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min The quicker way, and where 1500 comes from. In one minute the paper moves 25 mm/s × 60 s = 1500 mm, which is 1500 small squares. If one beat takes 22 squares, the number of beats in a minute is: 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68 beats/min. Same answer, one step. Counting large squares works the same way: 1500 small squares = 300 large squares, so 300 ÷ 4.4 large squares ≈ 68. General relationship HR = (60) / (RRin seconds) 25 mm/s · regular rhythm HR = (1500) / (N_small squares) Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves. No calculator? Use the countdown. Find an R wave that sits on a thick line. For each thick line after it, say the next number in the countdown. Stop where the next R wave lands: Next R wave lands after… 1 large square 2 3 4 5 6 Heart rate (beats/min) 300 150 100 75 60 50 Each number is just 300 ÷ the number of large squares. If the R wave falls between two lines, the rate lies between those two numbers. For the lecture's strip (4.4 large squares), it lands between 75 and 60, nearer 75: about 68. Rate ruler A strip of ECG paper with two R waves. A slider sets the gap between them, from 10 to 40 small squares. Underneath, the three steps update live: squares → seconds (× 0.04) → beats/min (60 ÷ RR), with the 1500 shortcut and the countdown range beside them. It starts at the lecture's 22 squares, 68 beats/min. A second example: 15 small squares. 15 × 0.04 = 0.6 s; 60 ÷ 0.6 = 100 beats/min. Check: 1500 ÷ 15 = 100, or 3 large squares on the countdown = 100. ✓ If the rhythm is irregular, the gaps between R waves vary, so one gap gives the rate for one beat only. Instead, count the QRS complexes in a 6-second strip (30 large squares) and multiply by 10, because 6 s is one tenth of a minute. Eight complexes in 6 s ≈ 80 beats/min. Three common mistakes Dividing 1500 by large squares. Use 1500 for small squares and 300 for large squares. Using the 1500 rule on an irregular rhythm. Count complexes in 6 s × 10 instead. Forgetting the paper speed. At 50 mm/s each small square is 0.02 s, so the rules become 3000 ÷ small squares and 600 ÷ large squares. Source note: The 1500 and 300 rules and the 22-square example are from the lecture. The countdown, the 6-second method and the 50 mm/s adjustment are standard supplementary teaching. 8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary) The lecture focuses on ECG basics, not rhythm diagnosis. But four questions turn the trace into a first look at the rhythm: Rate: is it between about 60 and 100 beats/min at rest? Regularity: are the RR intervals equal? P waves: is there one before every QRS, and do they all look the same? Intervals: is the PR interval 0.12–0.20 s, and the QRS narrow? Normal sinus rhythm answers yes to all four. The PR interval also shows whether the signal is getting through the AV node: What you see Meaning PR longer than 0.20 s, but every P is followed by a QRS First-degree heart block: delayed, but always gets through Some P waves are not followed by a QRS Second-degree heart block: some signals are dropped P waves and QRS complexes at their own separate rates Third-degree (complete) heart block: nothing gets through The mechanisms are explained in Cardiac Conduction. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §8) Understanding Check: Sections 6–8 1. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are 20 small squares apart. What is the heart rate? A. 50 beats/min, because 1000 ÷ 20 = 50 B. 60 beats/min, because 1200 ÷ 20 = 60 C. 75 beats/min, because 1500 ÷ 20 = 75 D. 80 beats/min, because 1600 ÷ 20 = 80 Show answer and explanation Answer: C. At 25 mm/s, 1500 small squares pass in a minute, so 1500 ÷ 20 = 75. Check: 20 × 0.04 = 0.8 s, and 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75. 2. On a regular strip, each R wave lands 3 large squares after the one before. What is the heart rate? A. About 75 beats/min B. About 100 beats/min C. About 150 beats/min D. About 300 beats/min Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The countdown runs 300, 150, 100, so 3 large squares is 100 beats/min (300 ÷ 3). 3. Two QRS complexes look identical, but one is twice as tall. What does the extra height tell you? A. That beat lasted twice as long B. That beat had a bigger voltage C. That beat came at twice the rate D. That beat had a longer PR interval Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Height is voltage; width is time. A taller complex is a bigger signal, not a longer one. 4. An irregular rhythm shows 9 QRS complexes in a 6-second strip. What is the approximate rate? A. About 54 beats/min, from 9 × 6 B. About 75 beats/min, from 1500 ÷ 20 squares C. About 90 beats/min, from 9 × 10 D. About 150 beats/min, from 9 ÷ 6 × 100 Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A 6-second strip is one tenth of a minute, so multiply by 10. For an irregular rhythm this is more reliable than measuring one RR interval. 5. Every P wave is followed by a QRS, but the PR interval is 0.28 s. What does this show? A. Complete heart block: no signals get through B. Second-degree heart block: some signals are dropped C. First-degree heart block: signals are delayed but all get through D. A normal rhythm, because every P has a QRS Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A PR interval over 0.20 s means the signal is slow through the AV node, but since every P is followed by a QRS, none are dropped. 9. Chapter Essentials The ECG records electricity, not pumping. A normal trace does not prove the heart is ejecting blood. Electrode = skin contact; lead = a view. Ten electrodes give twelve leads; the right-leg electrode is an earth. Direction rule: depolarisation towards a lead draws an upward deflection; away draws a downward one; across draws a small up-and-down one. The T wave is usually upright, because ventricles reset in the reverse order to how they switched on. Leads: I, II and III (bipolar, Einthoven's triangle, II = I + III); aVR, aVL and aVF; V1–V6 across the chest. P = atria switch on (0.08–0.10 s); QRS = ventricles switch on (0.06–0.10 s); T = ventricles reset. PR interval = start of P to start of QRS (0.12–0.20 s), including the AV delay. The PR segment starts at the end of P. ST segment = whole ventricle switched on (plateau): flat but active. It shifts in ischaemia. QT interval = start of QRS to end of T (0.2–0.4 s), shorter at higher heart rates. Paper: 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV. Small square = 0.04 s and 0.1 mV; large square = 0.20 s. Rate (regular rhythm): 60 ÷ RR (s), 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares. Irregular: QRS complexes in 6 s × 10. Heart block: first degree = long PR, all conducted; second degree = some P waves dropped; third degree = P and QRS independent."},{"slug":"cardiac-output","title":"Cardiac Output and Frank-Starling Law","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"35-51","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter","title":"1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?"},{"id":"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output","title":"2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?"},{"id":"3-what-determines-stroke-volume","title":"3. What determines stroke volume?"},{"id":"4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection","title":"4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?"},{"id":"5-how-do-we-read-frank-starling-curves","title":"5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?"},{"id":"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume","title":"6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?"},{"id":"7-what-changes-contractility","title":"7. What changes contractility?"},{"id":"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations","title":"8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?"},{"id":"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states","title":"9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?"},{"id":"10-chapter-essentials","title":"10. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?</h2>\n<p><strong>Cardiac output (CO)</strong> is the volume of blood <strong>one ventricle</strong> pumps out <strong>each minute</strong>. It is how much blood the heart delivers to the body, not how much it ejects in one beat.</p>\n<p>It depends on two things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart rate (HR):</strong> beats per minute.</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke volume (SV):</strong> the blood ejected by one ventricle in <strong>one beat</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac output</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"CO = HR × SV\">CO = HR × SV</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = <strong>5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min</strong></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why multiply?</strong> Each beat sends out one stroke volume. 75 beats of 70 mL each is 75 × 70 mL in a minute.</p>\n<p>At rest, cardiac output is about <strong>5–5.5 L/min</strong> in an adult. That is roughly the whole blood volume pumped round once a minute. It varies with body size and activity.</p>\n<h3>Why must the right and left ventricles pump the same amount?</h3>\n<p>The two circulations are joined <strong>in series</strong>. Everything the right ventricle pumps into the lungs comes back to the left ventricle. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more each minute than the left, blood would pile up in the lungs. So <strong>over time the two outputs must be equal</strong>, even though the left ventricle works at a much higher pressure. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">→ Introduction, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<h3>Cardiac index: allowing for body size</h3>\n<p>A bigger person needs more blood flow than a smaller one. <strong><span data-term=\"cardiac-index\">Cardiac index (CI)</span></strong> divides cardiac output by <strong>body surface area (BSA)</strong>, so people of different sizes can be compared fairly.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac index</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"CI = (CO) / (BSA)\">CI = (CO) / (BSA)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: <strong>CI = 3.0 L/min/m²</strong>.</span></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?</h2>\n<p>From CO = HR × SV, a faster heart rate raises cardiac output <strong>only if stroke volume holds up</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic nerves usually raise both.</strong> They speed the SA node (positive <strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">chronotropy</span></strong>) and make each contraction stronger (positive <strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">inotropy</span></strong>). So in exercise, HR and SV rise together, and CO rises a lot.</p>\n<p><strong>But a very fast heart can pump less.</strong> The ventricles fill during diastole. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. At very high rates there is too little time to fill, so each beat ejects less.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> 75 beats/min × 70 mL = <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. 130 beats/min × 35 mL = <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. The heart is beating faster but pumping less.</p>\n<div class=\"co-impact-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Two determinants of cardiac output\"><span><strong>HR ↑</strong><small>More beats per minute</small></span><span><strong>SV ↓ markedly</strong><small>Less blood each beat</small></span><span><strong>CO may ↓</strong><small>Depends on their product</small></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone. Always ask what happened to stroke volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A heart beats 80 times a minute and ejects 60 mL each beat. What is the cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 1.3 L/min, because 80 ÷ 60 = 1.3</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 4.8 L/min, because 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 14.0 L/min, because 80 + 60 = 140</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 48.0 L/min, because 80 × 60 = 48,000 mL/min</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> CO = HR × SV = 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why must right and left ventricular outputs be equal over time?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both ventricles generate exactly the same pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The two circulations are in series, so unequal outputs would pile up blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The septum lets blood mix until both ventricles hold the same volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The same nerve fires both ventricles at the same strength</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> All blood leaving the right ventricle returns to the left. Unequal outputs would move blood steadily from one circulation into the other.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, while stroke volume falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because the heart is beating faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It stays the same, because the two changes cancel out</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It falls, because SV fell proportionally more than HR rose</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It cannot change, because CO depends only on venous return</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> 75 × 70 = 5.25 L/min; 130 × 35 = 4.55 L/min. Shorter filling time cut SV by half, which outweighed the faster rate.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-what-determines-stroke-volume\">3. What determines stroke volume?</h2>\n<p>Stroke volume is what the ventricle starts with minus what it leaves behind: <strong>SV = EDV − ESV</strong> (end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume). <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Three things decide it:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Factor</th>\n  <th>The question it answers</th>\n  <th>If it rises (others unchanged)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"preload\">Preload</span></strong></td>\n  <td>How stretched is the ventricle before it contracts?</td>\n  <td>SV rises (within limits)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"afterload\">Afterload</span></strong></td>\n  <td>How hard is it to push the blood out?</td>\n  <td>SV falls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Contractility</strong></td>\n  <td>How strongly can the muscle contract at the same stretch?</td>\n  <td>SV rises</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Preload: the starting stretch</h3>\n<p><strong>Preload</strong> is how stretched the ventricular muscle is <strong>at the end of filling</strong>, just before it contracts. The more blood that fills the ventricle, the more it stretches. So <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong> is the usual way to estimate preload.</p>\n<p><strong>Volume and pressure are not the same.</strong> A stiff ventricle can have a high filling pressure while holding little blood, so filling pressure can mislead.</p>\n<h3>Afterload: the load against ejection</h3>\n<p><strong>Afterload</strong> is what the ventricle must push against to eject blood. For the left ventricle, it is mainly the <strong>pressure in the aorta</strong>: the aortic valve only opens once ventricular pressure exceeds it. A higher aortic pressure means more of each contraction is spent just reaching that pressure, so <strong>less blood is ejected</strong> and more is left behind (higher ESV).</p>\n<h3>Contractility: how strong the muscle is at a given stretch</h3>\n<p><strong>Contractility</strong> (inotropy) is how forcefully the muscle contracts <strong>at the same stretch and the same load</strong>. It depends mainly on how much calcium reaches the contractile proteins in each beat. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">→ Cardiac Muscle, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> a ventricle that contracts harder <strong>because it was stretched more</strong> is using the Frank–Starling mechanism. That is <strong>not</strong> a rise in contractility. A rise in contractility means a stronger contraction <strong>without</strong> extra stretch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?</h2>\n<p><strong>The law:</strong> within normal limits, <strong>the more the ventricle is filled, the more it ejects</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does stretched muscle contract harder?</strong> Two reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Better overlap of the filaments.</strong> At normal resting volumes, the heart's <span data-term=\"sarcomere\">sarcomeres</span> are shorter than their best length. Stretching them brings actin and myosin closer to the length where the most cross-bridges can form.</li>\n<li><strong>The muscle becomes more sensitive to calcium.</strong> Stretch makes the contractile proteins respond more strongly to the same amount of calcium.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Frank Starling mechanism\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV and fibre stretch ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Force generated ↑</span><b>→</b><span>SV ↑</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>What is it for? Matching output to input, beat by beat.</strong> If more blood returns to a ventricle, it stretches more and ejects more, so blood doesn't pile up. This is also what keeps the two ventricles in step: if the right ventricle sends a little extra to the lungs, the left ventricle fills a little more and pumps it out. No nerve signal is needed. It is built into the muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Does more filling always mean more output?</strong> No. At high filling, the curve <strong>flattens</strong>: extra volume adds less and less SV. A badly failing ventricle may sit on a flat curve, where extra filling mainly raises pressure behind the heart and causes congestion.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture's curve shows a dashed falling limb at extreme stretch. That is a teaching extension: an intact human heart does not normally work on a falling limb.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-we-read-frank-starling-curves\">5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?</h2>\n<p>A Frank–Starling curve plots <strong>stroke volume</strong> (or cardiac output) against <strong>filling</strong> (EDV). There are two ways the point can move.</p>\n<div class=\"view-switch\">\n<figure class=\"learning-figure cardiac-output-figure static-view\"><figcaption>Frank–Starling curves at a glance</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 295\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic stroke-volume versus end-diastolic-volume graph showing increased, normal and reduced cardiac contractility curves.\"><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M99 229 C168 100 285 57 580 55\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 232 C188 181 275 148 580 148\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 235 C205 222 320 208 580 207\" stroke=\"#c39431\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"190\" y=\"273\">End-diastolic volume (preload) →</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"130\" transform=\"rotate(-90,22,130)\">Stroke volume</text><text x=\"387\" y=\"40\">Greater contractility</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"138\">Reference</text><text x=\"432\" y=\"196\">Lower contractility</text></g></svg><p><strong>Read across one curve:</strong> changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. <strong>Compare curves vertically:</strong> at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements.</p></figure>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"starling-explorer\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Frank–Starling explorer</figcaption><p>Replaces the static curves. Three curves: low, normal and high contractility. A slider sets EDV, and the dot slides along the current curve. Buttons switch contractility, and the dot jumps up or down to another curve at the same EDV. A readout shows EDV, SV and CO (at 70 beats/min), and one sentence says which kind of change just happened.</p></figure>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Moving along the same curve: a change in preload</h3>\n<p>Someone who has been standing lies down. Blood stops pooling in the legs and returns to the heart, so EDV rises. The point moves <strong>right and up along the same curve</strong>: more filling, more SV. Contractility has not changed.</p>\n<h3>Jumping to another curve: a change in contractility</h3>\n<p>Two beats have <strong>the same EDV</strong>, but before the second one, sympathetic nerves raise calcium entry. The second beat ejects more <strong>without any extra stretch</strong>: the point jumps to a <strong>higher curve</strong>. A weakened heart muscle sits on a <strong>lower curve</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Careful:</strong> a higher afterload also lowers the curve. So a lower curve doesn't prove that contractility has fallen, unless afterload is the same.</p>\n<h3>Two lecture terms: heterometric and homometric</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Term</th>\n  <th>Meaning (break it down)</th>\n  <th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"heterometric\">Heterometric</span></strong> regulation</td>\n  <td><em>hetero</em> = different, <em>metric</em> = length: output changes because the <strong>starting length changes</strong></td>\n  <td>More venous return → more stretch → more SV (Frank–Starling)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"homometric\">Homometric</span></strong> regulation</td>\n  <td><em>homo</em> = same: output changes at the <strong>same starting length</strong></td>\n  <td>Sympathetic stimulation → stronger beat at the same EDV</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 3–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Two beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects more blood after sympathetic stimulation. What changed?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Preload rose, moving the beat along the same curve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Contractility rose, shifting the beat to a higher curve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Afterload rose, making ejection easier for the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Venous return rose, stretching the fibres more</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> With EDV unchanged, the stretch is the same. A bigger SV at the same stretch means stronger contraction: a higher curve (homometric regulation).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does a stretched ventricle contract more forcefully?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Stretch opens more gap junctions between the cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Stretch triggers a sympathetic reflex from the atria</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Stretch brings filaments to better overlap and raises calcium sensitivity</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Stretch lowers aortic pressure so ejection is easier</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Normal resting sarcomeres are shorter than optimal, so stretch improves actin–myosin overlap, and the proteins also respond more strongly to calcium.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Aortic pressure suddenly rises, with no change in filling or contractility. What happens to the next beat?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. SV rises, because the ventricle contracts against more force</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. SV is unchanged, because preload and contractility are the same</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. SV falls and more blood is left behind in the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. SV falls because the ventricle fills less during diastole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Higher afterload means more of the contraction is spent reaching aortic pressure, so less is ejected and ESV rises.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Someone who has stood still for a long time lies down. Where does their heart move on the Frank–Starling graph?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Along the same curve, to the right and up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. To a higher curve, at the same EDV</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Along the same curve, to the left and down</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. To a lower curve, at the same EDV</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Lying down returns pooled blood to the heart, raising EDV. More filling moves the point along the same curve: preload changed, not contractility.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume\">6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?</h2>\n<p>EDV depends on <strong>how much blood comes back</strong>, <strong>how long the ventricle has to fill</strong> and <strong>how easily it stretches</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Raises EDV</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>More blood volume</td>\n  <td>More blood available to return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Veins tightening (venous tone ↑)</td>\n  <td>Squeezes blood out of the veins towards the heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Skeletal muscle pump</td>\n  <td>Contracting muscles squeeze veins; valves keep blood moving towards the heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Breathing (respiratory pump)</td>\n  <td>Breathing in lowers chest pressure and draws blood in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Strong atrial contraction</td>\n  <td>Tops up the ventricle at the end of filling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Longer filling time (slower heart rate)</td>\n  <td>More time for blood to enter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lowers EDV</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Blood loss</td>\n  <td>Less blood to return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Standing still</td>\n  <td>Gravity pools blood in the leg veins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Fluid around the heart (tamponade)</td>\n  <td>Pressure from outside stops the ventricle expanding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>A stiff ventricle</td>\n  <td>It resists stretching, so it fills less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>A very fast heart rate</td>\n  <td>Too little time to fill</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture also lists <strong>heart attack (myocardial infarction)</strong> and <strong>endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF)</strong>. Both damage or stiffen the ventricle wall, so filling and pumping are affected, and the effect on EDV depends on the disease and its stage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-contractility\">7. What changes contractility?</h2>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation is the main way to raise contractility.</strong> Sympathetic nerves release <strong>noradrenaline</strong> onto the heart, and the adrenal medulla releases <strong>adrenaline</strong> into the blood. Both act on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> on heart muscle cells:</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Beta adrenergic inotropic pathway\"><span>β₁ receptor</span><b>→</b><span>Gs / adenylyl cyclase</span><b>→</b><span>cAMP / PKA ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Ca²⁺ cycling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stronger contraction</span></div>\n\n<p>In plain terms, the signal ends up letting <strong>more calcium in</strong> through L-type channels and <strong>more calcium out of the sarcoplasmic reticulum</strong>. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat (positive <strong>inotropy</strong>). The same signal also speeds the pumping of calcium back into storage, so the muscle <strong>relaxes faster</strong> too (positive <strong><span data-term=\"lusitropy\">lusitropy</span></strong>), which helps the ventricle refill at fast heart rates.</p>\n<p><strong>Caffeine and theophylline (<span data-term=\"xanthines\">xanthines</span>)</strong>, which the lecture lists, slow the breakdown of cAMP, so they can strengthen contraction. In everyday doses the effect is small.</p>\n<p><strong>The vagus nerve has little direct effect on ventricular strength.</strong> It mainly slows heart rate and AV conduction. Vagal nerve endings reach the atria and the nodes, but hardly reach the ventricular muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>What weakens contraction?</strong> The lecture lists <strong>too much CO₂ (hypercapnia), too little oxygen (hypoxia), acidosis, some drugs and heart failure</strong>. Low oxygen and acid both interfere with the cell's energy supply and its calcium handling, so each beat is weaker.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One lecture slide reverses the usual sources. Most of the noradrenaline reaching the heart comes from sympathetic nerves, while circulating adrenaline comes mainly from the adrenal medulla.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations\">8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?</h2>\n<div class=\"co-compare-grid\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Three ways stroke volume changes\"><div><strong>More venous return</strong><span>Preload ↑</span><small>Often moves <em>along</em> the curve → SV ↑</small></div><div><strong>Higher aortic pressure</strong><span>Afterload ↑</span><small>Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓</small></div><div><strong>β₁ stimulation</strong><span>Contractility ↑</span><small>More force at same EDV → SV ↑</small></div></div>\n\n<p>Three patients show why each factor must be thought about separately:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood loss:</strong> heart rate rises, but there is so little blood returning (low preload) that SV and CO can still fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> even a strong contraction has to force blood through a narrow valve, a high load against ejection.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> the curve is flat, so extra filling raises pressure and congestion more than SV.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states\">9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?</h2>\n<p><strong>In exercise, everything pushes cardiac output up at once:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sympathetic nerves raise <strong>heart rate</strong> and <strong>contractility</strong>.</li>\n<li>Contracting muscles and deeper breathing push more blood back to the heart (<strong>more venous return</strong>), so preload rises.</li>\n<li>Blood vessels in active muscle widen, lowering resistance so blood flows out more easily.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So both HR and SV rise, and CO can increase several-fold.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One lecture slide shows a downward arrow for venous return during exercise. The lecture's own diagrams, and standard physiology, show that the muscle and breathing pumps <strong>increase</strong> venous return in exercise.</aside>\n\n<p>The lecture lists other states that raise cardiac output: <strong>eating, a hot environment, pregnancy, anxiety and excitement, adrenaline-like drugs and an overactive thyroid (thyrotoxicosis)</strong>. Each works through different mechanisms.</p>\n<p><strong>Blood loss (link to shock):</strong> after a large bleed, venous return and preload fall. Reflex sympathetic activity raises heart rate and tightens blood vessels to hold up blood pressure for a while, but if SV falls far enough, CO and tissue blood flow still fall. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–9</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which change would lower end-diastolic volume?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Contracting leg muscles while walking briskly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A slower heart rate that lengthens diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Fluid around the heart pressing on the ventricles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Veins tightening under sympathetic stimulation</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Fluid in the pericardium (tamponade) presses on the heart from outside, so the ventricles can't expand and fill. The other three all increase filling.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does sympathetic stimulation make each contraction stronger?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It stretches the ventricle so filaments overlap better</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It raises cAMP, so more calcium reaches the filaments</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It slows the heart, giving more time for filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It lowers aortic pressure, making ejection easier</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases calcium entry and release. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat at the same stretch.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient in severe heart failure is given a large volume of fluid. Why might their SV barely rise?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Their ventricle sits on a flat part of a low curve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fluid lowers their heart rate and so their output</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Extra fluid raises their contractility too much</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Fluid reduces afterload, so the ventricle empties early</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> On a flat, low curve, extra filling adds little SV but raises the pressure behind the heart, worsening congestion.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why does dynamic exercise increase venous return?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Gravity drains blood from the legs more easily during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The vagus nerve opens the venous valves during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Lower heart rate gives the veins more time to empty</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Contracting muscles and deep breathing pump blood back to the heart</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Muscle contractions squeeze the veins (valves keep the flow heading to the heart), and deeper breathing draws blood into the chest.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> After a large bleed, a patient's heart rate is 130 beats/min, yet their tissues are poorly perfused. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A high heart rate always reduces cardiac output</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low preload cut SV so much that faster beating could not make up for it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Sympathetic stimulation weakens contraction after blood loss</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The extra beats raise afterload, so no blood is ejected</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Blood loss reduces venous return and EDV, so SV falls. Since CO = HR × SV, a large enough fall in SV beats the rise in HR.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-chapter-essentials\">10. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>, about 5–5.5 L/min at rest. Right and left outputs must be equal over time (in series).</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac index</strong> = CO ÷ body surface area (L/min/m²).</li>\n<li><strong>Heart rate alone doesn't predict CO.</strong> Very fast rates shorten filling and can lower SV enough to drop CO.</li>\n<li><strong>SV = EDV − ESV</strong>, set by preload, afterload and contractility.</li>\n<li><strong>Preload</strong> = the stretch before contraction (estimated by EDV). <strong>Afterload</strong> = what the ventricle pushes against (mainly aortic pressure). <strong>Contractility</strong> = strength at the same stretch.</li>\n<li><strong>Frank–Starling:</strong> more filling → more stretch → better filament overlap and calcium sensitivity → more SV. It matches output to input and keeps the two ventricles in step.</li>\n<li><strong>Along the curve</strong> = preload change (heterometric). <strong>To a new curve</strong> = contractility change (homometric). Afterload also shifts curves.</li>\n<li><strong>EDV rises</strong> with more blood volume, venous tone, muscle and breathing pumps, atrial contraction and longer filling. <strong>It falls</strong> with blood loss, standing, tamponade, stiff ventricles and very fast rates.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic β₁ → cAMP → more calcium</strong> → stronger contraction and faster relaxation. The vagus mainly slows the heart and barely weakens the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, some drugs and heart failure</strong> weaken contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise</strong> raises HR, contractility and venous return together. Blood loss lowers preload, and tachycardia may not save CO.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law 1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter? Cardiac output (CO) is the volume of blood one ventricle pumps out each minute. It is how much blood the heart delivers to the body, not how much it ejects in one beat. It depends on two things: Heart rate (HR): beats per minute. Stroke volume (SV): the blood ejected by one ventricle in one beat. Cardiac output CO = HR × SV Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min Why multiply? Each beat sends out one stroke volume. 75 beats of 70 mL each is 75 × 70 mL in a minute. At rest, cardiac output is about 5–5.5 L/min in an adult. That is roughly the whole blood volume pumped round once a minute. It varies with body size and activity. Why must the right and left ventricles pump the same amount? The two circulations are joined in series. Everything the right ventricle pumps into the lungs comes back to the left ventricle. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more each minute than the left, blood would pile up in the lungs. So over time the two outputs must be equal, even though the left ventricle works at a much higher pressure. ( → Introduction, §2) Cardiac index: allowing for body size A bigger person needs more blood flow than a smaller one. Cardiac index (CI) divides cardiac output by body surface area (BSA), so people of different sizes can be compared fairly. Cardiac index CI = (CO) / (BSA) For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: CI = 3.0 L/min/m². 2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output? From CO = HR × SV, a faster heart rate raises cardiac output only if stroke volume holds up. Sympathetic nerves usually raise both. They speed the SA node (positive chronotropy) and make each contraction stronger (positive inotropy). So in exercise, HR and SV rise together, and CO rises a lot. But a very fast heart can pump less. The ventricles fill during diastole. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. At very high rates there is too little time to fill, so each beat ejects less. Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL = 5.25 L/min. 130 beats/min × 35 mL = 4.55 L/min. The heart is beating faster but pumping less. HR ↑ More beats per minute SV ↓ markedly Less blood each beat CO may ↓ Depends on their product Rule: never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone. Always ask what happened to stroke volume. Understanding Check: Sections 1–2 1. A heart beats 80 times a minute and ejects 60 mL each beat. What is the cardiac output? A. 1.3 L/min, because 80 ÷ 60 = 1.3 B. 4.8 L/min, because 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min C. 14.0 L/min, because 80 + 60 = 140 D. 48.0 L/min, because 80 × 60 = 48,000 mL/min Show answer and explanation Answer: B. CO = HR × SV = 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min. 2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be equal over time? A. Both ventricles generate exactly the same pressure B. The two circulations are in series, so unequal outputs would pile up blood C. The septum lets blood mix until both ventricles hold the same volume D. The same nerve fires both ventricles at the same strength Show answer and explanation Answer: B. All blood leaving the right ventricle returns to the left. Unequal outputs would move blood steadily from one circulation into the other. 3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, while stroke volume falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to cardiac output? A. It rises, because the heart is beating faster B. It stays the same, because the two changes cancel out C. It falls, because SV fell proportionally more than HR rose D. It cannot change, because CO depends only on venous return Show answer and explanation Answer: C. 75 × 70 = 5.25 L/min; 130 × 35 = 4.55 L/min. Shorter filling time cut SV by half, which outweighed the faster rate. 3. What determines stroke volume? Stroke volume is what the ventricle starts with minus what it leaves behind: SV = EDV − ESV (end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume). ( → Cardiac Cycle, §7) Three things decide it: Factor The question it answers If it rises (others unchanged) Preload How stretched is the ventricle before it contracts? SV rises (within limits) Afterload How hard is it to push the blood out? SV falls Contractility How strongly can the muscle contract at the same stretch? SV rises Preload: the starting stretch Preload is how stretched the ventricular muscle is at the end of filling, just before it contracts. The more blood that fills the ventricle, the more it stretches. So end-diastolic volume (EDV) is the usual way to estimate preload. Volume and pressure are not the same. A stiff ventricle can have a high filling pressure while holding little blood, so filling pressure can mislead. Afterload: the load against ejection Afterload is what the ventricle must push against to eject blood. For the left ventricle, it is mainly the pressure in the aorta: the aortic valve only opens once ventricular pressure exceeds it. A higher aortic pressure means more of each contraction is spent just reaching that pressure, so less blood is ejected and more is left behind (higher ESV). Contractility: how strong the muscle is at a given stretch Contractility (inotropy) is how forcefully the muscle contracts at the same stretch and the same load. It depends mainly on how much calcium reaches the contractile proteins in each beat. ( → Cardiac Muscle, §4) Key distinction: a ventricle that contracts harder because it was stretched more is using the Frank–Starling mechanism. That is not a rise in contractility. A rise in contractility means a stronger contraction without extra stretch. 4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection? The law: within normal limits, the more the ventricle is filled, the more it ejects. Why does stretched muscle contract harder? Two reasons: Better overlap of the filaments. At normal resting volumes, the heart's sarcomeres are shorter than their best length. Stretching them brings actin and myosin closer to the length where the most cross-bridges can form. The muscle becomes more sensitive to calcium. Stretch makes the contractile proteins respond more strongly to the same amount of calcium. Venous return ↑ → EDV and fibre stretch ↑ → Force generated ↑ → SV ↑ What is it for? Matching output to input, beat by beat. If more blood returns to a ventricle, it stretches more and ejects more, so blood doesn't pile up. This is also what keeps the two ventricles in step: if the right ventricle sends a little extra to the lungs, the left ventricle fills a little more and pumps it out. No nerve signal is needed. It is built into the muscle. Does more filling always mean more output? No. At high filling, the curve flattens: extra volume adds less and less SV. A badly failing ventricle may sit on a flat curve, where extra filling mainly raises pressure behind the heart and causes congestion. Source note: The lecture's curve shows a dashed falling limb at extreme stretch. That is a teaching extension: an intact human heart does not normally work on a falling limb. 5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves? A Frank–Starling curve plots stroke volume (or cardiac output) against filling (EDV). There are two ways the point can move. Frank–Starling curves at a glance End-diastolic volume (preload) → Stroke volume Greater contractility Reference Lower contractility Read across one curve: changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. Compare curves vertically: at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements. Frank–Starling explorer Replaces the static curves. Three curves: low, normal and high contractility. A slider sets EDV, and the dot slides along the current curve. Buttons switch contractility, and the dot jumps up or down to another curve at the same EDV. A readout shows EDV, SV and CO (at 70 beats/min), and one sentence says which kind of change just happened. Moving along the same curve: a change in preload Someone who has been standing lies down. Blood stops pooling in the legs and returns to the heart, so EDV rises. The point moves right and up along the same curve: more filling, more SV. Contractility has not changed. Jumping to another curve: a change in contractility Two beats have the same EDV, but before the second one, sympathetic nerves raise calcium entry. The second beat ejects more without any extra stretch: the point jumps to a higher curve. A weakened heart muscle sits on a lower curve. Careful: a higher afterload also lowers the curve. So a lower curve doesn't prove that contractility has fallen, unless afterload is the same. Two lecture terms: heterometric and homometric Term Meaning (break it down) Example Heterometric regulation hetero = different, metric = length: output changes because the starting length changes More venous return → more stretch → more SV (Frank–Starling) Homometric regulation homo = same: output changes at the same starting length Sympathetic stimulation → stronger beat at the same EDV Understanding Check: Sections 3–5 1. Two beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects more blood after sympathetic stimulation. What changed? A. Preload rose, moving the beat along the same curve B. Contractility rose, shifting the beat to a higher curve C. Afterload rose, making ejection easier for the ventricle D. Venous return rose, stretching the fibres more Show answer and explanation Answer: B. With EDV unchanged, the stretch is the same. A bigger SV at the same stretch means stronger contraction: a higher curve (homometric regulation). 2. Why does a stretched ventricle contract more forcefully? A. Stretch opens more gap junctions between the cells B. Stretch triggers a sympathetic reflex from the atria C. Stretch brings filaments to better overlap and raises calcium sensitivity D. Stretch lowers aortic pressure so ejection is easier Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Normal resting sarcomeres are shorter than optimal, so stretch improves actin–myosin overlap, and the proteins also respond more strongly to calcium. 3. Aortic pressure suddenly rises, with no change in filling or contractility. What happens to the next beat? A. SV rises, because the ventricle contracts against more force B. SV is unchanged, because preload and contractility are the same C. SV falls and more blood is left behind in the ventricle D. SV falls because the ventricle fills less during diastole Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Higher afterload means more of the contraction is spent reaching aortic pressure, so less is ejected and ESV rises. 4. Someone who has stood still for a long time lies down. Where does their heart move on the Frank–Starling graph? A. Along the same curve, to the right and up B. To a higher curve, at the same EDV C. Along the same curve, to the left and down D. To a lower curve, at the same EDV Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Lying down returns pooled blood to the heart, raising EDV. More filling moves the point along the same curve: preload changed, not contractility. 6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume? EDV depends on how much blood comes back, how long the ventricle has to fill and how easily it stretches. Raises EDV Why More blood volume More blood available to return Veins tightening (venous tone ↑) Squeezes blood out of the veins towards the heart Skeletal muscle pump Contracting muscles squeeze veins; valves keep blood moving towards the heart Breathing (respiratory pump) Breathing in lowers chest pressure and draws blood in Strong atrial contraction Tops up the ventricle at the end of filling Longer filling time (slower heart rate) More time for blood to enter Lowers EDV Why Blood loss Less blood to return Standing still Gravity pools blood in the leg veins Fluid around the heart (tamponade) Pressure from outside stops the ventricle expanding A stiff ventricle It resists stretching, so it fills less A very fast heart rate Too little time to fill The lecture also lists heart attack (myocardial infarction) and endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF). Both damage or stiffen the ventricle wall, so filling and pumping are affected, and the effect on EDV depends on the disease and its stage. 7. What changes contractility? Sympathetic stimulation is the main way to raise contractility. Sympathetic nerves release noradrenaline onto the heart, and the adrenal medulla releases adrenaline into the blood. Both act on β₁ receptors on heart muscle cells: β₁ receptor → Gs / adenylyl cyclase → cAMP / PKA ↑ → Ca²⁺ cycling ↑ → Stronger contraction In plain terms, the signal ends up letting more calcium in through L-type channels and more calcium out of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat (positive inotropy). The same signal also speeds the pumping of calcium back into storage, so the muscle relaxes faster too (positive lusitropy), which helps the ventricle refill at fast heart rates. Caffeine and theophylline ( xanthines), which the lecture lists, slow the breakdown of cAMP, so they can strengthen contraction. In everyday doses the effect is small. The vagus nerve has little direct effect on ventricular strength. It mainly slows heart rate and AV conduction. Vagal nerve endings reach the atria and the nodes, but hardly reach the ventricular muscle. What weakens contraction? The lecture lists too much CO₂ (hypercapnia), too little oxygen (hypoxia), acidosis, some drugs and heart failure. Low oxygen and acid both interfere with the cell's energy supply and its calcium handling, so each beat is weaker. Source note: One lecture slide reverses the usual sources. Most of the noradrenaline reaching the heart comes from sympathetic nerves, while circulating adrenaline comes mainly from the adrenal medulla. 8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations? More venous return Preload ↑ Often moves along the curve → SV ↑ Higher aortic pressure Afterload ↑ Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓ β₁ stimulation Contractility ↑ More force at same EDV → SV ↑ Three patients show why each factor must be thought about separately: Blood loss: heart rate rises, but there is so little blood returning (low preload) that SV and CO can still fall. Aortic stenosis: even a strong contraction has to force blood through a narrow valve, a high load against ejection. Heart failure: the curve is flat, so extra filling raises pressure and congestion more than SV. 9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states? In exercise, everything pushes cardiac output up at once: Sympathetic nerves raise heart rate and contractility. Contracting muscles and deeper breathing push more blood back to the heart ( more venous return), so preload rises. Blood vessels in active muscle widen, lowering resistance so blood flows out more easily. So both HR and SV rise, and CO can increase several-fold. Source note: One lecture slide shows a downward arrow for venous return during exercise. The lecture's own diagrams, and standard physiology, show that the muscle and breathing pumps increase venous return in exercise. The lecture lists other states that raise cardiac output: eating, a hot environment, pregnancy, anxiety and excitement, adrenaline-like drugs and an overactive thyroid (thyrotoxicosis). Each works through different mechanisms. Blood loss (link to shock): after a large bleed, venous return and preload fall. Reflex sympathetic activity raises heart rate and tightens blood vessels to hold up blood pressure for a while, but if SV falls far enough, CO and tissue blood flow still fall. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 6–9 1. Which change would lower end-diastolic volume? A. Contracting leg muscles while walking briskly B. A slower heart rate that lengthens diastole C. Fluid around the heart pressing on the ventricles D. Veins tightening under sympathetic stimulation Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Fluid in the pericardium (tamponade) presses on the heart from outside, so the ventricles can't expand and fill. The other three all increase filling. 2. How does sympathetic stimulation make each contraction stronger? A. It stretches the ventricle so filaments overlap better B. It raises cAMP, so more calcium reaches the filaments C. It slows the heart, giving more time for filling D. It lowers aortic pressure, making ejection easier Show answer and explanation Answer: B. β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases calcium entry and release. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat at the same stretch. 3. A patient in severe heart failure is given a large volume of fluid. Why might their SV barely rise? A. Their ventricle sits on a flat part of a low curve B. Fluid lowers their heart rate and so their output C. Extra fluid raises their contractility too much D. Fluid reduces afterload, so the ventricle empties early Show answer and explanation Answer: A. On a flat, low curve, extra filling adds little SV but raises the pressure behind the heart, worsening congestion. 4. Why does dynamic exercise increase venous return? A. Gravity drains blood from the legs more easily during exercise B. The vagus nerve opens the venous valves during exercise C. Lower heart rate gives the veins more time to empty D. Contracting muscles and deep breathing pump blood back to the heart Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Muscle contractions squeeze the veins (valves keep the flow heading to the heart), and deeper breathing draws blood into the chest. 5. After a large bleed, a patient's heart rate is 130 beats/min, yet their tissues are poorly perfused. What is the best explanation? A. A high heart rate always reduces cardiac output B. Low preload cut SV so much that faster beating could not make up for it C. Sympathetic stimulation weakens contraction after blood loss D. The extra beats raise afterload, so no blood is ejected Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Blood loss reduces venous return and EDV, so SV falls. Since CO = HR × SV, a large enough fall in SV beats the rise in HR. 10. Chapter Essentials CO = HR × SV, about 5–5.5 L/min at rest. Right and left outputs must be equal over time (in series). Cardiac index = CO ÷ body surface area (L/min/m²). Heart rate alone doesn't predict CO. Very fast rates shorten filling and can lower SV enough to drop CO. SV = EDV − ESV, set by preload, afterload and contractility. Preload = the stretch before contraction (estimated by EDV). Afterload = what the ventricle pushes against (mainly aortic pressure). Contractility = strength at the same stretch. Frank–Starling: more filling → more stretch → better filament overlap and calcium sensitivity → more SV. It matches output to input and keeps the two ventricles in step. Along the curve = preload change (heterometric). To a new curve = contractility change (homometric). Afterload also shifts curves. EDV rises with more blood volume, venous tone, muscle and breathing pumps, atrial contraction and longer filling. It falls with blood loss, standing, tamponade, stiff ventricles and very fast rates. Sympathetic β₁ → cAMP → more calcium → stronger contraction and faster relaxation. The vagus mainly slows the heart and barely weakens the ventricles. Hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, some drugs and heart failure weaken contraction. Exercise raises HR, contractility and venous return together. Blood loss lowers preload, and tachycardia may not save CO."},{"slug":"co-measurement","title":"Cardiac Output Measurement and Work","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"52-54","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-measure-cardiac-output-instead-of-calculating-it-from-heart-rate-alone","title":"1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone?"},{"id":"2-fick-s-principle-working-out-flow-from-oxygen-use","title":"2. Fick's principle: working out flow from oxygen use"},{"id":"3-where-do-the-oxygen-measurements-come-from","title":"3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from?"},{"id":"4-other-ways-to-measure-flow-indicator-dilution-supplementary","title":"4. Other ways to measure flow: indicator dilution (supplementary)"},{"id":"5-what-does-it-mean-for-the-heart-to-perform-work","title":"5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work?"},{"id":"6-visualising-stroke-work-on-a-pressure-volume-loop","title":"6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop"},{"id":"7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand","title":"7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand?"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-measure-cardiac-output-instead-of-calculating-it-from-heart-rate-alone\">1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone?</h2>\n<p>CO = HR × SV, but at the bedside <strong>stroke volume can't be seen or felt</strong>. A pulse tells you the heart rate, not how much blood each beat pushes out. So we need a way to measure blood flow directly. The lecture's method is <strong><span data-term=\"fick\">Fick's principle</span></strong>, which works out flow from how much oxygen the body uses. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">→ Cardiac Output, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"2-fick-s-principle-working-out-flow-from-oxygen-use\">2. Fick's principle: working out flow from oxygen use</h2>\n<p><strong>The idea, with a delivery van.</strong> Vans carry parcels to a town. Each van arrives with 20 parcels and leaves with 15, so it drops off 5. The town takes in 250 parcels a minute. How many vans pass through each minute? 250 ÷ 5 = <strong>50 vans a minute</strong>.</p>\n<p>Blood works the same way:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oxygen used by the body each minute (V̇O₂)</strong> is the parcels delivered.</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen dropped off by each mL of blood (arterial − <span data-term=\"mixed-venous\">mixed venous</span> content)</strong> is the parcels per van.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood flow (cardiac output)</strong> is the number of vans.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"fick-process\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fick oxygen extraction concept\"><span><strong>Arterial O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ carried into tissues</small></span><b>−</b><span><strong>Mixed venous O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ remaining after tissues</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>O₂ extracted</strong><small>Amount removed from each unit of blood</small></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick's oxygen principle</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Oxygen consumption equals cardiac output times the arterial minus mixed venous oxygen content\">V̇O₂ = CO × (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂)</span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick cardiac output</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Cardiac output equals oxygen consumption divided by arterial minus mixed venous oxygen content\">CO = V̇O₂ / (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂)</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The lecture's example, in three steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Oxygen used:</strong> 250 mL O₂/min</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen dropped off per mL of blood:</strong> 0.20 − 0.15 = <strong>0.05 mL O₂ per mL of blood</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Flow:</strong> 250 ÷ 0.05 = <strong>5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Check the units:</strong> (mL O₂/min) ÷ (mL O₂ per mL blood) = mL blood/min. The oxygen cancels and leaves blood flow.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"fick-calculator\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Fick calculator</figcaption><p>Three sliders: oxygen use (150–1,000 mL/min), arterial O₂ content (0.10–0.22 mL/mL) and mixed venous O₂ content (0.05–0.18 mL/mL; it cannot exceed arterial). The three steps update live, with the answer in L/min. A small bar shows the oxygen dropped off per mL. It starts at the lecture's 250, 0.20 and 0.15 → 5.0 L/min. One line notes the pattern: when tissues take more oxygen from each mL, the same oxygen use needs less flow.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Content, not saturation.</strong> Fick needs the <strong>amount of oxygen in each mL of blood</strong> (content). <strong><span data-term=\"o2-saturation\">Saturation</span></strong> only tells you what percentage of haemoglobin is carrying oxygen. Someone with anaemia can be 98% saturated but carry much less oxygen per mL, because there is less haemoglobin. Using saturation in place of content gives the wrong answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-where-do-the-oxygen-measurements-come-from\">3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arterial content:</strong> from any systemic artery, since arterial blood is the same everywhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed venous content:</strong> from the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong>, through a catheter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the pulmonary artery?</strong> Veins from different organs carry very different amounts of oxygen. The kidney takes little oxygen out, while working muscle takes a lot. Their blood only becomes fully mixed after passing through the right heart. A sample from one arm vein shows only what the arm used.</p>\n<p><strong>Why not use a finger pulse oximeter?</strong> It measures arterial saturation only. It can't measure venous content or blood flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Direct versus estimated Fick.</strong> A true (direct) Fick measures oxygen use at the time. Some clinics use a predicted value instead, which adds error. Either way, the method assumes a <strong>steady state</strong>: oxygen use and flow must be stable while the samples are taken.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Oxygen use is 250 mL/min. Arterial content is 0.20 and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂ per mL blood. What is the cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 1.25 L/min, from 250 × 0.05 ÷ 10</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 5.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.05</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 12.5 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.02</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 50.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.005</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood. 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is mixed venous blood taken from the pulmonary artery, not an arm vein?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Arm veins contain no measurable oxygen at all</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Pulmonary artery blood has been oxygenated by the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood from every organ has mixed by the time it reaches there</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The pulmonary artery is the easiest vessel to reach</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Organs extract different amounts of oxygen. Only after the right heart is the venous blood from the whole body mixed. Pulmonary artery blood has <strong>not</strong> yet passed through the lungs.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient with severe anaemia has 98% arterial saturation. Why can't 98% be used in the Fick formula?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Saturation over 95% always makes the formula invalid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fick needs oxygen content, which is low when haemoglobin is low</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Anaemia stops oxygen from leaving the blood at the tissues</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Saturation is only measured in venous blood, not arterial</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Saturation is a percentage of haemoglobin. With little haemoglobin, each mL carries less oxygen even at 98%.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Oxygen use stays at 250 mL/min, but the tissues now take out 0.10 mL O₂ from each mL of blood instead of 0.05. What does this mean for cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It has doubled, to 10 L/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is unchanged, at 5 L/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It has halved, to 2.5 L/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It cannot be worked out without heart rate</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> 250 ÷ 0.10 = 2,500 mL/min. When each mL gives up more oxygen, less flow is needed for the same oxygen use. This is what happens when cardiac output falls.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-other-ways-to-measure-flow-indicator-dilution-supplementary\">4. Other ways to measure flow: indicator dilution (supplementary)</h2>\n<p>The same &quot;what goes in must come out&quot; idea underlies other methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Dye dilution.</strong> Inject a known amount of dye into a vein, then measure its concentration downstream over time. <strong>Fast flow dilutes the dye more and washes it past quickly</strong>, so the concentration curve is low and short. Slow flow gives a higher, longer curve. From the dose and the curve, flow can be worked out.</p>\n<p><strong>Thermodilution.</strong> The same idea, using <strong>cold</strong> saline instead of dye. A pulmonary artery catheter injects it, and a temperature sensor (<span data-term=\"thermodilution\">thermistor</span>) downstream records how much and for how long the blood cools.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Method</th>\n  <th>What is measured</th>\n  <th>Main pitfall</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fick</strong></td>\n  <td>Oxygen use, and arterial and mixed venous oxygen content</td>\n  <td>Needs a steady state and good samples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Dye dilution</strong></td>\n  <td>Dye concentration over time</td>\n  <td>Dye coming round a second time (recirculation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Thermodilution</strong></td>\n  <td>Blood temperature over time</td>\n  <td>Leaky valves and shunts distort the curve</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture covers Fick only. Dye dilution and thermodilution are standard supplementary methods that use the same mass-balance idea.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-what-does-it-mean-for-the-heart-to-perform-work\">5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work?</h2>\n<p>The heart doesn't just move blood. It pushes it <strong>against pressure</strong>, and that takes work. The lecture's simple formula:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Approximate external stroke work</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"SW ≈ SV × P\">SW ≈ SV × P</span><span class=\"equation-example\">P is the pressure the ventricle pumps against, usually mean arterial pressure. This is an estimate: the exact value is the area of the pressure–volume loop.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> SV = 70 mL, mean arterial pressure = 100 mmHg → <span data-term=\"stroke-work\">stroke work</span> ≈ 70 × 100 = <strong>7,000 mmHg·mL</strong> (about <strong>0.93 J</strong>) per beat.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does the left ventricle do more work than the right?</strong> Both pump the <strong>same volume</strong>, but the left pumps against about <strong>100 mmHg</strong> (aorta) and the right against about <strong>15 mmHg</strong> (pulmonary artery). Same volume against higher pressure means more work. That is why the left ventricular wall is so much thicker. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\">→ Introduction, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-visualising-stroke-work-on-a-pressure-volume-loop\">6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure work-pv-figure\" id=\"cvs08-pv\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 580 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic left ventricular pressure-volume loop with end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes marked, filling at low pressure, isovolumetric contraction, ejection and relaxation, and interior stroke-work area shaded.\"><defs><marker id=\"workloop-arrow\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L6 3 L0 6z\" fill=\"#2863dd\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"64\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"557\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M208 216 C270 226 374 228 441 216 L441 77 C389 35 314 43 208 86 Z\" fill=\"#93c5fd\" opacity=\".28\"/><path d=\"M208 216 C290 231 362 229 441 216\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M441 216 L441 77\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><path d=\"M441 77 C389 35 314 43 208 86\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><path d=\"M208 86 L208 216\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"310\" y=\"143\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"800\">Stroke work</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"159\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈ loop area</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"255\">ESV</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"270\">EDV</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"273\">Ventricular volume →</text><text x=\"21\" y=\"148\" transform=\"rotate(-90,21,148)\">Pressure</text><text x=\"458\" y=\"135\">Isovolumetric</text><text x=\"458\" y=\"150\">contraction</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"121\">Isovolumetric</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"136\">relaxation</text><text x=\"307\" y=\"37\">Ejection</text><text x=\"306\" y=\"235\">Filling</text></g></svg><p>Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume.</p></figure>\n\n<p>Follow the loop round:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bottom:</strong> the ventricle fills at low pressure (volume rises).</li>\n<li><strong>Right side:</strong> it contracts with all valves shut, so pressure rises at constant volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Top:</strong> it ejects (volume falls at high pressure).</li>\n<li><strong>Left side:</strong> it relaxes with all valves shut, so pressure falls at constant volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The area inside the loop is the stroke work.</strong> A taller loop (higher pressure) or a wider loop (bigger SV) means more work. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=8-the-whole-cycle-on-one-map\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §8</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The loop shows only the <strong>outside work</strong> of pushing blood. The muscle also uses energy to pump calcium, keep ion gradients and hold tension, so <strong>stroke work is not the same as the heart's total oxygen use</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand?</h2>\n<p>The lecture lists three main things that set how much oxygen the heart muscle uses:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"wall-tension\">Wall tension</span></strong>, which rises with the pressure the ventricle must develop</li>\n<li><strong>Contractility:</strong> a stronger beat costs more energy</li>\n<li><strong>Heart rate:</strong> more beats per minute</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Pressure work costs more oxygen than volume work.</strong> Raising pressure means building and holding high wall tension, which is expensive. Pumping more volume at the same pressure costs less. The lecture's examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aortic stenosis (pressure load):</strong> the ventricle must generate very high pressure to force blood through a narrow valve, so oxygen demand is high.</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic regurgitation (volume load):</strong> the ventricle pumps a bigger volume each beat, but at near-normal pressure, so oxygen demand rises less.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are general patterns. Individual patients vary with severity and coronary supply.</p>\n<p><strong>Why must coronary flow rise in exercise?</strong> Even at rest, the heart muscle extracts most of the oxygen from its blood (about 70%). There is little left to take, so when demand rises, <strong>the only way to get more oxygen is more coronary flow</strong>. If narrowed coronaries can't deliver it, the muscle runs short of oxygen and chest pain (angina) results. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary\">→ Coronary Circulation</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Careful:</strong> Fick uses the <strong>whole body's</strong> oxygen use to measure cardiac output. The heart's <strong>own</strong> oxygen use is a separate, much smaller quantity.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> In dye dilution, what does a low, short concentration curve downstream tell you?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Flow is fast, so the dye is diluted and washed past quickly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Flow is slow, so the dye stays in the vessel for longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The dye was injected into the wrong vessel entirely</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart rate is too slow for the method to work</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Fast flow spreads the same dose through more blood and carries it past quickly, giving a lower, shorter curve.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The two ventricles eject the same volume. Why does the left do more work?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It ejects blood more slowly than the right does</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It pumps against a much higher pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It has to pump blood through the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Its valves are smaller, so it ejects less</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure. The same SV against about 100 mmHg instead of about 15 mmHg means far more work.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> On a pressure–volume loop, what does the enclosed area represent?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The heart's total oxygen use</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The stroke volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The external stroke work of one beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The time for one cardiac cycle</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The area is the outside work of pushing blood against pressure. Total oxygen use is larger, and SV is the loop's width.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Which places the greatest oxygen demand on the left ventricle?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Pumping a larger volume at normal pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A slower heart rate with normal pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A smaller stroke volume at normal pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pumping a normal volume against a very high pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> High pressure means high wall tension, the most oxygen-expensive kind of work. That is why aortic stenosis raises demand more than regurgitation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Why must coronary blood flow rise when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Heart muscle already takes out most of the oxygen it receives</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Coronary arteries narrow during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood carries less oxygen during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Heart rate falls, so each beat needs more flow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> With extraction already near its maximum, the only way to get more oxygen is to deliver more blood.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fick's principle:</strong> oxygen used per minute = blood flow × oxygen dropped off per mL. So <strong>CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The lecture's example:</strong> 250 ÷ (0.20 − 0.15) = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min.</li>\n<li><strong>Use content, not saturation</strong>: content depends on haemoglobin.</li>\n<li><strong>Samples:</strong> arterial from any artery; <strong>mixed venous from the pulmonary artery</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fick needs a steady state</strong> and accurate samples.</li>\n<li><strong>Indicator dilution (supplementary):</strong> a known dose of dye or cold saline, measured downstream. Faster flow gives a lower, shorter curve.</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure.</strong> On a pressure–volume loop it is the area inside.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle does more work</strong> than the right: same volume, higher pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart muscle oxygen demand</strong> rises with wall tension, contractility and heart rate. <strong>Pressure work costs more than volume work.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>The heart already extracts most of its oxygen</strong>, so more demand needs <strong>more coronary flow</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart 1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone? CO = HR × SV, but at the bedside stroke volume can't be seen or felt. A pulse tells you the heart rate, not how much blood each beat pushes out. So we need a way to measure blood flow directly. The lecture's method is Fick's principle, which works out flow from how much oxygen the body uses. ( → Cardiac Output, §1) 2. Fick's principle: working out flow from oxygen use The idea, with a delivery van. Vans carry parcels to a town. Each van arrives with 20 parcels and leaves with 15, so it drops off 5. The town takes in 250 parcels a minute. How many vans pass through each minute? 250 ÷ 5 = 50 vans a minute. Blood works the same way: Oxygen used by the body each minute (V̇O₂) is the parcels delivered. Oxygen dropped off by each mL of blood (arterial − mixed venous content) is the parcels per van. Blood flow (cardiac output) is the number of vans. Arterial O₂ content O₂ carried into tissues − Mixed venous O₂ content O₂ remaining after tissues → O₂ extracted Amount removed from each unit of blood Fick's oxygen principle V̇O₂ = CO × (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂) Fick cardiac output CO = V̇O₂ / (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂) The lecture's example, in three steps: Oxygen used: 250 mL O₂/min Oxygen dropped off per mL of blood: 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL of blood Flow: 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min Check the units: (mL O₂/min) ÷ (mL O₂ per mL blood) = mL blood/min. The oxygen cancels and leaves blood flow. Fick calculator Three sliders: oxygen use (150–1,000 mL/min), arterial O₂ content (0.10–0.22 mL/mL) and mixed venous O₂ content (0.05–0.18 mL/mL; it cannot exceed arterial). The three steps update live, with the answer in L/min. A small bar shows the oxygen dropped off per mL. It starts at the lecture's 250, 0.20 and 0.15 → 5.0 L/min. One line notes the pattern: when tissues take more oxygen from each mL, the same oxygen use needs less flow. Content, not saturation. Fick needs the amount of oxygen in each mL of blood (content). Saturation only tells you what percentage of haemoglobin is carrying oxygen. Someone with anaemia can be 98% saturated but carry much less oxygen per mL, because there is less haemoglobin. Using saturation in place of content gives the wrong answer. 3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from? Arterial content: from any systemic artery, since arterial blood is the same everywhere. Mixed venous content: from the pulmonary artery, through a catheter. Why the pulmonary artery? Veins from different organs carry very different amounts of oxygen. The kidney takes little oxygen out, while working muscle takes a lot. Their blood only becomes fully mixed after passing through the right heart. A sample from one arm vein shows only what the arm used. Why not use a finger pulse oximeter? It measures arterial saturation only. It can't measure venous content or blood flow. Direct versus estimated Fick. A true (direct) Fick measures oxygen use at the time. Some clinics use a predicted value instead, which adds error. Either way, the method assumes a steady state: oxygen use and flow must be stable while the samples are taken. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Oxygen use is 250 mL/min. Arterial content is 0.20 and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂ per mL blood. What is the cardiac output? A. 1.25 L/min, from 250 × 0.05 ÷ 10 B. 5.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.05 C. 12.5 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.02 D. 50.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.005 Show answer and explanation Answer: B. 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood. 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min. 2. Why is mixed venous blood taken from the pulmonary artery, not an arm vein? A. Arm veins contain no measurable oxygen at all B. Pulmonary artery blood has been oxygenated by the lungs C. Blood from every organ has mixed by the time it reaches there D. The pulmonary artery is the easiest vessel to reach Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Organs extract different amounts of oxygen. Only after the right heart is the venous blood from the whole body mixed. Pulmonary artery blood has not yet passed through the lungs. 3. A patient with severe anaemia has 98% arterial saturation. Why can't 98% be used in the Fick formula? A. Saturation over 95% always makes the formula invalid B. Fick needs oxygen content, which is low when haemoglobin is low C. Anaemia stops oxygen from leaving the blood at the tissues D. Saturation is only measured in venous blood, not arterial Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Saturation is a percentage of haemoglobin. With little haemoglobin, each mL carries less oxygen even at 98%. 4. Oxygen use stays at 250 mL/min, but the tissues now take out 0.10 mL O₂ from each mL of blood instead of 0.05. What does this mean for cardiac output? A. It has doubled, to 10 L/min B. It is unchanged, at 5 L/min C. It has halved, to 2.5 L/min D. It cannot be worked out without heart rate Show answer and explanation Answer: C. 250 ÷ 0.10 = 2,500 mL/min. When each mL gives up more oxygen, less flow is needed for the same oxygen use. This is what happens when cardiac output falls. 4. Other ways to measure flow: indicator dilution (supplementary) The same \"what goes in must come out\" idea underlies other methods. Dye dilution. Inject a known amount of dye into a vein, then measure its concentration downstream over time. Fast flow dilutes the dye more and washes it past quickly, so the concentration curve is low and short. Slow flow gives a higher, longer curve. From the dose and the curve, flow can be worked out. Thermodilution. The same idea, using cold saline instead of dye. A pulmonary artery catheter injects it, and a temperature sensor ( thermistor) downstream records how much and for how long the blood cools. Method What is measured Main pitfall Fick Oxygen use, and arterial and mixed venous oxygen content Needs a steady state and good samples Dye dilution Dye concentration over time Dye coming round a second time (recirculation) Thermodilution Blood temperature over time Leaky valves and shunts distort the curve Source note: The lecture covers Fick only. Dye dilution and thermodilution are standard supplementary methods that use the same mass-balance idea. 5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work? The heart doesn't just move blood. It pushes it against pressure, and that takes work. The lecture's simple formula: Approximate external stroke work SW ≈ SV × P P is the pressure the ventricle pumps against, usually mean arterial pressure. This is an estimate: the exact value is the area of the pressure–volume loop. Example: SV = 70 mL, mean arterial pressure = 100 mmHg → stroke work ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL (about 0.93 J) per beat. Why does the left ventricle do more work than the right? Both pump the same volume, but the left pumps against about 100 mmHg (aorta) and the right against about 15 mmHg (pulmonary artery). Same volume against higher pressure means more work. That is why the left ventricular wall is so much thicker. ( → Introduction, §4) 6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop Figure 01. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work Stroke work ≈ loop area ESV EDV Ventricular volume → Pressure Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Ejection Filling Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume. Follow the loop round: Bottom: the ventricle fills at low pressure (volume rises). Right side: it contracts with all valves shut, so pressure rises at constant volume. Top: it ejects (volume falls at high pressure). Left side: it relaxes with all valves shut, so pressure falls at constant volume. The area inside the loop is the stroke work. A taller loop (higher pressure) or a wider loop (bigger SV) means more work. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §8) The loop shows only the outside work of pushing blood. The muscle also uses energy to pump calcium, keep ion gradients and hold tension, so stroke work is not the same as the heart's total oxygen use. 7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand? The lecture lists three main things that set how much oxygen the heart muscle uses: Wall tension, which rises with the pressure the ventricle must develop Contractility: a stronger beat costs more energy Heart rate: more beats per minute Pressure work costs more oxygen than volume work. Raising pressure means building and holding high wall tension, which is expensive. Pumping more volume at the same pressure costs less. The lecture's examples: Aortic stenosis (pressure load): the ventricle must generate very high pressure to force blood through a narrow valve, so oxygen demand is high. Aortic regurgitation (volume load): the ventricle pumps a bigger volume each beat, but at near-normal pressure, so oxygen demand rises less. These are general patterns. Individual patients vary with severity and coronary supply. Why must coronary flow rise in exercise? Even at rest, the heart muscle extracts most of the oxygen from its blood (about 70%). There is little left to take, so when demand rises, the only way to get more oxygen is more coronary flow. If narrowed coronaries can't deliver it, the muscle runs short of oxygen and chest pain (angina) results. ( → Coronary Circulation) Careful: Fick uses the whole body's oxygen use to measure cardiac output. The heart's own oxygen use is a separate, much smaller quantity. Understanding Check: Sections 4–7 1. In dye dilution, what does a low, short concentration curve downstream tell you? A. Flow is fast, so the dye is diluted and washed past quickly B. Flow is slow, so the dye stays in the vessel for longer C. The dye was injected into the wrong vessel entirely D. The heart rate is too slow for the method to work Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Fast flow spreads the same dose through more blood and carries it past quickly, giving a lower, shorter curve. 2. The two ventricles eject the same volume. Why does the left do more work? A. It ejects blood more slowly than the right does B. It pumps against a much higher pressure C. It has to pump blood through the lungs D. Its valves are smaller, so it ejects less Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure. The same SV against about 100 mmHg instead of about 15 mmHg means far more work. 3. On a pressure–volume loop, what does the enclosed area represent? A. The heart's total oxygen use B. The stroke volume C. The external stroke work of one beat D. The time for one cardiac cycle Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The area is the outside work of pushing blood against pressure. Total oxygen use is larger, and SV is the loop's width. 4. Which places the greatest oxygen demand on the left ventricle? A. Pumping a larger volume at normal pressure B. A slower heart rate with normal pressure C. A smaller stroke volume at normal pressure D. Pumping a normal volume against a very high pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: D. High pressure means high wall tension, the most oxygen-expensive kind of work. That is why aortic stenosis raises demand more than regurgitation. 5. Why must coronary blood flow rise when the heart works harder? A. Heart muscle already takes out most of the oxygen it receives B. Coronary arteries narrow during exercise C. Blood carries less oxygen during exercise D. Heart rate falls, so each beat needs more flow Show answer and explanation Answer: A. With extraction already near its maximum, the only way to get more oxygen is to deliver more blood. 8. Chapter Essentials Fick's principle: oxygen used per minute = blood flow × oxygen dropped off per mL. So CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂). The lecture's example: 250 ÷ (0.20 − 0.15) = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min. Use content, not saturation: content depends on haemoglobin. Samples: arterial from any artery; mixed venous from the pulmonary artery. Fick needs a steady state and accurate samples. Indicator dilution (supplementary): a known dose of dye or cold saline, measured downstream. Faster flow gives a lower, shorter curve. Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure. On a pressure–volume loop it is the area inside. The left ventricle does more work than the right: same volume, higher pressure. Heart muscle oxygen demand rises with wall tension, contractility and heart rate. Pressure work costs more than volume work. The heart already extracts most of its oxygen, so more demand needs more coronary flow."},{"slug":"hemodynamics","title":"Blood Flow and Hemodynamics","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"55-74","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-propels-blood-around-the-circulation","title":"1. What propels blood around the circulation?"},{"id":"2-what-actually-determines-flow","title":"2. What actually determines flow?"},{"id":"3-why-do-different-vessels-have-different-roles","title":"3. Why do different vessels have different roles?"},{"id":"4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries","title":"4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries?"},{"id":"5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number","title":"5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number"},{"id":"6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow","title":"6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow?"},{"id":"7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells","title":"7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells"},{"id":"8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels","title":"8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels?"},{"id":"9-how-can-blood-flow-be-measured","title":"9. How can blood flow be measured?"},{"id":"10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries","title":"10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries?"},{"id":"11-chapter-essentials","title":"11. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Blood Flow and Haemodynamics</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-propels-blood-around-the-circulation\">1. What propels blood around the circulation?</h2>\n<p><strong>The heart creates pressure, and blood flows from high pressure to low.</strong> Each beat pushes blood into the arteries at high pressure. The pressure falls step by step through <strong>arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins</strong>, back to the heart at nearly zero.</p>\n<p>Three helpers keep blood moving:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elastic recoil of large arteries.</strong> In systole the aorta stretches like a balloon. In diastole it springs back and keeps pushing blood forward, so flow continues between beats.</li>\n<li><strong>The skeletal muscle pump.</strong> Contracting muscles squeeze the veins. <strong><span data-term=\"venous-valves\">Valves</span></strong> in the veins let blood go only towards the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>The respiratory pump.</strong> Breathing in lowers pressure in the chest and draws blood into the large veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These help, but the <strong>heart is the main pressure source</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-actually-determines-flow\">2. What actually determines flow?</h2>\n<p><strong>Flow depends on the pressure <em>difference</em>, not the pressure.</strong> Picture two water tanks joined by a pipe. Water flows only if one tank is higher than the other, and faster the bigger the difference. Raise both tanks equally and nothing changes.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Pressure, flow and resistance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = (ΔP) / (R) R = (ΔP) / (Q)\">Q = (ΔP) / (R) R = (ΔP) / (Q)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance.</span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q</strong> = flow (volume per minute)</li>\n<li><strong>ΔP</strong> = pressure at the start minus pressure at the end</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong> = resistance: how hard the vessels make it for blood to pass</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> a pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L gives 80 ÷ 20 = <strong>4 L/min</strong>.</p>\n<p>So <strong>high pressure alone doesn't mean high flow.</strong> A blocked artery can have high pressure upstream and almost no flow through it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-do-different-vessels-have-different-roles\">3. Why do different vessels have different roles?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Vessel</th>\n  <th>What its wall is like</th>\n  <th>Its job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Large arteries</strong></td>\n  <td>Thick and elastic</td>\n  <td>Stretch in systole, recoil in diastole (keep flow going)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arterioles</strong></td>\n  <td>Thick smooth-muscle layer</td>\n  <td>Control resistance, and so flow into each organ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Capillaries</strong></td>\n  <td>One cell thick</td>\n  <td>Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Veins</strong></td>\n  <td>Thin, stretchy, with valves</td>\n  <td>Hold most of the blood; return it to the heart</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Arterioles are &quot;resistance vessels&quot;</strong> because their muscle can narrow or widen them quickly, and a small change in radius changes resistance hugely (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow\">§6</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Veins are &quot;capacitance vessels&quot;</strong> (<em>capacity</em> = how much they hold) because they stretch easily. They can take in a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure. When sympathetic nerves tighten them, that stored blood is pushed back to the heart (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries\">§10</a>).</p>\n<p>Capillary structure and lymph are covered in their own chapters. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation\">→ Microcirculation</a>)</em> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/lymphatics\">→ Lymphatics</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One slide gives capillary gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres to micrometres wide.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries\">4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries?</h2>\n<p><strong>Flow and velocity are different things.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flow (Q)</strong> = <strong>how much</strong> blood passes per minute (L/min).</li>\n<li><strong>Velocity (v)</strong> = <strong>how fast</strong> it moves (cm/s).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Continuity relation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = vA v = (Q) / (A)\">Q = vA v = (Q) / (A)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>A = the total cross-sectional area of all the vessels at that level.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Think of a motorway.</strong> The same number of cars per minute has to pass every point. Where the road is one lane, the cars must go fast. Where it splits into hundreds of lanes, each car can go slowly.</p>\n<p>Each capillary is tiny, but there are billions of them side by side. Their <strong>total</strong> area is far bigger than the aorta's, so blood moves through them <strong>most slowly</strong>. This gives time for exchange. Blood speeds up again as the capillaries join into fewer, larger veins.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does blood keep flowing in the arteries during ventricular diastole?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles keep ejecting a little blood in diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The stretched aorta recoils and keeps pushing blood forward</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Valves in the arteries pump blood towards the tissues</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The veins pull blood out of the arteries by suction</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The aorta stretches in systole and springs back in diastole, keeping the pressure up and the blood moving.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Pressure falls by 90 mmHg across a vascular bed with a resistance of 30 mmHg·min/L. What is the flow?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 0.3 L/min, from 30 ÷ 90</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 3 L/min, from 90 ÷ 30</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 60 L/min, from 90 − 30</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 2,700 L/min, from 90 × 30</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Q = ΔP ÷ R = 90 ÷ 30 = 3 L/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Each capillary is very narrow, yet blood moves slowest in capillaries. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillary walls create a lot of friction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Capillaries have the highest blood pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Their total cross-sectional area is enormous</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Red cells block the capillaries completely</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> v = Q ÷ A. The same flow spread across a huge total area moves slowly, like traffic spread across many lanes.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why are veins called capacitance vessels?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They can hold a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their muscle controls resistance to blood flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They are the main site of oxygen exchange</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They have the highest pressure in the circulation</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Veins stretch easily, so they store large volumes at low pressure. Arterioles are the resistance vessels.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"laminar-flow\">Laminar flow</span></strong> means blood moves in smooth layers (<em>lamina</em> = thin layer). The layer touching the wall is held back by friction and moves slowest. Each layer inward slides faster, and the centre is fastest.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure hemo-flow-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 540 165\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An idealised vessel with multiple flow arrows, the longest arrows at the centre and shortest nearest the vessel wall.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"34\" width=\"465\" height=\"99\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9f1ff\" stroke=\"#8caccb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g stroke=\"#2162be\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M89 49H179m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 72H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 96H446m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 118H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"41\" y=\"22\">Near the wall: slow</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"22\">Near centre: fast</text><text x=\"41\" y=\"153\">Original schematic — not a measured flow profile</text></g></svg><p>The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Laminar flow is silent.</strong> When flow becomes <strong><span data-term=\"turbulent-flow\">turbulent</span></strong>, swirling in eddies, it makes <strong>noise</strong>: murmurs in the heart, bruits in arteries. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=6-what-makes-a-murmur\">→ Heart Sounds, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>What makes turbulence more likely?</strong> The <strong><span data-term=\"reynolds\">Reynolds number (Re)</span></strong> sums it up:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Reynolds number</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Re = (ρvD) / (η)\">Re = (ρvD) / (η)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity.</span></div>\n\n<p>Turbulence is more likely when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>velocity (v) is high</strong>, as through a narrowed valve or artery;</li>\n<li><strong>the vessel is wide (D)</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>viscosity (η) is low</strong>, as in anaemia, where thinner blood flows more easily.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A high Re makes turbulence <strong>more likely</strong>, not certain. Branching and sudden narrowing also matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Don't confuse R with Re.</strong> R is resistance; Re is the Reynolds number, a pure number with no units.</p>\n<p><strong>Two examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anaemia:</strong> thinner blood (lower viscosity) and a faster circulation can cause a <strong>flow murmur</strong> with normal valves.</li>\n<li><strong>A narrowed artery:</strong> blood speeds up through the narrow part and becomes turbulent beyond it, causing a <strong><span data-term=\"bruit\">bruit</span></strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow\">6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow?</h2>\n<p>The <strong><span data-term=\"poiseuille\">Poiseuille equation</span></strong> describes smooth flow through a straight tube:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Hagen–Poiseuille equation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = (πΔPr^4) / (8ηL)\">Q = (πΔPr^4) / (8ηL)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity.</span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ideal tube resistance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"R = (8ηL) / (πr^4)\">R = (8ηL) / (πr^4)</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The key fact: resistance depends on radius to the fourth power (R ∝ 1/r⁴).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What does &quot;to the fourth power&quot; mean?</strong> Multiply the change by itself four times. Halve the radius, and resistance rises by <strong>2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 times</strong>. So with the same pressure difference, flow falls to <strong>1/16</strong>. Anyone who has tried to drink through a very thin straw knows the feeling.</p>\n<div class=\"hemo-radius-impact\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Effect of radius on ideal vessel resistance\"><div><strong>Radius halved</strong><span>Resistance ×16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16</small></div><div><strong>Radius doubled</strong><span>Resistance ÷16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold</small></div></div>\n\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"radius-dial\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Radius dial</figcaption><p>A slider sets the vessel's radius from 50% to 150% of normal, and the vessel drawing narrows or widens. It shows the radius change, r⁴ worked out step by step, resistance (×) and flow (% of normal, at the same pressure difference), with flow bars for normal and new. It starts at 100%, and presets mark 80% (flow falls to about 41%) and 50% (about 6%). One line: \"A 20% narrowing more than halves flow.\"</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> arterioles control flow by changing their radius only slightly. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow. This is how the body directs blood to the organs that need it (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">§8</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Caution:</strong> Poiseuille assumes a rigid, straight tube and a simple fluid. Blood vessels stretch and branch, and blood contains cells, so the numbers are a guide, not exact predictions.</p>\n<p><strong>A fast jet doesn't mean more flow.</strong> Blood speeds up through a narrowing (v = Q ÷ A), yet the narrowing <strong>raises resistance</strong>, so total flow can <strong>fall</strong>. Speed and flow are different things.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 5–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which change makes turbulent flow most likely?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Lower velocity and higher blood viscosity</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Higher velocity and lower blood viscosity</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A narrower vessel with slower flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Thicker blood in a smooth, straight vessel</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Re = ρvD ÷ η. Faster flow and thinner blood both raise Re.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A vessel's radius is halved, with pressure, length and viscosity unchanged. What happens to flow?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It halves</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls to a quarter</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It falls to an eighth</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls to a sixteenth</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> R ∝ 1/r⁴. Half the radius gives 2⁴ = 16 times the resistance, so flow falls to 1/16.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can anaemia cause a heart murmur with normal valves?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Thinner blood and faster flow make turbulence more likely</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Thicker blood increases friction against the valves</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Low oxygen damages the valve leaflets directly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The red cells clump together and strike the valves</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Lower viscosity and a higher cardiac output both raise the Reynolds number, so flow across normal valves can become turbulent.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Blood speeds up as it passes through a narrowed artery. Has total flow through the artery increased?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Yes, because faster blood always means more flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Yes, because the narrowing lowers resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. No: speed rises because the area falls, while flow may drop</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. No: blood actually slows down in a narrowing</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> v = Q ÷ A, so speed rises where area falls. The narrowing also raises resistance, so total flow can fall.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells\">7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells</h2>\n<p><strong>Viscosity</strong> is how thick or sticky a fluid is: honey is more viscous than water. Thicker blood flows less easily, so resistance rises.</p>\n<p>Blood viscosity depends mainly on the <strong><span data-term=\"haematocrit\">haematocrit</span></strong>, the percentage of blood made up of red cells. It also depends on plasma proteins, temperature and how flexible the red cells are.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polycythaemia</strong> (too many red cells): thicker blood → more resistance → more work for the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>Anaemia</strong> (too few red cells): thinner blood, but each mL carries less oxygen, so the heart pumps more blood to compensate. That also means more work, for a different reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Stiff or misshapen red cells</strong> (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis) flow less easily through small vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One slide suggests viscosity has little effect in small vessels. In very narrow vessels, red cells line up in the centre and blood behaves as if it were thinner. This is a real effect, but it depends on vessel size and does not hold everywhere.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels?</h2>\n<p>Vessels are arranged in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"in-series\">In series</span></strong> (one after another, like the arteries then arterioles then capillaries): the resistances <strong>add up</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"in-parallel\">In parallel</span></strong> (side by side, like the many capillaries in a bed, or the organs off the aorta): each extra path <strong>lowers</strong> the total resistance, because blood has more routes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>This solves a puzzle.</strong> Each capillary is narrower than an arteriole, so why aren't capillaries the main resistance? Because there are so many of them <strong>in parallel</strong> that their combined resistance is lower. The <strong>arterioles</strong>, with their muscular walls, are where the biggest pressure drop happens and where resistance can be <strong>adjusted</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>In practice:</strong> when an organ's arterioles constrict, its blood flow falls; when they dilate, its flow rises. The heart's output is the same, but it is shared out differently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-how-can-blood-flow-be-measured\">9. How can blood flow be measured?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Method</th>\n  <th>What it detects</th>\n  <th>How it gives flow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Electromagnetic flowmeter</strong></td>\n  <td>Voltage produced by blood moving through a magnetic field</td>\n  <td>Voltage rises with flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Doppler ultrasound</strong></td>\n  <td>The change in pitch of sound bouncing off moving red cells</td>\n  <td>Gives <strong>velocity</strong>; vessel area is needed to get flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Plethysmography</strong></td>\n  <td>Swelling of a limb when venous outflow is briefly blocked</td>\n  <td>The rate of swelling equals the arterial inflow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Doppler measures speed, not volume.</strong> To get flow, multiply velocity by the vessel's cross-sectional area (Q = vA).</p>\n<p>The lecture also gives an <strong>arm-to-tongue circulation time of about 15 seconds</strong>: a substance injected into an arm vein is tasted on the tongue about 15 s later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries\">10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries?</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"compliance\">Compliance</span></strong> is how much a vessel's volume increases for each rise in pressure:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Compliance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)\">C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)</span></div>\n\n<p>Veins are far more compliant than arteries: they balloon out with little pressure. So <strong>most of the blood volume sits in the veins</strong> (about two-thirds at rest), ready to be called on.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does tightening the veins help cardiac output?</strong> Sympathetic nerves make the veins less stretchy. The stored blood is squeezed towards the heart, so venous return, EDV and, through Frank–Starling, stroke volume all rise. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">→ Cardiac Output, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 7–10</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> How do polycythaemia and anaemia both increase the heart's work?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both make the blood thicker and harder to pump</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Polycythaemia thickens blood; anaemia makes the heart pump more blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Both lower blood pressure, so the heart beats faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Polycythaemia thins blood; anaemia thickens it</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Thicker blood raises resistance. Anaemic blood is thinner, but carries less oxygen, so cardiac output must rise.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Capillaries are narrower than arterioles. Why are arterioles still the main resistance vessels?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillaries have no walls, so they offer no resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Arterioles are longer than all the capillaries put together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Capillaries are in parallel in huge numbers, and arterioles can adjust their radius</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood flows faster through capillaries than through arterioles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> So many parallel capillaries share the flow that their total resistance is low. Arterioles have muscle to change their radius.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> What does Doppler ultrasound measure directly?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The volume of blood flowing per minute</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The pressure difference along the vessel</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The speed of the moving blood cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The viscosity of the blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Doppler measures velocity from the pitch change. Flow needs the vessel's area as well (Q = vA).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Sympathetic nerves tighten the veins. What happens to stroke volume?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because more blood returns to fill the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls, because resistance to flow increases</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It is unchanged, because veins don't affect the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls, because the heart beats faster</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Less stretchy veins push stored blood to the heart. More filling means more stroke volume (Frank–Starling).</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"11-chapter-essentials\">11. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Flow follows the pressure difference:</strong> Q = ΔP ÷ R. High pressure alone doesn't mean high flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Elastic arteries</strong> recoil in diastole and keep blood moving between beats.</li>\n<li><strong>Vessel roles:</strong> elastic arteries (recoil), arterioles (resistance), capillaries (exchange), veins (capacity).</li>\n<li><strong>Velocity ≠ flow:</strong> v = Q ÷ A. The capillaries' huge total area makes blood slowest there.</li>\n<li><strong>Laminar flow</strong> is silent and fastest at the centre. <strong>Turbulent flow</strong> is noisy (murmurs, bruits).</li>\n<li><strong>Reynolds number</strong> Re = ρvD ÷ η. High velocity, wide vessels and thin blood favour turbulence. Re is not resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>R ∝ 1/r⁴:</strong> halve the radius, and resistance rises 16-fold. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Viscosity</strong> rises with haematocrit. Polycythaemia and anaemia both raise cardiac work, by different routes.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterioles</strong> are the adjustable resistance. Parallel paths lower total resistance; series resistances add.</li>\n<li><strong>Doppler</strong> measures velocity; flow = velocity × area.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance</strong> C = ΔV ÷ ΔP. Veins hold most of the blood, and tightening them raises venous return and stroke volume.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Blood Flow and Haemodynamics 1. What propels blood around the circulation? The heart creates pressure, and blood flows from high pressure to low. Each beat pushes blood into the arteries at high pressure. The pressure falls step by step through arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins, back to the heart at nearly zero. Three helpers keep blood moving: Elastic recoil of large arteries. In systole the aorta stretches like a balloon. In diastole it springs back and keeps pushing blood forward, so flow continues between beats. The skeletal muscle pump. Contracting muscles squeeze the veins. Valves in the veins let blood go only towards the heart. The respiratory pump. Breathing in lowers pressure in the chest and draws blood into the large veins. These help, but the heart is the main pressure source. 2. What actually determines flow? Flow depends on the pressure difference, not the pressure. Picture two water tanks joined by a pipe. Water flows only if one tank is higher than the other, and faster the bigger the difference. Raise both tanks equally and nothing changes. Pressure, flow and resistance Q = (ΔP) / (R) R = (ΔP) / (Q) Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance. Q = flow (volume per minute) ΔP = pressure at the start minus pressure at the end R = resistance: how hard the vessels make it for blood to pass Example: a pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L gives 80 ÷ 20 = 4 L/min. So high pressure alone doesn't mean high flow. A blocked artery can have high pressure upstream and almost no flow through it. 3. Why do different vessels have different roles? Vessel What its wall is like Its job Large arteries Thick and elastic Stretch in systole, recoil in diastole (keep flow going) Arterioles Thick smooth-muscle layer Control resistance, and so flow into each organ Capillaries One cell thick Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid Veins Thin, stretchy, with valves Hold most of the blood; return it to the heart Arterioles are \"resistance vessels\" because their muscle can narrow or widen them quickly, and a small change in radius changes resistance hugely ( §6). Veins are \"capacitance vessels\" ( capacity = how much they hold) because they stretch easily. They can take in a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure. When sympathetic nerves tighten them, that stored blood is pushed back to the heart ( §10). Capillary structure and lymph are covered in their own chapters. ( → Microcirculation) ( → Lymphatics) Source note: One slide gives capillary gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres to micrometres wide. 4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries? Flow and velocity are different things. Flow (Q) = how much blood passes per minute (L/min). Velocity (v) = how fast it moves (cm/s). Continuity relation Q = vA v = (Q) / (A) A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered. A = the total cross-sectional area of all the vessels at that level. Think of a motorway. The same number of cars per minute has to pass every point. Where the road is one lane, the cars must go fast. Where it splits into hundreds of lanes, each car can go slowly. Each capillary is tiny, but there are billions of them side by side. Their total area is far bigger than the aorta's, so blood moves through them most slowly. This gives time for exchange. Blood speeds up again as the capillaries join into fewer, larger veins. Understanding Check: Sections 1–4 1. Why does blood keep flowing in the arteries during ventricular diastole? A. The ventricles keep ejecting a little blood in diastole B. The stretched aorta recoils and keeps pushing blood forward C. Valves in the arteries pump blood towards the tissues D. The veins pull blood out of the arteries by suction Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The aorta stretches in systole and springs back in diastole, keeping the pressure up and the blood moving. 2. Pressure falls by 90 mmHg across a vascular bed with a resistance of 30 mmHg·min/L. What is the flow? A. 0.3 L/min, from 30 ÷ 90 B. 3 L/min, from 90 ÷ 30 C. 60 L/min, from 90 − 30 D. 2,700 L/min, from 90 × 30 Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Q = ΔP ÷ R = 90 ÷ 30 = 3 L/min. 3. Each capillary is very narrow, yet blood moves slowest in capillaries. Why? A. Capillary walls create a lot of friction B. Capillaries have the highest blood pressure C. Their total cross-sectional area is enormous D. Red cells block the capillaries completely Show answer and explanation Answer: C. v = Q ÷ A. The same flow spread across a huge total area moves slowly, like traffic spread across many lanes. 4. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? A. They can hold a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure B. Their muscle controls resistance to blood flow C. They are the main site of oxygen exchange D. They have the highest pressure in the circulation Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Veins stretch easily, so they store large volumes at low pressure. Arterioles are the resistance vessels. 5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number Laminar flow means blood moves in smooth layers ( lamina = thin layer). The layer touching the wall is held back by friction and moves slowest. Each layer inward slides faster, and the centre is fastest. Figure 01. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile Near the wall: slow Near centre: fast Original schematic — not a measured flow profile The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic. Laminar flow is silent. When flow becomes turbulent, swirling in eddies, it makes noise: murmurs in the heart, bruits in arteries. ( → Heart Sounds, §6) What makes turbulence more likely? The Reynolds number (Re) sums it up: Reynolds number Re = (ρvD) / (η) ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity. Turbulence is more likely when: velocity (v) is high, as through a narrowed valve or artery; the vessel is wide (D); viscosity (η) is low, as in anaemia, where thinner blood flows more easily. A high Re makes turbulence more likely, not certain. Branching and sudden narrowing also matter. Don't confuse R with Re. R is resistance; Re is the Reynolds number, a pure number with no units. Two examples: Anaemia: thinner blood (lower viscosity) and a faster circulation can cause a flow murmur with normal valves. A narrowed artery: blood speeds up through the narrow part and becomes turbulent beyond it, causing a bruit. 6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow? The Poiseuille equation describes smooth flow through a straight tube: Hagen–Poiseuille equation Q = (πΔPr^4) / (8ηL) r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity. Ideal tube resistance R = (8ηL) / (πr^4) The key fact: resistance depends on radius to the fourth power (R ∝ 1/r⁴). What does \"to the fourth power\" mean? Multiply the change by itself four times. Halve the radius, and resistance rises by 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 times. So with the same pressure difference, flow falls to 1/16. Anyone who has tried to drink through a very thin straw knows the feeling. Radius halved Resistance ×16 At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16 Radius doubled Resistance ÷16 At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold Radius dial A slider sets the vessel's radius from 50% to 150% of normal, and the vessel drawing narrows or widens. It shows the radius change, r⁴ worked out step by step, resistance (×) and flow (% of normal, at the same pressure difference), with flow bars for normal and new. It starts at 100%, and presets mark 80% (flow falls to about 41%) and 50% (about 6%). One line: \"A 20% narrowing more than halves flow.\" Why this matters: arterioles control flow by changing their radius only slightly. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow. This is how the body directs blood to the organs that need it ( §8). Caution: Poiseuille assumes a rigid, straight tube and a simple fluid. Blood vessels stretch and branch, and blood contains cells, so the numbers are a guide, not exact predictions. A fast jet doesn't mean more flow. Blood speeds up through a narrowing (v = Q ÷ A), yet the narrowing raises resistance, so total flow can fall. Speed and flow are different things. Understanding Check: Sections 5–6 1. Which change makes turbulent flow most likely? A. Lower velocity and higher blood viscosity B. Higher velocity and lower blood viscosity C. A narrower vessel with slower flow D. Thicker blood in a smooth, straight vessel Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Re = ρvD ÷ η. Faster flow and thinner blood both raise Re. 2. A vessel's radius is halved, with pressure, length and viscosity unchanged. What happens to flow? A. It halves B. It falls to a quarter C. It falls to an eighth D. It falls to a sixteenth Show answer and explanation Answer: D. R ∝ 1/r⁴. Half the radius gives 2⁴ = 16 times the resistance, so flow falls to 1/16. 3. Why can anaemia cause a heart murmur with normal valves? A. Thinner blood and faster flow make turbulence more likely B. Thicker blood increases friction against the valves C. Low oxygen damages the valve leaflets directly D. The red cells clump together and strike the valves Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Lower viscosity and a higher cardiac output both raise the Reynolds number, so flow across normal valves can become turbulent. 4. Blood speeds up as it passes through a narrowed artery. Has total flow through the artery increased? A. Yes, because faster blood always means more flow B. Yes, because the narrowing lowers resistance C. No: speed rises because the area falls, while flow may drop D. No: blood actually slows down in a narrowing Show answer and explanation Answer: C. v = Q ÷ A, so speed rises where area falls. The narrowing also raises resistance, so total flow can fall. 7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells Viscosity is how thick or sticky a fluid is: honey is more viscous than water. Thicker blood flows less easily, so resistance rises. Blood viscosity depends mainly on the haematocrit, the percentage of blood made up of red cells. It also depends on plasma proteins, temperature and how flexible the red cells are. Polycythaemia (too many red cells): thicker blood → more resistance → more work for the heart. Anaemia (too few red cells): thinner blood, but each mL carries less oxygen, so the heart pumps more blood to compensate. That also means more work, for a different reason. Stiff or misshapen red cells (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis) flow less easily through small vessels. Source note: One slide suggests viscosity has little effect in small vessels. In very narrow vessels, red cells line up in the centre and blood behaves as if it were thinner. This is a real effect, but it depends on vessel size and does not hold everywhere. 8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels? Vessels are arranged in two ways: In series (one after another, like the arteries then arterioles then capillaries): the resistances add up. In parallel (side by side, like the many capillaries in a bed, or the organs off the aorta): each extra path lowers the total resistance, because blood has more routes. This solves a puzzle. Each capillary is narrower than an arteriole, so why aren't capillaries the main resistance? Because there are so many of them in parallel that their combined resistance is lower. The arterioles, with their muscular walls, are where the biggest pressure drop happens and where resistance can be adjusted. In practice: when an organ's arterioles constrict, its blood flow falls; when they dilate, its flow rises. The heart's output is the same, but it is shared out differently. 9. How can blood flow be measured? Method What it detects How it gives flow Electromagnetic flowmeter Voltage produced by blood moving through a magnetic field Voltage rises with flow Doppler ultrasound The change in pitch of sound bouncing off moving red cells Gives velocity; vessel area is needed to get flow Plethysmography Swelling of a limb when venous outflow is briefly blocked The rate of swelling equals the arterial inflow Doppler measures speed, not volume. To get flow, multiply velocity by the vessel's cross-sectional area (Q = vA). The lecture also gives an arm-to-tongue circulation time of about 15 seconds: a substance injected into an arm vein is tasted on the tongue about 15 s later. 10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries? Compliance is how much a vessel's volume increases for each rise in pressure: Compliance C = (ΔV) / (ΔP) Veins are far more compliant than arteries: they balloon out with little pressure. So most of the blood volume sits in the veins (about two-thirds at rest), ready to be called on. Why does tightening the veins help cardiac output? Sympathetic nerves make the veins less stretchy. The stored blood is squeezed towards the heart, so venous return, EDV and, through Frank–Starling, stroke volume all rise. ( → Cardiac Output, §4) Understanding Check: Sections 7–10 1. How do polycythaemia and anaemia both increase the heart's work? A. Both make the blood thicker and harder to pump B. Polycythaemia thickens blood; anaemia makes the heart pump more blood C. Both lower blood pressure, so the heart beats faster D. Polycythaemia thins blood; anaemia thickens it Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Thicker blood raises resistance. Anaemic blood is thinner, but carries less oxygen, so cardiac output must rise. 2. Capillaries are narrower than arterioles. Why are arterioles still the main resistance vessels? A. Capillaries have no walls, so they offer no resistance B. Arterioles are longer than all the capillaries put together C. Capillaries are in parallel in huge numbers, and arterioles can adjust their radius D. Blood flows faster through capillaries than through arterioles Show answer and explanation Answer: C. So many parallel capillaries share the flow that their total resistance is low. Arterioles have muscle to change their radius. 3. What does Doppler ultrasound measure directly? A. The volume of blood flowing per minute B. The pressure difference along the vessel C. The speed of the moving blood cells D. The viscosity of the blood Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Doppler measures velocity from the pitch change. Flow needs the vessel's area as well (Q = vA). 4. Sympathetic nerves tighten the veins. What happens to stroke volume? A. It rises, because more blood returns to fill the heart B. It falls, because resistance to flow increases C. It is unchanged, because veins don't affect the heart D. It falls, because the heart beats faster Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Less stretchy veins push stored blood to the heart. More filling means more stroke volume (Frank–Starling). 11. Chapter Essentials Flow follows the pressure difference: Q = ΔP ÷ R. High pressure alone doesn't mean high flow. Elastic arteries recoil in diastole and keep blood moving between beats. Vessel roles: elastic arteries (recoil), arterioles (resistance), capillaries (exchange), veins (capacity). Velocity ≠ flow: v = Q ÷ A. The capillaries' huge total area makes blood slowest there. Laminar flow is silent and fastest at the centre. Turbulent flow is noisy (murmurs, bruits). Reynolds number Re = ρvD ÷ η. High velocity, wide vessels and thin blood favour turbulence. Re is not resistance. R ∝ 1/r⁴: halve the radius, and resistance rises 16-fold. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow. Viscosity rises with haematocrit. Polycythaemia and anaemia both raise cardiac work, by different routes. Arterioles are the adjustable resistance. Parallel paths lower total resistance; series resistances add. Doppler measures velocity; flow = velocity × area. Compliance C = ΔV ÷ ΔP. Veins hold most of the blood, and tightening them raises venous return and stroke volume."},{"slug":"blood-pressure","title":"Arterial Blood Pressure","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"76-83","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-exactly-is-arterial-blood-pressure","title":"1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure?"},{"id":"2-why-doesn-t-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole","title":"2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole?"},{"id":"3-why-do-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure-depend-on-more-than-heart-rate","title":"3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate?"},{"id":"4-what-is-pulse-pressure","title":"4. What is pulse pressure?"},{"id":"5-what-is-mean-arterial-pressure-and-why-isn-t-it-simply-the-midpoint","title":"5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint?"},{"id":"6-why-does-measured-bp-depend-on-whether-the-arm-is-above-or-below-the-heart","title":"6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart?"},{"id":"7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure","title":"7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure?"},{"id":"8-how-should-bp-actually-be-measured-accurately","title":"8. How should BP actually be measured accurately?"},{"id":"9-why-does-pressure-decrease-as-blood-travels-through-the-circulation","title":"9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation?"},{"id":"10-chapter-essentials","title":"10. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Blood Pressure</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-exactly-is-arterial-blood-pressure\">1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure?</h2>\n<p><strong>Blood pressure</strong> is the push of blood against the artery walls. It rises and falls with every heartbeat, so we report two numbers:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Systolic (SBP)</strong></td>\n  <td>The <strong>highest</strong> pressure, reached as the ventricle ejects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Diastolic (DBP)</strong></td>\n  <td>The <strong>lowest</strong> pressure, just before the next ejection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>120/70 mmHg</strong> means systolic 120, diastolic 70. These are the lecture's example values. Diagnosing high blood pressure uses clinical guidelines, not one fixed number.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does pressure swing up and down?</strong> Each ejection pushes extra blood into the arteries, so pressure rises. Between beats, blood keeps draining into the small vessels, so pressure falls until the next beat.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-doesn-t-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole\">2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole?</h2>\n<p>In diastole the aortic valve is shut and no blood is being ejected, yet blood keeps flowing through the arteries. The reason is the <strong><span data-term=\"windkessel\">Windkessel effect</span></strong> (German for &quot;air chamber&quot;, after the air-filled chamber old fire pumps used to smooth out their spurts):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Systole:</strong> blood is ejected faster than it can drain away, so the elastic aorta <strong>stretches</strong>, storing some of the energy.</li>\n<li><strong>Diastole:</strong> the aorta <strong>springs back</strong>, pushing the stored blood onwards.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure bp-windkessel-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Windkessel mechanism — elastic arterial energy storage and recoil</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two original arterial diagrams: during systole the large artery widens as blood enters and the wall expands; during diastole the artery recoils and sustains forward flow with the aortic valve closed.\"><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\"><text x=\"15\" y=\"21\">Systole</text><text x=\"15\" y=\"105\">Diastole</text></g><g stroke=\"#bd515a\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#fce1e0\"><path d=\"M124 42 C200 23 290 23 357 42 L475 42 L475 81 L357 81 C290 100 200 100 124 81 Z\"/><path d=\"M124 118 C219 112 290 112 357 118 L475 118 L475 143 L357 143 C290 149 219 149 124 143 Z\"/></g><g stroke=\"#2162be\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M147 60H447m-13 -8 13 8 -13 8\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M147 131H447m-13 -8 13 8 -13 8\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11.5\"><text x=\"488\" y=\"64\">Flow →</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"137\">Flow →</text><text x=\"213\" y=\"34\">Artery expands</text><text x=\"213\" y=\"160\">Artery recoils</text></g></svg><p>Original schematic showing that arterial elasticity converts intermittent ventricular ejection into a more continuous downstream flow. Not drawn to scale.</p></figure>\n\n<p>So flow is <strong>smooth and continuous</strong> in the tissues even though the heart pumps in spurts. Pressure still falls in diastole; the recoil just slows the fall.</p>\n<p><strong>A stiff aorta can't stretch much.</strong> The same stroke volume then pushes systolic pressure higher, and with less recoil, diastolic pressure falls lower. That is why older people often have high systolic pressure with a <strong>wide <span data-term=\"pulse-pressure\">pulse pressure</span></strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=1-what-is-an-arterial-pulse\">→ Pulses and JVP, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-do-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure-depend-on-more-than-heart-rate\">3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate?</h2>\n<p>Blood pressure is set by <strong>how much blood the heart pumps</strong> and <strong>how hard it is for that blood to drain away</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Mean pressure and flow</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR\">MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR</span><span class=\"equation-example\">MAP = mean arterial pressure; CVP = central venous pressure; SVR = systemic vascular resistance.</span></div>\n\n<p>Because CVP is near zero, this is usually written <strong>MAP ≈ CO × <span data-term=\"svr\">SVR</span></strong> (systemic vascular resistance). It is the same rule as Q = ΔP ÷ R, rearranged. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=2-what-actually-determines-flow\">→ Haemodynamics, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<p>So pressure rises if <strong>cardiac output</strong> rises or if the <strong>arterioles tighten</strong> (SVR rises). Heart rate alone doesn't tell you what BP will do.</p>\n<p><strong>Blood pressure is not the same as blood flow.</strong> After a bleed, the arterioles tighten hard (high SVR), which can hold BP near normal even while cardiac output, and tissue blood flow, are falling. A normal BP doesn't prove the tissues are getting enough blood. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does blood keep flowing through the arteries in diastole, with the aortic valve shut?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricle keeps ejecting a small amount in diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The aorta stretched in systole recoils and pushes blood onwards</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The veins draw blood out of the arteries by suction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The aortic valve leaks slightly in every healthy heart</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The Windkessel effect: energy stored in the stretched aortic wall is released as it springs back.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> An older person's aorta has become stiff. What happens to their pulse pressure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It widens, because systolic rises and diastolic falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It narrows, because the aorta can't stretch</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It stays the same, because stroke volume is unchanged</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls to zero, because there is no recoil</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A stiff aorta can't absorb the stroke volume, so systolic rises. With less recoil, diastolic falls. The gap between them widens.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient who has lost a lot of blood has a BP of 110/80 but cold, poorly perfused hands. How can BP be near normal?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood loss raises cardiac output, keeping pressure up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Arterioles tighten (SVR rises), holding pressure up as flow falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart pumps more blood with each beat after a bleed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pressure and flow always change together, so the flow must be normal</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> MAP ≈ CO × SVR. A large rise in SVR can hold MAP up even while CO, and tissue flow, falls.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-what-is-pulse-pressure\">4. What is pulse pressure?</h2>\n<p><strong>Pulse pressure</strong> is the difference between the two numbers:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Pulse pressure</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"PP = SBP − DBP\">PP = SBP − DBP</span><span class=\"equation-example\">For 120/70 mmHg: <strong>PP = 50 mmHg</strong>.</span></div>\n\n<p>It is the size of the pressure rise with each beat, and it is what you feel as the strength of the pulse. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=2-what-do-we-assess-when-palpating-a-pulse\">→ Pulses and JVP, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Two things widen it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A bigger stroke volume</strong>: more blood pushed in per beat.</li>\n<li><strong>A stiffer aorta</strong>: less stretch, so the same volume raises pressure more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So a wide pulse pressure doesn't by itself prove a big stroke volume. It may be a stiff aorta.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-is-mean-arterial-pressure-and-why-isn-t-it-simply-the-midpoint\">5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint?</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"map\">Mean arterial pressure (MAP)</span></strong> is the <strong>average pressure over a whole beat</strong>. It is the pressure that actually drives blood into the tissues.</p>\n<p><strong>Why isn't it halfway between 120 and 70?</strong> At a resting heart rate, the heart spends about <strong>one-third of each beat in systole</strong> and <strong>two-thirds in diastole</strong>. The pressure sits near diastolic for longer, so the average is pulled towards it.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Approximate mean arterial pressure</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"MAP ≈ DBP + (1) / (3)(SBP − DBP)\">MAP ≈ DBP + (1) / (3)(SBP − DBP)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">At ordinary resting heart rates: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>For 120/70:</strong> pulse pressure = 50, and ⅓ × 50 ≈ 17, so MAP ≈ 70 + 17 = <strong>about 87 mmHg</strong>, lower than the midpoint of 95.</p>\n<p><strong>This is an estimate.</strong> At fast heart rates diastole shortens, the beat is closer to half systole and half diastole, and the ⅓ rule becomes less accurate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-does-measured-bp-depend-on-whether-the-arm-is-above-or-below-the-heart\">6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart?</h2>\n<p>Blood is a column of fluid, so <strong>gravity adds pressure below the heart and takes it away above</strong>. For blood, the change is about <strong>0.77 mmHg per centimetre</strong> of height. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/gravity\">→ Gravity</a>)</em></p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Hydrostatic pressure change</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"ΔP = ρgh\">ΔP = ρgh</span><span class=\"equation-example\">ρ = fluid density; g = gravitational acceleration; h = vertical height difference.</span></div>\n\n<div class=\"bp-height-rules\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Arm height measurement bias\"><div><strong>Arm 20 cm below heart</strong><span>About +15–16 mmHg</span><small>Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading higher</small></div><div><strong>Arm 20 cm above heart</strong><span>About −15–16 mmHg</span><small>Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading lower</small></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> measure with the cuff <strong>at heart level</strong>. Rest the arm on a support: if the patient holds it up with their own muscles, the effort itself raises BP.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A BP is 130/70 mmHg. What is the approximate mean arterial pressure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 60 mmHg, from 130 − 70</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 90 mmHg, from 70 + ⅓ × 60</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 100 mmHg, from the midpoint of 130 and 70</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 110 mmHg, from 130 − ⅓ × 60</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> PP = 60 mmHg; MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓PP = 70 + 20 = 90 mmHg.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is MAP closer to diastolic than to systolic pressure at rest?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Diastolic pressure is the more important number clinically</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The heart spends about two-thirds of each beat in diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Systolic pressure is measured less accurately</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The aorta's recoil raises diastolic pressure to match systolic</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> MAP is a time average. More time is spent at the lower, diastolic pressure, so the average is pulled towards it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> The cuff is on an arm hanging 20 cm below heart level. How will the reading be affected?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. About 15 mmHg too low</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Unchanged, because gravity doesn't affect arteries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. About 15 mmHg too high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. About 77 mmHg too high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> About 0.77 mmHg per cm × 20 cm ≈ 15 mmHg added by gravity below the heart.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Two people have the same stroke volume, but one has a wider pulse pressure. What is the most likely reason?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Their aorta is stiffer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their heart rate is slower</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Their arterioles are more dilated</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Their blood is thinner</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> With SV the same, a stiffer aorta stretches less, so the same volume raises pressure more and the pulse pressure widens.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure\">7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure?</h2>\n<p>The cuff squeezes the brachial artery in the upper arm. A stethoscope over the artery, just below the cuff, listens for <strong><span data-term=\"korotkoff\">Korotkoff sounds</span></strong>: taps made by blood forcing its way through the squeezed artery. <strong>The whole method is a contest: is the cuff squeezing harder than the blood is pushing?</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. Cuff above systolic (say 150; BP 120/70).</strong> The cuff squeezes harder than even the peak of each beat. The artery stays <strong>shut</strong>. No blood gets through, so there is <strong>silence</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Cuff falls just below systolic (119).</strong> At the peak of each beat, blood pressure briefly beats the cuff. A spurt of blood <strong>forces the artery open</strong> and makes a <strong>tap</strong>. <strong>The first tap (Korotkoff phase I) = systolic pressure.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3. Cuff between systolic and diastolic (100).</strong> Each beat forces the artery open for part of the beat, and it is squeezed shut again for the rest. The taps continue and may change in quality as the cuff falls.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Cuff falls below diastolic (69).</strong> Now even the lowest pressure in each beat is higher than the cuff. The artery <strong>stays open</strong> all the time, flow is smooth again, and the sounds <strong>stop</strong>. <strong>Silence (Korotkoff phase V) = diastolic pressure</strong> in adults. In some situations, such as pregnancy or children, the point where the sounds <strong>muffle</strong> (phase IV) is used instead.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Cuff pressure</th>\n  <th>The artery under the cuff</th>\n  <th>What you hear</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Above systolic</strong></td>\n  <td>Shut all the time</td>\n  <td>Silence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Just below systolic</strong></td>\n  <td>Forced open at each peak</td>\n  <td><strong>First tap = systolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Between the two</strong></td>\n  <td>Opens and closes with each beat</td>\n  <td>Taps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Below diastolic</strong></td>\n  <td>Open all the time</td>\n  <td><strong>Silence = diastolic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"cuff-simulator\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Cuff simulator</figcaption><p>A patient with BP 120/70 at 70 beats/min. Press Deflate, or drag the cuff gauge, to lower cuff pressure from 160 to 50 mmHg at about 2–3 mmHg per beat. A drawing shows the artery under the cuff shut, spurting open with each beat, or open. Taps play in time with each beat only between 120 and 70 (sound can be switched off). Markers drop at the first tap (\"systolic: 120\") and the last (\"diastolic: 70\"), with one line underneath saying which of the four stages you are in.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>These are not the heart sounds.</strong> Korotkoff sounds are made <strong>in the squeezed brachial artery</strong>, not by heart valves. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds\">→ Heart Sounds, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"8-how-should-bp-actually-be-measured-accurately\">8. How should BP actually be measured accurately?</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Rest first:</strong> about five minutes, seated, back supported, feet flat, not talking.</li>\n<li><strong>Right cuff, right place:</strong> the correct cuff size on a bare upper arm, supported <strong>at heart level</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Feel first:</strong> inflate while feeling the radial pulse. Note where it disappears; that is roughly the systolic pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Then listen:</strong> inflate about 20–30 mmHg above that point. Deflate <strong>slowly</strong>, about 2 mmHg per second, listening over the brachial artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Record:</strong> the first tap (systolic) and the point where the sounds disappear (diastolic).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why feel first?</strong> In some people the taps fade out for a while between systolic and diastolic (an <strong><span data-term=\"auscultatory-gap\">auscultatory gap</span></strong>). If you only listened, you might start below the real systolic pressure and miss it.</p>\n<p><strong>Feeling alone gives systolic only.</strong> The radial pulse returns when the cuff falls below systolic, but nothing you can feel marks diastolic.</p>\n<p><strong>Common errors:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Error</th>\n  <th>Reading will be</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Cuff too small</strong> for the arm</td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>high</strong>: a narrow cuff needs extra pressure to squeeze the artery shut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Cuff too large</strong></td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>low</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Arm <strong>below</strong> heart level</td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>high</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Arm <strong>above</strong> heart level</td>\n  <td>Falsely <strong>low</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Talking, arm unsupported, just exercised</td>\n  <td>Usually falsely <strong>high</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Cuff size goes by <strong>arm circumference</strong>: large arms need large cuffs, and children need child-sized cuffs.</p>\n<p><strong>Automatic machines</strong> (<span data-term=\"oscillometric\">oscillometric</span> monitors) don't listen for Korotkoff sounds. They sense pressure wobbles in the cuff and calculate the BP.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture covers auscultation, palpation, direct arterial measurement and cuff size. Resting time, deflation rate and the auscultatory gap are standard clinical guidance (American Heart Association).</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-why-does-pressure-decrease-as-blood-travels-through-the-circulation\">9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation?</h2>\n<p>Pressure is used up as blood pushes through resistance, just as Q = ΔP ÷ R predicts. The <strong>biggest drop is across the arterioles</strong>, the main resistance vessels. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">→ Haemodynamics, §8</a>)</em></p>\n<div class=\"bp-drop-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Main locations of systemic pressure decrease\"><span><strong>Aorta / arteries</strong><small>High pressure · strong pulses</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Arterioles</strong><small>Large pressure drop</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Capillaries / veins</strong><small>Lower pressure · damped pulses</small></span></div>\n\n<p>The <strong>pulse also fades</strong> along the way. The elastic arteries and the arteriolar resistance smooth out the beat-to-beat swings, so blood reaches the capillaries at a <strong>low, steady</strong> pressure. That protects the thin capillary walls.</p>\n<p>Because the arterioles hold back so much pressure, tightening them does two things at once: it <strong>lowers flow</strong> to the tissue beyond and <strong>raises pressure</strong> in the arteries before them.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 7–9</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> As the cuff deflates, what causes the first Korotkoff tap?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The aortic valve closing at the end of systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A spurt of blood forcing open the squeezed artery at the peak of the beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The cuff releasing air through its valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood flowing smoothly through a fully open artery</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> When cuff pressure falls just below systolic, each pressure peak forces the artery open, and the turbulent spurt makes a tap.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why do the sounds disappear once the cuff is below diastolic pressure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The artery is shut all the time, so no blood flows</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The heart's valves stop making noise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The artery stays open all the time, so flow is smooth again</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The stethoscope can't hear pressures that low</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Below diastolic, even the lowest pressure in each beat holds the artery open, so flow is no longer squeezed and turbulent.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A large man's BP is taken with a standard cuff that is too small for his arm. How will the reading be affected?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Falsely high, because extra pressure is needed to squeeze the artery shut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Falsely low, because the narrow cuff can't occlude the artery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Accurate, because cuff size only matters for children</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Accurate for systolic but falsely low for diastolic</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A narrow cuff passes its pressure poorly to the artery, so a higher cuff pressure is needed to close it, and both readings come out too high.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why can feeling the radial pulse give systolic but not diastolic pressure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The radial pulse disappears completely at diastolic pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The pulse returns at systolic, but nothing you can feel marks diastolic</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The radial artery is too small to carry a pulse below systolic</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Diastolic pressure can only be measured inside the artery</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> As the cuff falls below systolic, the pulse comes back, but it simply stays felt. There is no event at diastolic that you can feel.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Where does mean blood pressure fall the most?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Across the aorta and large arteries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Across the arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Across the capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Across the veins</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The arterioles are the main resistance vessels, so most of the pressure is used up there. This also smooths out the pulse before blood reaches the capillaries.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-chapter-essentials\">10. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Systolic</strong> = the peak pressure in a beat; <strong>diastolic</strong> = the lowest, just before the next beat.</li>\n<li><strong>Windkessel:</strong> the aorta stretches in systole and recoils in diastole, keeping blood moving between beats. A stiff aorta raises systolic and widens pulse pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR.</strong> BP depends on output and resistance, not heart rate alone. <strong>Normal BP ≠ normal flow.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pulse pressure</strong> = SBP − DBP. It widens with a bigger stroke volume or a stiffer aorta.</li>\n<li><strong>MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure</strong> (120/70 → about 87), closer to diastolic because diastole lasts longer.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity:</strong> about 0.77 mmHg per cm. Measure with the cuff at heart level.</li>\n<li><strong>Korotkoff:</strong> silence (cuff above systolic) → <strong>first tap = systolic</strong> → taps → <strong>silence = diastolic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The taps</strong> come from blood forcing through the squeezed brachial artery, not from the heart valves.</li>\n<li><strong>Feel, then listen:</strong> palpation gives systolic only and helps avoid the auscultatory gap.</li>\n<li><strong>Cuff too small → reads high; too large → reads low.</strong> Arm below the heart → reads high; above → reads low.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure falls most across the arterioles</strong>, which also smooth out the pulse.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Arterial Blood Pressure 1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure? Blood pressure is the push of blood against the artery walls. It rises and falls with every heartbeat, so we report two numbers: What it is Systolic (SBP) The highest pressure, reached as the ventricle ejects Diastolic (DBP) The lowest pressure, just before the next ejection 120/70 mmHg means systolic 120, diastolic 70. These are the lecture's example values. Diagnosing high blood pressure uses clinical guidelines, not one fixed number. Why does pressure swing up and down? Each ejection pushes extra blood into the arteries, so pressure rises. Between beats, blood keeps draining into the small vessels, so pressure falls until the next beat. 2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole? In diastole the aortic valve is shut and no blood is being ejected, yet blood keeps flowing through the arteries. The reason is the Windkessel effect (German for \"air chamber\", after the air-filled chamber old fire pumps used to smooth out their spurts): Systole: blood is ejected faster than it can drain away, so the elastic aorta stretches, storing some of the energy. Diastole: the aorta springs back, pushing the stored blood onwards. Figure 01. Windkessel mechanism — elastic arterial energy storage and recoil Systole Diastole Flow → Flow → Artery expands Artery recoils Original schematic showing that arterial elasticity converts intermittent ventricular ejection into a more continuous downstream flow. Not drawn to scale. So flow is smooth and continuous in the tissues even though the heart pumps in spurts. Pressure still falls in diastole; the recoil just slows the fall. A stiff aorta can't stretch much. The same stroke volume then pushes systolic pressure higher, and with less recoil, diastolic pressure falls lower. That is why older people often have high systolic pressure with a wide pulse pressure. ( → Pulses and JVP, §1) 3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate? Blood pressure is set by how much blood the heart pumps and how hard it is for that blood to drain away: Mean pressure and flow MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR MAP = mean arterial pressure; CVP = central venous pressure; SVR = systemic vascular resistance. Because CVP is near zero, this is usually written MAP ≈ CO × SVR (systemic vascular resistance). It is the same rule as Q = ΔP ÷ R, rearranged. ( → Haemodynamics, §2) So pressure rises if cardiac output rises or if the arterioles tighten (SVR rises). Heart rate alone doesn't tell you what BP will do. Blood pressure is not the same as blood flow. After a bleed, the arterioles tighten hard (high SVR), which can hold BP near normal even while cardiac output, and tissue blood flow, are falling. A normal BP doesn't prove the tissues are getting enough blood. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why does blood keep flowing through the arteries in diastole, with the aortic valve shut? A. The ventricle keeps ejecting a small amount in diastole B. The aorta stretched in systole recoils and pushes blood onwards C. The veins draw blood out of the arteries by suction D. The aortic valve leaks slightly in every healthy heart Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The Windkessel effect: energy stored in the stretched aortic wall is released as it springs back. 2. An older person's aorta has become stiff. What happens to their pulse pressure? A. It widens, because systolic rises and diastolic falls B. It narrows, because the aorta can't stretch C. It stays the same, because stroke volume is unchanged D. It falls to zero, because there is no recoil Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A stiff aorta can't absorb the stroke volume, so systolic rises. With less recoil, diastolic falls. The gap between them widens. 3. A patient who has lost a lot of blood has a BP of 110/80 but cold, poorly perfused hands. How can BP be near normal? A. Blood loss raises cardiac output, keeping pressure up B. Arterioles tighten (SVR rises), holding pressure up as flow falls C. The heart pumps more blood with each beat after a bleed D. Pressure and flow always change together, so the flow must be normal Show answer and explanation Answer: B. MAP ≈ CO × SVR. A large rise in SVR can hold MAP up even while CO, and tissue flow, falls. 4. What is pulse pressure? Pulse pressure is the difference between the two numbers: Pulse pressure PP = SBP − DBP For 120/70 mmHg: PP = 50 mmHg. It is the size of the pressure rise with each beat, and it is what you feel as the strength of the pulse. ( → Pulses and JVP, §2) Two things widen it: A bigger stroke volume: more blood pushed in per beat. A stiffer aorta: less stretch, so the same volume raises pressure more. So a wide pulse pressure doesn't by itself prove a big stroke volume. It may be a stiff aorta. 5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint? Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is the average pressure over a whole beat. It is the pressure that actually drives blood into the tissues. Why isn't it halfway between 120 and 70? At a resting heart rate, the heart spends about one-third of each beat in systole and two-thirds in diastole. The pressure sits near diastolic for longer, so the average is pulled towards it. Approximate mean arterial pressure MAP ≈ DBP + (1) / (3)(SBP − DBP) At ordinary resting heart rates: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure. For 120/70: pulse pressure = 50, and ⅓ × 50 ≈ 17, so MAP ≈ 70 + 17 = about 87 mmHg, lower than the midpoint of 95. This is an estimate. At fast heart rates diastole shortens, the beat is closer to half systole and half diastole, and the ⅓ rule becomes less accurate. 6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart? Blood is a column of fluid, so gravity adds pressure below the heart and takes it away above. For blood, the change is about 0.77 mmHg per centimetre of height. ( → Gravity) Hydrostatic pressure change ΔP = ρgh ρ = fluid density; g = gravitational acceleration; h = vertical height difference. Arm 20 cm below heart About +15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading higher Arm 20 cm above heart About −15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading lower Rule: measure with the cuff at heart level. Rest the arm on a support: if the patient holds it up with their own muscles, the effort itself raises BP. Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. A BP is 130/70 mmHg. What is the approximate mean arterial pressure? A. 60 mmHg, from 130 − 70 B. 90 mmHg, from 70 + ⅓ × 60 C. 100 mmHg, from the midpoint of 130 and 70 D. 110 mmHg, from 130 − ⅓ × 60 Show answer and explanation Answer: B. PP = 60 mmHg; MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓PP = 70 + 20 = 90 mmHg. 2. Why is MAP closer to diastolic than to systolic pressure at rest? A. Diastolic pressure is the more important number clinically B. The heart spends about two-thirds of each beat in diastole C. Systolic pressure is measured less accurately D. The aorta's recoil raises diastolic pressure to match systolic Show answer and explanation Answer: B. MAP is a time average. More time is spent at the lower, diastolic pressure, so the average is pulled towards it. 3. The cuff is on an arm hanging 20 cm below heart level. How will the reading be affected? A. About 15 mmHg too low B. Unchanged, because gravity doesn't affect arteries C. About 15 mmHg too high D. About 77 mmHg too high Show answer and explanation Answer: C. About 0.77 mmHg per cm × 20 cm ≈ 15 mmHg added by gravity below the heart. 4. Two people have the same stroke volume, but one has a wider pulse pressure. What is the most likely reason? A. Their aorta is stiffer B. Their heart rate is slower C. Their arterioles are more dilated D. Their blood is thinner Show answer and explanation Answer: A. With SV the same, a stiffer aorta stretches less, so the same volume raises pressure more and the pulse pressure widens. 7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure? The cuff squeezes the brachial artery in the upper arm. A stethoscope over the artery, just below the cuff, listens for Korotkoff sounds: taps made by blood forcing its way through the squeezed artery. The whole method is a contest: is the cuff squeezing harder than the blood is pushing? 1. Cuff above systolic (say 150; BP 120/70). The cuff squeezes harder than even the peak of each beat. The artery stays shut. No blood gets through, so there is silence. 2. Cuff falls just below systolic (119). At the peak of each beat, blood pressure briefly beats the cuff. A spurt of blood forces the artery open and makes a tap. The first tap (Korotkoff phase I) = systolic pressure. 3. Cuff between systolic and diastolic (100). Each beat forces the artery open for part of the beat, and it is squeezed shut again for the rest. The taps continue and may change in quality as the cuff falls. 4. Cuff falls below diastolic (69). Now even the lowest pressure in each beat is higher than the cuff. The artery stays open all the time, flow is smooth again, and the sounds stop. Silence (Korotkoff phase V) = diastolic pressure in adults. In some situations, such as pregnancy or children, the point where the sounds muffle (phase IV) is used instead. Cuff pressure The artery under the cuff What you hear Above systolic Shut all the time Silence Just below systolic Forced open at each peak First tap = systolic Between the two Opens and closes with each beat Taps Below diastolic Open all the time Silence = diastolic Cuff simulator A patient with BP 120/70 at 70 beats/min. Press Deflate, or drag the cuff gauge, to lower cuff pressure from 160 to 50 mmHg at about 2–3 mmHg per beat. A drawing shows the artery under the cuff shut, spurting open with each beat, or open. Taps play in time with each beat only between 120 and 70 (sound can be switched off). Markers drop at the first tap (\"systolic: 120\") and the last (\"diastolic: 70\"), with one line underneath saying which of the four stages you are in. These are not the heart sounds. Korotkoff sounds are made in the squeezed brachial artery, not by heart valves. ( → Heart Sounds, §1) 8. How should BP actually be measured accurately? Rest first: about five minutes, seated, back supported, feet flat, not talking. Right cuff, right place: the correct cuff size on a bare upper arm, supported at heart level. Feel first: inflate while feeling the radial pulse. Note where it disappears; that is roughly the systolic pressure. Then listen: inflate about 20–30 mmHg above that point. Deflate slowly, about 2 mmHg per second, listening over the brachial artery. Record: the first tap (systolic) and the point where the sounds disappear (diastolic). Why feel first? In some people the taps fade out for a while between systolic and diastolic (an auscultatory gap). If you only listened, you might start below the real systolic pressure and miss it. Feeling alone gives systolic only. The radial pulse returns when the cuff falls below systolic, but nothing you can feel marks diastolic. Common errors: Error Reading will be Cuff too small for the arm Falsely high: a narrow cuff needs extra pressure to squeeze the artery shut Cuff too large Falsely low Arm below heart level Falsely high Arm above heart level Falsely low Talking, arm unsupported, just exercised Usually falsely high Cuff size goes by arm circumference: large arms need large cuffs, and children need child-sized cuffs. Automatic machines ( oscillometric monitors) don't listen for Korotkoff sounds. They sense pressure wobbles in the cuff and calculate the BP. Source note: The lecture covers auscultation, palpation, direct arterial measurement and cuff size. Resting time, deflation rate and the auscultatory gap are standard clinical guidance (American Heart Association). 9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation? Pressure is used up as blood pushes through resistance, just as Q = ΔP ÷ R predicts. The biggest drop is across the arterioles, the main resistance vessels. ( → Haemodynamics, §8) Aorta / arteries High pressure · strong pulses → Arterioles Large pressure drop → Capillaries / veins Lower pressure · damped pulses The pulse also fades along the way. The elastic arteries and the arteriolar resistance smooth out the beat-to-beat swings, so blood reaches the capillaries at a low, steady pressure. That protects the thin capillary walls. Because the arterioles hold back so much pressure, tightening them does two things at once: it lowers flow to the tissue beyond and raises pressure in the arteries before them. Understanding Check: Sections 7–9 1. As the cuff deflates, what causes the first Korotkoff tap? A. The aortic valve closing at the end of systole B. A spurt of blood forcing open the squeezed artery at the peak of the beat C. The cuff releasing air through its valve D. Blood flowing smoothly through a fully open artery Show answer and explanation Answer: B. When cuff pressure falls just below systolic, each pressure peak forces the artery open, and the turbulent spurt makes a tap. 2. Why do the sounds disappear once the cuff is below diastolic pressure? A. The artery is shut all the time, so no blood flows B. The heart's valves stop making noise C. The artery stays open all the time, so flow is smooth again D. The stethoscope can't hear pressures that low Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Below diastolic, even the lowest pressure in each beat holds the artery open, so flow is no longer squeezed and turbulent. 3. A large man's BP is taken with a standard cuff that is too small for his arm. How will the reading be affected? A. Falsely high, because extra pressure is needed to squeeze the artery shut B. Falsely low, because the narrow cuff can't occlude the artery C. Accurate, because cuff size only matters for children D. Accurate for systolic but falsely low for diastolic Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A narrow cuff passes its pressure poorly to the artery, so a higher cuff pressure is needed to close it, and both readings come out too high. 4. Why can feeling the radial pulse give systolic but not diastolic pressure? A. The radial pulse disappears completely at diastolic pressure B. The pulse returns at systolic, but nothing you can feel marks diastolic C. The radial artery is too small to carry a pulse below systolic D. Diastolic pressure can only be measured inside the artery Show answer and explanation Answer: B. As the cuff falls below systolic, the pulse comes back, but it simply stays felt. There is no event at diastolic that you can feel. 5. Where does mean blood pressure fall the most? A. Across the aorta and large arteries B. Across the arterioles C. Across the capillaries D. Across the veins Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The arterioles are the main resistance vessels, so most of the pressure is used up there. This also smooths out the pulse before blood reaches the capillaries. 10. Chapter Essentials Systolic = the peak pressure in a beat; diastolic = the lowest, just before the next beat. Windkessel: the aorta stretches in systole and recoils in diastole, keeping blood moving between beats. A stiff aorta raises systolic and widens pulse pressure. MAP ≈ CO × SVR. BP depends on output and resistance, not heart rate alone. Normal BP ≠ normal flow. Pulse pressure = SBP − DBP. It widens with a bigger stroke volume or a stiffer aorta. MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure (120/70 → about 87), closer to diastolic because diastole lasts longer. Gravity: about 0.77 mmHg per cm. Measure with the cuff at heart level. Korotkoff: silence (cuff above systolic) → first tap = systolic → taps → silence = diastolic. The taps come from blood forcing through the squeezed brachial artery, not from the heart valves. Feel, then listen: palpation gives systolic only and helps avoid the auscultatory gap. Cuff too small → reads high; too large → reads low. Arm below the heart → reads high; above → reads low. Pressure falls most across the arterioles, which also smooth out the pulse."},{"slug":"pulses","title":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"22-26","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-an-arterial-pulse","title":"1. What is an arterial pulse?"},{"id":"2-what-do-we-assess-when-palpating-a-pulse","title":"2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse?"},{"id":"3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck","title":"3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck?"},{"id":"4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents","title":"4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents?"},{"id":"5-how-do-you-distinguish-the-jvp-from-the-carotid-pulse","title":"5. How do you distinguish the JVP from the carotid pulse?"},{"id":"6-how-is-the-jvp-estimated-at-the-bedside","title":"6. How is the JVP estimated at the bedside?"},{"id":"7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important","title":"7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important?"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-an-arterial-pulse\">1. What is an arterial pulse?</h2>\n<p>Each time the left ventricle ejects, it pushes a fresh volume of blood into an aorta that is already full. The aortic wall stretches and the pressure inside it rises. That stretch and pressure rise travel down the arteries as a <strong><span data-term=\"pulse-wave\">wave</span></strong>, like a ripple running along a tight hose. When the wave passes under your finger at the wrist, the artery wall bulges briefly: that bulge is the <strong>pulse</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"pulse-mechanism\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"How an arterial pulse arises\"><span>LV ejection</span><b>→</b><span>Aortic pressure ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Arterial wall expands</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure wave travels</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The pulse is the wave, not the blood.</strong> As the lecture stresses, the pulse does not depend on how fast the blood is moving. The wave races ahead: about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, and faster further out. It reaches the wrist in roughly a tenth of a second. The blood itself moves far more slowly, well under 1 m/s on average in the aorta. So when you feel the pulse, the blood that left the heart in that beat is still nowhere near your wrist.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does the wave speed up?</strong> A stiff tube passes a pressure wave on faster than a stretchy one, because a stiff wall barely gives, so the pressure change is handed on quickly. The lecture gives two examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The wave gets faster further from the heart.</strong> The smaller arteries are stiffer than the stretchy aorta.</li>\n<li><strong>The wave gets faster with age.</strong> The arteries become more rigid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In both cases it is the wave that speeds up, not the blood.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Wave speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall): about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, 7–10 m/s in large branches, and faster in small arteries.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-what-do-we-assess-when-palpating-a-pulse\">2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse?</h2>\n<p>The lecture lists five features:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Feature</th>\n  <th>What you check</th>\n  <th>What it tells you</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Rate</strong></td>\n  <td>Beats per minute</td>\n  <td>How many beats are reaching the arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Volume</strong></td>\n  <td>How big the bulge under your finger is</td>\n  <td>Mainly the <span data-term=\"pulse-pressure\">pulse pressure</span>: the rise from diastolic to systolic pressure, which grows with <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Strength</strong></td>\n  <td>How firmly it pushes against your finger</td>\n  <td>How much pressure is in the artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Character</strong></td>\n  <td>The shape of each beat: how fast it rises and falls</td>\n  <td>How the left ventricle ejects, and how the aortic valve is working</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Location</strong></td>\n  <td>Which artery (radial, carotid, femoral…), and whether both sides match</td>\n  <td>A weak or missing pulse on one side points to a blockage on that side</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Whether the rhythm is <strong>regular</strong> is also worth noting.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can the wrist count fewer beats than the heart makes?</strong> In a very fast or irregular rhythm, some beats come so soon that the ventricle has hardly filled. Those beats eject too little blood to make a pulse you can feel. The pulse at the wrist can then be slower than the heart rate counted with a stethoscope. This difference is a <strong><span data-term=\"pulse-deficit\">pulse deficit</span></strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck\">3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck?</h2>\n<p>There is no valve between the right atrium and the large veins of the neck. So, as the lecture explains, pressure changes in the right atrium pass straight up into the neck veins, and you can see them. The <strong><span data-term=\"jvp\">internal jugular vein</span></strong> acts like a gauge attached to the right atrium: the higher the right atrial pressure, the higher up the neck the column of blood reaches.</p>\n<p><strong>Pressure moves; the blood doesn't have to.</strong> A pressure change travels up the column of blood even while the blood itself keeps flowing down towards the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can you see it but not feel it?</strong> Venous pressure is only a few mmHg, far too low to push against a finger. Even light pressure squashes the vein flat. What you see is a soft, flickering movement of the skin. The lecture notes that the JVP is not palpable.</p>\n<p><strong>Breathing changes it.</strong> Breathing in lowers the pressure inside the chest and draws blood into the heart, so the level in the neck <strong>falls on inspiration</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> the arterial pulse is the left ventricle's ejection wave. The JVP shows the right atrium's pressure changes.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"Z4yRBhlK0uY\" data-youtube-start=\"23\" data-media-title=\"Understanding Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP)\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO</span><h3>Understanding Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP)</h3><p>Zero to Finals. Starts at 0:23. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z4yRBhlK0uY&amp;t=23s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Zero to Finals. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> You feel the radial pulse about a tenth of a second after the left ventricle ejects. What has travelled to the wrist in that time?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The blood ejected in that beat, moving at about 8 m/s</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A pressure wave travelling along the artery walls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A nerve signal from the heart to the arterial muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Sound from the closing aortic valve, carried by the blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The ejection starts a pressure and stretch wave that travels along the arteries far faster than the blood itself moves. You feel the wave, not the blood.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does the pulse wave travel faster in an elderly person?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Their blood is thicker, so it carries pressure better</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their heart ejects blood at a higher speed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Their stiffer arteries pass the pressure change on faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Their arteries are wider, so blood meets less resistance</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A stiff wall hardly stretches, so it hands the pressure change on quickly. Arteries stiffen with age, so the wave speeds up, even though the blood does not.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can the JVP be seen but not felt?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Venous pressure is too low to push against a finger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The jugular vein lies too deep to be touched</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The JVP only appears when the patient breathes in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Venous blood flows away from the heart in the neck</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Pressure in the neck veins is only a few mmHg. Any pressure from a finger squashes the vein, so you can only see the movement.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents\">4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents?</h2>\n<p>In one heartbeat, the pressure in the right atrium rises three times (the <strong>a</strong>, <strong>c</strong> and <strong>v</strong> waves) and falls twice (the <strong>x</strong> and <strong>y</strong> descents). Each one follows from what the right atrium and the tricuspid valve are doing. They are the same atrial waves you meet on the <span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §9</a>)</em></p>\n<p>In order through one beat:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a wave: the right atrium contracts.</strong> At the end of ventricular diastole, just after the <span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span>, the atrium squeezes and its pressure rises. There is no valve between the atrium and the great veins, so, as the lecture notes, venous inflow briefly stops and a little blood is pushed back up the veins.</li>\n<li><strong>c wave: the closed tricuspid valve bulges back.</strong> As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve so it bulges back into the atrium. The atrium itself is not contracting.</li>\n<li><strong>x descent: the atrium relaxes and is stretched open.</strong> The atrium relaxes after its contraction. As the right ventricle ejects, it also pulls the tricuspid valve ring downwards, making the atrium bigger. Pressure falls.</li>\n<li><strong>v wave: the atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut.</strong> Blood keeps returning from the body throughout ventricular systole. With the tricuspid valve shut, it can only collect in the atrium, so pressure rises slowly. The atrium is not contracting.</li>\n<li><strong>y descent: the tricuspid valve opens.</strong> When right ventricular pressure falls below right atrial pressure, the valve opens and the stored blood rushes into the ventricle. Atrial pressure drops.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Wave</th>\n  <th>What causes it</th>\n  <th>When in the cycle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>a</strong></td>\n  <td>The atrium contracts</td>\n  <td>End of ventricular diastole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>c</strong></td>\n  <td>The closed tricuspid valve bulges back</td>\n  <td>Start of ventricular contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>x</strong></td>\n  <td>The atrium relaxes, and the valve ring is pulled down</td>\n  <td>Ventricular ejection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>v</strong></td>\n  <td>The atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut</td>\n  <td>Late ventricular systole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>y</strong></td>\n  <td>The tricuspid valve opens and the atrium empties</td>\n  <td>Early ventricular diastole</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>A memory hook:</strong> <strong>a</strong> for <strong>a</strong>trial contraction, <strong>c</strong> for the <strong>c</strong>losed valve bulging, <strong>v</strong> for <strong>v</strong>enous filling.</p>\n<div class=\"view-switch\">\n<figure class=\"learning-figure jvp-figure static-view\"><figcaption>JVP waves at a glance</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 245\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original schematic jugular venous pressure curve showing an a wave from atrial contraction, c wave during early ventricular systole, x descent from atrial relaxation, v wave during atrial filling, and y descent during tricuspid opening.\"><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"203\" x2=\"611\" y2=\"203\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\".5\"/><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"36\" y2=\"203\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\".5\"/><path d=\"M45 168 C75 166 93 88 120 76 C144 72 152 142 178 132 C195 129 199 104 214 111 C233 120 244 179 287 181 C340 185 355 101 407 91 C451 83 467 179 509 179 C543 181 558 167 595 166\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"800\"><text x=\"115\" y=\"58\">a</text><text x=\"207\" y=\"97\">c</text><text x=\"283\" y=\"195\">x</text><text x=\"405\" y=\"75\">v</text><text x=\"512\" y=\"195\">y</text></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"42\" y=\"18\">Right atrial pressure ↑</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"230\">Time →</text></g></svg><p>Original teaching schematic (not a patient tracing). The <strong>a, c and v</strong> are pressure rises; <strong>x and y</strong> are falls. Timing and actual prominence vary between patients.</p></figure>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"jvp-explorer\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>JVP wave explorer</figcaption><p>Replaces the static curve. It shows one JVP trace, with the P wave, QRS, S1 and S2 marked above it, and a small right heart beside it. Next/Back steps through a → c → x → v → y. At each step the matching part of the trace is highlighted, and the heart shows what is happening: the atrium contracting, relaxing or filling; the tricuspid valve shut, bulging or opening; blood moving or collecting. One plain sentence underneath matches the bullet above. Schematic, not a patient tracing.</p></figure>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"l_rXk8GflL0\" data-media-title=\"JVP waveform explained\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO</span><h3>JVP waveform explained</h3><p>Catherine Tsai, UCSF School of Medicine. An animated walk through the JVP waveform. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l_rXk8GflL0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Catherine Tsai. Written, illustrated, animated and voiced by Catherine Tsai, UCSF School of Medicine, Spring 2015, during her Medicine Sub-Internship at the SF VA Hospital. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"9otb9X8hEeo\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Cycle | Atrial Pressure | Part 3 | Jugular Venous Pulse | JVP | Cardiac Physiology\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Cycle | Atrial Pressure | Part 3 | Jugular Venous Pulse | JVP | Cardiac Physiology</h3><p>Byte Size Med. How the atrial pressure changes of the cardiac cycle produce the jugular venous pulse. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9otb9X8hEeo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Byte Size Med. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-you-distinguish-the-jvp-from-the-carotid-pulse\">5. How do you distinguish the JVP from the carotid pulse?</h2>\n<p>Both pulsations sit side by side in the neck, so they are easy to confuse.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Jugular venous pulse</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Carotid arterial pulse</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Can you feel it?</td>\n  <td>No: pressure is too low</td>\n  <td>Yes: a firm thrust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>What it looks like</td>\n  <td>A soft flicker with more than one movement per beat</td>\n  <td>One sharp outward thrust per beat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>When the patient sits up</td>\n  <td>The level falls</td>\n  <td>No change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>On breathing in</td>\n  <td>The level falls</td>\n  <td>No regular change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Light pressure at the base of the neck</td>\n  <td>It disappears (the vein is squashed)</td>\n  <td>It continues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>What it shows</td>\n  <td>Right atrial pressure</td>\n  <td>Left ventricular ejection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Confusing the two leads to wrong conclusions about right atrial pressure.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture states that the JVP is not palpable and falls during inspiration. The other bedside differences are standard clinical teaching.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which JVP wave appears while the right atrium is filling and the tricuspid valve is shut?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The a wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The c wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The v wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The y descent</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> During ventricular systole the tricuspid valve is shut, so returning blood collects in the atrium and its pressure rises slowly: the v wave. The atrium is not contracting.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What causes the c wave?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The right atrium contracting at the end of diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The shut tricuspid valve bulging back into the atrium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood rushing into the ventricle as the valve opens</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The atrium filling while the ventricle ejects</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve back towards the atrium, giving a small rise in atrial pressure. A is the a wave, C the y descent, and D the v wave.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> At what moment does the y descent begin?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. When the pulmonary valve opens</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. When the right atrium contracts</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. When the tricuspid valve closes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. When the tricuspid valve opens</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Once the tricuspid valve opens, the blood stored in the atrium pours into the ventricle and atrial pressure falls.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A neck pulsation can be felt easily, shows one outward thrust per beat, and doesn't change when the patient sits up. What is it?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The carotid pulse, from the left ventricle's ejection</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The JVP, raised high by right-sided heart failure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A giant v wave from tricuspid regurgitation</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The JVP, seen with the patient lying unusually flat</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A pulsation you can feel, with a single thrust and no change with position, is arterial. The JVP cannot be felt, has more than one movement per beat, and changes with posture and breathing.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-how-is-the-jvp-estimated-at-the-bedside\">6. How is the JVP estimated at the bedside?</h2>\n<p>The lecture's method:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Recline the patient at about 45°</strong>, with the head turned slightly to the left, and look at the right internal jugular vein.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjust the angle until you can see the top of the pulsating column.</strong> Sit the patient more upright if the neck veins are very full, or lie them flatter if the column is low.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the <span data-term=\"sternal-angle\">sternal angle</span></strong>, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, level with the second rib.</li>\n<li><strong>Measure the vertical height</strong> from the sternal angle up to the top of the column.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why measure vertically?</strong> The pressure in a column of fluid depends only on its vertical height, not on how long or slanted it is.</p>\n<p><strong>Why from the sternal angle?</strong> It sits about 5 cm above the middle of the right atrium in most positions, so it makes a fixed reference point.</p>\n<p><strong>Normal:</strong> the top of the column is no more than about <strong>3–4 cm</strong> above the sternal angle. Higher than that means raised right atrial pressure.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs11-jvp-measurement\">\n<img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/chapter11/jvp-measurement.jpg\" alt=\"An elderly patient reclined with the head turned to the left. A clear ruler stands upright on the sternal angle, and a thin metal pointer is held level from the top of the visible venous column in the neck across to the ruler, where the height is read.\" width=\"1110\" height=\"599\">\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Measuring the JVP at the bedside.</strong> (1) The pointer is held level with the top of the venous column. (2) A ruler stands vertically on the sternal angle. (3) Where the level pointer crosses the ruler gives the JVP height in cm.<details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Bedside photograph supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project; numbered labels added. Original source to be confirmed.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture gives the 45° position, sitting upright if very engorged, and measuring from the sternum. The normal value (no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle) and the 5 cm offset are standard clinical teaching; the offset varies between people.</aside>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"PecnLMSAAlw\" data-youtube-start=\"481\" data-media-title=\"Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) Examination - Clinical Skills - Dr Gill\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO</span><h3>Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) Examination - Clinical Skills - Dr Gill</h3><p>Dr James Gill. Examining the JVP at the bedside: internal versus external jugular veins, interpreting the height, and the hepatojugular reflux test. Starts at 8:01. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PecnLMSAAlw&amp;t=481s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Dr James Gill. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important\">7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important?</h2>\n<p>The lecture links three conditions to the JVP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right-sided heart failure: a raised JVP.</strong> The right heart cannot pump on all the blood returning to it. Pressure backs up into the right atrium and the neck veins, so the column sits high.</li>\n<li><strong>Tricuspid <span data-term=\"regurgitation\">regurgitation</span>: a large wave during systole.</strong> The leaky tricuspid valve lets the contracting right ventricle push blood back into the atrium, on top of the normal venous filling. The rise during systole becomes large. The lecture calls it a &quot;big c wave&quot;; because it joins the c and v waves, it is usually described as a giant <strong>v</strong> (or <strong>cv</strong>) wave.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete <span data-term=\"heart-block\">heart block</span>: giant (&quot;cannon&quot;) a waves.</strong> The atria and ventricles beat independently. Sometimes the atrium contracts while the tricuspid valve is shut, because the ventricle is contracting at the same moment. The blood cannot go forward, so the pressure spikes up the neck. This only happens on the beats where the timing collides, so the <span data-term=\"cannon-a-wave\">giant a waves</span> come and go. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §8</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration teaching-illustration-portrait\" id=\"cvs11-distended-neck-veins\">\n<img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/chapter11/distended-neck-veins.jpg\" alt=\"Side view of a woman lying almost flat with her face covered. A thick, swollen vein bulges along the side of her neck from above the collarbone up towards the angle of the jaw.\" width=\"625\" height=\"615\">\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. A very high JVP.</strong> Grossly distended neck veins in a patient lying almost flat. Right atrial pressure is so high that the venous column reaches the angle of the jaw. (Cause in this patient: cardiac tamponade, where fluid around the heart stops it filling.)<details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Photograph believed to be by Ferencga, Wikimedia Commons; supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project. Licence to be confirmed.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> a giant <strong>a</strong> wave is the <strong>atrium contracting against a shut valve</strong>. A giant <strong>v</strong> wave is the <strong>ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve</strong>.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture describes a \"big C wave\" in tricuspid incompetence. In practice, the regurgitant wave merges the c and v waves and is usually called a giant v or cv wave.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding Check</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why is the JVP height measured vertically from the sternal angle?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The sternal angle lies directly over the tricuspid valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The jugular vein runs straight up from the sternal angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pressure in a column of fluid depends on its vertical height</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Measuring along the neck would include the carotid pulse</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Only vertical height sets the pressure in a fluid column. The sternal angle is used because it sits a fairly constant distance above the right atrium.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient's JVP is 8 cm above the sternal angle, and their ankles are swollen. What does this most suggest?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. High right atrial pressure from right-sided heart failure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low right atrial pressure from blood loss</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A stiff aorta raising the speed of the pulse wave</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Tricuspid stenosis reducing the return of venous blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A column well above 3–4 cm means raised right atrial pressure. With swollen ankles, this fits a failing right heart that cannot pump on the blood returning to it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In complete heart block, giant \"a\" waves appear in the neck only now and then. Why only sometimes?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The atria contract only when the ventricles are relaxed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Only some atrial beats fall while the tricuspid valve is shut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The vagus nerve blocks every other atrial contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Giant a waves appear only during deep inspiration</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The atria and ventricles beat independently, so only now and then does an atrial contraction land while the ventricle is contracting and the tricuspid valve is shut. Those beats give the giant waves.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> How does the giant wave of tricuspid regurgitation differ from a giant (\"cannon\") a wave?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It comes from the atrium contracting against a shut valve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It appears only in diastole, while the tricuspid valve is open</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It can be felt as an outward thrust, like the carotid pulse</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It comes from the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> In tricuspid regurgitation the ventricle drives blood back into the atrium during systole. A cannon a wave is the atrium itself contracting against a shut valve.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The arterial pulse is a pressure wave</strong>, started by left ventricular ejection. It travels far faster than the blood itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Stiffer arteries carry the wave faster:</strong> further from the heart, and with age.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulse features (lecture):</strong> rate, volume, strength, character and location, plus rhythm. A pulse deficit means some beats are too weak to reach the wrist.</li>\n<li><strong>The JVP shows right atrial pressure</strong>, because no valve separates the right atrium from the neck veins. It can be seen but not felt, and it falls on inspiration.</li>\n<li><strong>a</strong> = the atrium contracts; <strong>c</strong> = the closed tricuspid valve bulges back; <strong>x</strong> = the atrium relaxes and the valve ring is pulled down; <strong>v</strong> = the atrium fills with the valve shut; <strong>y</strong> = the valve opens and the atrium empties.</li>\n<li><strong>JVP versus carotid:</strong> the JVP is not palpable, flickers more than once per beat, and changes with position, breathing and light pressure. The carotid is a single palpable thrust.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement:</strong> recline at about 45° (adjust as needed) and measure the vertical height above the sternal angle. Normal is no more than about 3–4 cm.</li>\n<li><strong>A raised JVP</strong> means high right atrial pressure, as in right-sided heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Giant v (cv) wave:</strong> tricuspid regurgitation, the ventricle pushing blood back.</li>\n<li><strong>Giant (cannon) a wave:</strong> complete heart block, the atrium contracting against a shut tricuspid valve.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse 1. What is an arterial pulse? Each time the left ventricle ejects, it pushes a fresh volume of blood into an aorta that is already full. The aortic wall stretches and the pressure inside it rises. That stretch and pressure rise travel down the arteries as a wave, like a ripple running along a tight hose. When the wave passes under your finger at the wrist, the artery wall bulges briefly: that bulge is the pulse. LV ejection → Aortic pressure ↑ → Arterial wall expands → Pressure wave travels The pulse is the wave, not the blood. As the lecture stresses, the pulse does not depend on how fast the blood is moving. The wave races ahead: about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, and faster further out. It reaches the wrist in roughly a tenth of a second. The blood itself moves far more slowly, well under 1 m/s on average in the aorta. So when you feel the pulse, the blood that left the heart in that beat is still nowhere near your wrist. Why does the wave speed up? A stiff tube passes a pressure wave on faster than a stretchy one, because a stiff wall barely gives, so the pressure change is handed on quickly. The lecture gives two examples: The wave gets faster further from the heart. The smaller arteries are stiffer than the stretchy aorta. The wave gets faster with age. The arteries become more rigid. In both cases it is the wave that speeds up, not the blood. Source note: Wave speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall): about 3–5 m/s in the aorta, 7–10 m/s in large branches, and faster in small arteries. 2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse? The lecture lists five features: Feature What you check What it tells you Rate Beats per minute How many beats are reaching the arteries Volume How big the bulge under your finger is Mainly the pulse pressure: the rise from diastolic to systolic pressure, which grows with stroke volume Strength How firmly it pushes against your finger How much pressure is in the artery Character The shape of each beat: how fast it rises and falls How the left ventricle ejects, and how the aortic valve is working Location Which artery (radial, carotid, femoral…), and whether both sides match A weak or missing pulse on one side points to a blockage on that side Whether the rhythm is regular is also worth noting. Why can the wrist count fewer beats than the heart makes? In a very fast or irregular rhythm, some beats come so soon that the ventricle has hardly filled. Those beats eject too little blood to make a pulse you can feel. The pulse at the wrist can then be slower than the heart rate counted with a stethoscope. This difference is a pulse deficit. 3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck? There is no valve between the right atrium and the large veins of the neck. So, as the lecture explains, pressure changes in the right atrium pass straight up into the neck veins, and you can see them. The internal jugular vein acts like a gauge attached to the right atrium: the higher the right atrial pressure, the higher up the neck the column of blood reaches. Pressure moves; the blood doesn't have to. A pressure change travels up the column of blood even while the blood itself keeps flowing down towards the heart. Why can you see it but not feel it? Venous pressure is only a few mmHg, far too low to push against a finger. Even light pressure squashes the vein flat. What you see is a soft, flickering movement of the skin. The lecture notes that the JVP is not palpable. Breathing changes it. Breathing in lowers the pressure inside the chest and draws blood into the heart, so the level in the neck falls on inspiration. Key distinction: the arterial pulse is the left ventricle's ejection wave. The JVP shows the right atrium's pressure changes. OPTIONAL VIDEO Understanding Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) Zero to Finals. Starts at 0:23. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Zero to Finals. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. You feel the radial pulse about a tenth of a second after the left ventricle ejects. What has travelled to the wrist in that time? A. The blood ejected in that beat, moving at about 8 m/s B. A pressure wave travelling along the artery walls C. A nerve signal from the heart to the arterial muscle D. Sound from the closing aortic valve, carried by the blood Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The ejection starts a pressure and stretch wave that travels along the arteries far faster than the blood itself moves. You feel the wave, not the blood. 2. Why does the pulse wave travel faster in an elderly person? A. Their blood is thicker, so it carries pressure better B. Their heart ejects blood at a higher speed C. Their stiffer arteries pass the pressure change on faster D. Their arteries are wider, so blood meets less resistance Show answer and explanation Answer: C. A stiff wall hardly stretches, so it hands the pressure change on quickly. Arteries stiffen with age, so the wave speeds up, even though the blood does not. 3. Why can the JVP be seen but not felt? A. Venous pressure is too low to push against a finger B. The jugular vein lies too deep to be touched C. The JVP only appears when the patient breathes in D. Venous blood flows away from the heart in the neck Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pressure in the neck veins is only a few mmHg. Any pressure from a finger squashes the vein, so you can only see the movement. 4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents? In one heartbeat, the pressure in the right atrium rises three times (the a, c and v waves) and falls twice (the x and y descents). Each one follows from what the right atrium and the tricuspid valve are doing. They are the same atrial waves you meet on the Wiggers diagram. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §9) In order through one beat: a wave: the right atrium contracts. At the end of ventricular diastole, just after the P wave, the atrium squeezes and its pressure rises. There is no valve between the atrium and the great veins, so, as the lecture notes, venous inflow briefly stops and a little blood is pushed back up the veins. c wave: the closed tricuspid valve bulges back. As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve so it bulges back into the atrium. The atrium itself is not contracting. x descent: the atrium relaxes and is stretched open. The atrium relaxes after its contraction. As the right ventricle ejects, it also pulls the tricuspid valve ring downwards, making the atrium bigger. Pressure falls. v wave: the atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut. Blood keeps returning from the body throughout ventricular systole. With the tricuspid valve shut, it can only collect in the atrium, so pressure rises slowly. The atrium is not contracting. y descent: the tricuspid valve opens. When right ventricular pressure falls below right atrial pressure, the valve opens and the stored blood rushes into the ventricle. Atrial pressure drops. Wave What causes it When in the cycle a The atrium contracts End of ventricular diastole c The closed tricuspid valve bulges back Start of ventricular contraction x The atrium relaxes, and the valve ring is pulled down Ventricular ejection v The atrium fills with the tricuspid valve shut Late ventricular systole y The tricuspid valve opens and the atrium empties Early ventricular diastole A memory hook: a for a trial contraction, c for the c losed valve bulging, v for v enous filling. JVP waves at a glance a c x v y Right atrial pressure ↑ Time → Original teaching schematic (not a patient tracing). The a, c and v are pressure rises; x and y are falls. Timing and actual prominence vary between patients. JVP wave explorer Replaces the static curve. It shows one JVP trace, with the P wave, QRS, S1 and S2 marked above it, and a small right heart beside it. Next/Back steps through a → c → x → v → y. At each step the matching part of the trace is highlighted, and the heart shows what is happening: the atrium contracting, relaxing or filling; the tricuspid valve shut, bulging or opening; blood moving or collecting. One plain sentence underneath matches the bullet above. Schematic, not a patient tracing. OPTIONAL VIDEO JVP waveform explained Catherine Tsai, UCSF School of Medicine. An animated walk through the JVP waveform. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Catherine Tsai. Written, illustrated, animated and voiced by Catherine Tsai, UCSF School of Medicine, Spring 2015, during her Medicine Sub-Internship at the SF VA Hospital. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. OPTIONAL VIDEO Cardiac Cycle | Atrial Pressure | Part 3 | Jugular Venous Pulse | JVP | Cardiac Physiology Byte Size Med. How the atrial pressure changes of the cardiac cycle produce the jugular venous pulse. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Byte Size Med. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. 5. How do you distinguish the JVP from the carotid pulse? Both pulsations sit side by side in the neck, so they are easy to confuse. Jugular venous pulse Carotid arterial pulse Can you feel it? No: pressure is too low Yes: a firm thrust What it looks like A soft flicker with more than one movement per beat One sharp outward thrust per beat When the patient sits up The level falls No change On breathing in The level falls No regular change Light pressure at the base of the neck It disappears (the vein is squashed) It continues What it shows Right atrial pressure Left ventricular ejection Confusing the two leads to wrong conclusions about right atrial pressure. Source note: The lecture states that the JVP is not palpable and falls during inspiration. The other bedside differences are standard clinical teaching. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. Which JVP wave appears while the right atrium is filling and the tricuspid valve is shut? A. The a wave B. The c wave C. The v wave D. The y descent Show answer and explanation Answer: C. During ventricular systole the tricuspid valve is shut, so returning blood collects in the atrium and its pressure rises slowly: the v wave. The atrium is not contracting. 2. What causes the c wave? A. The right atrium contracting at the end of diastole B. The shut tricuspid valve bulging back into the atrium C. Blood rushing into the ventricle as the valve opens D. The atrium filling while the ventricle ejects Show answer and explanation Answer: B. As the right ventricle starts contracting, its rising pressure pushes the closed tricuspid valve back towards the atrium, giving a small rise in atrial pressure. A is the a wave, C the y descent, and D the v wave. 3. At what moment does the y descent begin? A. When the pulmonary valve opens B. When the right atrium contracts C. When the tricuspid valve closes D. When the tricuspid valve opens Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Once the tricuspid valve opens, the blood stored in the atrium pours into the ventricle and atrial pressure falls. 4. A neck pulsation can be felt easily, shows one outward thrust per beat, and doesn't change when the patient sits up. What is it? A. The carotid pulse, from the left ventricle's ejection B. The JVP, raised high by right-sided heart failure C. A giant v wave from tricuspid regurgitation D. The JVP, seen with the patient lying unusually flat Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A pulsation you can feel, with a single thrust and no change with position, is arterial. The JVP cannot be felt, has more than one movement per beat, and changes with posture and breathing. 6. How is the JVP estimated at the bedside? The lecture's method: Recline the patient at about 45°, with the head turned slightly to the left, and look at the right internal jugular vein. Adjust the angle until you can see the top of the pulsating column. Sit the patient more upright if the neck veins are very full, or lie them flatter if the column is low. Find the sternal angle, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, level with the second rib. Measure the vertical height from the sternal angle up to the top of the column. Why measure vertically? The pressure in a column of fluid depends only on its vertical height, not on how long or slanted it is. Why from the sternal angle? It sits about 5 cm above the middle of the right atrium in most positions, so it makes a fixed reference point. Normal: the top of the column is no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle. Higher than that means raised right atrial pressure. Figure 01. Measuring the JVP at the bedside. (1) The pointer is held level with the top of the venous column. (2) A ruler stands vertically on the sternal angle. (3) Where the level pointer crosses the ruler gives the JVP height in cm. Image credit Bedside photograph supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project; numbered labels added. Original source to be confirmed. Source note: The lecture gives the 45° position, sitting upright if very engorged, and measuring from the sternum. The normal value (no more than about 3–4 cm above the sternal angle) and the 5 cm offset are standard clinical teaching; the offset varies between people. OPTIONAL VIDEO Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) Examination - Clinical Skills - Dr Gill Dr James Gill. Examining the JVP at the bedside: internal versus external jugular veins, interpreting the height, and the hepatojugular reflux test. Starts at 8:01. It loads only when tapped. Play video Open on YouTube © Dr James Gill. Shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation above does not depend on this video. 7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important? The lecture links three conditions to the JVP: Right-sided heart failure: a raised JVP. The right heart cannot pump on all the blood returning to it. Pressure backs up into the right atrium and the neck veins, so the column sits high. Tricuspid regurgitation: a large wave during systole. The leaky tricuspid valve lets the contracting right ventricle push blood back into the atrium, on top of the normal venous filling. The rise during systole becomes large. The lecture calls it a \"big c wave\"; because it joins the c and v waves, it is usually described as a giant v (or cv) wave. Complete heart block: giant (\"cannon\") a waves. The atria and ventricles beat independently. Sometimes the atrium contracts while the tricuspid valve is shut, because the ventricle is contracting at the same moment. The blood cannot go forward, so the pressure spikes up the neck. This only happens on the beats where the timing collides, so the giant a waves come and go. ( → Cardiac Conduction, §8) Figure 02. A very high JVP. Grossly distended neck veins in a patient lying almost flat. Right atrial pressure is so high that the venous column reaches the angle of the jaw. (Cause in this patient: cardiac tamponade, where fluid around the heart stops it filling.) Image credit Photograph believed to be by Ferencga, Wikimedia Commons; supplied by the DOMiNANCE Breakdowns project. Licence to be confirmed. Key distinction: a giant a wave is the atrium contracting against a shut valve. A giant v wave is the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve. Source note: The lecture describes a \"big C wave\" in tricuspid incompetence. In practice, the regurgitant wave merges the c and v waves and is usually called a giant v or cv wave. Understanding Check 1. Why is the JVP height measured vertically from the sternal angle? A. The sternal angle lies directly over the tricuspid valve B. The jugular vein runs straight up from the sternal angle C. Pressure in a column of fluid depends on its vertical height D. Measuring along the neck would include the carotid pulse Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Only vertical height sets the pressure in a fluid column. The sternal angle is used because it sits a fairly constant distance above the right atrium. 2. A patient's JVP is 8 cm above the sternal angle, and their ankles are swollen. What does this most suggest? A. High right atrial pressure from right-sided heart failure B. Low right atrial pressure from blood loss C. A stiff aorta raising the speed of the pulse wave D. Tricuspid stenosis reducing the return of venous blood Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A column well above 3–4 cm means raised right atrial pressure. With swollen ankles, this fits a failing right heart that cannot pump on the blood returning to it. 3. In complete heart block, giant \"a\" waves appear in the neck only now and then. Why only sometimes? A. The atria contract only when the ventricles are relaxed B. Only some atrial beats fall while the tricuspid valve is shut C. The vagus nerve blocks every other atrial contraction D. Giant a waves appear only during deep inspiration Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The atria and ventricles beat independently, so only now and then does an atrial contraction land while the ventricle is contracting and the tricuspid valve is shut. Those beats give the giant waves. 4. How does the giant wave of tricuspid regurgitation differ from a giant (\"cannon\") a wave? A. It comes from the atrium contracting against a shut valve B. It appears only in diastole, while the tricuspid valve is open C. It can be felt as an outward thrust, like the carotid pulse D. It comes from the ventricle pushing blood back through a leaky valve Show answer and explanation Answer: D. In tricuspid regurgitation the ventricle drives blood back into the atrium during systole. A cannon a wave is the atrium itself contracting against a shut valve. 8. Chapter Essentials The arterial pulse is a pressure wave, started by left ventricular ejection. It travels far faster than the blood itself. Stiffer arteries carry the wave faster: further from the heart, and with age. Pulse features (lecture): rate, volume, strength, character and location, plus rhythm. A pulse deficit means some beats are too weak to reach the wrist. The JVP shows right atrial pressure, because no valve separates the right atrium from the neck veins. It can be seen but not felt, and it falls on inspiration. a = the atrium contracts; c = the closed tricuspid valve bulges back; x = the atrium relaxes and the valve ring is pulled down; v = the atrium fills with the valve shut; y = the valve opens and the atrium empties. JVP versus carotid: the JVP is not palpable, flickers more than once per beat, and changes with position, breathing and light pressure. The carotid is a single palpable thrust. Measurement: recline at about 45° (adjust as needed) and measure the vertical height above the sternal angle. Normal is no more than about 3–4 cm. A raised JVP means high right atrial pressure, as in right-sided heart failure. Giant v (cv) wave: tricuspid regurgitation, the ventricle pushing blood back. Giant (cannon) a wave: complete heart block, the atrium contracting against a shut tricuspid valve."},{"slug":"venous-return","title":"Veins and Venous Return","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"43-44 and 73-75 and 97-98","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-venous-return-and-why-is-it-essential","title":"1. What is venous return, and why is it essential?"},{"id":"2-why-are-veins-called-capacitance-vessels","title":"2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels?"},{"id":"3-what-pressure-actually-drives-venous-return","title":"3. What pressure actually drives venous return?"},{"id":"4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work","title":"4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work?"},{"id":"5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return","title":"5. How does breathing help venous return?"},{"id":"6-what-does-central-venous-pressure-tell-us","title":"6. What does central venous pressure tell us?"},{"id":"7-what-happens-to-veins-in-the-head-when-standing","title":"7. What happens to veins in the head when standing?"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Veins and Venous Return</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-venous-return-and-why-is-it-essential\">1. What is venous return, and why is it essential?</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"venous-return\">Venous return</span></strong> is the volume of blood flowing <strong>back into the right atrium each minute</strong>. Over time it must equal cardiac output: the heart can only pump out what comes back to it. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">→ Cardiac Output, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>The heart can't pump blood it doesn't receive.</strong> Even a strong heart ejects little if it is poorly filled. More venous return fills the ventricles more, and through Frank–Starling they eject more. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">→ Cardiac Output, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<div class=\"vr-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Venous return to stroke volume\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Right-heart filling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stroke volume ↑</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why does the left side respond a few beats later?</strong> Extra returning blood enters the right heart first, then has to pass through the lungs before it reaches the left ventricle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-are-veins-called-capacitance-vessels\">2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels?</h2>\n<p>Veins have <strong>thin, stretchy walls</strong>. They can hold a lot of blood without much rise in pressure, like a half-filled balloon that takes more air easily. Arteries are stiffer, so the same added volume raises their pressure sharply.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Vascular compliance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)\">C = (ΔV) / (ΔP)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">The higher C of veins permits greater volume storage for a given pressure rise.</span></div>\n\n<p>So the veins are the body's <strong>blood reservoir</strong>: about half or more of the blood volume sits in the systemic veins at rest. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries\">→ Haemodynamics, §10</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Venous tone: squeezing the reservoir.</strong> Sympathetic nerves can tighten the smooth muscle in vein walls (<strong><span data-term=\"venoconstriction\">venoconstriction</span></strong>). The veins then hold less, so stored blood is pushed towards the heart, <strong>without adding any new blood</strong>. This is how the body quickly tops up venous return after a bleed or on standing.</p>\n<p><strong>This is different from tightening arterioles.</strong> Arterioles control <strong>resistance</strong> and so flow into organs. Veins mainly control <strong>how much blood is stored</strong> and how much is sent back to fill the heart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-pressure-actually-drives-venous-return\">3. What pressure actually drives venous return?</h2>\n<p>Blood flows back because <strong>pressure in the veins is higher than pressure in the right atrium</strong>. Venous return depends on the <strong>difference</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Venous-return relation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"VR = (P_msf − P_RA) / (R_VR)\">VR = (P_msf − P_RA) / (R_VR)</span><span class=\"equation-example\">Pmsf = mean systemic filling pressure; PRA = right atrial pressure; RVR = resistance to venous return.</span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"pmsf\">Pmsf (mean systemic filling pressure)</span>:</strong> how &quot;full&quot; the circulation is: the pressure that would exist everywhere if the heart stopped and pressures evened out. It rises with <strong>more blood volume</strong> or <strong>tighter veins</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>PRA (right atrial pressure):</strong> the pressure the returning blood flows <strong>into</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>RVR:</strong> the resistance of the veins on the way back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Picture a sink draining into a bucket.</strong> The flow depends on how much higher the sink's water level is than the bucket's. If the bucket fills up (right atrial pressure rises), flow slows, even with the sink unchanged.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Venous return rises</strong> with more blood volume or venoconstriction (Pmsf ↑).</li>\n<li><strong>Venous return falls</strong> if right atrial pressure rises, as when a failing right ventricle can't empty.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>There's a limit.</strong> Lowering right atrial pressure helps only so far. Once it falls below zero, the large veins entering the chest collapse and stop extra blood getting through.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why must venous return equal cardiac output over time?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The heart can only pump out the blood that comes back to it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Veins and arteries always have the same pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The right atrium stores any extra blood indefinitely</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Valves in the veins measure the blood flow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The circulation is a closed loop, so any lasting difference would pile blood up in one place.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Sympathetic nerves tighten the veins, with no change in blood volume. What happens to venous return?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It falls, because veins now resist flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It rises, because stored blood is pushed towards the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It is unchanged, because no blood has been added</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls, because right atrial pressure drops</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Tighter veins hold less, so mean systemic filling pressure rises and more blood returns.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A failing right ventricle can't empty, so right atrial pressure rises. What happens to venous return?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because the atrium pulls harder</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls, because the pressure difference driving it shrinks</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It is unchanged, because Pmsf is the same</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It rises, because the veins tighten in response</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> VR depends on Pmsf − PRA. A higher PRA leaves a smaller difference, so less blood flows back.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work\">4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work?</h2>\n<p>The deep veins of the legs run <strong>between muscles</strong> and contain <strong><span data-term=\"venous-valves\">one-way valves</span></strong> that open only towards the heart.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Muscle contracts:</strong> it squeezes the vein. The blood can only go <strong>up</strong>, because the valve below snaps shut and the valve above is pushed open.</li>\n<li><strong>Muscle relaxes:</strong> the squeezed section expands and refills <strong>from below</strong>. The valve above shuts, so blood that has moved up doesn't fall back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each contraction pumps blood a step closer to the heart. <strong>Walking is a pump.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"view-switch\">\n<figure class=\"learning-figure vr-pump-figure static-view\"><figcaption>The muscle pump at a glance</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 540 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original diagram of a vertical leg vein, one-way valves and surrounding muscle compression moving venous blood upwards towards the heart.\"><rect x=\"230\" y=\"16\" width=\"82\" height=\"128\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#d7eafe\" stroke=\"#4381c1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M230 55 Q271 65 273 79 Q273 65 312 55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#174f92\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M230 107 Q271 117 273 132 Q273 117 312 107\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#174f92\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M273 114V23 M264 35 L273 23 L282 35\" stroke=\"#1463bd\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g stroke=\"#e38c38\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M172 80H222 M213 71 L222 80 L213 89\"/><path d=\"M371 80H318 M327 71 L318 80 L327 89\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"163\" y=\"64\">Muscle</text><text x=\"163\" y=\"104\">squeezes</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"64\">Muscle</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"104\">squeezes</text><text x=\"276\" y=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\">To heart ↑</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"149\">Valves favour one-way upward movement during compression</text></g></svg><p>Illustration of the principle, not a scale drawing. Valves limit backward flow when muscle pressure is released.</p></figure>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"muscle-pump\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Muscle pump</figcaption><p>Shown with the original static diagram behind the Diagram | Interactive switch. A leg vein between two calf muscles, with a valve above and below. Squeeze shows the muscles pressing in, the lower valve shut, the upper valve open and blood moving up. Relax shows the muscles easing, the lower valve open (refilling from below) and the upper valve shut (no backflow). Play alternates them. A Stand still toggle stops the pump and shows blood pooling in a swollen lower vein, with the line \"Less blood returns → less filling → lower stroke volume\".</p></figure>\n</div>\n\n<p><strong>Why do soldiers faint on parade?</strong> Standing still, gravity pools blood in the leg veins and <strong>the muscle pump isn't working</strong>. Less blood returns, so filling and stroke volume fall, blood pressure drops and the brain gets less blood. Gently tensing the calves or rising on the toes keeps the pump going. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/gravity\">→ Gravity</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return\">5. How does breathing help venous return?</h2>\n<p><strong>Breathing in</strong> does two things at once:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The chest expands, so <strong>pressure inside the chest falls</strong>. The veins in the chest and the right atrium are pulled open, and blood is drawn in from the veins outside the chest.</li>\n<li>The diaphragm moves down and <strong>raises pressure in the abdomen</strong>, squeezing blood up from the abdominal veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both push blood towards the right heart, so <strong>venous return rises during inspiration</strong>. This is the respiratory pump. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck\">→ Pulses and JVP, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>On a ventilator it reverses.</strong> A ventilator pushes air in, so chest pressure <strong>rises</strong> on inspiration and venous return falls.</p>\n<p>The lecture also notes that <strong>pulsing arteries</strong> next to veins squeeze them slightly with each beat, helping blood along.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> When the calf muscle contracts, why does blood in the deep vein move up rather than down?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Gravity pulls blood upwards during muscle contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The valve below shuts and the valve above opens</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The vein's own muscle pumps blood upwards</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The arteries next to it pull blood up by suction</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The one-way valves let squeezed blood leave only towards the heart.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A soldier stands perfectly still for an hour and faints. What is the main reason?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood pools in the leg veins with no muscle pump, so filling falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Standing raises blood pressure too high for the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart muscle tires from beating while upright</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Breathing stops being able to help venous return</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Pooling lowers venous return, EDV, stroke volume and blood pressure, so the brain is underperfused.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> How does breathing in help blood return to the heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It raises chest pressure and pushes blood into the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It slows the heart, giving more time to fill</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It lowers chest pressure and raises abdominal pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It closes the venous valves in the legs</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Low chest pressure draws blood in, while the diaphragm pushes blood up from the abdomen.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-what-does-central-venous-pressure-tell-us\">6. What does central venous pressure tell us?</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"cvp\">Central venous pressure (CVP)</span></strong> is the pressure in the large veins next to the right atrium. It is about the same as right atrial pressure, and it is what the JVP shows at the bedside. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck\">→ Pulses and JVP, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p>It is <strong>low</strong>, only a few mmHg. The lecture's example is about 4.6 mmHg, but the exact value depends on posture, breathing and how it is measured.</p>\n<p><strong>CVP is not a measure of blood volume.</strong> A high CVP can mean too much fluid, or it can mean the <strong>right ventricle is failing</strong> and blood is backing up behind it, even while cardiac output is low. CVP has to be read together with the rest of the patient.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-happens-to-veins-in-the-head-when-standing\">7. What happens to veins in the head when standing?</h2>\n<p>Above the heart, gravity <strong>lowers</strong> venous pressure. In the neck, the veins can fall below atmospheric pressure. Ordinary neck veins simply <strong>collapse</strong>, which is why they look empty when you stand.</p>\n<p>The <strong><span data-term=\"dural-sinus\">dural venous sinuses</span></strong> inside the skull are different: they are held open by tough dura. If one is cut open above heart level, as in a head wound or neurosurgery with the patient sitting, its below-atmospheric pressure can <strong>suck air in</strong>. An air bubble then travels to the heart and lungs: a dangerous <strong><span data-term=\"air-embolism\">air embolism</span></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A patient has a high CVP. Which conclusion is safest?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They definitely have too much fluid in their circulation</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their cardiac output must be high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It may be fluid overload or a failing right ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Their left ventricle must be failing alone</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> CVP reflects both filling and how well the right ventricle empties. It is not a simple blood-volume gauge.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why can an open dural venous sinus draw in air when the head is above the heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Its pressure is below atmospheric and its walls can't collapse</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its pressure is high, so it bursts and draws in air</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The brain pumps air into the venous blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Breathing out pushes air into the sinus</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Ordinary veins would collapse at below-atmospheric pressure, but the dura holds the sinus open, so air is sucked in.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why do neck veins look empty in someone standing upright?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Gravity drops their pressure below atmospheric, so they collapse</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. All the blood is pumped into the arteries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The valves in the neck veins close</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Their walls tighten and squeeze them shut</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Above the heart, venous pressure falls. Thin neck veins collapse rather than stay open.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Venous return</strong> = blood returning to the right atrium per minute. It equals cardiac output over time.</li>\n<li><strong>No filling, no output:</strong> even a strong heart can't pump blood it doesn't receive.</li>\n<li><strong>Veins are capacitance vessels:</strong> stretchy, low-pressure, holding most of the blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Venoconstriction</strong> squeezes the reservoir and raises venous return without adding blood.</li>\n<li><strong>VR = (Pmsf − PRA) ÷ RVR.</strong> More fullness raises return; higher right atrial pressure lowers it.</li>\n<li><strong>Muscle pump:</strong> contracting muscles squeeze deep veins, and one-way valves send blood only towards the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>Standing still</strong> pools blood in the legs and can cause fainting; moving the calves helps.</li>\n<li><strong>Breathing in</strong> lowers chest pressure and raises abdominal pressure, so venous return rises.</li>\n<li><strong>CVP</strong> ≈ right atrial pressure (a few mmHg). High CVP can mean overload or right heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Head veins</strong> collapse when you stand; <strong>dural sinuses</strong> can't, so an open sinus above the heart risks air embolism.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Veins and Venous Return 1. What is venous return, and why is it essential? Venous return is the volume of blood flowing back into the right atrium each minute. Over time it must equal cardiac output: the heart can only pump out what comes back to it. ( → Cardiac Output, §1) The heart can't pump blood it doesn't receive. Even a strong heart ejects little if it is poorly filled. More venous return fills the ventricles more, and through Frank–Starling they eject more. ( → Cardiac Output, §4) Venous return ↑ → Right-heart filling ↑ → EDV ↑ → Stroke volume ↑ Why does the left side respond a few beats later? Extra returning blood enters the right heart first, then has to pass through the lungs before it reaches the left ventricle. 2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? Veins have thin, stretchy walls. They can hold a lot of blood without much rise in pressure, like a half-filled balloon that takes more air easily. Arteries are stiffer, so the same added volume raises their pressure sharply. Vascular compliance C = (ΔV) / (ΔP) The higher C of veins permits greater volume storage for a given pressure rise. So the veins are the body's blood reservoir: about half or more of the blood volume sits in the systemic veins at rest. ( → Haemodynamics, §10) Venous tone: squeezing the reservoir. Sympathetic nerves can tighten the smooth muscle in vein walls ( venoconstriction). The veins then hold less, so stored blood is pushed towards the heart, without adding any new blood. This is how the body quickly tops up venous return after a bleed or on standing. This is different from tightening arterioles. Arterioles control resistance and so flow into organs. Veins mainly control how much blood is stored and how much is sent back to fill the heart. 3. What pressure actually drives venous return? Blood flows back because pressure in the veins is higher than pressure in the right atrium. Venous return depends on the difference: Venous-return relation VR = (P_msf − P_RA) / (R_VR) Pmsf = mean systemic filling pressure; PRA = right atrial pressure; RVR = resistance to venous return. Pmsf (mean systemic filling pressure): how \"full\" the circulation is: the pressure that would exist everywhere if the heart stopped and pressures evened out. It rises with more blood volume or tighter veins. PRA (right atrial pressure): the pressure the returning blood flows into. RVR: the resistance of the veins on the way back. Picture a sink draining into a bucket. The flow depends on how much higher the sink's water level is than the bucket's. If the bucket fills up (right atrial pressure rises), flow slows, even with the sink unchanged. So: Venous return rises with more blood volume or venoconstriction (Pmsf ↑). Venous return falls if right atrial pressure rises, as when a failing right ventricle can't empty. There's a limit. Lowering right atrial pressure helps only so far. Once it falls below zero, the large veins entering the chest collapse and stop extra blood getting through. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why must venous return equal cardiac output over time? A. The heart can only pump out the blood that comes back to it B. Veins and arteries always have the same pressure C. The right atrium stores any extra blood indefinitely D. Valves in the veins measure the blood flow Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The circulation is a closed loop, so any lasting difference would pile blood up in one place. 2. Sympathetic nerves tighten the veins, with no change in blood volume. What happens to venous return? A. It falls, because veins now resist flow B. It rises, because stored blood is pushed towards the heart C. It is unchanged, because no blood has been added D. It falls, because right atrial pressure drops Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Tighter veins hold less, so mean systemic filling pressure rises and more blood returns. 3. A failing right ventricle can't empty, so right atrial pressure rises. What happens to venous return? A. It rises, because the atrium pulls harder B. It falls, because the pressure difference driving it shrinks C. It is unchanged, because Pmsf is the same D. It rises, because the veins tighten in response Show answer and explanation Answer: B. VR depends on Pmsf − PRA. A higher PRA leaves a smaller difference, so less blood flows back. 4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work? The deep veins of the legs run between muscles and contain one-way valves that open only towards the heart. Muscle contracts: it squeezes the vein. The blood can only go up, because the valve below snaps shut and the valve above is pushed open. Muscle relaxes: the squeezed section expands and refills from below. The valve above shuts, so blood that has moved up doesn't fall back. Each contraction pumps blood a step closer to the heart. Walking is a pump. The muscle pump at a glance Muscle squeezes Muscle squeezes To heart ↑ Valves favour one-way upward movement during compression Illustration of the principle, not a scale drawing. Valves limit backward flow when muscle pressure is released. Muscle pump Shown with the original static diagram behind the Diagram | Interactive switch. A leg vein between two calf muscles, with a valve above and below. Squeeze shows the muscles pressing in, the lower valve shut, the upper valve open and blood moving up. Relax shows the muscles easing, the lower valve open (refilling from below) and the upper valve shut (no backflow). Play alternates them. A Stand still toggle stops the pump and shows blood pooling in a swollen lower vein, with the line \"Less blood returns → less filling → lower stroke volume\". Why do soldiers faint on parade? Standing still, gravity pools blood in the leg veins and the muscle pump isn't working. Less blood returns, so filling and stroke volume fall, blood pressure drops and the brain gets less blood. Gently tensing the calves or rising on the toes keeps the pump going. ( → Gravity) 5. How does breathing help venous return? Breathing in does two things at once: The chest expands, so pressure inside the chest falls. The veins in the chest and the right atrium are pulled open, and blood is drawn in from the veins outside the chest. The diaphragm moves down and raises pressure in the abdomen, squeezing blood up from the abdominal veins. Both push blood towards the right heart, so venous return rises during inspiration. This is the respiratory pump. ( → Pulses and JVP, §3) On a ventilator it reverses. A ventilator pushes air in, so chest pressure rises on inspiration and venous return falls. The lecture also notes that pulsing arteries next to veins squeeze them slightly with each beat, helping blood along. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. When the calf muscle contracts, why does blood in the deep vein move up rather than down? A. Gravity pulls blood upwards during muscle contraction B. The valve below shuts and the valve above opens C. The vein's own muscle pumps blood upwards D. The arteries next to it pull blood up by suction Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The one-way valves let squeezed blood leave only towards the heart. 2. A soldier stands perfectly still for an hour and faints. What is the main reason? A. Blood pools in the leg veins with no muscle pump, so filling falls B. Standing raises blood pressure too high for the brain C. The heart muscle tires from beating while upright D. Breathing stops being able to help venous return Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pooling lowers venous return, EDV, stroke volume and blood pressure, so the brain is underperfused. 3. How does breathing in help blood return to the heart? A. It raises chest pressure and pushes blood into the heart B. It slows the heart, giving more time to fill C. It lowers chest pressure and raises abdominal pressure D. It closes the venous valves in the legs Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Low chest pressure draws blood in, while the diaphragm pushes blood up from the abdomen. 6. What does central venous pressure tell us? Central venous pressure (CVP) is the pressure in the large veins next to the right atrium. It is about the same as right atrial pressure, and it is what the JVP shows at the bedside. ( → Pulses and JVP, §3) It is low, only a few mmHg. The lecture's example is about 4.6 mmHg, but the exact value depends on posture, breathing and how it is measured. CVP is not a measure of blood volume. A high CVP can mean too much fluid, or it can mean the right ventricle is failing and blood is backing up behind it, even while cardiac output is low. CVP has to be read together with the rest of the patient. 7. What happens to veins in the head when standing? Above the heart, gravity lowers venous pressure. In the neck, the veins can fall below atmospheric pressure. Ordinary neck veins simply collapse, which is why they look empty when you stand. The dural venous sinuses inside the skull are different: they are held open by tough dura. If one is cut open above heart level, as in a head wound or neurosurgery with the patient sitting, its below-atmospheric pressure can suck air in. An air bubble then travels to the heart and lungs: a dangerous air embolism. Understanding Check: Sections 6–7 1. A patient has a high CVP. Which conclusion is safest? A. They definitely have too much fluid in their circulation B. Their cardiac output must be high C. It may be fluid overload or a failing right ventricle D. Their left ventricle must be failing alone Show answer and explanation Answer: C. CVP reflects both filling and how well the right ventricle empties. It is not a simple blood-volume gauge. 2. Why can an open dural venous sinus draw in air when the head is above the heart? A. Its pressure is below atmospheric and its walls can't collapse B. Its pressure is high, so it bursts and draws in air C. The brain pumps air into the venous blood D. Breathing out pushes air into the sinus Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Ordinary veins would collapse at below-atmospheric pressure, but the dura holds the sinus open, so air is sucked in. 3. Why do neck veins look empty in someone standing upright? A. Gravity drops their pressure below atmospheric, so they collapse B. All the blood is pumped into the arteries C. The valves in the neck veins close D. Their walls tighten and squeeze them shut Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Above the heart, venous pressure falls. Thin neck veins collapse rather than stay open. 8. Chapter Essentials Venous return = blood returning to the right atrium per minute. It equals cardiac output over time. No filling, no output: even a strong heart can't pump blood it doesn't receive. Veins are capacitance vessels: stretchy, low-pressure, holding most of the blood. Venoconstriction squeezes the reservoir and raises venous return without adding blood. VR = (Pmsf − PRA) ÷ RVR. More fullness raises return; higher right atrial pressure lowers it. Muscle pump: contracting muscles squeeze deep veins, and one-way valves send blood only towards the heart. Standing still pools blood in the legs and can cause fainting; moving the calves helps. Breathing in lowers chest pressure and raises abdominal pressure, so venous return rises. CVP ≈ right atrial pressure (a few mmHg). High CVP can mean overload or right heart failure. Head veins collapse when you stand; dural sinuses can't, so an open sinus above the heart risks air embolism."},{"slug":"microcirculation","title":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"57-60 and 84-89","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-is-the-microcirculation-necessary","title":"1. Why is the microcirculation necessary?"},{"id":"2-what-is-a-capillary-wall-made-of","title":"2. What is a capillary wall made of?"},{"id":"3-how-do-substances-actually-cross-the-capillary-wall","title":"3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall?"},{"id":"4-what-decides-which-capillaries-receive-more-blood","title":"4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood?"},{"id":"5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood","title":"5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood?"},{"id":"6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding","title":"6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding"},{"id":"7-what-changes-microvascular-filtration-or-permeability","title":"7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability?"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-is-the-microcirculation-necessary\">1. Why is the microcirculation necessary?</h2>\n<p>Every cell needs oxygen and nutrients, but the big arteries can't deliver them directly. The handover happens in the <strong><span data-term=\"microcirculation\">microcirculation</span></strong>: the tiny vessels inside each tissue.</p>\n<div class=\"micro-path\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Microcirculatory sequence\"><span>Small arteriole</span><b>→</b><span>Capillary exchange</span><b>→</b><span>Venule</span><b>→</b><span>Venous return</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Capillaries are built for exchange:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thin walls</strong>, so the distance for diffusion is tiny.</li>\n<li><strong>Huge numbers</strong>, giving an enormous surface area. Almost every cell is within a few cell-widths of one.</li>\n<li><strong>Slow flow</strong>, because their total cross-section is so large, which gives time for exchange. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries\">→ Haemodynamics, §4</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Yet capillaries hold only about <strong>5% of the blood</strong> at any moment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-is-a-capillary-wall-made-of\">2. What is a capillary wall made of?</h2>\n<p>A capillary wall is just <strong>one layer of endothelial cells</strong> sitting on a thin <strong>basement membrane</strong>, sometimes wrapped by <strong><span data-term=\"pericytes\">pericytes</span></strong>. There is <strong>no muscle layer</strong>: capillaries can't constrict themselves. That job belongs to the arterioles that feed them.</p>\n<p><strong>Three types</strong>, from tightest to leakiest:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Type</th>\n  <th>What the wall looks like</th>\n  <th>Where</th>\n  <th>What gets through</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Continuous</strong></td>\n  <td>Unbroken lining, with narrow gaps between cells</td>\n  <td>Muscle, skin, lungs; brain (tightest of all)</td>\n  <td>Water, gases and small molecules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"fenestrated\">Fenestrated</span></strong></td>\n  <td>Cells with tiny pores (<em>fenestra</em> = window)</td>\n  <td>Gut villi, endocrine glands, kidney glomeruli</td>\n  <td>Water and small solutes, quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Discontinuous (sinusoidal)</strong></td>\n  <td>Large gaps, broken basement membrane</td>\n  <td>Liver, spleen, bone marrow</td>\n  <td>Even proteins and blood cells</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The tissue's job decides the type. The gut and glands move a lot of fluid quickly, so they have fenestrated capillaries. The liver handles proteins, so it has the leakiest kind. The brain must be protected, so it has the tightest.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> A lecture slide gives the size of gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres wide, far smaller than one cell.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-how-do-substances-actually-cross-the-capillary-wall\">3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Route</th>\n  <th>What crosses</th>\n  <th>What drives it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Diffusion</strong></td>\n  <td>O₂, CO₂, glucose, ions</td>\n  <td>Concentration difference: from more to less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Gaps and pores</strong></td>\n  <td>Water and small water-soluble molecules</td>\n  <td>The same gradients, through the openings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Vesicles</strong></td>\n  <td>Some large molecules</td>\n  <td>The cell carries them across in little bubbles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Filtration</strong></td>\n  <td>Bulk water with its dissolved solutes</td>\n  <td>Pressure differences (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">§5</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Diffusion and filtration are different.</strong> In diffusion, individual molecules move down their own concentration difference: O₂ out to the cells, CO₂ back in. In filtration, water as a whole is <strong>pushed</strong> out by pressure. Most gas and nutrient exchange is diffusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why are capillaries so good at exchange?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Their thick walls protect the solutes as they cross</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Their fast flow brings fresh blood to cells quickly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Their muscle layer squeezes solutes out into tissue</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> A short diffusion distance, a huge surface area and plenty of time all favour exchange.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which capillaries let even proteins and blood cells through?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The continuous capillaries of the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The fenestrated capillaries of the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The continuous capillaries of muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The sinusoidal capillaries of the liver</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Sinusoids have large gaps and a broken basement membrane. Brain capillaries are the tightest.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> How does oxygen get from capillary blood into a working muscle cell?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. By diffusion, from higher to lower concentration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. By filtration, pushed out by blood pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Carried across inside vesicles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pumped across by the endothelial cells</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The cell uses oxygen, keeping its own level low, so oxygen diffuses out of the blood towards it.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-what-decides-which-capillaries-receive-more-blood\">4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood?</h2>\n<p>Capillaries have no muscle, so they can't control their own flow. Flow is set <strong>upstream</strong>, by the <strong>arterioles</strong> (and, in classic teaching, by rings of muscle at the capillary entrances: <strong><span data-term=\"precapillary-sphincter\">precapillary sphincters</span></strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Active tissue calls for more blood.</strong> Working muscle releases metabolites (CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺) that relax the nearby arterioles. More blood flows in, and <strong>more capillaries open up</strong> (recruitment), so more surface area is available for exchange. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Regulation</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Precapillary sphincters are classic teaching, but they are not found at every capillary. In many tissues the arterioles do the controlling.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood?</h2>\n<p>Fluid movement across the capillary wall is a <strong>tug-of-war between two kinds of pressure</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"hydrostatic-pressure\">Hydrostatic pressure</span></strong> is the <strong>push</strong> of fluid against the wall. Blood pressure inside the capillary (<strong>Pc</strong>) pushes water <strong>out</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"oncotic-pressure\">Oncotic pressure</span></strong> is the <strong>pull</strong> of proteins. Proteins can't cross the wall easily, and they hold water with them. Plasma proteins, mainly <strong>albumin</strong>, pull water <strong>in</strong> (<strong>πc</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The tissue side has its own, smaller, versions of each:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Force</th>\n  <th>Symbol</th>\n  <th>Direction</th>\n  <th>Typical size (classic values)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Capillary blood pressure</td>\n  <td><strong>Pc</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Out</strong></td>\n  <td>About 30 mmHg (arterial end), falling to about 10 (venous end)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Plasma protein pull</td>\n  <td><strong>πc</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>In</strong></td>\n  <td>About 28 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Tissue fluid pressure</td>\n  <td><strong>Pi</strong></td>\n  <td>Usually slightly out (it is slightly below zero)</td>\n  <td>About −3 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Tissue protein pull</td>\n  <td><strong>πi</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Out</strong></td>\n  <td>About 8 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does fluid leave even though proteins stay in?</strong> Because the <strong>push out</strong> (Pc, with help from the tissue forces) beats the <strong>pull in</strong> (πc), at least at the arterial end. The proteins stay behind, but water still filters out.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure micro-force-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Hydrostatic and oncotic influences on capillary filtration</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 570 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original schematic capillary containing plasma proteins, with an outward hydrostatic arrow from blood to tissue and an opposing inward plasma oncotic arrow.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"51\" width=\"490\" height=\"76\" rx=\"30\" fill=\"#f5d8df\" stroke=\"#b66b80\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g fill=\"#ae536c\"><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"327\" cy=\"88\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"458\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/></g><path d=\"M239 77 V14 M230 27 L239 14 L248 27\" stroke=\"#d27c26\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M389 14 V75 M380 62 L389 75 L398 62\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"166\" y=\"13\">Pc: outward</text><text x=\"400\" y=\"13\">Plasma oncotic: inward</text><text x=\"167\" y=\"96\">Capillary blood</text><text x=\"181\" y=\"151\">Interstitial tissue space</text></g></svg><p>A conceptual comparison of two important forces. Actual net filtration also depends on interstitial forces, surface area, permeability and the endothelial glycocalyx.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"starling-balance\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Starling balance</figcaption><p>One capillary drawn left (arterial end) to right (venous end). At each end, an outward arrow shows the push (Pc) and an inward arrow the pull (πc), sized to scale, with the net result: \"fluid leaves\" or \"fluid returns\". Sliders for capillary pressure and plasma protein. Scenario buttons: Normal, Heart failure (Pc ↑), Low albumin (πc ↓), Leaky capillary (proteins escape, so the pull weakens). A line underneath states the net effect in plain words. Classic textbook values.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why does albumin matter so much?</strong> It provides most of the pull. If albumin falls (liver disease, kidney protein loss, malnutrition), the pull weakens, more fluid stays out, and tissues swell. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\">6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding</h2>\n<p><strong>The classic model (the lecture's):</strong> the push wins at the arterial end, so fluid filters out. At the venous end, Pc has fallen below the protein pull, so fluid is drawn back in. Of about <strong>24 L filtered a day, about 85% is reabsorbed</strong> into the capillaries, and <strong>15% is returned by the lymph</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What we now know:</strong> the inner surface of the capillary is coated with a gel-like layer, the <strong><span data-term=\"glycocalyx\">glycocalyx</span></strong>. The protein pull that matters is the one across this layer, and it stays strong along the whole capillary. So in most tissues, <strong>little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end</strong>. Instead, a small net filtration continues all along, and the <strong>lymphatics return almost all of it</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to learn:</strong> know the four forces and the lecture's classic numbers for the exam. But understand that <strong>the lymphatics are the main return route, not a back-up</strong>. That is why blocked lymphatics cause swelling so readily. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/lymphatics\">→ Lymphatics</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The 24 L/day and 85%/15% figures are the lecture's classic model. The revised (glycocalyx) model is current physiology (Levick and Michel, 2010).</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-microvascular-filtration-or-permeability\">7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Change</th>\n  <th>Effect on filtration</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arterioles dilate</strong></td>\n  <td>↑</td>\n  <td>More upstream pressure reaches the capillary, so Pc rises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Veins congested or blocked</strong></td>\n  <td>↑</td>\n  <td>Blood backs up, so Pc rises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Leaky capillaries</strong> (histamine, bradykinin, substance P)</td>\n  <td>↑</td>\n  <td>Proteins escape, weakening the pull and widening the gaps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>More capillaries open</strong></td>\n  <td>↑ total exchange</td>\n  <td>More surface area</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Two very different causes of swelling:</strong> a <strong>raised pressure</strong> (a blocked vein) pushes more water through a normal wall. A <strong>leaky wall</strong> (inflammation) lets water and proteins through even at normal pressure. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema\">→ Oedema</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Capillary blood pressure rises with nothing else changed. What happens to filtration?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because the push out is stronger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls, because the pull in is stronger</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It stays the same, because proteins stay in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It reverses, so fluid is drawn back in</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Pc is the main outward force. Raising it increases the net push out.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient's plasma albumin is very low. Why might their tissues swell?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Albumin pushes water out of capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Capillary blood pressure rises when albumin falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Less protein in the blood weakens the pull holding water in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The lymphatics stop working without albumin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Albumin provides most of the plasma oncotic pull. With less of it, more fluid stays out in the tissues.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In the revised (glycocalyx) model, how does most filtered fluid get back to the blood?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It is reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is returned through the lymphatics</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It evaporates through the skin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is excreted by the kidneys</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end in most tissues. The lymphatics are the main return route.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Capillaries exchange</strong>: thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow; they hold only about 5% of the blood.</li>\n<li><strong>The wall</strong> is one layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, with no muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Three types:</strong> continuous (tightest in the brain), fenestrated (gut, glands, kidney), sinusoidal (liver, spleen, marrow).</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong> moves gases and nutrients down their gradients. <strong>Filtration</strong> pushes bulk water out with pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterioles and precapillary sphincters</strong> control which capillaries get blood. Active tissue opens more.</li>\n<li><strong>Starling tug-of-war:</strong> Pc pushes out, πc (albumin) pulls in; the tissue forces Pi and πi are smaller.</li>\n<li><strong>Classic model:</strong> filtration at the arterial end, reabsorption at the venous end; 24 L/day, 85% back to the capillaries, 15% to the lymph.</li>\n<li><strong>Revised model:</strong> the glycocalyx keeps the pull strong, so little is reabsorbed, and <strong>the lymph returns most filtered fluid</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Filtration rises</strong> with higher Pc (arteriolar dilation, venous congestion), lower πc (low albumin) or a leaky wall (histamine, bradykinin, substance P).</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange 1. Why is the microcirculation necessary? Every cell needs oxygen and nutrients, but the big arteries can't deliver them directly. The handover happens in the microcirculation: the tiny vessels inside each tissue. Small arteriole → Capillary exchange → Venule → Venous return Capillaries are built for exchange: Thin walls, so the distance for diffusion is tiny. Huge numbers, giving an enormous surface area. Almost every cell is within a few cell-widths of one. Slow flow, because their total cross-section is so large, which gives time for exchange. ( → Haemodynamics, §4) Yet capillaries hold only about 5% of the blood at any moment. 2. What is a capillary wall made of? A capillary wall is just one layer of endothelial cells sitting on a thin basement membrane, sometimes wrapped by pericytes. There is no muscle layer: capillaries can't constrict themselves. That job belongs to the arterioles that feed them. Three types, from tightest to leakiest: Type What the wall looks like Where What gets through Continuous Unbroken lining, with narrow gaps between cells Muscle, skin, lungs; brain (tightest of all) Water, gases and small molecules Fenestrated Cells with tiny pores ( fenestra = window) Gut villi, endocrine glands, kidney glomeruli Water and small solutes, quickly Discontinuous (sinusoidal) Large gaps, broken basement membrane Liver, spleen, bone marrow Even proteins and blood cells The tissue's job decides the type. The gut and glands move a lot of fluid quickly, so they have fenestrated capillaries. The liver handles proteins, so it has the leakiest kind. The brain must be protected, so it has the tightest. Source note: A lecture slide gives the size of gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres wide, far smaller than one cell. 3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall? Route What crosses What drives it Diffusion O₂, CO₂, glucose, ions Concentration difference: from more to less Gaps and pores Water and small water-soluble molecules The same gradients, through the openings Vesicles Some large molecules The cell carries them across in little bubbles Filtration Bulk water with its dissolved solutes Pressure differences ( §5) Diffusion and filtration are different. In diffusion, individual molecules move down their own concentration difference: O₂ out to the cells, CO₂ back in. In filtration, water as a whole is pushed out by pressure. Most gas and nutrient exchange is diffusion. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why are capillaries so good at exchange? A. Their thick walls protect the solutes as they cross B. Their thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow C. Their fast flow brings fresh blood to cells quickly D. Their muscle layer squeezes solutes out into tissue Show answer and explanation Answer: B. A short diffusion distance, a huge surface area and plenty of time all favour exchange. 2. Which capillaries let even proteins and blood cells through? A. The continuous capillaries of the brain B. The fenestrated capillaries of the gut C. The continuous capillaries of muscle D. The sinusoidal capillaries of the liver Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Sinusoids have large gaps and a broken basement membrane. Brain capillaries are the tightest. 3. How does oxygen get from capillary blood into a working muscle cell? A. By diffusion, from higher to lower concentration B. By filtration, pushed out by blood pressure C. Carried across inside vesicles D. Pumped across by the endothelial cells Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The cell uses oxygen, keeping its own level low, so oxygen diffuses out of the blood towards it. 4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood? Capillaries have no muscle, so they can't control their own flow. Flow is set upstream, by the arterioles (and, in classic teaching, by rings of muscle at the capillary entrances: precapillary sphincters). Active tissue calls for more blood. Working muscle releases metabolites (CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺) that relax the nearby arterioles. More blood flows in, and more capillaries open up (recruitment), so more surface area is available for exchange. ( → Local Regulation) Source note: Precapillary sphincters are classic teaching, but they are not found at every capillary. In many tissues the arterioles do the controlling. 5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood? Fluid movement across the capillary wall is a tug-of-war between two kinds of pressure: Hydrostatic pressure is the push of fluid against the wall. Blood pressure inside the capillary ( Pc) pushes water out. Oncotic pressure is the pull of proteins. Proteins can't cross the wall easily, and they hold water with them. Plasma proteins, mainly albumin, pull water in ( πc). The tissue side has its own, smaller, versions of each: Force Symbol Direction Typical size (classic values) Capillary blood pressure Pc Out About 30 mmHg (arterial end), falling to about 10 (venous end) Plasma protein pull πc In About 28 mmHg Tissue fluid pressure Pi Usually slightly out (it is slightly below zero) About −3 mmHg Tissue protein pull πi Out About 8 mmHg Why does fluid leave even though proteins stay in? Because the push out (Pc, with help from the tissue forces) beats the pull in (πc), at least at the arterial end. The proteins stay behind, but water still filters out. Figure 01. Hydrostatic and oncotic influences on capillary filtration Pc: outward Plasma oncotic: inward Capillary blood Interstitial tissue space A conceptual comparison of two important forces. Actual net filtration also depends on interstitial forces, surface area, permeability and the endothelial glycocalyx. Starling balance One capillary drawn left (arterial end) to right (venous end). At each end, an outward arrow shows the push (Pc) and an inward arrow the pull (πc), sized to scale, with the net result: \"fluid leaves\" or \"fluid returns\". Sliders for capillary pressure and plasma protein. Scenario buttons: Normal, Heart failure (Pc ↑), Low albumin (πc ↓), Leaky capillary (proteins escape, so the pull weakens). A line underneath states the net effect in plain words. Classic textbook values. Why does albumin matter so much? It provides most of the pull. If albumin falls (liver disease, kidney protein loss, malnutrition), the pull weakens, more fluid stays out, and tissues swell. ( → Oedema, §5) 6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding The classic model (the lecture's): the push wins at the arterial end, so fluid filters out. At the venous end, Pc has fallen below the protein pull, so fluid is drawn back in. Of about 24 L filtered a day, about 85% is reabsorbed into the capillaries, and 15% is returned by the lymph. What we now know: the inner surface of the capillary is coated with a gel-like layer, the glycocalyx. The protein pull that matters is the one across this layer, and it stays strong along the whole capillary. So in most tissues, little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end. Instead, a small net filtration continues all along, and the lymphatics return almost all of it. What to learn: know the four forces and the lecture's classic numbers for the exam. But understand that the lymphatics are the main return route, not a back-up. That is why blocked lymphatics cause swelling so readily. ( → Lymphatics) Source note: The 24 L/day and 85%/15% figures are the lecture's classic model. The revised (glycocalyx) model is current physiology (Levick and Michel, 2010). 7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability? Change Effect on filtration Why Arterioles dilate ↑ More upstream pressure reaches the capillary, so Pc rises Veins congested or blocked ↑ Blood backs up, so Pc rises Leaky capillaries (histamine, bradykinin, substance P) ↑ Proteins escape, weakening the pull and widening the gaps More capillaries open ↑ total exchange More surface area Two very different causes of swelling: a raised pressure (a blocked vein) pushes more water through a normal wall. A leaky wall (inflammation) lets water and proteins through even at normal pressure. ( → Oedema) Understanding Check: Sections 4–7 1. Capillary blood pressure rises with nothing else changed. What happens to filtration? A. It rises, because the push out is stronger B. It falls, because the pull in is stronger C. It stays the same, because proteins stay in D. It reverses, so fluid is drawn back in Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pc is the main outward force. Raising it increases the net push out. 2. A patient's plasma albumin is very low. Why might their tissues swell? A. Albumin pushes water out of capillaries B. Capillary blood pressure rises when albumin falls C. Less protein in the blood weakens the pull holding water in D. The lymphatics stop working without albumin Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Albumin provides most of the plasma oncotic pull. With less of it, more fluid stays out in the tissues. 3. In the revised (glycocalyx) model, how does most filtered fluid get back to the blood? A. It is reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillaries B. It is returned through the lymphatics C. It evaporates through the skin D. It is excreted by the kidneys Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Little fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end in most tissues. The lymphatics are the main return route. 8. Chapter Essentials Capillaries exchange: thin walls, huge surface area and slow flow; they hold only about 5% of the blood. The wall is one layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, with no muscle. Three types: continuous (tightest in the brain), fenestrated (gut, glands, kidney), sinusoidal (liver, spleen, marrow). Diffusion moves gases and nutrients down their gradients. Filtration pushes bulk water out with pressure. Arterioles and precapillary sphincters control which capillaries get blood. Active tissue opens more. Starling tug-of-war: Pc pushes out, πc (albumin) pulls in; the tissue forces Pi and πi are smaller. Classic model: filtration at the arterial end, reabsorption at the venous end; 24 L/day, 85% back to the capillaries, 15% to the lymph. Revised model: the glycocalyx keeps the pull strong, so little is reabsorbed, and the lymph returns most filtered fluid. Filtration rises with higher Pc (arteriolar dilation, venous congestion), lower πc (low albumin) or a leaky wall (histamine, bradykinin, substance P)."},{"slug":"edema","title":"Formation of Oedema","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"89-95","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-exactly-is-oedema","title":"1. What exactly is oedema?"},{"id":"2-what-physical-forces-govern-filtration","title":"2. What physical forces govern filtration?"},{"id":"3-five-routes-to-the-same-outcome-excess-interstitial-fluid","title":"3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid"},{"id":"4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema","title":"4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema?"},{"id":"5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema","title":"5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema?"},{"id":"6-how-does-inflammation-increase-oedema","title":"6. How does inflammation increase oedema?"},{"id":"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-persistent-swelling","title":"7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling?"},{"id":"8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different","title":"8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different?"},{"id":"9-which-location-of-oedema-suggests-which-mechanism","title":"9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism?"},{"id":"10-chapter-essentials","title":"10. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Formation of <span data-term=\"oedema\">Oedema</span></h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-exactly-is-oedema\">1. What exactly is oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Oedema</strong> is too much fluid in the spaces <strong>between the cells</strong> (the interstitium), enough to make tissue swell.</p>\n<p>Fluid is always leaving the capillaries, and the lymphatics are always carrying it back. <strong>Oedema happens when more leaves than can be carried away.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"edema-balance\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fluid balance causes oedema\"><span><strong>Filtration ↑</strong><small>More fluid entering tissue</small></span><b>+</b><span><strong>Drainage insufficient</strong><small>Less fluid leaving tissue</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Oedema</strong><small>Fluid accumulates</small></span></div>\n\n<p>So there are only two ways to get oedema: <strong>too much filtration</strong>, or <strong>too little drainage</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-physical-forces-govern-filtration\">2. What physical forces govern filtration?</h2>\n<p>The four Starling forces decide how much fluid leaves the capillaries <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">→ Microcirculation, §5</a>)</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pc</strong>, capillary blood pressure: pushes out.</li>\n<li><strong>πc</strong>, plasma protein (albumin) pull: holds water in.</li>\n<li><strong>Pi</strong> and <strong>πi</strong>, the tissue's own pressure and protein pull: smaller.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Traditional Starling filtration model</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)]\">J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)]</span></div>\n\n<p>Two extra terms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"kf\">Kf</span></strong>, how leaky the wall is (and how much wall there is). A leaky, inflamed wall has a high Kf.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"reflection-coefficient\">σ</span></strong>, how well the wall holds back protein. <strong>σ = 1</strong> means proteins can't cross at all, so their full pull counts. In leaky, inflamed capillaries <strong>σ falls</strong>, proteins slip through, and their pull is lost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-five-routes-to-the-same-outcome-excess-interstitial-fluid\">3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>What fails</th>\n  <th>What changes</th>\n  <th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Push too high</strong></td>\n  <td>Pc ↑</td>\n  <td>Blocked veins, heart failure, standing still, leaky vein valves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Pull too weak</strong></td>\n  <td>πc ↓ (low albumin)</td>\n  <td>Liver disease, kidney protein loss (<span data-term=\"nephrotic\">nephrotic syndrome</span>), malnutrition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Wall too leaky</strong></td>\n  <td>Kf ↑, σ ↓</td>\n  <td>Inflammation, burns, allergy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Drain blocked</strong></td>\n  <td>Lymph flow ↓</td>\n  <td>Filariasis, lymph nodes removed in breast cancer surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Too much fluid overall</strong></td>\n  <td>Salt and water retained</td>\n  <td>Kidney failure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"starling-balance\" data-start=\"hf\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Starling balance: what tips it towards oedema?</figcaption><p>The Starling balance interactive from Chapter 13, opening on Heart failure. Each scenario button shows which arrow changes and the net result.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The key question for any swelling: which part failed?</strong> Different diseases can look alike on the outside but work in completely different ways.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\">4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema?</h2>\n<p>If blood can't drain out of the veins, it <strong>backs up into the capillaries</strong>, raising Pc and pushing more fluid out. The lecture lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blocked veins</strong> (a clot in a leg vein): one leg swells.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaky vein valves</strong> (varicose veins): blood falls back and pools.</li>\n<li><strong>Standing still for long periods</strong>: gravity raises leg venous pressure, and the muscle pump isn't working, so the ankles swell. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work\">→ Venous Return, §4</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Heart failure</strong>:<ul>\n<li>A failing <strong>right</strong> heart raises systemic venous pressure, causing swollen ankles.</li>\n<li>A failing <strong>left</strong> heart raises pressure in the lung veins, causing <strong><span data-term=\"pulmonary-oedema\">pulmonary oedema</span></strong>: fluid in the lungs, which stops oxygen getting into the blood and makes the patient breathless.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arteriole and venule tone matter too.</strong> Widening the arterioles lets more of the arterial pressure through to the capillaries. Tightening the venules holds blood back in them. Both raise Pc, and the lecture lists both as causes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\">5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Albumin is the main protein holding water in the blood.</strong> If it falls, that pull weakens and fluid stays out in the tissues, often all over the body.</p>\n<p>Albumin falls when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The liver can't make enough:</strong> liver failure (cirrhosis).</li>\n<li><strong>The kidneys leak it into the urine:</strong> nephrotic syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>The diet lacks protein:</strong> severe malnutrition (kwashiorkor).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture also mentions <strong>osmotically active substances building up in the tissues</strong>: they pull water out into the tissue from the other side.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-inflammation-increase-oedema\">6. How does inflammation increase oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Histamine, bradykinin and substance P</strong> make the capillary wall <strong>leaky</strong>: the gaps between endothelial cells widen. Water and proteins pour out. The escaped proteins now pull water <strong>outwards</strong>, adding to the swelling.</p>\n<p>This is why a sting or a sprain swells within minutes. It is <strong>not a pressure problem; the wall itself has changed.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A man has a clot blocking the main vein of one leg. Why does that leg swell?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood backs up and raises capillary pressure, pushing more fluid out</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The clot releases proteins that pull water into the tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The lymphatics in that leg are blocked by the clot</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Albumin in that leg is destroyed by the clot</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> A blocked vein raises Pc upstream, so filtration rises in that leg only.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A child with severe protein malnutrition has a swollen belly and legs. What is the main mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised capillary blood pressure from heart failure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low albumin weakens the pull holding water in the blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Inflammation makes every capillary leaky</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blocked lymphatics in the abdomen</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Without enough dietary protein, albumin falls, and the plasma oncotic pull weakens throughout the body.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does a bee sting swell within minutes?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The sting raises blood pressure in the arm</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The venom blocks the local lymph vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Histamine makes capillaries leaky, so fluid and proteins escape</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The sting lowers the level of plasma albumin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Inflammatory mediators open the gaps in the capillary wall. The leaked proteins then pull still more water out.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why can left-sided heart failure make a patient breathless?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Fluid collects around the heart and squeezes it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Raised lung venous pressure pushes fluid into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The right ventricle stops pumping blood to the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Low albumin causes swelling throughout the chest</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Back pressure into the lung capillaries causes pulmonary oedema, which blocks oxygen getting into the blood.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-persistent-swelling\">7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling?</h2>\n<p>Even with normal pressures, some fluid and protein leave the capillaries all the time, and <strong>only the lymphatics can take the protein back</strong>. If they are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. Over time it causes inflammation and scarring, and the swelling becomes firm and permanent: <strong><span data-term=\"lymphoedema\">lymphoedema</span></strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture's examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filariasis (elephantiasis):</strong> parasitic worms block the lymph vessels, so the legs or scrotum swell enormously.</li>\n<li><strong>Radical mastectomy:</strong> removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery leaves that arm swollen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/lymphatics\">→ Lymphatics</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different\">8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different?</h2>\n<p>Press a thumb firmly into swollen skin for a few seconds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"pitting\">Pitting oedema</span>:</strong> a dent stays after you let go. The extra fluid is free to move and is pushed aside. This is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-pitting oedema:</strong> no dent forms. The tissue is firm, because the fluid is bound up with protein and the tissue has become scarred.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lymphoedema</strong> is usually non-pitting, but <strong>only once it is long-standing</strong>. Early lymphoedema can still pit, and it becomes firm as scarring develops.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture states that lymphoedema does not pit. That describes its usual late stage; early lymphoedema can pit.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-which-location-of-oedema-suggests-which-mechanism\">9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Where the swelling is</th>\n  <th>What it suggests</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Both ankles, worse at the end of the day</strong></td>\n  <td>Gravity plus raised venous pressure (standing, right heart failure)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>The lungs</strong> (breathlessness, crackles)</td>\n  <td>Left heart failure, or leaky lung capillaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>All over, including around the eyes</strong></td>\n  <td>Low albumin (kidney or liver disease, malnutrition)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>One arm or leg, firm</strong></td>\n  <td>Blocked lymphatics, or a blocked vein</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are clues, not diagnoses. Heart and kidney disease often cause oedema by <strong>more than one route at once</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 7–9</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A woman's arm has been firmly swollen since her armpit lymph nodes were removed. What is the mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised venous pressure from a clot in the arm</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fluid and protein can't drain away through the lymphatics</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Low plasma albumin from the surgery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Histamine released by the scar tissue</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> With the nodes removed, protein-rich fluid can't drain, so lymphoedema develops.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> You press on a patient's shin and a dent remains. What does that tell you?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The extra fluid is free to move and was pushed aside</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The tissue is scarred and firm</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The swelling must be caused by blocked lymphatics</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The patient's albumin level must be normal</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Pitting means mobile fluid. It is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes, and of early lymphoedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why do proteins matter so much in lymphatic oedema?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Only the lymphatics can return escaped proteins to the blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Proteins block the capillary pores and raise pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Lymph contains no protein, so it dilutes the tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Proteins make the veins collapse and stop draining</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Capillaries can't take protein back. If the lymph is blocked, protein builds up and holds water in the tissue.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"10-chapter-essentials\">10. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Oedema</strong> = too much fluid between the cells. More leaves the capillaries than the lymphatics can carry away.</li>\n<li><strong>Only two ways:</strong> too much filtration, or too little drainage.</li>\n<li><strong>Five routes:</strong> push too high (Pc ↑), pull too weak (albumin ↓), wall too leaky, drain blocked, or too much fluid overall.</li>\n<li><strong>Raised venous pressure:</strong> blocked veins, leaky valves, standing still, heart failure. Right failure → swollen ankles; left failure → pulmonary oedema.</li>\n<li><strong>Low albumin:</strong> liver failure, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition → swelling everywhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflammation:</strong> histamine, bradykinin and substance P make the wall leaky; escaped proteins pull more water out.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic blockage:</strong> filariasis, removal of lymph nodes → protein-rich, firm lymphoedema.</li>\n<li><strong>Pitting</strong> = mobile fluid. <strong>Non-pitting</strong> = firm, scarred tissue (late lymphoedema).</li>\n<li><strong>Ask where the swelling is</strong>: it points to the mechanism that failed.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Formation of Oedema 1. What exactly is oedema? Oedema is too much fluid in the spaces between the cells (the interstitium), enough to make tissue swell. Fluid is always leaving the capillaries, and the lymphatics are always carrying it back. Oedema happens when more leaves than can be carried away. Filtration ↑ More fluid entering tissue + Drainage insufficient Less fluid leaving tissue → Oedema Fluid accumulates So there are only two ways to get oedema: too much filtration, or too little drainage. 2. What physical forces govern filtration? The four Starling forces decide how much fluid leaves the capillaries ( → Microcirculation, §5): Pc, capillary blood pressure: pushes out. πc, plasma protein (albumin) pull: holds water in. Pi and πi, the tissue's own pressure and protein pull: smaller. Traditional Starling filtration model J_v = K_f[(P_c − P_i) − σ(π_c − π_i)] Two extra terms: Kf, how leaky the wall is (and how much wall there is). A leaky, inflamed wall has a high Kf. σ, how well the wall holds back protein. σ = 1 means proteins can't cross at all, so their full pull counts. In leaky, inflamed capillaries σ falls, proteins slip through, and their pull is lost. 3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid What fails What changes Examples Push too high Pc ↑ Blocked veins, heart failure, standing still, leaky vein valves Pull too weak πc ↓ (low albumin) Liver disease, kidney protein loss ( nephrotic syndrome), malnutrition Wall too leaky Kf ↑, σ ↓ Inflammation, burns, allergy Drain blocked Lymph flow ↓ Filariasis, lymph nodes removed in breast cancer surgery Too much fluid overall Salt and water retained Kidney failure Starling balance: what tips it towards oedema? The Starling balance interactive from Chapter 13, opening on Heart failure. Each scenario button shows which arrow changes and the net result. The key question for any swelling: which part failed? Different diseases can look alike on the outside but work in completely different ways. 4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema? If blood can't drain out of the veins, it backs up into the capillaries, raising Pc and pushing more fluid out. The lecture lists: Blocked veins (a clot in a leg vein): one leg swells. Leaky vein valves (varicose veins): blood falls back and pools. Standing still for long periods: gravity raises leg venous pressure, and the muscle pump isn't working, so the ankles swell. ( → Venous Return, §4) Heart failure: A failing right heart raises systemic venous pressure, causing swollen ankles. A failing left heart raises pressure in the lung veins, causing pulmonary oedema: fluid in the lungs, which stops oxygen getting into the blood and makes the patient breathless. Arteriole and venule tone matter too. Widening the arterioles lets more of the arterial pressure through to the capillaries. Tightening the venules holds blood back in them. Both raise Pc, and the lecture lists both as causes. 5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema? Albumin is the main protein holding water in the blood. If it falls, that pull weakens and fluid stays out in the tissues, often all over the body. Albumin falls when: The liver can't make enough: liver failure (cirrhosis). The kidneys leak it into the urine: nephrotic syndrome. The diet lacks protein: severe malnutrition (kwashiorkor). The lecture also mentions osmotically active substances building up in the tissues: they pull water out into the tissue from the other side. 6. How does inflammation increase oedema? Histamine, bradykinin and substance P make the capillary wall leaky: the gaps between endothelial cells widen. Water and proteins pour out. The escaped proteins now pull water outwards, adding to the swelling. This is why a sting or a sprain swells within minutes. It is not a pressure problem; the wall itself has changed. Understanding Check: Sections 1–6 1. A man has a clot blocking the main vein of one leg. Why does that leg swell? A. Blood backs up and raises capillary pressure, pushing more fluid out B. The clot releases proteins that pull water into the tissue C. The lymphatics in that leg are blocked by the clot D. Albumin in that leg is destroyed by the clot Show answer and explanation Answer: A. A blocked vein raises Pc upstream, so filtration rises in that leg only. 2. A child with severe protein malnutrition has a swollen belly and legs. What is the main mechanism? A. Raised capillary blood pressure from heart failure B. Low albumin weakens the pull holding water in the blood C. Inflammation makes every capillary leaky D. Blocked lymphatics in the abdomen Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Without enough dietary protein, albumin falls, and the plasma oncotic pull weakens throughout the body. 3. Why does a bee sting swell within minutes? A. The sting raises blood pressure in the arm B. The venom blocks the local lymph vessels C. Histamine makes capillaries leaky, so fluid and proteins escape D. The sting lowers the level of plasma albumin Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Inflammatory mediators open the gaps in the capillary wall. The leaked proteins then pull still more water out. 4. Why can left-sided heart failure make a patient breathless? A. Fluid collects around the heart and squeezes it B. Raised lung venous pressure pushes fluid into the lungs C. The right ventricle stops pumping blood to the lungs D. Low albumin causes swelling throughout the chest Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Back pressure into the lung capillaries causes pulmonary oedema, which blocks oxygen getting into the blood. 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling? Even with normal pressures, some fluid and protein leave the capillaries all the time, and only the lymphatics can take the protein back. If they are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. Over time it causes inflammation and scarring, and the swelling becomes firm and permanent: lymphoedema. The lecture's examples: Filariasis (elephantiasis): parasitic worms block the lymph vessels, so the legs or scrotum swell enormously. Radical mastectomy: removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery leaves that arm swollen. ( → Lymphatics) 8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different? Press a thumb firmly into swollen skin for a few seconds. Pitting oedema: a dent stays after you let go. The extra fluid is free to move and is pushed aside. This is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes. Non-pitting oedema: no dent forms. The tissue is firm, because the fluid is bound up with protein and the tissue has become scarred. Lymphoedema is usually non-pitting, but only once it is long-standing. Early lymphoedema can still pit, and it becomes firm as scarring develops. Source note: The lecture states that lymphoedema does not pit. That describes its usual late stage; early lymphoedema can pit. 9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism? Where the swelling is What it suggests Both ankles, worse at the end of the day Gravity plus raised venous pressure (standing, right heart failure) The lungs (breathlessness, crackles) Left heart failure, or leaky lung capillaries All over, including around the eyes Low albumin (kidney or liver disease, malnutrition) One arm or leg, firm Blocked lymphatics, or a blocked vein These are clues, not diagnoses. Heart and kidney disease often cause oedema by more than one route at once. Understanding Check: Sections 7–9 1. A woman's arm has been firmly swollen since her armpit lymph nodes were removed. What is the mechanism? A. Raised venous pressure from a clot in the arm B. Fluid and protein can't drain away through the lymphatics C. Low plasma albumin from the surgery D. Histamine released by the scar tissue Show answer and explanation Answer: B. With the nodes removed, protein-rich fluid can't drain, so lymphoedema develops. 2. You press on a patient's shin and a dent remains. What does that tell you? A. The extra fluid is free to move and was pushed aside B. The tissue is scarred and firm C. The swelling must be caused by blocked lymphatics D. The patient's albumin level must be normal Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Pitting means mobile fluid. It is typical of heart failure, low albumin and venous causes, and of early lymphoedema. 3. Why do proteins matter so much in lymphatic oedema? A. Only the lymphatics can return escaped proteins to the blood B. Proteins block the capillary pores and raise pressure C. Lymph contains no protein, so it dilutes the tissue D. Proteins make the veins collapse and stop draining Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Capillaries can't take protein back. If the lymph is blocked, protein builds up and holds water in the tissue. 10. Chapter Essentials Oedema = too much fluid between the cells. More leaves the capillaries than the lymphatics can carry away. Only two ways: too much filtration, or too little drainage. Five routes: push too high (Pc ↑), pull too weak (albumin ↓), wall too leaky, drain blocked, or too much fluid overall. Raised venous pressure: blocked veins, leaky valves, standing still, heart failure. Right failure → swollen ankles; left failure → pulmonary oedema. Low albumin: liver failure, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition → swelling everywhere. Inflammation: histamine, bradykinin and substance P make the wall leaky; escaped proteins pull more water out. Lymphatic blockage: filariasis, removal of lymph nodes → protein-rich, firm lymphoedema. Pitting = mobile fluid. Non-pitting = firm, scarred tissue (late lymphoedema). Ask where the swelling is: it points to the mechanism that failed."},{"slug":"lymphatics","title":"Lymphatic Circulation","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"60 and 94-95; Regional Circulation 47-48","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-lymphatic-vessels-if-we-already-have-veins","title":"1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins?"},{"id":"2-how-does-lymph-enter-a-lymphatic-capillary","title":"2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary?"},{"id":"3-how-can-lymph-move-when-there-is-no-central-lymphatic-heart","title":"3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart?"},{"id":"4-where-does-lymph-finally-return-to-blood","title":"4. Where does lymph finally return to blood?"},{"id":"5-why-is-the-intestinal-lymphatic-system-important-in-fat-absorption","title":"5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption?"},{"id":"6-how-does-lymphatic-drainage-protect-tissue-fluid-balance","title":"6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance?"},{"id":"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-lymphoedema","title":"7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema?"},{"id":"8-what-happens-if-lymphatic-function-is-lost","title":"8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost?"},{"id":"9-chapter-essentials","title":"9. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Lymphatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-lymphatic-vessels-if-we-already-have-veins\">1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins?</h2>\n<p>Every day, capillaries filter fluid and a little protein into the tissues, and most of it doesn't go straight back into the blood. <strong>Capillaries can't take protein back at all.</strong> Without another route, tissues would steadily swell. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\">→ Microcirculation, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The <strong><span data-term=\"lymphatic-system\">lymphatic system</span></strong> is that route. It collects the excess fluid, proteins and particles from the tissues and returns them to the veins. Once inside a lymph vessel, the fluid is called <strong>lymph</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Lymphatic return route\"><span>Capillary filtration</span><b>→</b><span>Interstitial fluid</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph capillaries</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph vessels and ducts</span><b>→</b><span>Venous blood</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The lymphatics are a main route, not a back-up.</strong> In most tissues, they return most of the fluid filtered from the capillaries. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\">→ Microcirculation, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"2-how-does-lymph-enter-a-lymphatic-capillary\">2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary?</h2>\n<p><strong>Lymph capillaries start as blind-ended tubes</strong> in the tissues, closed at one end like the finger of a glove. Their wall is one layer of endothelial cells whose edges <strong>overlap like roof tiles</strong>. Fine <strong><span data-term=\"anchoring-filaments\">anchoring filaments</span></strong> tie these cells to the surrounding tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>One-way flaps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When tissue fluid builds up, it pushes on the overlapping edges, and the anchoring filaments pull them apart, so the <strong>flaps open inwards</strong> and fluid, proteins and even bacteria flow in.</li>\n<li>When pressure inside the lymph capillary rises, the flaps are <strong>pushed shut</strong>, so nothing leaks back out.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure lymph-entry-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. From capillary filtration to a one-way lymphatic entry route</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 590 185\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original diagram of a blood capillary above an interstitial space, with arrows showing fluid filtration into a blind-ended green lymphatic capillary and one-way lymph movement towards a collecting vessel.\"><rect x=\"38\" y=\"28\" width=\"370\" height=\"40\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"#efc9cc\" stroke=\"#bb626e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g fill=\"#ac4857\"><circle cx=\"108\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"196\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"326\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/></g><path d=\"M220 73 V113m-7 -10 7 10 7 -10\" stroke=\"#d38428\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M36 145 Q37 121 70 121 L407 121 L407 166 L70 166 Q37 166 36 145 Z\" fill=\"#d9f3e6\" stroke=\"#3b9474\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M104 145H360m-10 -8 10 8 -10 8\" stroke=\"#16805e\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><g font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\"><text x=\"43\" y=\"17\">Blood capillary</text><text x=\"237\" y=\"94\">Filtered fluid ↓</text><text x=\"65\" y=\"112\">Interstitial space</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"174\">Initial lymphatic capillary →</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"144\">To larger</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"159\">lymphatics</text></g></svg><p>Original conceptual schematic based on the lecturer's lymphatic motion/pressure-gradient diagram. It illustrates fluid entry and one-way transport, not exact vessel anatomy.</p></figure>\n\n<p>This is how <strong>proteins</strong> get back into the circulation. They are too big to re-enter blood capillaries, but they pass easily through the lymphatic flaps.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-can-lymph-move-when-there-is-no-central-lymphatic-heart\">3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart?</h2>\n<p>Lymph flows at very low pressure, with no heart to pump it. Several things keep it moving:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The vessels pump themselves.</strong> Larger lymph vessels have smooth muscle that contracts rhythmically, segment by segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Valves</strong> inside the vessels allow flow only towards the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>The skeletal muscle pump.</strong> Moving muscles squeeze the lymph vessels, just as they squeeze veins. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work\">→ Venous Return, §4</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Breathing.</strong> Pressure changes in the chest and abdomen draw lymph upwards.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulsing arteries</strong> next to lymph vessels squeeze them with each beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Like veins, but with one key difference:</strong> veins continue on from blood capillaries in a closed circuit. Lymphatics <strong>start blind, in the tissue</strong>, and only join the blood at the very end.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why do we need lymphatics if capillaries already return some fluid?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillaries can't take back protein that has leaked into the tissues</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Lymphatics carry oxygen to tissues that capillaries miss</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Capillaries only filter fluid at night, so lymph covers the day</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Lymphatics are needed only when blood pressure is high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Only the lymphatics can return escaped protein, and in most tissues they also return most of the filtered fluid.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How do lymph capillaries let fluid in but not out?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They have thick muscular walls that pump fluid in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their overlapping endothelial flaps open inwards and shut against backflow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Proteins inside them pull fluid in by oncotic pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They are connected directly to the blood capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Fluid pressure opens the overlapping edges inwards. A rise in pressure inside pushes them shut.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Lymph has no heart to pump it. Which of these does NOT help it move?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Contractions of smooth muscle in the lymph vessel walls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. One-way valves inside the lymph vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Squeezing by nearby skeletal muscles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pumping by the lymph nodes</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Lymph nodes filter lymph for the immune system. They are not pumps.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-where-does-lymph-finally-return-to-blood\">4. Where does lymph finally return to blood?</h2>\n<p>All lymph drains into two main ducts. Each empties into the veins at the <strong><span data-term=\"venous-angle\">venous angle</span></strong>, where the internal jugular vein joins the subclavian vein:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Duct</th>\n  <th>What it drains</th>\n  <th>Where it empties</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Thoracic duct</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Everything except the right upper quarter</strong>: both legs, the abdomen, the left side of the chest, left arm, left head and neck</td>\n  <td><strong>Left</strong> venous angle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Right lymphatic duct</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>The right upper quarter only</strong>: the right side of the head and neck, the right arm, the right side of the chest</td>\n  <td><strong>Right</strong> venous angle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure lymph-duct-figure\" id=\"cvs15-ducts\"><figcaption>Figure 02. Which duct drains where?</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 430\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Front view of a body. The patient's right side of the head and neck, right arm and right side of the chest are shaded as drained by the right lymphatic duct. Everything else, including both legs, the abdomen and the whole left side, is shaded as drained by the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct runs up the middle of the body to the left venous angle at the base of the neck; the short right lymphatic duct ends at the right venous angle.\"><defs><clipPath id=\"ly-ruq\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"200\" height=\"190\"/></clipPath><g id=\"ly-trunk\"><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"52\" r=\"30\"/><rect x=\"188\" y=\"80\" width=\"24\" height=\"22\"/><path d=\"M130 102 H270 Q282 104 280 120 L262 262 H138 L120 120 Q118 104 130 102 Z\"/></g></defs><g class=\"ly-td\"><use href=\"#ly-trunk\"/><rect x=\"88\" y=\"108\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\"/><rect x=\"284\" y=\"108\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\"/><rect x=\"142\" y=\"262\" width=\"50\" height=\"150\" rx=\"16\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"262\" width=\"50\" height=\"150\" rx=\"16\"/></g><g class=\"ly-rd\"><use href=\"#ly-trunk\" clip-path=\"url(#ly-ruq)\"/><rect x=\"88\" y=\"108\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\"/></g><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"190\" class=\"ly-dia\"/><path d=\"M200 232 C204 190 196 150 202 118 Q206 104 222 100\" class=\"ly-duct\"/><path d=\"M190 116 Q184 106 176 100\" class=\"ly-duct\"/><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" class=\"ly-angle\"/><circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" class=\"ly-angle\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"30\" class=\"ly-lab ly-lab-rd\">Right lymphatic duct:</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"46\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">right upper quarter</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"330\" class=\"ly-lab ly-lab-td\">Thoracic duct:</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"346\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">everything else</text><text x=\"232\" y=\"96\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">left venous angle</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"96\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">right venous angle →</text><text x=\"124\" y=\"185\" class=\"ly-lab-xs\">level of the diaphragm</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"425\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\" text-anchor=\"middle\">patient's right</text><text x=\"298\" y=\"425\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\" text-anchor=\"middle\">patient's left</text></svg><p>Original DOMiNANCE diagram, front view (the patient's right is on your left). The thoracic duct starts in the abdomen and runs up the middle of the body to the left venous angle. The short right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper quarter, into the right venous angle.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Memory hook:</strong> the thoracic duct takes <strong>three-quarters</strong> of the body; the right lymphatic duct takes the <strong>right upper quarter</strong>.</p>\n<h3>What do lymph nodes do?</h3>\n<p>On its way, lymph passes through <strong>lymph nodes</strong>: bean-shaped filters packed with immune cells. They check the lymph for bacteria, viruses and cancer cells. That is why nodes swell during an infection, and why cancer often spreads first to the nearest nodes. <strong>Nodes filter; they don't pump.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-is-the-intestinal-lymphatic-system-important-in-fat-absorption\">5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption?</h2>\n<p>Each finger-like villus of the small intestine contains a lymph capillary called a <strong><span data-term=\"lacteal\">lacteal</span></strong> (<em>lac</em> = milk, after the milky look of lymph after a fatty meal).</p>\n<p><strong>How fat gets into the blood:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Intestinal lipid absorption through lymphatics\"><span>Long-chain dietary lipids</span><b>→</b><span>Enterocyte chylomicrons</span><b>→</b><span>Villus lacteal</span><b>→</b><span>Mesenteric lymph</span><b>→</b><span>Thoracic duct → Blood</span></div>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Gut cells absorb the digested fats and repackage them into large fat droplets wrapped in protein: <strong><span data-term=\"chylomicron\">chylomicrons</span></strong>.</li>\n<li>Chylomicrons are <strong>too big</strong> to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the leaky <strong>lacteals</strong> instead.</li>\n<li>They travel up the lymphatics and the thoracic duct, and enter the blood at the left venous angle.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This milky, fat-rich lymph from the gut is called <strong><span data-term=\"chyle\">chyle</span></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Not all fat goes this way.</strong> Short- and medium-chain fatty acids are small enough to go straight into the blood capillaries, and on to the liver in the portal vein. It is the <strong>long-chain fats</strong> (and fat-soluble vitamins) that need the lymph.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Lymph from the left leg reaches the blood through which duct?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The right lymphatic duct, at the right venous angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The thoracic duct, at the left venous angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The thoracic duct, at the right venous angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The portal vein, through the liver</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper quarter, and empties at the left venous angle.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why do long-chain dietary fats enter lacteals rather than blood capillaries?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood capillaries in the gut have no openings</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They are packaged into chylomicrons, too big for blood capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Lacteals actively pump fat out of the gut cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Fat would raise blood pressure if it entered the blood directly</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Chylomicrons are large particles. They pass through the leaky lacteal wall but not into blood capillaries.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why do lymph nodes in the neck swell during a throat infection?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Lymph backs up behind the nodes because they stop pumping</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Immune cells in the nodes multiply as they respond to the infection</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Fat from the throat collects in the nodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The nodes fill with blood from inflamed capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Nodes are immune filters. When they trap microbes, their immune cells multiply and the nodes enlarge.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-lymphatic-drainage-protect-tissue-fluid-balance\">6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance?</h2>\n<p>The lymphatics keep the tissues from swelling by matching what the capillaries filter. When filtration rises, lymph flow can rise several-fold to keep up, which is a safety margin. <strong>Oedema appears only when filtration outstrips this, or when the lymphatics themselves fail.</strong> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=1-what-exactly-is-oedema\">→ Oedema, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Problem</th>\n  <th>What fails</th>\n  <th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Too much filtration</strong></td>\n  <td>The lymphatics can't keep up</td>\n  <td>Venous congestion, heart failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Too little drainage</strong></td>\n  <td>Normal filtration, but it can't be removed</td>\n  <td>Filariasis, surgery that removes lymph nodes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-lymphoedema\">7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema?</h2>\n<p>When lymphatics are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. The protein holds the water in the tissue, and over time causes inflammation and scarring. The result is <strong>lymphoedema</strong>: swelling that is soft at first and becomes <strong>firm and non-pitting</strong> as it lasts. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different\">→ Oedema, §8</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The lecture's examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filariasis:</strong> parasitic worms (spread by mosquitoes) live in and damage the lymph vessels, and can cause the gross swelling of <strong><span data-term=\"elephantiasis\">elephantiasis</span></strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Radical mastectomy:</strong> removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery can leave the arm swollen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cancer blocking lymph nodes, and lymphatics that never formed properly (congenital), can do the same.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-what-happens-if-lymphatic-function-is-lost\">8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost?</h2>\n<p>Three jobs fail:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluid balance:</strong> fluid and protein stay in the tissues, causing lymphoedema.</li>\n<li><strong>Immune defence:</strong> lymph no longer reaches the nodes, so infections are detected and fought less well, and swollen limbs get infected easily.</li>\n<li><strong>Fat absorption:</strong> if the gut lymphatics fail, long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins can't be absorbed properly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–8</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Filtration into a tissue doubles, but the tissue doesn't swell. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillaries reabsorb all the extra fluid at the venous end</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Lymph flow rises to carry away the extra fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The extra fluid is absorbed by the tissue cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Filtration can't double without the tissue swelling</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Lymph flow can increase several-fold, a safety margin against oedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A man from an area where filariasis is common has one hugely swollen, thickened leg. What is the mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Worms in the lymph vessels block drainage, so protein-rich fluid builds up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Worms in the veins block blood returning from the leg</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The infection lowers his plasma albumin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The worms release histamine that makes capillaries leaky</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Filarial worms damage the lymphatics. Protein-rich fluid accumulates, and chronic inflammation causes the thick, firm swelling of elephantiasis.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which problem would loss of the gut lymphatics specifically cause?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Inability to absorb glucose from the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Inability to absorb long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Inability to absorb water from the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Inability to absorb amino acids from the gut</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Long-chain fats travel as chylomicrons through the lacteals. Glucose, amino acids and water go straight into the blood.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\">9. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lymphatics return</strong> excess tissue fluid and <strong>all escaped protein</strong> to the blood. They are a main route, not a back-up.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph capillaries</strong> are blind-ended, with overlapping flaps that open inwards and shut against backflow.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph moves</strong> by vessel-wall contractions, valves, the muscle pump, breathing and pulsing arteries. There is no heart, and nodes don't pump.</li>\n<li><strong>Thoracic duct</strong> drains three-quarters of the body into the <strong>left</strong> venous angle. <strong>Right lymphatic duct</strong> drains the right upper quarter into the <strong>right</strong> venous angle.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph nodes</strong> filter lymph for the immune system.</li>\n<li><strong>Lacteals</strong> carry long-chain fats as <strong>chylomicrons</strong>. Milky gut lymph is <strong>chyle</strong>. Short- and medium-chain fats go straight to the blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph flow can rise several-fold</strong> to prevent oedema. Swelling appears when filtration outstrips it or drainage fails.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphoedema</strong> (filariasis, node removal, cancer): protein-rich, becoming firm and non-pitting.</li>\n<li><strong>Lost lymphatics</strong> → swelling, weaker immune defence, poor fat absorption.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Lymphatic Circulation 1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins? Every day, capillaries filter fluid and a little protein into the tissues, and most of it doesn't go straight back into the blood. Capillaries can't take protein back at all. Without another route, tissues would steadily swell. ( → Microcirculation, §6) The lymphatic system is that route. It collects the excess fluid, proteins and particles from the tissues and returns them to the veins. Once inside a lymph vessel, the fluid is called lymph. Capillary filtration → Interstitial fluid → Lymph capillaries → Lymph vessels and ducts → Venous blood The lymphatics are a main route, not a back-up. In most tissues, they return most of the fluid filtered from the capillaries. ( → Microcirculation, §6) 2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary? Lymph capillaries start as blind-ended tubes in the tissues, closed at one end like the finger of a glove. Their wall is one layer of endothelial cells whose edges overlap like roof tiles. Fine anchoring filaments tie these cells to the surrounding tissue. One-way flaps: When tissue fluid builds up, it pushes on the overlapping edges, and the anchoring filaments pull them apart, so the flaps open inwards and fluid, proteins and even bacteria flow in. When pressure inside the lymph capillary rises, the flaps are pushed shut, so nothing leaks back out. Figure 01. From capillary filtration to a one-way lymphatic entry route Blood capillary Filtered fluid ↓ Interstitial space Initial lymphatic capillary → To larger lymphatics Original conceptual schematic based on the lecturer's lymphatic motion/pressure-gradient diagram. It illustrates fluid entry and one-way transport, not exact vessel anatomy. This is how proteins get back into the circulation. They are too big to re-enter blood capillaries, but they pass easily through the lymphatic flaps. 3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart? Lymph flows at very low pressure, with no heart to pump it. Several things keep it moving: The vessels pump themselves. Larger lymph vessels have smooth muscle that contracts rhythmically, segment by segment. Valves inside the vessels allow flow only towards the heart. The skeletal muscle pump. Moving muscles squeeze the lymph vessels, just as they squeeze veins. ( → Venous Return, §4) Breathing. Pressure changes in the chest and abdomen draw lymph upwards. Pulsing arteries next to lymph vessels squeeze them with each beat. Like veins, but with one key difference: veins continue on from blood capillaries in a closed circuit. Lymphatics start blind, in the tissue, and only join the blood at the very end. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why do we need lymphatics if capillaries already return some fluid? A. Capillaries can't take back protein that has leaked into the tissues B. Lymphatics carry oxygen to tissues that capillaries miss C. Capillaries only filter fluid at night, so lymph covers the day D. Lymphatics are needed only when blood pressure is high Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Only the lymphatics can return escaped protein, and in most tissues they also return most of the filtered fluid. 2. How do lymph capillaries let fluid in but not out? A. They have thick muscular walls that pump fluid in B. Their overlapping endothelial flaps open inwards and shut against backflow C. Proteins inside them pull fluid in by oncotic pressure D. They are connected directly to the blood capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Fluid pressure opens the overlapping edges inwards. A rise in pressure inside pushes them shut. 3. Lymph has no heart to pump it. Which of these does NOT help it move? A. Contractions of smooth muscle in the lymph vessel walls B. One-way valves inside the lymph vessels C. Squeezing by nearby skeletal muscles D. Pumping by the lymph nodes Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Lymph nodes filter lymph for the immune system. They are not pumps. 4. Where does lymph finally return to blood? All lymph drains into two main ducts. Each empties into the veins at the venous angle, where the internal jugular vein joins the subclavian vein: Duct What it drains Where it empties Thoracic duct Everything except the right upper quarter: both legs, the abdomen, the left side of the chest, left arm, left head and neck Left venous angle Right lymphatic duct The right upper quarter only: the right side of the head and neck, the right arm, the right side of the chest Right venous angle Figure 02. Which duct drains where? Right lymphatic duct: right upper quarter Thoracic duct: everything else left venous angle right venous angle → level of the diaphragm patient's right patient's left Original DOMiNANCE diagram, front view (the patient's right is on your left). The thoracic duct starts in the abdomen and runs up the middle of the body to the left venous angle. The short right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper quarter, into the right venous angle. Memory hook: the thoracic duct takes three-quarters of the body; the right lymphatic duct takes the right upper quarter. What do lymph nodes do? On its way, lymph passes through lymph nodes: bean-shaped filters packed with immune cells. They check the lymph for bacteria, viruses and cancer cells. That is why nodes swell during an infection, and why cancer often spreads first to the nearest nodes. Nodes filter; they don't pump. 5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption? Each finger-like villus of the small intestine contains a lymph capillary called a lacteal ( lac = milk, after the milky look of lymph after a fatty meal). How fat gets into the blood: Long-chain dietary lipids → Enterocyte chylomicrons → Villus lacteal → Mesenteric lymph → Thoracic duct → Blood Gut cells absorb the digested fats and repackage them into large fat droplets wrapped in protein: chylomicrons. Chylomicrons are too big to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the leaky lacteals instead. They travel up the lymphatics and the thoracic duct, and enter the blood at the left venous angle. This milky, fat-rich lymph from the gut is called chyle. Not all fat goes this way. Short- and medium-chain fatty acids are small enough to go straight into the blood capillaries, and on to the liver in the portal vein. It is the long-chain fats (and fat-soluble vitamins) that need the lymph. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. Lymph from the left leg reaches the blood through which duct? A. The right lymphatic duct, at the right venous angle B. The thoracic duct, at the left venous angle C. The thoracic duct, at the right venous angle D. The portal vein, through the liver Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper quarter, and empties at the left venous angle. 2. Why do long-chain dietary fats enter lacteals rather than blood capillaries? A. Blood capillaries in the gut have no openings B. They are packaged into chylomicrons, too big for blood capillaries C. Lacteals actively pump fat out of the gut cells D. Fat would raise blood pressure if it entered the blood directly Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Chylomicrons are large particles. They pass through the leaky lacteal wall but not into blood capillaries. 3. Why do lymph nodes in the neck swell during a throat infection? A. Lymph backs up behind the nodes because they stop pumping B. Immune cells in the nodes multiply as they respond to the infection C. Fat from the throat collects in the nodes D. The nodes fill with blood from inflamed capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Nodes are immune filters. When they trap microbes, their immune cells multiply and the nodes enlarge. 6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance? The lymphatics keep the tissues from swelling by matching what the capillaries filter. When filtration rises, lymph flow can rise several-fold to keep up, which is a safety margin. Oedema appears only when filtration outstrips this, or when the lymphatics themselves fail. ( → Oedema, §1) Problem What fails Example Too much filtration The lymphatics can't keep up Venous congestion, heart failure Too little drainage Normal filtration, but it can't be removed Filariasis, surgery that removes lymph nodes 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema? When lymphatics are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. The protein holds the water in the tissue, and over time causes inflammation and scarring. The result is lymphoedema: swelling that is soft at first and becomes firm and non-pitting as it lasts. ( → Oedema, §8) The lecture's examples: Filariasis: parasitic worms (spread by mosquitoes) live in and damage the lymph vessels, and can cause the gross swelling of elephantiasis. Radical mastectomy: removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery can leave the arm swollen. Cancer blocking lymph nodes, and lymphatics that never formed properly (congenital), can do the same. 8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost? Three jobs fail: Fluid balance: fluid and protein stay in the tissues, causing lymphoedema. Immune defence: lymph no longer reaches the nodes, so infections are detected and fought less well, and swollen limbs get infected easily. Fat absorption: if the gut lymphatics fail, long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins can't be absorbed properly. Understanding Check: Sections 6–8 1. Filtration into a tissue doubles, but the tissue doesn't swell. Why? A. Capillaries reabsorb all the extra fluid at the venous end B. Lymph flow rises to carry away the extra fluid C. The extra fluid is absorbed by the tissue cells D. Filtration can't double without the tissue swelling Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Lymph flow can increase several-fold, a safety margin against oedema. 2. A man from an area where filariasis is common has one hugely swollen, thickened leg. What is the mechanism? A. Worms in the lymph vessels block drainage, so protein-rich fluid builds up B. Worms in the veins block blood returning from the leg C. The infection lowers his plasma albumin D. The worms release histamine that makes capillaries leaky Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Filarial worms damage the lymphatics. Protein-rich fluid accumulates, and chronic inflammation causes the thick, firm swelling of elephantiasis. 3. Which problem would loss of the gut lymphatics specifically cause? A. Inability to absorb glucose from the gut B. Inability to absorb long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins C. Inability to absorb water from the gut D. Inability to absorb amino acids from the gut Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Long-chain fats travel as chylomicrons through the lacteals. Glucose, amino acids and water go straight into the blood. 9. Chapter Essentials Lymphatics return excess tissue fluid and all escaped protein to the blood. They are a main route, not a back-up. Lymph capillaries are blind-ended, with overlapping flaps that open inwards and shut against backflow. Lymph moves by vessel-wall contractions, valves, the muscle pump, breathing and pulsing arteries. There is no heart, and nodes don't pump. Thoracic duct drains three-quarters of the body into the left venous angle. Right lymphatic duct drains the right upper quarter into the right venous angle. Lymph nodes filter lymph for the immune system. Lacteals carry long-chain fats as chylomicrons. Milky gut lymph is chyle. Short- and medium-chain fats go straight to the blood. Lymph flow can rise several-fold to prevent oedema. Swelling appears when filtration outstrips it or drainage fails. Lymphoedema (filariasis, node removal, cancer): protein-rich, becoming firm and non-pitting. Lost lymphatics → swelling, weaker immune defence, poor fat absorption."},{"slug":"local-regulation","title":"Local Control of Blood Flow","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"1-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-local-control","title":"1. Why does a tissue need local control?"},{"id":"2-autoregulation-keeping-blood-flow-relatively-steady","title":"2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady"},{"id":"3-two-complementary-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","title":"3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic"},{"id":"4-active-versus-reactive-hyperaemia","title":"4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia"},{"id":"5-what-does-the-endothelium-contribute","title":"5. What does the endothelium contribute?"},{"id":"6-laplace-s-principle-pressure-radius-tension-and-stress","title":"6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress"},{"id":"7-bringing-local-and-whole-body-control-together","title":"7. Bringing local and whole-body control together"},{"id":"more-connections-worth-understanding","title":"More connections worth understanding"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"references-and-scope-notes","title":"References and scope notes"}],"html":"<h1>Local Control of Blood Flow</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-local-control\">1. Why does a tissue need local control?</h2>\n<p>The heart generates flow, but every organ must receive a supply matched to its moment-to-moment needs. During exercise, contracting muscles require more oxygen and nutrient delivery; meanwhile the brain needs fairly stable perfusion despite ordinary blood-pressure fluctuations. <strong>Local blood-flow control</strong> means a vascular bed adjusts the calibre of its own resistance vessels, especially small arteries and arterioles.</p>\n<p>The starting relationship is <strong>Q = ΔP / R</strong>, where Q is volume flow (for example mL/min), ΔP is the pressure difference across a vascular bed (mmHg), and R is vascular resistance (mmHg·min/mL). If pressure rises at unchanged resistance, flow rises. If local arterioles constrict enough to raise resistance, that rise in flow can be opposed.</p>\n<p>In an ideal rigid tube with laminar flow, <strong>R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)</strong> (Poiseuille relationship). η is dynamic viscosity, L is length and r is radius. Living vessels are not rigid tubes, but the fourth-power dependence explains why even a modest arteriolar radius change can strongly affect resistance. Halving radius would increase idealized resistance <strong>16-fold</strong> when the other variables are unchanged; do not treat this as an exact prediction for an intact circulation.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Local flow adjustment\"><span>Tissue activity or perfusion pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Local sensing</span><b>→</b><span>Arteriolar tone changes</span><b>→</b><span>Resistance and flow adjust</span></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-autoregulation-keeping-blood-flow-relatively-steady\">2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady</h2>\n<p><strong>Autoregulation</strong> is the ability of an organ to maintain relatively constant blood flow across a <em>range</em> of perfusion pressures by changing local vascular resistance. It does not mean perfect flow constancy, and it fails outside the organ's effective autoregulatory range. Cerebral and renal circulations are especially important examples; coronary regulation also strongly matches flow to metabolic demand.</p>\n<p>If perfusion pressure suddenly rises, flow initially tends to increase. Local arterioles then <strong>constrict</strong>, increasing resistance to counter the rise. If pressure falls moderately, they tend to <strong>dilate</strong> and lower resistance to defend flow. This is a local response and need not wait for a change in the heart's output.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Disturbance</th>\n  <th>Initial passive tendency</th>\n  <th>Compensatory change</th>\n  <th>Intended result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Increased perfusion pressure</td>\n  <td>Flow rises</td>\n  <td>Arteriolar constriction increases R</td>\n  <td>Flow moves back toward baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Decreased perfusion pressure</td>\n  <td>Flow falls</td>\n  <td>Arteriolar dilation decreases R</td>\n  <td>Flow moves back toward baseline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> If pressure becomes too low for further dilation, blood flow falls despite maximal compensation. Thus autoregulation is not a guarantee against shock or severe hypotension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-two-complementary-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic\">3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic</h2>\n<h3>Myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch</h3>\n<p>An arteriole experiences <strong>transmural pressure = intravascular pressure − surrounding tissue pressure</strong>. Higher transmural pressure distends its wall, activating vascular smooth-muscle signalling that increases contraction. Constriction reduces radius and raises resistance. Lower pressure reduces wall stretch, permitting relaxation and dilation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this useful?</strong> Without the active response, increased pressure would push extra blood through vulnerable small vessels. Myogenic autoregulation helps moderate flow variation and microvascular pressure, notably in cerebral and renal beds.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 140\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Higher transmural pressure stretches an arteriole, triggering smooth-muscle contraction, raising resistance and opposing a large rise in flow\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e9f3fd\" stroke=\"#4d84aa\"/><rect x=\"224\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e4f4ea\" stroke=\"#4e9b74\"/><rect x=\"443\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2d6\" stroke=\"#b88933\"/><g fill=\"#20324b\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"91\" y=\"57\">Pressure ↑</text><text x=\"91\" y=\"77\">Wall stretch ↑</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"57\">Smooth muscle</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"77\">contracts</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"57\">Radius ↓; R ↑</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"77\">Flow rise opposed</text></g><path d=\"M181 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7M400 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7\" stroke=\"#527895\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></svg><p>Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>Metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs</h3>\n<p>When metabolism increases or perfusion is inadequate, local vasodilator influences rise. Depending on the tissue, these may include <strong>adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, extracellular K⁺ and reduced tissue oxygen tension</strong>. The relative importance of each signal differs among organs. Dilation lowers local resistance and improves delivery; increasing flow can also wash out accumulated signals.</p>\n<p>Example: A vigorously contracting skeletal muscle consumes oxygen faster and produces metabolites. Its small resistance vessels dilate; muscle flow increases <strong>even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Distinguish the triggers:</strong> Myogenic control principally senses <strong>vessel stretch/pressure</strong>, whereas metabolic control senses <strong>local supply relative to demand</strong>. Both can act simultaneously; this is not an either/or choice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-active-versus-reactive-hyperaemia\">4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia</h2>\n<p><strong>Hyperaemia</strong> means increased blood flow.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Active hyperaemia</th>\n  <th>Reactive hyperaemia</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Occurs <strong>during increased tissue activity</strong></td>\n  <td>Occurs <strong>after a period of reduced or interrupted flow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Example: working skeletal muscle</td>\n  <td>Example: increased hand flow after releasing a temporary occlusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Increased metabolism promotes dilation</td>\n  <td>Ischaemia-associated metabolites, myogenic changes and other signals contribute; release restores driving pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Flow rises to meet ongoing demand</td>\n  <td>A temporary overshoot helps repay the supply deficit and dissipate vasodilator influences</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A useful sequence for <strong>reactive hyperaemia</strong> is: brief arterial occlusion → oxygen delivery falls and vasodilator influences develop → obstruction is released → previously dilated vascular bed receives a transient flow surge → flow gradually returns toward baseline. The magnitude and duration depend on the degree and length of occlusion and on the tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse these with autoregulation.</strong> Autoregulation describes relative <strong>stability</strong> of flow when perfusion pressure changes; hyperaemia describes an <strong>increase</strong> in flow in response to activity or prior underperfusion. Some underlying mechanisms overlap.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-does-the-endothelium-contribute\">5. What does the endothelium contribute?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>endothelium</strong> is more than a passive lining. It senses shear stress and chemical signals and releases substances that modify underlying smooth-muscle tone.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nitric oxide (NO):</strong> endothelial NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates <strong>soluble guanylyl cyclase</strong>, raises <strong>cGMP</strong>, and promotes relaxation. Shear stress from flowing blood can stimulate endothelial NO production.</li>\n<li><strong>Endothelin-1:</strong> a powerful vasoconstrictor peptide acting principally via smooth-muscle ETᴀ receptors; endothelial ETʙ signalling can have different effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Prostacyclin (PGI₂):</strong> an endothelial vasodilator that also inhibits platelet aggregation.</li>\n<li><strong>Histamine and bradykinin:</strong> can promote endothelial-dependent vasodilation, and in suitable vascular beds increase permeability; their effects vary with receptor and tissue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Application:</strong> Endothelial dysfunction can reduce NO bioavailability, contributing to impaired vasodilation. It should not be equated with the complete disappearance of myogenic or metabolic control.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-laplace-s-principle-pressure-radius-tension-and-stress\">6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress</h2>\n<p>One useful relationship is Laplace's law near myogenic regulation. It is important to specify <strong>which geometry</strong> and <strong>which quantity</strong> a formula describes.</p>\n<p>For an ideal <strong>thin-walled cylindrical vessel</strong>, circumferential <em>wall tension per unit axial length</em> is approximately <strong>T = Ptm × r</strong>, where Ptm is transmural pressure and r is internal radius. For average circumferential <strong>wall stress</strong>, <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r / h</strong>, with wall thickness h. For an ideal thin-walled <em>spherical</em> membrane, <strong>T = Ptm × r / 2</strong>. These idealizations are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p>At a fixed pressure and thickness, larger vessel radius implies greater average circumferential stress. However, <strong>Laplace's relationship does not by itself explain myogenic signalling</strong>: living vascular smooth muscle detects deformation and activates ion-channel and contractile pathways. Real arteries have finite wall thickness and active, nonlinear walls.</p>\n<p><strong>Numerical reasoning:</strong> Two thin-walled cylindrical vessels have equal Ptm and h. If one has twice the radius, its estimated wall tension and stress are each twice as large. It would be incorrect to say resistance also doubles: resistance responds oppositely and much more strongly to radius in the simplified Poiseuille model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-bringing-local-and-whole-body-control-together\">7. Bringing local and whole-body control together</h2>\n<p>Local regulation operates alongside the autonomic nervous system and circulating hormones. In exercise, <strong>active skeletal muscle</strong> can show powerful metabolic vasodilation even while sympathetic activity increases elsewhere. This is one reason blood supply can be redistributed to working tissue rather than rising equally in every organ.</p>\n<p>A fall in systemic pressure can trigger <strong>baroreflex sympathetic vasoconstriction</strong>, while critically underperfused tissues simultaneously accumulate local dilator signals. Which effect dominates depends on organ, severity, receptors, and physiological state. The next chapters examine the systemic side of this interaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical application:</strong> If systemic shock drives perfusion pressure below the lower autoregulatory limit, local dilation cannot maintain normal cerebral or renal flow. This connects local autoregulation directly to shock and organ dysfunction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"more-connections-worth-understanding\">More connections worth understanding</h2>\n<p>The lecture lists rising temperature, low pH, increased carbon dioxide, potassium, lactate, cardiac ADP, bradykinin, substance P, histamine H2 signalling and ANP among local vasodilator-associated influences. Effects vary by tissue and receptor. It lists serotonin, cooling and angiotensin among constrictor influences. The two-curvature Laplace form P = T(1/R1 + 1/R2) is not mathematically equivalent to the slide's later expression P = T/(R1 + R2); that second expression must not be taught as a rearrangement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01.</strong> Perfusion pressure through a vascular bed doubles while its resistance remains unchanged. What happens to flow initially, and what local adjustment can defend its baseline?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>From Q = ΔP/R, flow initially doubles if R is fixed. Autoregulation can increase arteriolar resistance through constriction, opposing the rise. A perfect twofold adjustment is an idealization; real responses vary by tissue and pressure range.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02.</strong> Why are arterioles more effective local flow regulators than large veins?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Arterioles contain actively controlled vascular smooth muscle and supply a large share of systemic vascular resistance. Small radius changes alter resistance substantially. Veins primarily serve as capacitance vessels and modulate venous return rather than controlling each tissue's entry resistance.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03.</strong> When a renal resistance arteriole experiences increased transmural pressure, why may its smooth muscle contract rather than simply remain stretched?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Stretch activates a myogenic smooth-muscle response that increases tone. A smaller radius raises resistance and helps counter an otherwise excessive pressure-driven rise in flow. The response is active, not merely elastic recoil.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04.</strong> A student says myogenic and metabolic autoregulation are the same because both change arteriolar radius. What is missing?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The initiating signals differ. Myogenic regulation responds chiefly to pressure-driven vessel stretch. Metabolic regulation responds to a mismatch between local perfusion and metabolic demand, via signals such as adenosine, H⁺ and K⁺. They can cooperate in the same vascular bed.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05.</strong> During a sprint, muscle perfusion increases even though arterial pressure barely changes. Explain the local mechanism.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Working muscle increases its metabolic demand. Local vasodilator signals lower arteriolar resistance; therefore flow can rise at nearly constant driving pressure according to Q = ΔP/R. This is active hyperaemia.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06.</strong> Blood flow surges briefly after a pressure cuff is released. Is this active hyperaemia, reactive hyperaemia or autoregulation?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reactive hyperaemia. Temporary occlusion reduced oxygen supply and fostered vasodilator influences; reopening permits a transient overshoot before signals dissipate. The defining feature is increased flow after preceding underperfusion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07.</strong> Does adenosine have identical importance in every vascular bed? Explain.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Local metabolic mediators differ by organ and context. Adenosine is influential in some settings, especially coronary metabolic regulation, while combinations of oxygen, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, endothelial and myogenic influences contribute elsewhere. Avoid memorising a universal single-metabolite explanation.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08.</strong> Trace the intracellular pathway by which endothelial NO relaxes adjacent vascular smooth muscle.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Endothelial NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and promoting smooth-muscle relaxation. This increases vessel radius, decreases resistance and can increase flow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09.</strong> Why can endothelin-1 and nitric oxide have opposite effects on local flow?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>They affect contractile tone differently: endothelin-1 typically stimulates smooth-muscle contraction, whereas NO promotes cGMP-dependent relaxation. The final response depends on their relative signalling and the receptors of the vascular bed.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10.</strong> Two ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessels face equal transmural pressure. One has double the radius and the same wall thickness. Compare estimated wall stress.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Average circumferential stress is approximately σθ = Ptm × r/h. With equal pressure and thickness, doubling radius doubles estimated stress. This is distinct from Poiseuille resistance, which decreases sharply as radius increases in the ideal rigid-tube model.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11.</strong> Why can autoregulation not fully protect the brain during profound haemorrhagic shock?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Autoregulation is effective only over a bounded pressure range. Below its lower limit, resistance vessels cannot dilate sufficiently to preserve flow; critically reduced perfusion pressure then leads to inadequate cerebral blood delivery despite local compensation.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12.</strong> A patient exercising has sympathetic activation, yet blood flow rises in active muscle. Is there a contradiction?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Sympathetic vasoconstrictor influence and local metabolic vasodilation act together. In active muscle, local demand-related dilation can strongly attenuate sympathetic constriction, so that perfusion increases while flow in less active beds may be restricted.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Tissue flow depends on pressure difference and vascular resistance: <strong>Q = ΔP/R</strong>.</li>\n<li>Arteriolar calibre is a major adjustable determinant of local resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> defends relatively stable flow only within a limited pressure range.</li>\n<li>Increased vessel stretch often produces <strong>myogenic constriction</strong>.</li>\n<li>A tissue with increased metabolic demand tends to develop <strong>metabolic vasodilation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Active hyperaemia</strong> accompanies increased tissue activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong> follows temporary underperfusion.</li>\n<li>Endothelial <strong>NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → cGMP</strong> promotes smooth-muscle relaxation.</li>\n<li><strong>Endothelin-1</strong> commonly promotes vasoconstriction; endothelial responses are context-dependent.</li>\n<li>Cylindrical wall tension <strong>T ≈ Ptm × r</strong> and wall stress <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r/h</strong> require their assumptions.</li>\n<li>Local and systemic controls may compete or cooperate in one vascular bed.</li>\n<li>Severe shock can push perfusion below the effective autoregulatory range.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"references-and-scope-notes\">References and scope notes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lecturer scope:</strong> CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, pp. 1–11 (local regulation, myogenic/metabolic theories, Laplace and mediators). Slide numbering/source mapping should be rechecked against the exact uploaded lecture PDF before merging.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference physiology:</strong> Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>, chapters on local and humoral control of tissue blood flow; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>, vascular smooth-muscle and regional-flow control.</li>\n<li><strong>Supplementary precision:</strong> Autoregulatory ranges are tissue-specific; mediator examples are not universal; Laplace equations refer to ideal geometries; Poiseuille's law is an explanatory approximation in biological vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Local Control of Blood Flow 1. Why does a tissue need local control? The heart generates flow, but every organ must receive a supply matched to its moment-to-moment needs. During exercise, contracting muscles require more oxygen and nutrient delivery; meanwhile the brain needs fairly stable perfusion despite ordinary blood-pressure fluctuations. Local blood-flow control means a vascular bed adjusts the calibre of its own resistance vessels, especially small arteries and arterioles. The starting relationship is Q = ΔP / R, where Q is volume flow (for example mL/min), ΔP is the pressure difference across a vascular bed (mmHg), and R is vascular resistance (mmHg·min/mL). If pressure rises at unchanged resistance, flow rises. If local arterioles constrict enough to raise resistance, that rise in flow can be opposed. In an ideal rigid tube with laminar flow, R = 8ηL/(πr⁴) (Poiseuille relationship). η is dynamic viscosity, L is length and r is radius. Living vessels are not rigid tubes, but the fourth-power dependence explains why even a modest arteriolar radius change can strongly affect resistance. Halving radius would increase idealized resistance 16-fold when the other variables are unchanged; do not treat this as an exact prediction for an intact circulation. Tissue activity or perfusion pressure changes → Local sensing → Arteriolar tone changes → Resistance and flow adjust 2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady Autoregulation is the ability of an organ to maintain relatively constant blood flow across a range of perfusion pressures by changing local vascular resistance. It does not mean perfect flow constancy, and it fails outside the organ's effective autoregulatory range. Cerebral and renal circulations are especially important examples; coronary regulation also strongly matches flow to metabolic demand. If perfusion pressure suddenly rises, flow initially tends to increase. Local arterioles then constrict, increasing resistance to counter the rise. If pressure falls moderately, they tend to dilate and lower resistance to defend flow. This is a local response and need not wait for a change in the heart's output. Disturbance Initial passive tendency Compensatory change Intended result Increased perfusion pressure Flow rises Arteriolar constriction increases R Flow moves back toward baseline Decreased perfusion pressure Flow falls Arteriolar dilation decreases R Flow moves back toward baseline Limitation: If pressure becomes too low for further dilation, blood flow falls despite maximal compensation. Thus autoregulation is not a guarantee against shock or severe hypotension. 3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic Myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch An arteriole experiences transmural pressure = intravascular pressure − surrounding tissue pressure. Higher transmural pressure distends its wall, activating vascular smooth-muscle signalling that increases contraction. Constriction reduces radius and raises resistance. Lower pressure reduces wall stretch, permitting relaxation and dilation. Why is this useful? Without the active response, increased pressure would push extra blood through vulnerable small vessels. Myogenic autoregulation helps moderate flow variation and microvascular pressure, notably in cerebral and renal beds. Figure 01. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure Pressure ↑ Wall stretch ↑ Smooth muscle contracts Radius ↓; R ↑ Flow rise opposed Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response. Metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs When metabolism increases or perfusion is inadequate, local vasodilator influences rise. Depending on the tissue, these may include adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, extracellular K⁺ and reduced tissue oxygen tension. The relative importance of each signal differs among organs. Dilation lowers local resistance and improves delivery; increasing flow can also wash out accumulated signals. Example: A vigorously contracting skeletal muscle consumes oxygen faster and produces metabolites. Its small resistance vessels dilate; muscle flow increases even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen. Distinguish the triggers: Myogenic control principally senses vessel stretch/pressure, whereas metabolic control senses local supply relative to demand. Both can act simultaneously; this is not an either/or choice. 4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia Hyperaemia means increased blood flow. Active hyperaemia Reactive hyperaemia Occurs during increased tissue activity Occurs after a period of reduced or interrupted flow Example: working skeletal muscle Example: increased hand flow after releasing a temporary occlusion Increased metabolism promotes dilation Ischaemia-associated metabolites, myogenic changes and other signals contribute; release restores driving pressure Flow rises to meet ongoing demand A temporary overshoot helps repay the supply deficit and dissipate vasodilator influences A useful sequence for reactive hyperaemia is: brief arterial occlusion → oxygen delivery falls and vasodilator influences develop → obstruction is released → previously dilated vascular bed receives a transient flow surge → flow gradually returns toward baseline. The magnitude and duration depend on the degree and length of occlusion and on the tissue. Do not confuse these with autoregulation. Autoregulation describes relative stability of flow when perfusion pressure changes; hyperaemia describes an increase in flow in response to activity or prior underperfusion. Some underlying mechanisms overlap. 5. What does the endothelium contribute? The endothelium is more than a passive lining. It senses shear stress and chemical signals and releases substances that modify underlying smooth-muscle tone. Nitric oxide (NO): endothelial NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raises cGMP, and promotes relaxation. Shear stress from flowing blood can stimulate endothelial NO production. Endothelin-1: a powerful vasoconstrictor peptide acting principally via smooth-muscle ETᴀ receptors; endothelial ETʙ signalling can have different effects. Prostacyclin (PGI₂): an endothelial vasodilator that also inhibits platelet aggregation. Histamine and bradykinin: can promote endothelial-dependent vasodilation, and in suitable vascular beds increase permeability; their effects vary with receptor and tissue. Application: Endothelial dysfunction can reduce NO bioavailability, contributing to impaired vasodilation. It should not be equated with the complete disappearance of myogenic or metabolic control. 6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress One useful relationship is Laplace's law near myogenic regulation. It is important to specify which geometry and which quantity a formula describes. For an ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessel, circumferential wall tension per unit axial length is approximately T = Ptm × r, where Ptm is transmural pressure and r is internal radius. For average circumferential wall stress, σθ ≈ Ptm × r / h, with wall thickness h. For an ideal thin-walled spherical membrane, T = Ptm × r / 2. These idealizations are not interchangeable. At a fixed pressure and thickness, larger vessel radius implies greater average circumferential stress. However, Laplace's relationship does not by itself explain myogenic signalling: living vascular smooth muscle detects deformation and activates ion-channel and contractile pathways. Real arteries have finite wall thickness and active, nonlinear walls. Numerical reasoning: Two thin-walled cylindrical vessels have equal Ptm and h. If one has twice the radius, its estimated wall tension and stress are each twice as large. It would be incorrect to say resistance also doubles: resistance responds oppositely and much more strongly to radius in the simplified Poiseuille model. 7. Bringing local and whole-body control together Local regulation operates alongside the autonomic nervous system and circulating hormones. In exercise, active skeletal muscle can show powerful metabolic vasodilation even while sympathetic activity increases elsewhere. This is one reason blood supply can be redistributed to working tissue rather than rising equally in every organ. A fall in systemic pressure can trigger baroreflex sympathetic vasoconstriction, while critically underperfused tissues simultaneously accumulate local dilator signals. Which effect dominates depends on organ, severity, receptors, and physiological state. The next chapters examine the systemic side of this interaction. Clinical application: If systemic shock drives perfusion pressure below the lower autoregulatory limit, local dilation cannot maintain normal cerebral or renal flow. This connects local autoregulation directly to shock and organ dysfunction. More connections worth understanding The lecture lists rising temperature, low pH, increased carbon dioxide, potassium, lactate, cardiac ADP, bradykinin, substance P, histamine H2 signalling and ANP among local vasodilator-associated influences. Effects vary by tissue and receptor. It lists serotonin, cooling and angiotensin among constrictor influences. The two-curvature Laplace form P = T(1/R1 + 1/R2) is not mathematically equivalent to the slide's later expression P = T/(R1 + R2); that second expression must not be taught as a rearrangement. Understanding Checks 01. Perfusion pressure through a vascular bed doubles while its resistance remains unchanged. What happens to flow initially, and what local adjustment can defend its baseline? Show explanation From Q = ΔP/R, flow initially doubles if R is fixed. Autoregulation can increase arteriolar resistance through constriction, opposing the rise. A perfect twofold adjustment is an idealization; real responses vary by tissue and pressure range. 02. Why are arterioles more effective local flow regulators than large veins? Show explanation Arterioles contain actively controlled vascular smooth muscle and supply a large share of systemic vascular resistance. Small radius changes alter resistance substantially. Veins primarily serve as capacitance vessels and modulate venous return rather than controlling each tissue's entry resistance. 03. When a renal resistance arteriole experiences increased transmural pressure, why may its smooth muscle contract rather than simply remain stretched? Show explanation Stretch activates a myogenic smooth-muscle response that increases tone. A smaller radius raises resistance and helps counter an otherwise excessive pressure-driven rise in flow. The response is active, not merely elastic recoil. 04. A student says myogenic and metabolic autoregulation are the same because both change arteriolar radius. What is missing? Show explanation The initiating signals differ. Myogenic regulation responds chiefly to pressure-driven vessel stretch. Metabolic regulation responds to a mismatch between local perfusion and metabolic demand, via signals such as adenosine, H⁺ and K⁺. They can cooperate in the same vascular bed. 05. During a sprint, muscle perfusion increases even though arterial pressure barely changes. Explain the local mechanism. Show explanation Working muscle increases its metabolic demand. Local vasodilator signals lower arteriolar resistance; therefore flow can rise at nearly constant driving pressure according to Q = ΔP/R. This is active hyperaemia. 06. Blood flow surges briefly after a pressure cuff is released. Is this active hyperaemia, reactive hyperaemia or autoregulation? Show explanation Reactive hyperaemia. Temporary occlusion reduced oxygen supply and fostered vasodilator influences; reopening permits a transient overshoot before signals dissipate. The defining feature is increased flow after preceding underperfusion. 07. Does adenosine have identical importance in every vascular bed? Explain. Show explanation No. Local metabolic mediators differ by organ and context. Adenosine is influential in some settings, especially coronary metabolic regulation, while combinations of oxygen, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, endothelial and myogenic influences contribute elsewhere. Avoid memorising a universal single-metabolite explanation. 08. Trace the intracellular pathway by which endothelial NO relaxes adjacent vascular smooth muscle. Show explanation Endothelial NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and promoting smooth-muscle relaxation. This increases vessel radius, decreases resistance and can increase flow. 09. Why can endothelin-1 and nitric oxide have opposite effects on local flow? Show explanation They affect contractile tone differently: endothelin-1 typically stimulates smooth-muscle contraction, whereas NO promotes cGMP-dependent relaxation. The final response depends on their relative signalling and the receptors of the vascular bed. 10. Two ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessels face equal transmural pressure. One has double the radius and the same wall thickness. Compare estimated wall stress. Show explanation Average circumferential stress is approximately σθ = Ptm × r/h. With equal pressure and thickness, doubling radius doubles estimated stress. This is distinct from Poiseuille resistance, which decreases sharply as radius increases in the ideal rigid-tube model. 11. Why can autoregulation not fully protect the brain during profound haemorrhagic shock? Show explanation Autoregulation is effective only over a bounded pressure range. Below its lower limit, resistance vessels cannot dilate sufficiently to preserve flow; critically reduced perfusion pressure then leads to inadequate cerebral blood delivery despite local compensation. 12. A patient exercising has sympathetic activation, yet blood flow rises in active muscle. Is there a contradiction? Show explanation No. Sympathetic vasoconstrictor influence and local metabolic vasodilation act together. In active muscle, local demand-related dilation can strongly attenuate sympathetic constriction, so that perfusion increases while flow in less active beds may be restricted. Chapter Essentials Tissue flow depends on pressure difference and vascular resistance: Q = ΔP/R. Arteriolar calibre is a major adjustable determinant of local resistance. Autoregulation defends relatively stable flow only within a limited pressure range. Increased vessel stretch often produces myogenic constriction. A tissue with increased metabolic demand tends to develop metabolic vasodilation. Active hyperaemia accompanies increased tissue activity. Reactive hyperaemia follows temporary underperfusion. Endothelial NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → cGMP promotes smooth-muscle relaxation. Endothelin-1 commonly promotes vasoconstriction; endothelial responses are context-dependent. Cylindrical wall tension T ≈ Ptm × r and wall stress σθ ≈ Ptm × r/h require their assumptions. Local and systemic controls may compete or cooperate in one vascular bed. Severe shock can push perfusion below the effective autoregulatory range. References and scope notes Lecturer scope: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, pp. 1–11 (local regulation, myogenic/metabolic theories, Laplace and mediators). Slide numbering/source mapping should be rechecked against the exact uploaded lecture PDF before merging. Reference physiology: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, chapters on local and humoral control of tissue blood flow; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology, vascular smooth-muscle and regional-flow control. Supplementary precision: Autoregulatory ranges are tissue-specific; mediator examples are not universal; Laplace equations refer to ideal geometries; Poiseuille's law is an explanatory approximation in biological vessels."},{"slug":"neural-humoral","title":"Neural and Hormonal Regulation","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-whole-body-need-cardiovascular-regulation","title":"1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?"},{"id":"2-the-medullary-control-network","title":"2. The medullary control network"},{"id":"3-sympathetic-effects-on-the-heart-arterioles-and-veins","title":"3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins"},{"id":"4-parasympathetic-effects-and-tonic-vagal-restraint","title":"4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint"},{"id":"5-hormones-and-circulating-vasomotor-agents","title":"5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents"},{"id":"6-what-changes-in-haemorrhage-and-in-exercise","title":"6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?"},{"id":"7-time-scales-and-reset-limits","title":"7. Time scales and reset limits"},{"id":"8-clinical-application","title":"8. Clinical application"},{"id":"further-physiological-insights","title":"Further physiological insights"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Neural and Hormonal Regulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-whole-body-need-cardiovascular-regulation\">1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?</h2>\n<p>Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes. <strong>MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × SVR</strong>, and <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>. These are interconnected: changing venous tone can alter preload and stroke volume, while arteriolar tone changes afterload. A higher heart rate is therefore not proof of a higher output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-medullary-control-network\">2. The medullary control network</h2>\n<p>The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sensory information reaches the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong>. The <strong>rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)</strong> provides a major tonic sympathoexcitatory output; the <strong>caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)</strong> contributes inhibitory relay activity. Cardiac vagal output involves nucleus ambiguus and dorsal motor nucleus of vagus. The <em>pressor</em> and <em>depressor</em> regions describe the net effects of stimulating these networks. Increased sympathetic vasomotor firing narrows many arterioles; reducing its tonic activity permits dilation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-sympathetic-effects-on-the-heart-arterioles-and-veins\">3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins</h2>\n<p>Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release <strong>noradrenaline (norepinephrine)</strong>. Cardiac <strong>β₁</strong> signalling increases SA node firing, AV conduction and ventricular contractility. Vascular <strong>α₁</strong> stimulation constricts many arterioles, increasing resistance, and many veins, reducing venous capacitance and mobilising blood toward the thorax. <strong>Venoconstriction does not create blood</strong>: its contribution is limited by circulating volume and right-heart function. During haemorrhage, systemic vascular resistance can preserve MAP while tissue flow remains inadequate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-parasympathetic-effects-and-tonic-vagal-restraint\">4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint</h2>\n<p>Vagal <strong>acetylcholine</strong> binds cardiac <strong>M₂ muscarinic receptors</strong>, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic innervation is relatively modest in most systemic resistance vessels; the vagus therefore regulates the heart much more directly than systemic vascular resistance. At rest, vagal restraint often keeps heart rate below the intrinsic SA nodal rate. Atropine blocks muscarinic signalling and usually increases resting heart rate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-hormones-and-circulating-vasomotor-agents\">5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents</h2>\n<p><strong>Adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution. <strong>Angiotensin II</strong> acts mainly at AT₁ receptors to constrict arterioles, stimulate aldosterone and thirst, and support ADH release. <strong>Aldosterone</strong> increases distal nephron sodium reabsorption over hours to days; water retention follows depending on conditions. <strong>Vasopressin (ADH)</strong> increases renal water reabsorption through V₂ receptors and can cause V₁-mediated vasoconstriction at higher levels. <strong>ANP/BNP</strong> counter volume overload by promoting natriuresis and related effects. Endothelial NO and endothelin modify this systemic control locally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-changes-in-haemorrhage-and-in-exercise\">6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?</h2>\n<p><strong>Haemorrhage:</strong> reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Baroreflex unloading increases sympathetic activity and reduces vagal tone, raising heart rate, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. RAAS and ADH contribute over a longer time scale. If haemorrhage is severe, increased resistance cannot restore lost oxygen delivery. <strong>Exercise:</strong> sympathetic cardiac stimulation raises output while local metabolic dilation in active muscles can overcome much of the local vasoconstrictor influence. Other vascular beds may constrict, allowing redistribution. The final response is the sum of central and regional signals.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-time-scales-and-reset-limits\">7. Time scales and reset limits</h2>\n<p>Fast <strong>neural</strong> adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. The arterial baroreflex can reset during sustained pressure changes and does not by itself determine long-term blood pressure. Kidney-mediated sodium and water balance are central to long-term volume and pressure regulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-application\">8. Clinical application</h2>\n<p>An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. An individual taking an α₁ blocker may develop postural symptoms because peripheral vasoconstriction is impaired. <strong>Do not diagnose shock from blood pressure alone</strong>; follow perfusion, mental state, urine output, capillary refill and the clinical context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"further-physiological-insights\">Further physiological insights</h2>\n<p>The lecture additionally names kinins (bradykinin), vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP) and ANP as vasodilator influences. It describes specialised sympathetic cholinergic vasodilator pathways, although widespread human sympathetic vascular regulation is predominantly noradrenergic and constrictor. Cortical and hypothalamic signals, pain, lung inflation afferents, hypoxia and hypercapnia also modify medullary cardiovascular output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Sympathetic activity rises but pulse barely changes. How can MAP still rise?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>α₁-mediated arteriolar constriction increases systemic vascular resistance; venoconstriction can support preload. MAP depends on both output and resistance, not heart rate alone.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Why does vagal withdrawal increase SA nodal firing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Removing ACh–M₂ signalling reduces inhibitory effects on pacemaker activity, allowing rate to approach the intrinsic SA nodal rate.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Which receptor most directly increases ventricular contractility during sympathetic activation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Myocardial β₁ adrenergic receptor stimulation increases calcium availability and excitation–contraction coupling, increasing inotropy.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. What is the difference between constricting an arteriole and constricting a vein?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Arteriolar constriction mainly increases resistance and alters tissue flow; venoconstriction reduces capacitance and can move stored venous blood centrally to support preload.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Can a strong sympathetic response restore the blood volume lost in severe haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. It redistributes remaining blood and increases vascular tone but cannot manufacture lost volume; fluid replacement and control of bleeding address the primary deficit.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. How does the NTS participate in rapid BP regulation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It receives baroreceptor afferent signals and engages medullary pathways that adjust sympathetic and vagal outflow.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can adrenaline dilate some vessels yet constrict others?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Adrenergic receptor subtype distribution and concentration matter: β₂ can favour skeletal-muscle vasodilation, while α activation causes constriction in other beds.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. Why is aldosterone slower than direct sympathetic vasoconstriction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Aldosterone modifies renal tubular transporter activity and sodium retention, influencing circulating volume over hours or days rather than acting as an immediate nerve impulse.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. How can ADH influence both vascular tone and water retention?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>V₂ receptor stimulation increases collecting-duct water reabsorption; V₁ receptors mediate vasoconstriction, especially at higher vasopressin levels.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why might active muscle receive more blood during exercise despite sympathetic activity?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Local metabolic vasodilator signals in active muscle can attenuate vasoconstriction, while cardiac output rises and blood is redistributed.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. If heart rate doubles but stroke volume halves, what happens to cardiac output?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Since CO = HR × SV, the product remains unchanged. Tachycardia alone is not proof of improved blood delivery.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why is the baroreflex insufficient as the sole explanation for chronic BP control?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It resets and buffers short-term changes; renal pressure natriuresis, fluid balance and longer-term hormonal mechanisms matter critically for persistent pressure regulation.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?</strong> — Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes.</li>\n<li><strong>The medullary control network</strong> — The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins</strong> — Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine).</li>\n<li><strong>Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint</strong> — Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction.</li>\n<li><strong>Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents</strong> — Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?</strong> — Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch.</li>\n<li><strong>Time scales and reset limits</strong> — Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days.</li>\n<li><strong>Clinical application</strong> — An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Neural and Hormonal Regulation 1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation? Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes. MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × SVR, and CO = HR × SV. These are interconnected: changing venous tone can alter preload and stroke volume, while arteriolar tone changes afterload. A higher heart rate is therefore not proof of a higher output. 2. The medullary control network The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sensory information reaches the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS). The rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM) provides a major tonic sympathoexcitatory output; the caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM) contributes inhibitory relay activity. Cardiac vagal output involves nucleus ambiguus and dorsal motor nucleus of vagus. The pressor and depressor regions describe the net effects of stimulating these networks. Increased sympathetic vasomotor firing narrows many arterioles; reducing its tonic activity permits dilation. 3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine). Cardiac β₁ signalling increases SA node firing, AV conduction and ventricular contractility. Vascular α₁ stimulation constricts many arterioles, increasing resistance, and many veins, reducing venous capacitance and mobilising blood toward the thorax. Venoconstriction does not create blood: its contribution is limited by circulating volume and right-heart function. During haemorrhage, systemic vascular resistance can preserve MAP while tissue flow remains inadequate. 4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic innervation is relatively modest in most systemic resistance vessels; the vagus therefore regulates the heart much more directly than systemic vascular resistance. At rest, vagal restraint often keeps heart rate below the intrinsic SA nodal rate. Atropine blocks muscarinic signalling and usually increases resting heart rate. 5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution. Angiotensin II acts mainly at AT₁ receptors to constrict arterioles, stimulate aldosterone and thirst, and support ADH release. Aldosterone increases distal nephron sodium reabsorption over hours to days; water retention follows depending on conditions. Vasopressin (ADH) increases renal water reabsorption through V₂ receptors and can cause V₁-mediated vasoconstriction at higher levels. ANP/BNP counter volume overload by promoting natriuresis and related effects. Endothelial NO and endothelin modify this systemic control locally. 6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise? Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Baroreflex unloading increases sympathetic activity and reduces vagal tone, raising heart rate, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. RAAS and ADH contribute over a longer time scale. If haemorrhage is severe, increased resistance cannot restore lost oxygen delivery. Exercise: sympathetic cardiac stimulation raises output while local metabolic dilation in active muscles can overcome much of the local vasoconstrictor influence. Other vascular beds may constrict, allowing redistribution. The final response is the sum of central and regional signals. 7. Time scales and reset limits Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. The arterial baroreflex can reset during sustained pressure changes and does not by itself determine long-term blood pressure. Kidney-mediated sodium and water balance are central to long-term volume and pressure regulation. 8. Clinical application An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. An individual taking an α₁ blocker may develop postural symptoms because peripheral vasoconstriction is impaired. Do not diagnose shock from blood pressure alone; follow perfusion, mental state, urine output, capillary refill and the clinical context. Further physiological insights The lecture additionally names kinins (bradykinin), vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP) and ANP as vasodilator influences. It describes specialised sympathetic cholinergic vasodilator pathways, although widespread human sympathetic vascular regulation is predominantly noradrenergic and constrictor. Cortical and hypothalamic signals, pain, lung inflation afferents, hypoxia and hypercapnia also modify medullary cardiovascular output. Understanding Checks 01. Sympathetic activity rises but pulse barely changes. How can MAP still rise? Show explanation α₁-mediated arteriolar constriction increases systemic vascular resistance; venoconstriction can support preload. MAP depends on both output and resistance, not heart rate alone. 02. Why does vagal withdrawal increase SA nodal firing? Show explanation Removing ACh–M₂ signalling reduces inhibitory effects on pacemaker activity, allowing rate to approach the intrinsic SA nodal rate. 03. Which receptor most directly increases ventricular contractility during sympathetic activation? Show explanation Myocardial β₁ adrenergic receptor stimulation increases calcium availability and excitation–contraction coupling, increasing inotropy. 04. What is the difference between constricting an arteriole and constricting a vein? Show explanation Arteriolar constriction mainly increases resistance and alters tissue flow; venoconstriction reduces capacitance and can move stored venous blood centrally to support preload. 05. Can a strong sympathetic response restore the blood volume lost in severe haemorrhage? Show explanation No. It redistributes remaining blood and increases vascular tone but cannot manufacture lost volume; fluid replacement and control of bleeding address the primary deficit. 06. How does the NTS participate in rapid BP regulation? Show explanation It receives baroreceptor afferent signals and engages medullary pathways that adjust sympathetic and vagal outflow. 07. Why can adrenaline dilate some vessels yet constrict others? Show explanation Adrenergic receptor subtype distribution and concentration matter: β₂ can favour skeletal-muscle vasodilation, while α activation causes constriction in other beds. 08. Why is aldosterone slower than direct sympathetic vasoconstriction? Show explanation Aldosterone modifies renal tubular transporter activity and sodium retention, influencing circulating volume over hours or days rather than acting as an immediate nerve impulse. 09. How can ADH influence both vascular tone and water retention? Show explanation V₂ receptor stimulation increases collecting-duct water reabsorption; V₁ receptors mediate vasoconstriction, especially at higher vasopressin levels. 10. Why might active muscle receive more blood during exercise despite sympathetic activity? Show explanation Local metabolic vasodilator signals in active muscle can attenuate vasoconstriction, while cardiac output rises and blood is redistributed. 11. If heart rate doubles but stroke volume halves, what happens to cardiac output? Show explanation Since CO = HR × SV, the product remains unchanged. Tachycardia alone is not proof of improved blood delivery. 12. Why is the baroreflex insufficient as the sole explanation for chronic BP control? Show explanation It resets and buffers short-term changes; renal pressure natriuresis, fluid balance and longer-term hormonal mechanisms matter critically for persistent pressure regulation. Chapter Essentials Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation? — Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes. The medullary control network — The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins — Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine). Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint — Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents — Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise? — Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Time scales and reset limits — Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. Clinical application — An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology."},{"slug":"reflexes","title":"Cardiovascular Reflexes and Cushing Response","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"18-36","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-makes-a-cardiovascular-reflex","title":"1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex?"},{"id":"2-baroreceptors-and-their-afferents","title":"2. Baroreceptors and their afferents"},{"id":"3-arterial-pressure-rises-trace-negative-feedback","title":"3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback"},{"id":"4-arterial-pressure-falls-trace-the-reverse-response","title":"4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response"},{"id":"5-chemoreceptor-reflexes","title":"5. Chemoreceptor reflexes"},{"id":"6-low-pressure-receptors-and-the-bainbridge-response","title":"6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response"},{"id":"7-cushing-response-threatened-cerebral-perfusion","title":"7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion"},{"id":"8-compare-apparently-opposite-reflex-responses","title":"8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses"},{"id":"further-physiological-insights","title":"Further physiological insights"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-makes-a-cardiovascular-reflex\">1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex?</h2>\n<p>A reflex involves a <strong>stimulus</strong>, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Reflexes use negative feedback to stabilise arterial pressure or support filling. Understanding the direction of each link avoids memorising isolated arrows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-baroreceptors-and-their-afferents\">2. Baroreceptors and their afferents</h2>\n<p>Arterial <strong>stretch-sensitive endings</strong> in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Carotid sinus signals pass through <strong>Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) → medullary NTS</strong>. Aortic arch signals travel primarily through <strong>vagus (CN X) → NTS</strong>. They respond to mechanical stretch associated with pressure, not directly to blood oxygen concentration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-arterial-pressure-rises-trace-negative-feedback\">3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback</h2>\n<p>Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. The medulla decreases sympathetic cardiac/vascular drive and increases cardiac vagal action. <strong>Heart rate, contractility, arteriolar resistance and often venous tone fall</strong>, opposing the rise in MAP. The baroreflex buffers <em>rapid</em> changes; prolonged hypertension can reset its operating point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-arterial-pressure-falls-trace-the-reverse-response\">4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response</h2>\n<p>Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. HR and contractility tend to rise; arterioles constrict and veins mobilise blood centrally. These responses defend pressure but cannot replace major blood loss. Reflex tachycardia can be absent or blunted because of medications, conduction disease or other competing influences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-chemoreceptor-reflexes\">5. Chemoreceptor reflexes</h2>\n<p>Peripheral chemoreceptors in <strong>carotid and aortic bodies</strong> sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Carotid body afferents travel via CN IX; aortic body afferents primarily via CN X. Their chief role includes stimulating ventilation, while cardiovascular effects depend on the interaction with respiratory and other reflexes. <strong>Do not confuse carotid body chemoreceptors with carotid sinus baroreceptors.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-low-pressure-receptors-and-the-bainbridge-response\">6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response</h2>\n<p>Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. Physiology distinguishes <strong>type A atrial receptor discharge around atrial contraction</strong> and <strong>type B discharge around filling</strong>. Stretch at the atrial–venous junction can promote a <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong> increase in heart rate, helping manage increased venous return. The outcome depends on concurrent baroreflex input; atrial filling does not invariably produce tachycardia. Atrial stretch can also promote ANP release and alter volume-related hormonal responses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-cushing-response-threatened-cerebral-perfusion\">7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion</h2>\n<p><strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Markedly rising intracranial pressure reduces perfusion. Severe brainstem ischaemia can provoke <strong>sympathetic vasoconstriction and hypertension</strong> to sustain perfusion. The resulting raised MAP activates <strong>arterial baroreceptors</strong>, contributing to <strong>bradycardia</strong>. Abnormal respiration can develop from brainstem dysfunction. Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration comprise the classic <strong>Cushing triad</strong>—an ominous, often late sign, not a universal early presentation of elevated ICP.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compare-apparently-opposite-reflex-responses\">8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses</h2>\n<p><strong>Ordinary blood loss</strong> tends to cause baroreflex <strong>tachycardia</strong> and vasoconstriction. <strong>Increased ICP</strong> may create a Cushing sequence of sympathetic <strong>hypertension</strong> followed by <strong>baroreflex bradycardia</strong>. <strong>Increased atrial filling</strong> can stimulate a Bainbridge tendency toward tachycardia. Different primary stimuli can therefore yield different HR directions; always identify the initiating stimulus before predicting output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"further-physiological-insights\">Further physiological insights</h2>\n<p>Additional lecture inputs include cortical and hypothalamic influences, pulmonary inflation afferents, hypoxia, hypercapnia and pain. Brief painful stimuli may increase sympathetic pressure responses, while emotional or sustained-pain triggers can evoke vasovagal hypotension. Increased atrial filling can also suppress ADH and stimulate natriuretic peptide signalling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Where are the two major high-pressure arterial baroreceptor sites?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>At the carotid sinus and aortic arch, where stretch-sensitive endings report pressure-related changes in vessel-wall distension.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Which cranial nerve carries carotid sinus baroreceptor signals centrally?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The carotid sinus stretch receptors send impulses via the carotid sinus nerve (Hering's nerve), a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla. Aortic arch receptors instead travel mainly through the vagus nerve.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Which cranial nerve primarily carries aortic arch baroreceptor afferents?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Aortic arch baroreceptor afferents travel through the vagus nerve (CN X) to the medullary nucleus tractus solitarius. This sensory input allows the brainstem to alter sympathetic and parasympathetic output to the heart and blood vessels.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. A sudden rise in MAP activates the baroreflex. What happens to HR and resistance?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Increased baroreceptor firing promotes vagal activity and decreases sympathetic output, lowering HR and arteriolar resistance to oppose high MAP.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Why is haemorrhagic reflex tachycardia not evidence of restored oxygen delivery?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>HR may rise while reduced venous return and stroke volume lower cardiac output; tissue delivery can remain insufficient.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. How does a carotid body differ physiologically from a carotid sinus?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The carotid body is a peripheral chemoreceptor sensing blood gas and acid-base changes; the sinus is a stretch-sensitive arterial baroreceptor.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. What stimulus is most closely associated with the Bainbridge reflex?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Increased venous return and stretch at atrial or venous junctions may increase HR, depending on competing reflexes.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. What are the lecturer's atrial receptor type A and B timing distinctions?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Type A impulses occur around atrial contraction; type B impulses are associated with atrial filling. They are low-pressure volume-related receptors.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why does the Cushing response initially increase systemic BP?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Severe intracranial hypertension compromises brainstem perfusion, provoking intense sympathetic vasoconstriction that raises systemic arterial pressure.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why can the pulse slow while BP rises in the Cushing response?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>As intracranial pressure rises and cerebral perfusion falls, the brainstem can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction, raising systemic arterial pressure. This elevated pressure stimulates arterial baroreceptors and can produce reflex slowing of heart rate. Bradycardia is therefore not a direct effect of raised intracranial pressure on the SA node in every case.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. What does CPP approximate when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Cerebral perfusion pressure is approximated by mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, provided ICP is the main downstream pressure. If ICP rises while MAP remains unchanged, the pressure available to drive cerebral blood flow falls. Actual flow also depends on cerebrovascular resistance and autoregulatory capacity.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Is the Cushing triad a reliable early screening test for raised ICP?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration together form the classic Cushing triad, usually suggesting severe or late intracranial hypertension and brainstem compromise. Earlier raised ICP can occur without the complete triad, so its absence cannot exclude dangerous pathology.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>What makes a cardiovascular reflex?</strong> — A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response.</li>\n<li><strong>Baroreceptors and their afferents</strong> — Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback</strong> — Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response</strong> — Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemoreceptor reflexes</strong> — Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response</strong> — Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling.</li>\n<li><strong>Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion</strong> — Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare apparently opposite reflex responses</strong> — Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response 1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex? A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Reflexes use negative feedback to stabilise arterial pressure or support filling. Understanding the direction of each link avoids memorising isolated arrows. 2. Baroreceptors and their afferents Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Carotid sinus signals pass through Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) → medullary NTS. Aortic arch signals travel primarily through vagus (CN X) → NTS. They respond to mechanical stretch associated with pressure, not directly to blood oxygen concentration. 3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. The medulla decreases sympathetic cardiac/vascular drive and increases cardiac vagal action. Heart rate, contractility, arteriolar resistance and often venous tone fall, opposing the rise in MAP. The baroreflex buffers rapid changes; prolonged hypertension can reset its operating point. 4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. HR and contractility tend to rise; arterioles constrict and veins mobilise blood centrally. These responses defend pressure but cannot replace major blood loss. Reflex tachycardia can be absent or blunted because of medications, conduction disease or other competing influences. 5. Chemoreceptor reflexes Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Carotid body afferents travel via CN IX; aortic body afferents primarily via CN X. Their chief role includes stimulating ventilation, while cardiovascular effects depend on the interaction with respiratory and other reflexes. Do not confuse carotid body chemoreceptors with carotid sinus baroreceptors. 6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. Physiology distinguishes type A atrial receptor discharge around atrial contraction and type B discharge around filling. Stretch at the atrial–venous junction can promote a Bainbridge reflex increase in heart rate, helping manage increased venous return. The outcome depends on concurrent baroreflex input; atrial filling does not invariably produce tachycardia. Atrial stretch can also promote ANP release and alter volume-related hormonal responses. 7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Markedly rising intracranial pressure reduces perfusion. Severe brainstem ischaemia can provoke sympathetic vasoconstriction and hypertension to sustain perfusion. The resulting raised MAP activates arterial baroreceptors, contributing to bradycardia. Abnormal respiration can develop from brainstem dysfunction. Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration comprise the classic Cushing triad —an ominous, often late sign, not a universal early presentation of elevated ICP. 8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction. Increased ICP may create a Cushing sequence of sympathetic hypertension followed by baroreflex bradycardia. Increased atrial filling can stimulate a Bainbridge tendency toward tachycardia. Different primary stimuli can therefore yield different HR directions; always identify the initiating stimulus before predicting output. Further physiological insights Additional lecture inputs include cortical and hypothalamic influences, pulmonary inflation afferents, hypoxia, hypercapnia and pain. Brief painful stimuli may increase sympathetic pressure responses, while emotional or sustained-pain triggers can evoke vasovagal hypotension. Increased atrial filling can also suppress ADH and stimulate natriuretic peptide signalling. Understanding Checks 01. Where are the two major high-pressure arterial baroreceptor sites? Show explanation At the carotid sinus and aortic arch, where stretch-sensitive endings report pressure-related changes in vessel-wall distension. 02. Which cranial nerve carries carotid sinus baroreceptor signals centrally? Show explanation The carotid sinus stretch receptors send impulses via the carotid sinus nerve (Hering's nerve), a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla. Aortic arch receptors instead travel mainly through the vagus nerve. 03. Which cranial nerve primarily carries aortic arch baroreceptor afferents? Show explanation Aortic arch baroreceptor afferents travel through the vagus nerve (CN X) to the medullary nucleus tractus solitarius. This sensory input allows the brainstem to alter sympathetic and parasympathetic output to the heart and blood vessels. 04. A sudden rise in MAP activates the baroreflex. What happens to HR and resistance? Show explanation Increased baroreceptor firing promotes vagal activity and decreases sympathetic output, lowering HR and arteriolar resistance to oppose high MAP. 05. Why is haemorrhagic reflex tachycardia not evidence of restored oxygen delivery? Show explanation HR may rise while reduced venous return and stroke volume lower cardiac output; tissue delivery can remain insufficient. 06. How does a carotid body differ physiologically from a carotid sinus? Show explanation The carotid body is a peripheral chemoreceptor sensing blood gas and acid-base changes; the sinus is a stretch-sensitive arterial baroreceptor. 07. What stimulus is most closely associated with the Bainbridge reflex? Show explanation Increased venous return and stretch at atrial or venous junctions may increase HR, depending on competing reflexes. 08. What are the lecturer's atrial receptor type A and B timing distinctions? Show explanation Type A impulses occur around atrial contraction; type B impulses are associated with atrial filling. They are low-pressure volume-related receptors. 09. Why does the Cushing response initially increase systemic BP? Show explanation Severe intracranial hypertension compromises brainstem perfusion, provoking intense sympathetic vasoconstriction that raises systemic arterial pressure. 10. Why can the pulse slow while BP rises in the Cushing response? Show explanation As intracranial pressure rises and cerebral perfusion falls, the brainstem can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction, raising systemic arterial pressure. This elevated pressure stimulates arterial baroreceptors and can produce reflex slowing of heart rate. Bradycardia is therefore not a direct effect of raised intracranial pressure on the SA node in every case. 11. What does CPP approximate when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure? Show explanation Cerebral perfusion pressure is approximated by mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, provided ICP is the main downstream pressure. If ICP rises while MAP remains unchanged, the pressure available to drive cerebral blood flow falls. Actual flow also depends on cerebrovascular resistance and autoregulatory capacity. 12. Is the Cushing triad a reliable early screening test for raised ICP? Show explanation No. Hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration together form the classic Cushing triad, usually suggesting severe or late intracranial hypertension and brainstem compromise. Earlier raised ICP can occur without the complete triad, so its absence cannot exclude dangerous pathology. Chapter Essentials What makes a cardiovascular reflex? — A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Baroreceptors and their afferents — Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback — Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response — Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. Chemoreceptor reflexes — Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response — Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion — Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses — Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology."},{"slug":"gravity","title":"Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"2-11; duplicate Lecture 5 pp2-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-standing-changes-the-hydrostatic-column","title":"1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column"},{"id":"2-why-venous-pooling-lowers-cardiac-output","title":"2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output"},{"id":"3-the-seconds-long-baroreflex-rescue","title":"3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue"},{"id":"4-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-standing-at-attention","title":"4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention"},{"id":"5-orthostatic-hypotension-and-its-clinical-definition","title":"5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition"},{"id":"6-vasovagal-syncope-is-not-simply-normal-orthostatic-compensation","title":"6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation"},{"id":"7-gravity-also-explains-dependent-oedema","title":"7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema"},{"id":"8-acceleration-aviation-and-clinical-consequences","title":"8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences"},{"id":"additional-mechanisms-worth-understanding","title":"Additional mechanisms worth understanding"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-standing-changes-the-hydrostatic-column\">1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column</h2>\n<p>In the upright body, pressure at points <strong>below</strong> heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure <strong>above</strong> heart level is relatively lower. Hydrostatic pressure depends on density, gravitational acceleration and vertical height difference (<strong>ΔP = ρgh</strong>). This is distinct from the pressure generated by the heart itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-venous-pooling-lowers-cardiac-output\">2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output</h2>\n<p>Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. Central blood volume and right atrial filling decline, lowering ventricular <strong>end-diastolic volume → stroke volume</strong> by the Frank–Starling mechanism. CO can transiently fall, endangering blood pressure at head level and cerebral blood flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-seconds-long-baroreflex-rescue\">3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue</h2>\n<p>Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Vagal restraint decreases and sympathetic drive increases: <strong>HR rises, contractility rises, arterioles constrict and veins constrict</strong>. This response raises systemic resistance and supports venous return. A strong heartbeat alone cannot compensate if preload is critically low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-standing-at-attention\">4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention</h2>\n<p>Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Walking and subtle <strong>isometric calf contraction</strong> can reduce dependent pooling. Soldiers standing still may perform small muscular contractions to support venous return. The muscle pump complements—not replaces—the baroreflex.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-orthostatic-hypotension-and-its-clinical-definition\">5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition</h2>\n<p>Classical <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong> is a sustained drop of at least <strong>20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing</strong> from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Dehydration, medications, autonomic neuropathy, haemorrhage and prolonged bed rest can contribute. Symptoms include dizziness, visual darkening and syncope from impaired cerebral perfusion. Some forms occur later than three minutes or immediately; formal definitions vary by timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-vasovagal-syncope-is-not-simply-normal-orthostatic-compensation\">6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation</h2>\n<p>Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate <strong>vasodilation and/or bradycardia</strong>, leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Contrast this with the usual immediate compensation to standing, which produces <strong>sympathetic activation and tachycardia</strong>. A patient can also have mixed mechanisms; clinical history matters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-gravity-also-explains-dependent-oedema\">7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema</h2>\n<p>Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Lymphatic return and muscular movement limit swelling; long immobility can overcome these defences. This connects Chapter 19 directly to capillary exchange and oedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-acceleration-aviation-and-clinical-consequences\">8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences</h2>\n<p>Under positive <strong>+Gz</strong> (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Anti-G manoeuvres and suits reduce dependent pooling. Under <strong>−Gz</strong>, blood shifts toward the head, contributing to congestion and visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, intensity and duration. Supine positioning reduces the head-heart hydrostatic gradient during a simple fainting episode, but persistent or unexplained loss of consciousness needs medical assessment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"additional-mechanisms-worth-understanding\">Additional mechanisms worth understanding</h2>\n<p>Another important effect is partial cerebral flow protection because both head-level arterial and venous pressures fall on standing; a decrease in cerebral venous or intracranial pressure and local cerebral vasodilation can partially defend perfusion, but these mechanisms fail in severe hypotension. Autonomic neuropathy, sympatholytic drugs and perioperative autonomic impairment can cause orthostatic hypotension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Why is ankle venous pressure higher than heart-level venous pressure when standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>When a person stands, gravity creates a hydrostatic pressure difference proportional to the vertical distance from heart level: ΔP = ρgh. Pressure increases in dependent vessels, promoting venous pooling, while pressure at head level is lower. This is a distribution of pressures, not a creation of extra blood volume.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. What is the first cardiac consequence of sudden dependent venous pooling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced central venous return lowers ventricular filling and end-diastolic volume, reducing stroke volume through Frank–Starling physiology.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. What happens to baroreceptor firing after a sudden orthostatic fall in arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Firing falls due to reduced arterial stretch, increasing sympathetic output and withdrawing cardiac vagal restraint.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why does heart rate usually rise on standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The baroreflex increases sympathetic cardiac drive and reduces vagal inhibition to support cardiac output and MAP.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. How do brief calf contractions help a person standing still?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Muscle compression of deep veins, with one-way valves, propels venous blood toward the heart and reduces pooling.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. What is the standard three-minute definition of classical orthostatic hypotension?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Classical orthostatic hypotension is generally defined as a sustained fall in systolic pressure of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic pressure of at least 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing or head-up tilt. Symptoms may accompany the fall, but the definition is based on measured pressure.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can a dehydrated person develop more severe orthostatic symptoms?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced circulating volume limits venous return and preload, making gravitational pooling harder to compensate for.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. How does vasovagal syncope differ from ordinary compensation to standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Instead of protective vasoconstriction and tachycardia, vasovagal reflexes may cause inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, dropping cerebral perfusion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why can walking relieve dizziness felt during prolonged motionless standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Contraction of leg muscles compresses deep veins. One-way venous valves favour movement of blood toward the heart rather than back toward the feet, reducing pooling and helping restore ventricular filling and stroke volume during standing or walking.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why does ankle oedema worsen after standing motionless for hours?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure increases net filtration; prolonged excess filtration can exceed lymphatic drainage.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Why can positive head-to-foot acceleration impair vision?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It drives blood toward dependent tissues and reduces pressure and flow to the retina and brain.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Does lying flat restore lost blood volume after haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. It can improve the gravitational relationship and venous return transiently but does not replace lost volume or treat the source of bleeding.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Standing changes the hydrostatic column</strong> — In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower.</li>\n<li><strong>Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output</strong> — Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins.</li>\n<li><strong>The seconds-long baroreflex rescue</strong> — Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing.</li>\n<li><strong>Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention</strong> — Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement.</li>\n<li><strong>Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition</strong> — Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation</strong> — Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity also explains dependent oedema</strong> — Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing.</li>\n<li><strong>Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences</strong> — Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n<li>Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis 1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower. Hydrostatic pressure depends on density, gravitational acceleration and vertical height difference ( ΔP = ρgh). This is distinct from the pressure generated by the heart itself. 2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. Central blood volume and right atrial filling decline, lowering ventricular end-diastolic volume → stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. CO can transiently fall, endangering blood pressure at head level and cerebral blood flow. 3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Vagal restraint decreases and sympathetic drive increases: HR rises, contractility rises, arterioles constrict and veins constrict. This response raises systemic resistance and supports venous return. A strong heartbeat alone cannot compensate if preload is critically low. 4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Walking and subtle isometric calf contraction can reduce dependent pooling. Soldiers standing still may perform small muscular contractions to support venous return. The muscle pump complements—not replaces—the baroreflex. 5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Dehydration, medications, autonomic neuropathy, haemorrhage and prolonged bed rest can contribute. Symptoms include dizziness, visual darkening and syncope from impaired cerebral perfusion. Some forms occur later than three minutes or immediately; formal definitions vary by timing. 6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Contrast this with the usual immediate compensation to standing, which produces sympathetic activation and tachycardia. A patient can also have mixed mechanisms; clinical history matters. 7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Lymphatic return and muscular movement limit swelling; long immobility can overcome these defences. This connects Chapter 19 directly to capillary exchange and oedema. 8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Anti-G manoeuvres and suits reduce dependent pooling. Under −Gz, blood shifts toward the head, contributing to congestion and visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, intensity and duration. Supine positioning reduces the head-heart hydrostatic gradient during a simple fainting episode, but persistent or unexplained loss of consciousness needs medical assessment. Additional mechanisms worth understanding Another important effect is partial cerebral flow protection because both head-level arterial and venous pressures fall on standing; a decrease in cerebral venous or intracranial pressure and local cerebral vasodilation can partially defend perfusion, but these mechanisms fail in severe hypotension. Autonomic neuropathy, sympatholytic drugs and perioperative autonomic impairment can cause orthostatic hypotension. Understanding Checks 01. Why is ankle venous pressure higher than heart-level venous pressure when standing? Show explanation When a person stands, gravity creates a hydrostatic pressure difference proportional to the vertical distance from heart level: ΔP = ρgh. Pressure increases in dependent vessels, promoting venous pooling, while pressure at head level is lower. This is a distribution of pressures, not a creation of extra blood volume. 02. What is the first cardiac consequence of sudden dependent venous pooling? Show explanation Reduced central venous return lowers ventricular filling and end-diastolic volume, reducing stroke volume through Frank–Starling physiology. 03. What happens to baroreceptor firing after a sudden orthostatic fall in arterial pressure? Show explanation Firing falls due to reduced arterial stretch, increasing sympathetic output and withdrawing cardiac vagal restraint. 04. Why does heart rate usually rise on standing? Show explanation The baroreflex increases sympathetic cardiac drive and reduces vagal inhibition to support cardiac output and MAP. 05. How do brief calf contractions help a person standing still? Show explanation Muscle compression of deep veins, with one-way valves, propels venous blood toward the heart and reduces pooling. 06. What is the standard three-minute definition of classical orthostatic hypotension? Show explanation Classical orthostatic hypotension is generally defined as a sustained fall in systolic pressure of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic pressure of at least 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing or head-up tilt. Symptoms may accompany the fall, but the definition is based on measured pressure. 07. Why can a dehydrated person develop more severe orthostatic symptoms? Show explanation Reduced circulating volume limits venous return and preload, making gravitational pooling harder to compensate for. 08. How does vasovagal syncope differ from ordinary compensation to standing? Show explanation Instead of protective vasoconstriction and tachycardia, vasovagal reflexes may cause inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, dropping cerebral perfusion. 09. Why can walking relieve dizziness felt during prolonged motionless standing? Show explanation Contraction of leg muscles compresses deep veins. One-way venous valves favour movement of blood toward the heart rather than back toward the feet, reducing pooling and helping restore ventricular filling and stroke volume during standing or walking. 10. Why does ankle oedema worsen after standing motionless for hours? Show explanation Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure increases net filtration; prolonged excess filtration can exceed lymphatic drainage. 11. Why can positive head-to-foot acceleration impair vision? Show explanation It drives blood toward dependent tissues and reduces pressure and flow to the retina and brain. 12. Does lying flat restore lost blood volume after haemorrhage? Show explanation No. It can improve the gravitational relationship and venous return transiently but does not replace lost volume or treat the source of bleeding. Chapter Essentials Standing changes the hydrostatic column — In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output — Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue — Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention — Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition — Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation — Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Gravity also explains dependent oedema — Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences — Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology. Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims."},{"slug":"shock","title":"Circulatory Shock","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"12-30; duplicate Lecture 5 pp12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-shock-is-failure-of-tissue-perfusion-not-a-bp-threshold","title":"1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold"},{"id":"2-oxygen-delivery-connects-physiology-to-cellular-injury","title":"2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury"},{"id":"3-four-mechanistic-classes","title":"3. Four mechanistic classes"},{"id":"4-follow-blood-loss-all-the-way-to-organ-hypoperfusion","title":"4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion"},{"id":"5-compensation-can-preserve-pressure-while-organs-suffer","title":"5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer"},{"id":"6-progressive-shock-and-positive-feedback","title":"6. Progressive shock and positive feedback"},{"id":"7-why-acute-haemorrhage-may-initially-show-normal-hb-and-haematocrit","title":"7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit"},{"id":"8-the-physiology-behind-bedside-signs","title":"8. The physiology behind bedside signs"},{"id":"9-hypovolaemic-cardiogenic-distributive-and-obstructive-reasoning","title":"9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning"},{"id":"10-clinical-synthesis-and-limits","title":"10. Clinical synthesis and limits"},{"id":"further-physiological-insights","title":"Further physiological insights"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Circulatory Shock</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-shock-is-failure-of-tissue-perfusion-not-a-bp-threshold\">1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold</h2>\n<p><strong>Circulatory shock</strong> is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Blood pressure can be near normal early, because systemic resistance rises while cardiac output and regional perfusion fall. <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> describes how a low CO can be masked by compensatory constriction. Evaluate circulation and tissue perfusion, not one cuff reading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-oxygen-delivery-connects-physiology-to-cellular-injury\">2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury</h2>\n<p>Systemic oxygen delivery <strong>DO₂ = CO × CaO₂</strong>, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Approximately <strong>CaO₂ = 1.34 × Hb × SaO₂ + 0.003 × PaO₂</strong> (mL O₂/dL, with Hb in g/dL and PaO₂ in mmHg). Thus haemorrhage can reduce delivery by reducing CO even before a measured Hb concentration falls. Failure of oxygen delivery/utilisation impairs ATP production; lactate can rise, although elevated lactate is not specific proof of tissue hypoxia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-four-mechanistic-classes\">3. Four mechanistic classes</h2>\n<p><strong>Hypovolaemic:</strong> reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns). <strong>Cardiogenic:</strong> inadequate pumping (large myocardial infarction, critical arrhythmia). <strong>Distributive:</strong> abnormal vascular tone and flow distribution (sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock). <strong>Obstructive:</strong> impeded filling or forward flow (tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism). Causes can overlap; early septic shock may have warm extremities, so cool skin is not universal.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-follow-blood-loss-all-the-way-to-organ-hypoperfusion\">4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion</h2>\n<p>Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. The fall in arterial stretch unloads baroreceptors, driving sympathetic <strong>tachycardia, increased contractility, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction</strong>. Skin may become pale and cool while renal perfusion and urine output decline. Reduced venous filling limits the benefit of simply making the heart beat faster.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-compensation-can-preserve-pressure-while-organs-suffer\">5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer</h2>\n<p>Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow. <strong>Excessive tachycardia</strong> shortens diastolic filling time, potentially making stroke volume smaller. Strong vasoconstriction diverts flow away from skin and some organs; it cannot restore absent circulating volume. Angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH and thirst help support volume over longer periods but are insufficient without control of major ongoing losses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-progressive-shock-and-positive-feedback\">6. Progressive shock and positive feedback</h2>\n<p>Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Capillary leak and microvascular dysfunction can reduce effective circulating volume and oxygen extraction further. <strong>Worsening flow → tissue injury → poorer pump/vascular function → still worse flow</strong> is a reinforcing loop. Severe stages may become refractory despite treatment; there is no universal clock at which shock becomes irreversible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-acute-haemorrhage-may-initially-show-normal-hb-and-haematocrit\">7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit</h2>\n<p>A person loses <strong>whole blood</strong>, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. Their <em>ratio</em>—haematocrit—and Hb <em>concentration</em> may remain near baseline immediately. Subsequent interstitial fluid recruitment and infused IV fluid dilute the residual red cells; Hb and haematocrit can then fall. Initial laboratory concentrations must not override evidence of acute bleeding and hypoperfusion. Erythropoietin-driven red cell replacement takes much longer than volume adjustments.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-the-physiology-behind-bedside-signs\">8. The physiology behind bedside signs</h2>\n<p><strong>Tachycardia</strong> reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes. <strong>Cool skin</strong> often reflects peripheral constriction; <strong>warm skin</strong> can occur early in distributive shock. <strong>Oliguria</strong> suggests reduced renal perfusion or renal dysfunction; altered mentation may signal cerebral hypoperfusion. Lactate, capillary refill and trends can contribute, but none alone establishes or excludes shock.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-hypovolaemic-cardiogenic-distributive-and-obstructive-reasoning\">9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning</h2>\n<p>In <strong>hypovolaemia</strong>, filling pressure is often low; in <strong>cardiogenic pump failure</strong>, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in <strong>distributive</strong> forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in <strong>obstructive</strong> forms flow is mechanically impeded. These are <strong>typical patterns</strong>, not hard rules. Identify the failing link rather than treating all hypotension as a lack of fluid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-clinical-synthesis-and-limits\">10. Clinical synthesis and limits</h2>\n<p>The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting. <strong>Bleeding requires haemorrhage control</strong>, pump failure requires cause-directed management, obstructive causes need relief, and distributive causes need targeted treatment. This chapter teaches physiology, not a substitute for emergency clinical protocols.</p>\n<h2 id=\"further-physiological-insights\">Further physiological insights</h2>\n<p>The lecture includes concealed internal bleeding after femoral fracture, splenic rupture or ectopic pregnancy; burn-related plasma losses may initially produce haemoconcentration rather than the typical immediate whole-blood Hb concentration pattern. It also covers myoglobin-associated renal injury in trauma, low urine output and tubular injury, myocardial ischaemia, capillary stasis and eventual vascular hyporesponsiveness. Vasovagal, postural, micturition and carotid-sinus syncope have distinct triggers; ordinary orthostatic syncope does not require bradycardia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Can a patient be shocked with a near-normal MAP after major bleeding?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Yes. Raised SVR may sustain MAP while reduced venous return and cardiac output impair tissue oxygen delivery. Clinical perfusion matters.</details>\n\n<p><strong>02. Write the equation connecting oxygen delivery to cardiac output.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Systemic oxygen delivery equals cardiac output multiplied by arterial oxygen content: DO₂ = CO × CaO₂. During haemorrhage, cardiac output may fall immediately from reduced venous return, even while haemoglobin concentration initially remains near its starting value. Normal early Hb does not imply normal oxygen delivery.</details>\n\n<p><strong>03. Why does severe tachycardia sometimes worsen haemorrhagic shock?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>A compensatory faster heartbeat initially supports output, but extreme tachycardia shortens diastole, the period for ventricular filling. If blood volume and venous return are already low, each beat may start with less blood, decreasing stroke volume enough to offset the increased rate.</details>\n\n<p><strong>04. Why is vasoconstriction initially adaptive in haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It increases SVR and supports arterial pressure while diverting limited blood toward vital organs, but cannot replace lost blood.</details>\n\n<p><strong>05. Which shock type is most directly caused by cardiac tamponade?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Obstructive shock, because pericardial pressure mechanically restricts ventricular filling and forward output.</details>\n\n<p><strong>06. Which shock type usually follows a large infarct with severe pump dysfunction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Cardiogenic shock: inadequate myocardial pumping reduces forward output, sometimes despite elevated filling pressures.</details>\n\n<p><strong>07. Why can early septic shock have warm skin?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Distributive vasodilation can increase cutaneous blood flow despite severe circulatory dysfunction.</details>\n\n<p><strong>08. Why may Hb and haematocrit be nearly unchanged immediately after acute whole-blood loss?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Immediately after an acute whole-blood haemorrhage, both red cells and plasma are lost, so the concentration of haemoglobin and haematocrit may initially change little despite a substantial reduction in total circulating red-cell mass. Later interstitial fluid shifts or intravenous fluids dilute the remaining blood.</details>\n\n<p><strong>09. Why does measured Hb often fall later after bleeding?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Over time, fluid moves from the interstitium into the vascular space, and administered crystalloid may add further plasma volume. Because the lost red cells have not yet been replaced, haemoglobin concentration and haematocrit commonly fall after this haemodilution.</details>\n\n<p><strong>10. Why can lactate rise in shock, and why is it not fully specific?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Low oxygen delivery may increase anaerobic glycolysis and lactate, but adrenergic stimulation, clearance changes and other causes also affect lactate.</details>\n\n<p><strong>11. Explain one positive feedback loop in progressive shock.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Low perfusion produces acidosis and myocardial/endothelial injury, worsening cardiac function, microvascular flow and effective volume; this further lowers perfusion.</details>\n\n<p><strong>12. Why is the treatment target different in obstructive and hypovolaemic shock?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Obstructive shock requires relief of the mechanical block; hypovolaemic shock requires correcting volume loss and its cause. Treating every low BP identically misses the mechanism.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold</strong> — Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury</strong> — Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content.</li>\n<li><strong>Four mechanistic classes</strong> — Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns).</li>\n<li><strong>Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion</strong> — Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓.</li>\n<li><strong>Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer</strong> — Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive shock and positive feedback</strong> — Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury.</li>\n<li><strong>Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit</strong> — A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together.</li>\n<li><strong>The physiology behind bedside signs</strong> — Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning</strong> — In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded.</li>\n<li><strong>Clinical synthesis and limits</strong> — The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30.</li>\n<li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n","plain":"Circulatory Shock 1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Blood pressure can be near normal early, because systemic resistance rises while cardiac output and regional perfusion fall. MAP ≈ CO × SVR describes how a low CO can be masked by compensatory constriction. Evaluate circulation and tissue perfusion, not one cuff reading. 2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Approximately CaO₂ = 1.34 × Hb × SaO₂ + 0.003 × PaO₂ (mL O₂/dL, with Hb in g/dL and PaO₂ in mmHg). Thus haemorrhage can reduce delivery by reducing CO even before a measured Hb concentration falls. Failure of oxygen delivery/utilisation impairs ATP production; lactate can rise, although elevated lactate is not specific proof of tissue hypoxia. 3. Four mechanistic classes Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns). Cardiogenic: inadequate pumping (large myocardial infarction, critical arrhythmia). Distributive: abnormal vascular tone and flow distribution (sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock). Obstructive: impeded filling or forward flow (tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism). Causes can overlap; early septic shock may have warm extremities, so cool skin is not universal. 4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. The fall in arterial stretch unloads baroreceptors, driving sympathetic tachycardia, increased contractility, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction. Skin may become pale and cool while renal perfusion and urine output decline. Reduced venous filling limits the benefit of simply making the heart beat faster. 5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow. Excessive tachycardia shortens diastolic filling time, potentially making stroke volume smaller. Strong vasoconstriction diverts flow away from skin and some organs; it cannot restore absent circulating volume. Angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH and thirst help support volume over longer periods but are insufficient without control of major ongoing losses. 6. Progressive shock and positive feedback Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Capillary leak and microvascular dysfunction can reduce effective circulating volume and oxygen extraction further. Worsening flow → tissue injury → poorer pump/vascular function → still worse flow is a reinforcing loop. Severe stages may become refractory despite treatment; there is no universal clock at which shock becomes irreversible. 7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. Their ratio —haematocrit—and Hb concentration may remain near baseline immediately. Subsequent interstitial fluid recruitment and infused IV fluid dilute the residual red cells; Hb and haematocrit can then fall. Initial laboratory concentrations must not override evidence of acute bleeding and hypoperfusion. Erythropoietin-driven red cell replacement takes much longer than volume adjustments. 8. The physiology behind bedside signs Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes. Cool skin often reflects peripheral constriction; warm skin can occur early in distributive shock. Oliguria suggests reduced renal perfusion or renal dysfunction; altered mentation may signal cerebral hypoperfusion. Lactate, capillary refill and trends can contribute, but none alone establishes or excludes shock. 9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded. These are typical patterns, not hard rules. Identify the failing link rather than treating all hypotension as a lack of fluid. 10. Clinical synthesis and limits The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting. Bleeding requires haemorrhage control, pump failure requires cause-directed management, obstructive causes need relief, and distributive causes need targeted treatment. This chapter teaches physiology, not a substitute for emergency clinical protocols. Further physiological insights The lecture includes concealed internal bleeding after femoral fracture, splenic rupture or ectopic pregnancy; burn-related plasma losses may initially produce haemoconcentration rather than the typical immediate whole-blood Hb concentration pattern. It also covers myoglobin-associated renal injury in trauma, low urine output and tubular injury, myocardial ischaemia, capillary stasis and eventual vascular hyporesponsiveness. Vasovagal, postural, micturition and carotid-sinus syncope have distinct triggers; ordinary orthostatic syncope does not require bradycardia. Understanding Checks 01. Can a patient be shocked with a near-normal MAP after major bleeding? Show explanation Yes. Raised SVR may sustain MAP while reduced venous return and cardiac output impair tissue oxygen delivery. Clinical perfusion matters. 02. Write the equation connecting oxygen delivery to cardiac output. Show explanation Systemic oxygen delivery equals cardiac output multiplied by arterial oxygen content: DO₂ = CO × CaO₂. During haemorrhage, cardiac output may fall immediately from reduced venous return, even while haemoglobin concentration initially remains near its starting value. Normal early Hb does not imply normal oxygen delivery. 03. Why does severe tachycardia sometimes worsen haemorrhagic shock? Show explanation A compensatory faster heartbeat initially supports output, but extreme tachycardia shortens diastole, the period for ventricular filling. If blood volume and venous return are already low, each beat may start with less blood, decreasing stroke volume enough to offset the increased rate. 04. Why is vasoconstriction initially adaptive in haemorrhage? Show explanation It increases SVR and supports arterial pressure while diverting limited blood toward vital organs, but cannot replace lost blood. 05. Which shock type is most directly caused by cardiac tamponade? Show explanation Obstructive shock, because pericardial pressure mechanically restricts ventricular filling and forward output. 06. Which shock type usually follows a large infarct with severe pump dysfunction? Show explanation Cardiogenic shock: inadequate myocardial pumping reduces forward output, sometimes despite elevated filling pressures. 07. Why can early septic shock have warm skin? Show explanation Distributive vasodilation can increase cutaneous blood flow despite severe circulatory dysfunction. 08. Why may Hb and haematocrit be nearly unchanged immediately after acute whole-blood loss? Show explanation Immediately after an acute whole-blood haemorrhage, both red cells and plasma are lost, so the concentration of haemoglobin and haematocrit may initially change little despite a substantial reduction in total circulating red-cell mass. Later interstitial fluid shifts or intravenous fluids dilute the remaining blood. 09. Why does measured Hb often fall later after bleeding? Show explanation Over time, fluid moves from the interstitium into the vascular space, and administered crystalloid may add further plasma volume. Because the lost red cells have not yet been replaced, haemoglobin concentration and haematocrit commonly fall after this haemodilution. 10. Why can lactate rise in shock, and why is it not fully specific? Show explanation Low oxygen delivery may increase anaerobic glycolysis and lactate, but adrenergic stimulation, clearance changes and other causes also affect lactate. 11. Explain one positive feedback loop in progressive shock. Show explanation Low perfusion produces acidosis and myocardial/endothelial injury, worsening cardiac function, microvascular flow and effective volume; this further lowers perfusion. 12. Why is the treatment target different in obstructive and hypovolaemic shock? Show explanation Obstructive shock requires relief of the mechanical block; hypovolaemic shock requires correcting volume loss and its cause. Treating every low BP identically misses the mechanism. Chapter Essentials Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold — Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury — Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Four mechanistic classes — Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns). Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion — Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer — Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow. Progressive shock and positive feedback — Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit — A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. The physiology behind bedside signs — Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning — In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded. Clinical synthesis and limits — The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting. Sources and scope Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30. Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology; Boron and Boulpaep, Medical Physiology."},{"slug":"heart-failure","title":"Heart Failure Physiology","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"31-33; duplicate Lecture 5 pp31-33","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-heart-failure","title":"1. What is heart failure?"},{"id":"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill","title":"2. Systolic and diastolic failure: can't squeeze, or can't fill?"},{"id":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","title":"3. Left versus right failure: where does the blood back up?"},{"id":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","title":"4. What does the body do about it, and why does that make it worse?"},{"id":"5-chapter-essentials","title":"5. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Failure Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-heart-failure\">1. What is heart failure?</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"heart-failure\">Heart failure</span></strong> is when the heart <strong>can't pump enough blood for the body's needs</strong>, or can only do so <strong>at abnormally high filling pressures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both parts matter. Some patients have a near-normal cardiac output at rest, but only because their filling pressures are very high, and that high pressure is what causes their symptoms. So heart failure is <strong>not simply &quot;low cardiac output&quot;</strong>.</p>\n<p>Every failing heart causes two kinds of problem:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"forward-failure\">Forward failure</span>:</strong> too little blood goes <strong>out</strong>, so the organs are underperfused. This causes tiredness, poor exercise tolerance, and kidneys that hold on to salt and water.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"backward-failure\">Backward failure</span>:</strong> blood <strong>backs up</strong> behind the failing chamber, so the veins upstream become congested. This causes fluid in the lungs or the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are <strong>two sides of the same failing pump</strong>, not two different diseases.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill\">2. Systolic and diastolic failure: can't squeeze, or can't fill?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Systolic failure</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Diastolic failure</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>The problem</strong></td>\n  <td>The ventricle can't <strong>squeeze</strong> properly</td>\n  <td>The ventricle can't <strong>relax and fill</strong> properly (it is stiff)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"ejection-fraction\">Ejection fraction</span></strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Reduced</strong> (it ejects a smaller fraction)</td>\n  <td>Often <strong>preserved</strong> (normal fraction of a small volume)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Filling pressure</strong></td>\n  <td>High, because blood is left behind after each beat</td>\n  <td>High, because a stiff ventricle needs high pressure to fill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Typical causes</strong></td>\n  <td>Heart attack, cardiomyopathy</td>\n  <td>Long-standing high blood pressure, ageing, hypertrophy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Both raise filling pressure, so both cause congestion.</strong> A normal ejection fraction does <strong>not</strong> rule out heart failure: a stiff ventricle can eject a normal percentage of a small, high-pressure fill. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>A fast heart makes both worse.</strong> Diastole shortens, and a stiff ventricle, which already fills slowly, gets even less time to fill. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">→ Cardiac Output, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up\">3. Left versus right failure: where does the blood back up?</h2>\n<p><strong>Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle.</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure hf-sides-figure\" id=\"cvs21-sides\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Left versus right failure: where the blood backs up</figcaption><div class=\"hf-sides\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Left and right heart failure chains\"><div class=\"hf-col hf-left\"><strong>Left ventricle fails</strong><span>Left atrial pressure ↑</span><span>Lung vein and capillary pressure ↑</span><span>Fluid filters into the lungs</span><span class=\"hf-end\">Breathlessness, worse lying flat (pulmonary oedema)</span></div><div class=\"hf-col hf-right\"><strong>Right ventricle fails</strong><span>Right atrial pressure ↑</span><span>Systemic vein pressure ↑</span><span>Congested liver and tissues</span><span class=\"hf-end\">Raised JVP, swollen liver, ascites, swollen ankles</span></div></div><p>Original DOMiNANCE diagram. Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle. Left failure is the commonest cause of right failure.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Left heart failure → the lungs.</strong> The left ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the left atrium and the lung veins. The lung capillaries filter fluid into the lungs faster than the lymphatics can clear it. This is <strong><span data-term=\"pulmonary-oedema\">pulmonary oedema</span></strong>, and it causes <strong>breathlessness</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why worse lying flat (<span data-term=\"orthopnoea\">orthopnoea</span>)?</strong> Lying down, blood that pooled in the legs returns to the chest. The failing left ventricle can't pump the extra on, so lung pressure rises further. Patients prop themselves up on pillows to breathe.</p>\n<p><strong>Right heart failure → the body.</strong> The right ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the right atrium and all the systemic veins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Raised JVP</strong>, the visible sign of high right atrial pressure <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses?section=7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important\">→ Pulses and JVP, §7</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Swollen, tender liver</strong>, congested with blood</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"ascites\">Ascites</span></strong>: fluid in the abdomen</li>\n<li><strong>Swollen ankles</strong>: dependent, pitting oedema</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The two often go together.</strong> The commonest cause of right failure is left failure: high lung pressure strains the right ventricle, which has to pump into it.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why can a patient have heart failure with a normal ejection fraction?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A stiff ventricle ejects a normal fraction of a small volume at high pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A normal ejection fraction always means a normal cardiac output</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Ejection fraction is measured only in the right ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart compensates fully by beating more slowly</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> In diastolic failure the ventricle fills poorly and needs high pressure. The fraction ejected is normal, but filling is not.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient with left ventricular failure becomes breathless. What is the mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Low blood pressure reduces oxygen delivery to the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Pressure backs up into the lung veins, so fluid leaks into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The right ventricle pumps too much blood into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Fluid in the abdomen pushes up on the diaphragm</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> High left atrial pressure raises lung capillary pressure, causing pulmonary oedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which finding points to right-sided rather than left-sided failure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Breathlessness when lying flat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fluid in the lungs on a chest X-ray</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Breathlessness on exertion</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A raised JVP with swollen ankles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Right failure raises systemic venous pressure: a raised JVP, liver congestion and ankle oedema. The others reflect lung congestion from left failure.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse\">4. What does the body do about it, and why does that make it worse?</h2>\n<p>When the heart pumps too little, the <strong>kidneys and baroreceptors sense a low arterial pressure</strong> (the arteries are &quot;underfilled&quot;) and switch on three rescue systems:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>System</th>\n  <th>What it does</th>\n  <th>Short-term help</th>\n  <th>Long-term harm</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Sympathetic nerves</strong></td>\n  <td>Faster, stronger heart; tighter arterioles</td>\n  <td>Holds up blood pressure and output</td>\n  <td>Fast rate shortens filling; tight arterioles raise <strong><span data-term=\"afterload\">afterload</span></strong>; the heart muscle is overworked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone (RAAS)</strong></td>\n  <td>Constricts vessels; the kidneys keep salt and water</td>\n  <td>Raises blood volume and pressure</td>\n  <td>More volume → <strong>more congestion and oedema</strong>; higher afterload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>ADH</strong></td>\n  <td>The kidneys keep water</td>\n  <td>Raises blood volume</td>\n  <td>Worsens fluid overload</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The vicious circle:</strong> the failing heart → the body senses &quot;too little blood&quot; → it keeps more fluid and tightens vessels → the heart faces more filling pressure and more afterload → it fails further.</p>\n<p><strong>The paradox:</strong> a patient can be <strong>waterlogged</strong> (oedema everywhere) while the kidneys still act as if the body is <strong>short of blood</strong>. The total body fluid is high, but the arteries are underfilled because the pump is weak. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Frank–Starling helps less and less.</strong> At first, extra filling stretches the ventricle and raises stroke volume. But a failing ventricle sits on a <strong>flat, low curve</strong>, so extra filling mainly raises pressure and congestion. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=5-how-do-we-read-frank-starling-curves\">→ Cardiac Output, §5</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"remodelling\">Remodelling</span>.</strong> Over months, the ventricle dilates or thickens and becomes less efficient: another long-term harm of chronic stimulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why do the kidneys retain salt and water in heart failure, even when the patient is oedematous?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The kidneys are damaged directly by the failing heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They sense underfilled arteries from the weak pump and activate RAAS</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Oedema fluid blocks the kidneys' filtration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The patient drinks more water because of thirst alone</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Low effective arterial filling triggers RAAS and ADH, so the kidneys keep fluid despite the total body excess.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does sympathetic activation eventually harm the failing heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It slows the heart, so cardiac output falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It dilates the arteries, so blood pressure falls too far</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It raises afterload and heart rate, increasing the heart's work</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It lowers blood volume through more urine production</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Tighter arterioles raise afterload, and a faster rate shortens filling and raises oxygen demand, so the weak heart works harder.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does giving extra fluid help a failing heart only a little?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Its Frank–Starling curve is low and flat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Extra fluid lowers the heart's contractility</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The extra fluid goes straight to the kidneys</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Extra fluid lowers the heart rate too much</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> On a flat curve, more filling adds little stroke volume but raises pressure and congestion.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-chapter-essentials\">5. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> the heart can't meet the body's needs, or only at high filling pressures. It is not just low output.</li>\n<li><strong>Forward failure</strong> = too little output. <strong>Backward failure</strong> = congestion upstream. They are two sides of one problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Systolic failure</strong> = can't squeeze (low EF). <strong>Diastolic failure</strong> = can't fill (stiff; EF often preserved). Both raise filling pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Left failure → lungs:</strong> pulmonary oedema, breathlessness, worse lying flat (orthopnoea).</li>\n<li><strong>Right failure → body:</strong> raised JVP, congested liver, ascites, ankle oedema. Left failure is its commonest cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Rescue systems</strong> (sympathetic, RAAS, ADH) hold up pressure at first, but raise afterload and fluid, so they worsen failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Paradox:</strong> waterlogged, yet the kidneys sense &quot;too little blood&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Frank–Starling</strong> gives diminishing returns on a failing, flat curve.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Heart Failure Physiology 1. What is heart failure? Heart failure is when the heart can't pump enough blood for the body's needs, or can only do so at abnormally high filling pressures. Both parts matter. Some patients have a near-normal cardiac output at rest, but only because their filling pressures are very high, and that high pressure is what causes their symptoms. So heart failure is not simply \"low cardiac output\". Every failing heart causes two kinds of problem: Forward failure: too little blood goes out, so the organs are underperfused. This causes tiredness, poor exercise tolerance, and kidneys that hold on to salt and water. Backward failure: blood backs up behind the failing chamber, so the veins upstream become congested. This causes fluid in the lungs or the body. These are two sides of the same failing pump, not two different diseases. 2. Systolic and diastolic failure: can't squeeze, or can't fill? Systolic failure Diastolic failure The problem The ventricle can't squeeze properly The ventricle can't relax and fill properly (it is stiff) Ejection fraction Reduced (it ejects a smaller fraction) Often preserved (normal fraction of a small volume) Filling pressure High, because blood is left behind after each beat High, because a stiff ventricle needs high pressure to fill Typical causes Heart attack, cardiomyopathy Long-standing high blood pressure, ageing, hypertrophy Both raise filling pressure, so both cause congestion. A normal ejection fraction does not rule out heart failure: a stiff ventricle can eject a normal percentage of a small, high-pressure fill. ( → Cardiac Cycle, §7) A fast heart makes both worse. Diastole shortens, and a stiff ventricle, which already fills slowly, gets even less time to fill. ( → Cardiac Output, §2) 3. Left versus right failure: where does the blood back up? Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle. Figure 01. Left versus right failure: where the blood backs up Left ventricle fails Left atrial pressure ↑ Lung vein and capillary pressure ↑ Fluid filters into the lungs Breathlessness, worse lying flat (pulmonary oedema) Right ventricle fails Right atrial pressure ↑ Systemic vein pressure ↑ Congested liver and tissues Raised JVP, swollen liver, ascites, swollen ankles Original DOMiNANCE diagram. Blood backs up into the circulation that feeds the failing ventricle. Left failure is the commonest cause of right failure. Left heart failure → the lungs. The left ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the left atrium and the lung veins. The lung capillaries filter fluid into the lungs faster than the lymphatics can clear it. This is pulmonary oedema, and it causes breathlessness. ( → Oedema, §4) Why worse lying flat ( orthopnoea)? Lying down, blood that pooled in the legs returns to the chest. The failing left ventricle can't pump the extra on, so lung pressure rises further. Patients prop themselves up on pillows to breathe. Right heart failure → the body. The right ventricle can't empty, so pressure rises in the right atrium and all the systemic veins: Raised JVP, the visible sign of high right atrial pressure ( → Pulses and JVP, §7) Swollen, tender liver, congested with blood Ascites: fluid in the abdomen Swollen ankles: dependent, pitting oedema The two often go together. The commonest cause of right failure is left failure: high lung pressure strains the right ventricle, which has to pump into it. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why can a patient have heart failure with a normal ejection fraction? A. A stiff ventricle ejects a normal fraction of a small volume at high pressure B. A normal ejection fraction always means a normal cardiac output C. Ejection fraction is measured only in the right ventricle D. The heart compensates fully by beating more slowly Show answer and explanation Answer: A. In diastolic failure the ventricle fills poorly and needs high pressure. The fraction ejected is normal, but filling is not. 2. A patient with left ventricular failure becomes breathless. What is the mechanism? A. Low blood pressure reduces oxygen delivery to the lungs B. Pressure backs up into the lung veins, so fluid leaks into the lungs C. The right ventricle pumps too much blood into the lungs D. Fluid in the abdomen pushes up on the diaphragm Show answer and explanation Answer: B. High left atrial pressure raises lung capillary pressure, causing pulmonary oedema. 3. Which finding points to right-sided rather than left-sided failure? A. Breathlessness when lying flat B. Fluid in the lungs on a chest X-ray C. Breathlessness on exertion D. A raised JVP with swollen ankles Show answer and explanation Answer: D. Right failure raises systemic venous pressure: a raised JVP, liver congestion and ankle oedema. The others reflect lung congestion from left failure. 4. What does the body do about it, and why does that make it worse? When the heart pumps too little, the kidneys and baroreceptors sense a low arterial pressure (the arteries are \"underfilled\") and switch on three rescue systems: System What it does Short-term help Long-term harm Sympathetic nerves Faster, stronger heart; tighter arterioles Holds up blood pressure and output Fast rate shortens filling; tight arterioles raise afterload; the heart muscle is overworked Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone (RAAS) Constricts vessels; the kidneys keep salt and water Raises blood volume and pressure More volume → more congestion and oedema; higher afterload ADH The kidneys keep water Raises blood volume Worsens fluid overload The vicious circle: the failing heart → the body senses \"too little blood\" → it keeps more fluid and tightens vessels → the heart faces more filling pressure and more afterload → it fails further. The paradox: a patient can be waterlogged (oedema everywhere) while the kidneys still act as if the body is short of blood. The total body fluid is high, but the arteries are underfilled because the pump is weak. ( → Shock) Frank–Starling helps less and less. At first, extra filling stretches the ventricle and raises stroke volume. But a failing ventricle sits on a flat, low curve, so extra filling mainly raises pressure and congestion. ( → Cardiac Output, §5) Remodelling. Over months, the ventricle dilates or thickens and becomes less efficient: another long-term harm of chronic stimulation. Understanding Check: Section 4 1. Why do the kidneys retain salt and water in heart failure, even when the patient is oedematous? A. The kidneys are damaged directly by the failing heart B. They sense underfilled arteries from the weak pump and activate RAAS C. Oedema fluid blocks the kidneys' filtration D. The patient drinks more water because of thirst alone Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Low effective arterial filling triggers RAAS and ADH, so the kidneys keep fluid despite the total body excess. 2. How does sympathetic activation eventually harm the failing heart? A. It slows the heart, so cardiac output falls B. It dilates the arteries, so blood pressure falls too far C. It raises afterload and heart rate, increasing the heart's work D. It lowers blood volume through more urine production Show answer and explanation Answer: C. Tighter arterioles raise afterload, and a faster rate shortens filling and raises oxygen demand, so the weak heart works harder. 3. Why does giving extra fluid help a failing heart only a little? A. Its Frank–Starling curve is low and flat B. Extra fluid lowers the heart's contractility C. The extra fluid goes straight to the kidneys D. Extra fluid lowers the heart rate too much Show answer and explanation Answer: A. On a flat curve, more filling adds little stroke volume but raises pressure and congestion. 5. Chapter Essentials Heart failure: the heart can't meet the body's needs, or only at high filling pressures. It is not just low output. Forward failure = too little output. Backward failure = congestion upstream. They are two sides of one problem. Systolic failure = can't squeeze (low EF). Diastolic failure = can't fill (stiff; EF often preserved). Both raise filling pressure. Left failure → lungs: pulmonary oedema, breathlessness, worse lying flat (orthopnoea). Right failure → body: raised JVP, congested liver, ascites, ankle oedema. Left failure is its commonest cause. Rescue systems (sympathetic, RAAS, ADH) hold up pressure at first, but raise afterload and fluid, so they worsen failure. Paradox: waterlogged, yet the kidneys sense \"too little blood\". Frank–Starling gives diminishing returns on a failing, flat curve."},{"slug":"cerebral","title":"Cerebral Circulation and CSF","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"2-25; extra diagrams Circulation 3 39-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-the-brain-can-t-wait-for-blood","title":"1. Why the brain can't wait for blood"},{"id":"2-where-does-the-blood-come-from","title":"2. Where does the blood come from?"},{"id":"3-how-does-the-brain-keep-its-blood-flow-steady","title":"3. How does the brain keep its blood flow steady?"},{"id":"4-a-rigid-box-intracranial-pressure-and-perfusion","title":"4. A rigid box: intracranial pressure and perfusion"},{"id":"5-the-coverings-the-ventricles-and-csf","title":"5. The coverings, the ventricles and CSF"},{"id":"6-the-blood-brain-barrier","title":"6. The blood–brain barrier"},{"id":"7-stroke","title":"7. Stroke"},{"id":"8-chapter-essentials","title":"8. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-the-brain-can-t-wait-for-blood\">1. Why the brain can't wait for blood</h2>\n<p>The brain is about 2% of body weight but uses about <strong>15% of the cardiac output</strong> and about <strong>20% of the body's oxygen</strong> at rest. Yet it stores almost no oxygen and very little glucose. So it depends on a <strong>continuous</strong> blood supply:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>About <strong>10 seconds</strong> without blood flow causes <strong>unconsciousness</strong>.</li>\n<li>A few minutes causes permanent damage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Not every part is equally fragile.</strong> The cerebral cortex, <strong><span data-term=\"basal-ganglia\">basal ganglia</span></strong> and <strong>thalamus</strong> are the most sensitive to low oxygen. The <strong>brainstem</strong>, which runs breathing and circulation, is more resistant. That is why a patient can survive a severe hypoxic injury with brainstem functions intact.</p>\n<p><strong>Fuel:</strong> glucose supplies about <strong>90%</strong> of the brain's energy. Brain cells take it up <strong>without needing insulin</strong>. So <strong>hypoglycaemia</strong> hits the brain fast: confusion, then <strong>convulsions</strong> and <strong>coma</strong>. In <strong>prolonged starvation</strong>, the brain switches partly to <strong><span data-term=\"ketone-bodies\">ketone bodies</span></strong>, made by the liver from fat.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says that in starvation the brain uses \"other substances (amino acids, lipids and proteins)\". More precisely, the main alternative fuel is ketone bodies, which the liver makes from fatty acids; ordinary fatty acids are not a major brain fuel.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-where-does-the-blood-come-from\">2. Where does the blood come from?</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Circle_of_Willis_en.svg\" alt=\"Detailed inferior view of cerebral arterial anatomy showing vertebral and basilar arteries, internal carotids, anterior communicating arteries, posterior communicating arteries and branches of the Circle of Willis.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. The real anatomical arrangement of the Circle of Willis.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Rhcastilhos, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Circle_of_Willis_en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Two pairs of arteries supply the brain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The two <strong>internal carotid arteries</strong> supply most of the front and middle of the brain.</li>\n<li>The two <strong>vertebral arteries</strong> join to form the <strong>basilar artery</strong>, which supplies the back of the brain and the brainstem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the base of the brain, these connect in a ring: the <strong><span data-term=\"circle-of-willis\">Circle of Willis</span></strong>. In principle, if one feeding artery narrows slowly, blood can come round the ring from the others. In practice, the ring is often incomplete or has thin segments, so it can't always compensate.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Cerebral arterial supply — conceptual route map</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 248\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Internal carotid systems lead to anterior and middle cerebral arteries; paired vertebral arteries join into basilar artery and reach posterior cerebral arteries; communicating vessels link the two systems\"><g font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#22354e\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"16\" width=\"240\" height=\"46\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6efff\" stroke=\"#638bd0\"/><text x=\"134\" y=\"44\">Internal carotid systems</text><rect x=\"408\" y=\"16\" width=\"238\" height=\"46\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f5ea\" stroke=\"#76a586\"/><text x=\"527\" y=\"44\">Vertebral arteries → basilar</text><rect x=\"12\" y=\"128\" width=\"192\" height=\"48\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6efff\" stroke=\"#638bd0\"/><text x=\"108\" y=\"148\">ACA: medial</text><text x=\"108\" y=\"166\">hemisphere</text><rect x=\"218\" y=\"128\" width=\"192\" height=\"48\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6efff\" stroke=\"#638bd0\"/><text x=\"314\" y=\"148\">MCA: lateral</text><text x=\"314\" y=\"166\">hemisphere</text><rect x=\"424\" y=\"128\" width=\"222\" height=\"48\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f5ea\" stroke=\"#76a586\"/><text x=\"535\" y=\"148\">PCA: occipital</text><text x=\"535\" y=\"166\">and related regions</text><rect x=\"167\" y=\"195\" width=\"332\" height=\"36\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fff3d9\" stroke=\"#bb9e5e\"/><text x=\"333\" y=\"218\">Circle of Willis: potential collateral links</text></g><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#587ca6\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><path d=\"M134 62v33H108v32M134 95h180v32M527 62v65M134 95v-8h393v40\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M108 176v19M314 176v19M535 176v19H498\"/></g></svg><p>This is a flow-and-territory learning schematic, not an anatomical depiction of the ring's exact vessel positions. The MCA arises from the internal carotid but is not part of the Circle of Willis.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Beyond the ring, there is little backup.</strong> The cerebral arteries that leave the circle have <strong>few connections (<span data-term=\"anastomosis\">anastomoses</span>)</strong> with each other. So when one is blocked suddenly, the tissue it supplies has no other source, and it dies.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Artery</th>\n  <th>Main territory</th>\n  <th>Blockage typically affects</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Anterior cerebral (ACA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Inner (medial) surface of the frontal and parietal lobes</td>\n  <td>The opposite <strong>leg</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Middle cerebral (MCA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Most of the outer (lateral) surface of the hemisphere</td>\n  <td>The opposite <strong>face and arm</strong>; <strong>speech</strong>, if the dominant side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Posterior cerebral (PCA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Occipital lobe</td>\n  <td><strong>Vision</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>middle cerebral artery</strong> is a branch of the internal carotid. It is <strong>not</strong> part of the ring itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-does-the-brain-keep-its-blood-flow-steady\">3. How does the brain keep its blood flow steady?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Pressure autoregulation — conceptual blood-flow curve</figcaption><svg role=\"img\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 235\" aria-label=\"Cerebral blood flow rises at low pressure, changes relatively little across an autoregulated range, then rises again at high pressure; exact limits vary\"><g stroke=\"#62758d\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M65 20v180h510\"/></g><path d=\"M75 187Q125 178 170 100Q230 83 335 85Q415 87 455 79Q500 65 565 24\" stroke=\"#2468c5\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#263d61\"><text x=\"18\" y=\"19\">CBF</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"222\">Cerebral perfusion pressure →</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"153\">Low pressure</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"67\">Buffered flow</text><text x=\"462\" y=\"116\">High pressure</text></g><g stroke=\"#a7aeb8\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 5\"><path d=\"M190 45v152M448 45v152\"/></g></svg><p>As driving pressure changes, cerebral arterioles adjust resistance to buffer cerebral blood flow. The apparent plateau is approximate, not perfectly flat; its boundaries vary and can shift with disease or chronic hypertension.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"autoregulation\">Autoregulation</span>.</strong> When perfusion pressure rises, the cerebral arterioles <strong>constrict</strong>; when it falls, they <strong>dilate</strong>. So cerebral blood flow stays nearly constant across a wide range of blood pressures (classically quoted as a mean arterial pressure of about 60–150 mmHg; the limits vary between people). Outside that range, or after a brain injury, flow simply follows pressure. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Regulation</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Carbon dioxide: the strongest controller.</strong> A rise in arterial CO₂ raises H⁺ around the vessels, and the arterioles <strong>dilate</strong>, so flow rises. A fall in CO₂ <strong>constricts</strong> them. That is why <strong>overbreathing</strong> (which blows off CO₂) can make you <strong>dizzy</strong>: cerebral blood flow falls. Severe hypoxia also dilates cerebral vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Active areas get more blood.</strong> When a brain region works harder, local signals dilate its arterioles, so flow rises <strong>just in that region</strong>. This is what brain-activity scans detect.</p>\n<p><strong>Nerves play only a small part.</strong> Cerebral vessels are supplied by <strong>sympathetic (adrenergic)</strong>, <strong>cholinergic</strong> and <strong>sensory</strong> nerves. The sensory nerves explain why the <strong>vessels and meninges feel pain</strong> (a cause of headache), although brain tissue itself does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which part of the brain is most resistant to a period of low oxygen?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The brainstem, which controls breathing and circulation</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The cerebral cortex, which controls conscious thought</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The basal ganglia, which help to control movement</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The thalamus, which relays sensory signals to the cortex</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are the most sensitive. The brainstem tolerates hypoxia better.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why can a sudden blockage of the middle cerebral artery kill brain tissue, even with a Circle of Willis?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Beyond the circle, cerebral arteries have few connections with each other</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The middle cerebral artery carries blood away from the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The Circle of Willis closes completely during a stroke</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The middle cerebral artery has no branches into the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The ring sits upstream. The arteries leaving it have little anastomosis, so a blocked branch leaves its territory without supply.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A student hyperventilates and feels dizzy. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Low CO₂ constricts the cerebral arterioles, so brain blood flow falls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. High CO₂ dilates the cerebral arterioles, so brain pressure rises</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Low oxygen in the blood constricts the cerebral arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Fast breathing raises blood pressure above the autoregulation range</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Overbreathing blows off CO₂. Cerebral arterioles constrict, and flow drops.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-a-rigid-box-intracranial-pressure-and-perfusion\">4. A rigid box: intracranial pressure and perfusion</h2>\n<p><strong>The Monro–Kellie doctrine.</strong> The skull is a closed, rigid box. It holds three things whose total volume stays nearly constant:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brain</strong>, about 1400 g</li>\n<li><strong>Blood</strong>, about 75 mL</li>\n<li><strong>CSF</strong>, about 75 mL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If one increases (a bleed, a tumour, swelling), something else must leave. At first, CSF and venous blood are squeezed out, so the pressure barely rises. Once that reserve is used up, even a small extra volume makes the <strong><span data-term=\"icp\">intracranial pressure (ICP)</span></strong> shoot up, and the rising pressure <strong>compresses the cerebral vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"cpp\">Cerebral perfusion pressure</span>: the pressure that actually drives blood through the brain.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal: MAP 90, ICP 10 → <strong>CPP 80 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n<li>ICP rises to 35, MAP still 90 → <strong>CPP 55 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The arm blood pressure hasn't changed, yet the brain is getting less blood.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Increasing ICP reduces the driving pressure available to perfuse the brain</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 630 170\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"At constant MAP 90 mmHg, ICP rising from 10 to 35 mmHg lowers CPP from 80 to 55 mmHg\"><g font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" fill=\"#253b57\" font-size=\"15\"><text x=\"10\" y=\"25\" font-weight=\"bold\">Example at MAP = 90 mmHg</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"69\">ICP = 10</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"129\">ICP = 35</text><rect x=\"115\" y=\"47\" width=\"400\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e2ebf6\"/><rect x=\"115\" y=\"47\" width=\"356\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#73a4dc\"/><rect x=\"115\" y=\"107\" width=\"400\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e2ebf6\"/><rect x=\"115\" y=\"107\" width=\"244\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dc9873\"/><text x=\"480\" y=\"68\">CPP 80</text><text x=\"369\" y=\"128\">CPP 55</text></g></svg><p>Illustrative pressure calculation; these bars are not a patient-specific treatment threshold. Cerebral flow additionally depends on resistance.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The Cushing reflex: a last-ditch defence.</strong> When ICP climbs very high (the lecture gives more than about <strong>33 mmHg</strong>), the brainstem itself becomes ischaemic. Its vasomotor centre fires a massive <strong>sympathetic</strong> discharge that <strong>raises arterial pressure</strong>, trying to force blood back in. The baroreceptors detect the high pressure and <strong>slow the heart</strong>. <strong>Irregular breathing</strong> may follow.</p>\n<p>So the <strong><span data-term=\"cushing-triad\">Cushing triad</span></strong> is <strong>high blood pressure, slow heart rate and irregular breathing</strong>. It is a <strong>late, dangerous sign</strong>: the brain is about to be compressed past its limit. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes\">→ Reflexes</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-the-coverings-the-ventricles-and-csf\">5. The coverings, the ventricles and CSF</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meninges-en.svg\" alt=\"Cross-sectional anatomical illustration identifying dura mater, arachnoid mater, subarachnoid space and pia mater\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Meninges and the subarachnoid compartment.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Mysid and Wikimedia contributors, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meninges-en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The meninges</strong>, from outside in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Dura mater:</strong> tough, attached to the skull.</li>\n<li><strong>Arachnoid mater:</strong> thin, joined to the brain surface by fine strands (trabeculae).</li>\n<li><strong>Subarachnoid space:</strong> contains <strong>CSF</strong> and the cerebral blood vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Pia mater:</strong> clings to the brain surface, following every fold.</li>\n</ol>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says the subdural space also contains CSF. In normal anatomy, the subdural space is only a potential space; CSF lies in the subarachnoid space.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Where CSF is made and where it goes.</strong> CSF is made mainly by the <strong><span data-term=\"choroid-plexus\">choroid plexus</span></strong> in the lateral, third and fourth ventricles. It then flows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lateral ventricles</strong> → through the <strong>foramen of Monro</strong> (interventricular foramen) →</li>\n<li><strong>Third ventricle</strong> → through the <strong>aqueduct of Sylvius</strong> (cerebral aqueduct) →</li>\n<li><strong>Fourth ventricle</strong> → out through the <strong>foramen of Magendie</strong> (middle) and the <strong>foramina of Luschka</strong> (sides) →</li>\n<li><strong>Subarachnoid space</strong>, around the brain and spinal cord →</li>\n<li>Absorbed through the <strong><span data-term=\"arachnoid-villi\">arachnoid villi</span></strong> into the venous sinuses, and so into the blood.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/CSF_circulation.png\" alt=\"Illustration of the brain ventricles, subarachnoid cerebrospinal fluid spaces and CSF circulation.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Ventricular and subarachnoid CSF compartments.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\"><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CSF_circulation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons source and contributors</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. CSF route from ventricular production to subarachnoid circulation</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flow sequence from lateral ventricles via Monro, third ventricle, aqueduct and fourth ventricle to subarachnoid space\"><g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#1b3557\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"32\" width=\"112\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e7f0ff\" stroke=\"#5c8bd7\"/><text x=\"64\" y=\"55\">Lateral</text><text x=\"64\" y=\"75\">ventricles</text><rect x=\"152\" y=\"32\" width=\"106\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f6ed\" stroke=\"#5d9d7e\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"55\">Third</text><text x=\"205\" y=\"75\">ventricle</text><rect x=\"290\" y=\"32\" width=\"105\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff1d7\" stroke=\"#bf953e\"/><text x=\"342\" y=\"55\">Cerebral</text><text x=\"342\" y=\"75\">aqueduct</text><rect x=\"427\" y=\"32\" width=\"105\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e5f6ed\" stroke=\"#5d9d7e\"/><text x=\"479\" y=\"55\">Fourth</text><text x=\"479\" y=\"75\">ventricle</text><rect x=\"564\" y=\"32\" width=\"108\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e7f0ff\" stroke=\"#5c8bd7\"/><text x=\"618\" y=\"55\">Subarachnoid</text><text x=\"618\" y=\"75\">space</text></g><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#436f9f\" stroke-width=\"3\"><path d=\"M121 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6M259 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6M396 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6M533 63h25m-7 -6 7 6 -7 6\"/></g><g font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#415772\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"136\" y=\"119\">Monro</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"118\">Magendie/</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"133\">Luschka</text></g></svg><p>Original conceptual diagram; the choroid plexus produces most CSF, and the arrows represent bulk flow rather than exact anatomical proportions.</p></figure>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>CSF value</th>\n  <th>Typical adult</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Volume</td>\n  <td>About <strong>150 mL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Production</td>\n  <td>About <strong>550 mL/day</strong>, so it is replaced several times a day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure (lumbar, lying down)</td>\n  <td><strong>70–180 mm CSF</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Protein</td>\n  <td>About <strong>20 mg/dL</strong>, far less than plasma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Glucose</td>\n  <td>About <strong>64 mg/dL</strong>, roughly two-thirds of blood glucose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What CSF does:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cushions</strong> the brain against blows to the head.</li>\n<li><strong>Buoyancy:</strong> a brain weighing about 1400 g in air weighs only about <strong>50 g</strong> in CSF, so it doesn't crush its own base.</li>\n<li><strong>Removes waste:</strong> the one-way flow into the blood carries metabolites and drugs away.</li>\n<li><strong>Carries hormones</strong> to distant parts of the brain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hydrocephalus: when CSF builds up.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"hydrocephalus\">Non-communicating</span> (obstructive):</strong> something <strong>blocks the pathway</strong> inside the ventricles, for example at the narrow aqueduct. The ventricles upstream of the block swell.</li>\n<li><strong>Communicating:</strong> the ventricles connect normally, but the <strong>arachnoid villi can't absorb</strong> CSF fast enough.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lumbar puncture</strong> samples CSF from the <strong>subarachnoid space in the lower back</strong>, below where the spinal cord ends. If ICP may be raised by a mass, it must be checked first, because releasing pressure from below can pull the brain downwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A patient's MAP is 90 mmHg and ICP rises from 10 to 40 mmHg. What happens to cerebral perfusion pressure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It falls from 80 to 50 mmHg, although arm blood pressure is unchanged</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It stays at 80 mmHg, because autoregulation keeps it constant</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It rises from 80 to 130 mmHg, because ICP adds to the MAP</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls to 40 mmHg, because CPP always equals ICP</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> CPP = MAP − ICP: 90 − 10 = 80, then 90 − 40 = 50 mmHg.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> In the Cushing reflex, why does the heart rate fall?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The baroreceptors respond to the sympathetic rise in blood pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The vagus nerve is crushed directly by the swollen brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Raised ICP blocks sympathetic nerves to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart receives less blood when ICP is high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Brainstem ischaemia drives blood pressure up. The baroreflex then slows the heart.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A tumour blocks the aqueduct of Sylvius. Which ventricles enlarge?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The lateral and third ventricles, upstream of the block</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The fourth ventricle only, downstream of the block</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. All four ventricles equally, because CSF absorption fails</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. None, because CSF can bypass the aqueduct easily</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> CSF made in the lateral and third ventricles can't get past the aqueduct, so they swell: non-communicating hydrocephalus.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-the-blood-brain-barrier\">6. The blood–brain barrier</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blood_brain_barrier.png\" alt=\"Comparison of brain capillary endothelial barrier with the more permeable capillaries of other tissues\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. What makes cerebral capillaries different?</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">David Nascari and Alan Sved, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blood_brain_barrier.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>In most capillaries, small molecules slip through gaps between the endothelial cells. In the brain, the endothelial cells are sealed together by <strong><span data-term=\"tight-junctions\">tight junctions</span></strong>, and there are no gaps or pores. So anything leaving the blood must pass <strong>through</strong> the cells. This is the <strong><span data-term=\"bbb\">blood–brain barrier</span></strong>, and it lies in the <strong>capillary endothelium</strong>. Surrounding pericytes and astrocyte endfeet help maintain it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Crosses easily</th>\n  <th>Crosses poorly</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Water, O₂, CO₂</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Proteins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Lipid-soluble</strong> substances (e.g. steroid hormones)</td>\n  <td>Substances <strong>bound to plasma proteins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Glucose</strong>, quickly, but only by a <strong>carrier</strong> (GLUT1)</td>\n  <td>Most <strong>ions and water-soluble</strong> molecules</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why have it?</strong> It keeps the neurons' environment <strong>constant</strong>, despite swings in blood ions and hormones, and <strong>shields</strong> them from toxins and circulating antigens.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A drug acting on the brain must be able to <strong>cross</strong> the barrier.</li>\n<li>The barrier <strong>breaks down</strong> in infection, injury, tumours and inflammation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>choroid plexus</strong> has its own barrier between blood and CSF, but there the seal is formed by the <strong>epithelial cells</strong>, not the capillaries.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-stroke\">7. Stroke</h2>\n<p>A <strong>stroke</strong> is a sudden loss of blood supply to part of the brain, causing ischaemia and cell death. The signs depend on which artery is affected (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=2-where-does-the-blood-come-from\">§2</a>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Ischaemic stroke</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Haemorrhagic stroke</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>What happens</td>\n  <td>An artery is <strong>blocked</strong></td>\n  <td>An artery <strong>ruptures</strong> and bleeds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Causes</td>\n  <td><strong>Atherosclerotic plaque</strong>, <strong>thrombus</strong> or <strong>embolus</strong></td>\n  <td>Often a ruptured <strong>aneurysm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Extra danger</td>\n  <td>—</td>\n  <td>The blood adds volume, so <strong>ICP rises</strong> (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=4-a-rigid-box-intracranial-pressure-and-perfusion\">§4</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Where does the blood–brain barrier lie?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the tight junctions of the brain's capillary endothelium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the arachnoid villi that absorb the cerebrospinal fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the pia mater that covers the surface of the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the smooth muscle wall of the cerebral arterioles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions force substances to pass through the cells, not between them.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does glucose cross the blood–brain barrier?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Quickly, by a specific carrier in the endothelial cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Slowly, by diffusing between the endothelial cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Only when insulin opens channels in the endothelium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Bound to plasma proteins that carry it across</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Glucose is water-soluble, so it needs a carrier (GLUT1). The carrier moves it fast, and no insulin is needed.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can a haemorrhagic stroke damage brain tissue far from the bleeding artery?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The blood adds volume inside the skull, raising ICP everywhere</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The bleeding artery stops all flow through the Circle of Willis</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The blood dissolves the blood–brain barrier throughout the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The bleed lowers the CSF pressure, so the brain collapses</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> In a rigid skull, extra volume raises ICP, lowering perfusion pressure across the whole brain.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\">8. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The brain can't store oxygen:</strong> about 10 seconds without flow causes unconsciousness. The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are most sensitive; the brainstem is more resistant.</li>\n<li><strong>Fuel:</strong> glucose (about 90%), taken up without insulin. Prolonged starvation: ketone bodies.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply:</strong> internal carotids + vertebrals → basilar, joined in the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>. Beyond the ring, <strong>little anastomosis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> keeps flow steady across a wide pressure range. <strong>CO₂</strong> is the strongest controller: high CO₂ dilates, low CO₂ constricts.</li>\n<li><strong>Monro–Kellie:</strong> brain + blood + CSF in a rigid skull. <strong>CPP = MAP − ICP.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cushing reflex:</strong> very high ICP → brainstem ischaemia → hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing. A late sign.</li>\n<li><strong>CSF:</strong> made by the choroid plexus; lateral → Monro → third → Sylvius → fourth → Magendie/Luschka → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi. About 150 mL, about 550 mL/day.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrocephalus:</strong> non-communicating (pathway blocked) or communicating (absorption fails).</li>\n<li><strong>Blood–brain barrier:</strong> tight junctions in the capillary endothelium. Water, gases and lipid-soluble substances cross; glucose crosses by carrier; proteins don't.</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke:</strong> ischaemic (blocked) or haemorrhagic (ruptured, often an aneurysm).</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier 1. Why the brain can't wait for blood The brain is about 2% of body weight but uses about 15% of the cardiac output and about 20% of the body's oxygen at rest. Yet it stores almost no oxygen and very little glucose. So it depends on a continuous blood supply: About 10 seconds without blood flow causes unconsciousness. A few minutes causes permanent damage. Not every part is equally fragile. The cerebral cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are the most sensitive to low oxygen. The brainstem, which runs breathing and circulation, is more resistant. That is why a patient can survive a severe hypoxic injury with brainstem functions intact. Fuel: glucose supplies about 90% of the brain's energy. Brain cells take it up without needing insulin. So hypoglycaemia hits the brain fast: confusion, then convulsions and coma. In prolonged starvation, the brain switches partly to ketone bodies, made by the liver from fat. Source note: The lecture says that in starvation the brain uses \"other substances (amino acids, lipids and proteins)\". More precisely, the main alternative fuel is ketone bodies, which the liver makes from fatty acids; ordinary fatty acids are not a major brain fuel. 2. Where does the blood come from? Figure. The real anatomical arrangement of the Circle of Willis. Image credit Rhcastilhos, Wikimedia Commons · public domain · unmodified. Two pairs of arteries supply the brain: The two internal carotid arteries supply most of the front and middle of the brain. The two vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery, which supplies the back of the brain and the brainstem. At the base of the brain, these connect in a ring: the Circle of Willis. In principle, if one feeding artery narrows slowly, blood can come round the ring from the others. In practice, the ring is often incomplete or has thin segments, so it can't always compensate. Figure. Cerebral arterial supply — conceptual route map Internal carotid systems Vertebral arteries → basilar ACA: medial hemisphere MCA: lateral hemisphere PCA: occipital and related regions Circle of Willis: potential collateral links This is a flow-and-territory learning schematic, not an anatomical depiction of the ring's exact vessel positions. The MCA arises from the internal carotid but is not part of the Circle of Willis. Beyond the ring, there is little backup. The cerebral arteries that leave the circle have few connections ( anastomoses) with each other. So when one is blocked suddenly, the tissue it supplies has no other source, and it dies. Artery Main territory Blockage typically affects Anterior cerebral (ACA) Inner (medial) surface of the frontal and parietal lobes The opposite leg Middle cerebral (MCA) Most of the outer (lateral) surface of the hemisphere The opposite face and arm; speech, if the dominant side Posterior cerebral (PCA) Occipital lobe Vision The middle cerebral artery is a branch of the internal carotid. It is not part of the ring itself. 3. How does the brain keep its blood flow steady? Pressure autoregulation — conceptual blood-flow curve CBF Cerebral perfusion pressure → Low pressure Buffered flow High pressure As driving pressure changes, cerebral arterioles adjust resistance to buffer cerebral blood flow. The apparent plateau is approximate, not perfectly flat; its boundaries vary and can shift with disease or chronic hypertension. Autoregulation. When perfusion pressure rises, the cerebral arterioles constrict; when it falls, they dilate. So cerebral blood flow stays nearly constant across a wide range of blood pressures (classically quoted as a mean arterial pressure of about 60–150 mmHg; the limits vary between people). Outside that range, or after a brain injury, flow simply follows pressure. ( → Local Regulation) Carbon dioxide: the strongest controller. A rise in arterial CO₂ raises H⁺ around the vessels, and the arterioles dilate, so flow rises. A fall in CO₂ constricts them. That is why overbreathing (which blows off CO₂) can make you dizzy: cerebral blood flow falls. Severe hypoxia also dilates cerebral vessels. Active areas get more blood. When a brain region works harder, local signals dilate its arterioles, so flow rises just in that region. This is what brain-activity scans detect. Nerves play only a small part. Cerebral vessels are supplied by sympathetic (adrenergic), cholinergic and sensory nerves. The sensory nerves explain why the vessels and meninges feel pain (a cause of headache), although brain tissue itself does not. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Which part of the brain is most resistant to a period of low oxygen? A. The brainstem, which controls breathing and circulation B. The cerebral cortex, which controls conscious thought C. The basal ganglia, which help to control movement D. The thalamus, which relays sensory signals to the cortex Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are the most sensitive. The brainstem tolerates hypoxia better. 2. Why can a sudden blockage of the middle cerebral artery kill brain tissue, even with a Circle of Willis? A. Beyond the circle, cerebral arteries have few connections with each other B. The middle cerebral artery carries blood away from the brain C. The Circle of Willis closes completely during a stroke D. The middle cerebral artery has no branches into the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The ring sits upstream. The arteries leaving it have little anastomosis, so a blocked branch leaves its territory without supply. 3. A student hyperventilates and feels dizzy. Why? A. Low CO₂ constricts the cerebral arterioles, so brain blood flow falls B. High CO₂ dilates the cerebral arterioles, so brain pressure rises C. Low oxygen in the blood constricts the cerebral arterioles D. Fast breathing raises blood pressure above the autoregulation range Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Overbreathing blows off CO₂. Cerebral arterioles constrict, and flow drops. 4. A rigid box: intracranial pressure and perfusion The Monro–Kellie doctrine. The skull is a closed, rigid box. It holds three things whose total volume stays nearly constant: Brain, about 1400 g Blood, about 75 mL CSF, about 75 mL If one increases (a bleed, a tumour, swelling), something else must leave. At first, CSF and venous blood are squeezed out, so the pressure barely rises. Once that reserve is used up, even a small extra volume makes the intracranial pressure (ICP) shoot up, and the rising pressure compresses the cerebral vessels. Cerebral perfusion pressure: the pressure that actually drives blood through the brain. CPP = MAP − ICP Normal: MAP 90, ICP 10 → CPP 80 mmHg. ICP rises to 35, MAP still 90 → CPP 55 mmHg. The arm blood pressure hasn't changed, yet the brain is getting less blood. Figure. Increasing ICP reduces the driving pressure available to perfuse the brain Example at MAP = 90 mmHg ICP = 10 ICP = 35 CPP 80 CPP 55 Illustrative pressure calculation; these bars are not a patient-specific treatment threshold. Cerebral flow additionally depends on resistance. The Cushing reflex: a last-ditch defence. When ICP climbs very high (the lecture gives more than about 33 mmHg), the brainstem itself becomes ischaemic. Its vasomotor centre fires a massive sympathetic discharge that raises arterial pressure, trying to force blood back in. The baroreceptors detect the high pressure and slow the heart. Irregular breathing may follow. So the Cushing triad is high blood pressure, slow heart rate and irregular breathing. It is a late, dangerous sign: the brain is about to be compressed past its limit. ( → Reflexes) 5. The coverings, the ventricles and CSF Figure. Meninges and the subarachnoid compartment. Image credit Mysid and Wikimedia contributors, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. The meninges, from outside in: Dura mater: tough, attached to the skull. Arachnoid mater: thin, joined to the brain surface by fine strands (trabeculae). Subarachnoid space: contains CSF and the cerebral blood vessels. Pia mater: clings to the brain surface, following every fold. Source note: The lecture says the subdural space also contains CSF. In normal anatomy, the subdural space is only a potential space; CSF lies in the subarachnoid space. Where CSF is made and where it goes. CSF is made mainly by the choroid plexus in the lateral, third and fourth ventricles. It then flows: Lateral ventricles → through the foramen of Monro (interventricular foramen) → Third ventricle → through the aqueduct of Sylvius (cerebral aqueduct) → Fourth ventricle → out through the foramen of Magendie (middle) and the foramina of Luschka (sides) → Subarachnoid space, around the brain and spinal cord → Absorbed through the arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses, and so into the blood. Figure. Ventricular and subarachnoid CSF compartments. Image credit Wikimedia Commons source and contributors · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. Figure. CSF route from ventricular production to subarachnoid circulation Lateral ventricles Third ventricle Cerebral aqueduct Fourth ventricle Subarachnoid space Monro Magendie/ Luschka Original conceptual diagram; the choroid plexus produces most CSF, and the arrows represent bulk flow rather than exact anatomical proportions. CSF value Typical adult Volume About 150 mL Production About 550 mL/day, so it is replaced several times a day Pressure (lumbar, lying down) 70–180 mm CSF Protein About 20 mg/dL, far less than plasma Glucose About 64 mg/dL, roughly two-thirds of blood glucose What CSF does: Cushions the brain against blows to the head. Buoyancy: a brain weighing about 1400 g in air weighs only about 50 g in CSF, so it doesn't crush its own base. Removes waste: the one-way flow into the blood carries metabolites and drugs away. Carries hormones to distant parts of the brain. Hydrocephalus: when CSF builds up. Non-communicating (obstructive): something blocks the pathway inside the ventricles, for example at the narrow aqueduct. The ventricles upstream of the block swell. Communicating: the ventricles connect normally, but the arachnoid villi can't absorb CSF fast enough. Lumbar puncture samples CSF from the subarachnoid space in the lower back, below where the spinal cord ends. If ICP may be raised by a mass, it must be checked first, because releasing pressure from below can pull the brain downwards. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. A patient's MAP is 90 mmHg and ICP rises from 10 to 40 mmHg. What happens to cerebral perfusion pressure? A. It falls from 80 to 50 mmHg, although arm blood pressure is unchanged B. It stays at 80 mmHg, because autoregulation keeps it constant C. It rises from 80 to 130 mmHg, because ICP adds to the MAP D. It falls to 40 mmHg, because CPP always equals ICP Show answer and explanation Answer: A. CPP = MAP − ICP: 90 − 10 = 80, then 90 − 40 = 50 mmHg. 2. In the Cushing reflex, why does the heart rate fall? A. The baroreceptors respond to the sympathetic rise in blood pressure B. The vagus nerve is crushed directly by the swollen brain C. Raised ICP blocks sympathetic nerves to the heart D. The heart receives less blood when ICP is high Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Brainstem ischaemia drives blood pressure up. The baroreflex then slows the heart. 3. A tumour blocks the aqueduct of Sylvius. Which ventricles enlarge? A. The lateral and third ventricles, upstream of the block B. The fourth ventricle only, downstream of the block C. All four ventricles equally, because CSF absorption fails D. None, because CSF can bypass the aqueduct easily Show answer and explanation Answer: A. CSF made in the lateral and third ventricles can't get past the aqueduct, so they swell: non-communicating hydrocephalus. 6. The blood–brain barrier Figure. What makes cerebral capillaries different? Image credit David Nascari and Alan Sved, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. In most capillaries, small molecules slip through gaps between the endothelial cells. In the brain, the endothelial cells are sealed together by tight junctions, and there are no gaps or pores. So anything leaving the blood must pass through the cells. This is the blood–brain barrier, and it lies in the capillary endothelium. Surrounding pericytes and astrocyte endfeet help maintain it. Crosses easily Crosses poorly Water, O₂, CO₂ Proteins Lipid-soluble substances (e.g. steroid hormones) Substances bound to plasma proteins Glucose, quickly, but only by a carrier (GLUT1) Most ions and water-soluble molecules Why have it? It keeps the neurons' environment constant, despite swings in blood ions and hormones, and shields them from toxins and circulating antigens. Clinical points: A drug acting on the brain must be able to cross the barrier. The barrier breaks down in infection, injury, tumours and inflammation. The choroid plexus has its own barrier between blood and CSF, but there the seal is formed by the epithelial cells, not the capillaries. 7. Stroke A stroke is a sudden loss of blood supply to part of the brain, causing ischaemia and cell death. The signs depend on which artery is affected ( §2). Ischaemic stroke Haemorrhagic stroke What happens An artery is blocked An artery ruptures and bleeds Causes Atherosclerotic plaque, thrombus or embolus Often a ruptured aneurysm Extra danger — The blood adds volume, so ICP rises ( §4) Understanding Check: Sections 6–7 1. Where does the blood–brain barrier lie? A. In the tight junctions of the brain's capillary endothelium B. In the arachnoid villi that absorb the cerebrospinal fluid C. In the pia mater that covers the surface of the brain D. In the smooth muscle wall of the cerebral arterioles Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions force substances to pass through the cells, not between them. 2. How does glucose cross the blood–brain barrier? A. Quickly, by a specific carrier in the endothelial cells B. Slowly, by diffusing between the endothelial cells C. Only when insulin opens channels in the endothelium D. Bound to plasma proteins that carry it across Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Glucose is water-soluble, so it needs a carrier (GLUT1). The carrier moves it fast, and no insulin is needed. 3. Why can a haemorrhagic stroke damage brain tissue far from the bleeding artery? A. The blood adds volume inside the skull, raising ICP everywhere B. The bleeding artery stops all flow through the Circle of Willis C. The blood dissolves the blood–brain barrier throughout the brain D. The bleed lowers the CSF pressure, so the brain collapses Show answer and explanation Answer: A. In a rigid skull, extra volume raises ICP, lowering perfusion pressure across the whole brain. 8. Chapter Essentials The brain can't store oxygen: about 10 seconds without flow causes unconsciousness. The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are most sensitive; the brainstem is more resistant. Fuel: glucose (about 90%), taken up without insulin. Prolonged starvation: ketone bodies. Supply: internal carotids + vertebrals → basilar, joined in the Circle of Willis. Beyond the ring, little anastomosis. Autoregulation keeps flow steady across a wide pressure range. CO₂ is the strongest controller: high CO₂ dilates, low CO₂ constricts. Monro–Kellie: brain + blood + CSF in a rigid skull. CPP = MAP − ICP. Cushing reflex: very high ICP → brainstem ischaemia → hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing. A late sign. CSF: made by the choroid plexus; lateral → Monro → third → Sylvius → fourth → Magendie/Luschka → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi. About 150 mL, about 550 mL/day. Hydrocephalus: non-communicating (pathway blocked) or communicating (absorption fails). Blood–brain barrier: tight junctions in the capillary endothelium. Water, gases and lipid-soluble substances cross; glucose crosses by carrier; proteins don't. Stroke: ischaemic (blocked) or haemorrhagic (ruptured, often an aneurysm)."},{"slug":"coronary","title":"Coronary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"26-29; extra diagrams Circulation 3 42-43","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply","title":"1. Why does the heart need a separate blood supply?"},{"id":"2-trace-coronary-inflow-and-venous-drainage","title":"2. Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage"},{"id":"3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole","title":"3. Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole?"},{"id":"4-how-does-coronary-flow-match-changing-myocardial-work","title":"4. How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work?"},{"id":"5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia","title":"5. Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia?"},{"id":"6-coronary-flow-reserve-angina-and-myocardial-infarction","title":"6. Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction"},{"id":"7-chapter-essentials","title":"7. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Coronary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\">1. Why does the heart need a separate blood supply?</h2>\n<p>The blood inside the chambers can't nourish the thick ventricular walls: oxygen can only diffuse a fraction of a millimetre. So the heart has its own arteries, the <strong>right and left coronary arteries</strong>. They arise from the <strong><span data-term=\"aortic-sinuses\">aortic sinuses</span></strong>, the small pouches just above the aortic valve cusps.</p>\n<p><strong>The heart already takes out most of its oxygen at rest</strong> (about 70%, compared with about 25% for the body as a whole). So when it works harder, it can't simply extract more. <strong>Coronary blood flow has to rise.</strong> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/co-measurement?section=7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">→ Measuring Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Oxygen delivered = coronary blood flow × oxygen content of the blood.</strong> If either falls, the heart muscle runs short.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-trace-coronary-inflow-and-venous-drainage\">2. Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png\" alt=\"Anatomical anterior view of the human heart showing the left and right ventricles and coronary arteries running over their surfaces\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Anterior heart and ventricular coronary vessels.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_arteries.svg\" alt=\"Medical illustration of the heart with labelled right coronary, left coronary, left anterior descending, circumflex and posterior descending coronary artery branches.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Coronary arteries in their anatomical locations.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Patrick J. Lynch; derivative and labels by Fred the Oyster and Mikael Häggström, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronary_arteries.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left main coronary artery</strong> → divides into:<ul>\n<li>the <strong>left anterior descending (LAD)</strong>, running down the front, which supplies the <strong>front of the left ventricle and most of the septum</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>left circumflex (LCx)</strong>, curving round the left side, which supplies the <strong>side wall of the left ventricle</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Right coronary artery (RCA)</strong> supplies the <strong>right ventricle</strong> and, usually, the <strong>bottom (inferior) wall</strong> of the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"coronary-dominance\">Dominance</span></strong> just means which artery gives off the <strong>posterior descending artery</strong>, which supplies the back of the septum. It is the RCA in most people (&quot;right dominant&quot;). It does <strong>not</strong> mean that artery supplies one whole side.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Major coronary routes and drainage — simplified flow map</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 250\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Aortic root branches into left main and right coronary arteries. Left main divides into LAD and circumflex; posterior descending artery origin defines dominance. Venous return passes chiefly via coronary sinus to right atrium\"><g font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" fill=\"#22354e\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><rect x=\"240\" y=\"9\" width=\"200\" height=\"38\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e8f0ff\" stroke=\"#638dce\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"15\">Aortic root</text><rect x=\"67\" y=\"84\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6f3ff\" stroke=\"#6d96c7\"/><text x=\"172\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Left main coronary</text><rect x=\"405\" y=\"84\" width=\"212\" height=\"43\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6f3ff\" stroke=\"#6d96c7\"/><text x=\"511\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Right coronary</text><rect x=\"16\" y=\"160\" width=\"152\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#e8f6ec\" stroke=\"#70a987\"/><text x=\"92\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">LAD</text><text x=\"92\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"12\">anterior + septal</text><rect x=\"183\" y=\"160\" width=\"150\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#e8f6ec\" stroke=\"#70a987\"/><text x=\"258\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">Circumflex</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"12\">lateral LV</text><rect x=\"404\" y=\"160\" width=\"250\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fff0da\" stroke=\"#c4a16b\"/><text x=\"529\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">PDA origin defines</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"13\">coronary dominance</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"239\" font-size=\"13\">Venous blood: cardiac veins → coronary sinus → right atrium</text></g><g stroke=\"#5278a5\" stroke-width=\"2.6\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M340 47v18H172v19M340 65h171v19M172 127v16H92v17M172 143h86v17M511 127v33M333 183h71\"/></g></svg><p>Conceptual branches, not an anatomical map of coronary vessel positions. Dominance and individual territories vary.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Drainage:</strong> most coronary venous blood collects in the <strong><span data-term=\"coronary-sinus\">coronary sinus</span></strong>, which empties into the <strong>right atrium</strong>. A little drains straight into the chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole\">3. Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle — qualitative waveform</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 650 235\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Illustrative left coronary flow falls during early systolic contraction, remains relatively low in systole and rises sharply in early diastole before gradually declining\"><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#667b93\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M62 24v172h560\"/></g><rect x=\"88\" y=\"28\" width=\"240\" height=\"164\" fill=\"#fff0e6\"/><rect x=\"328\" y=\"28\" width=\"275\" height=\"164\" fill=\"#e9f5ff\"/><path d=\"M88 88C112 93 117 159 150 162S255 133 328 143C347 145 351 32 380 39C432 38 472 75 509 94S571 109 603 121\" stroke=\"#1d72c8\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"#293d58\" font-size=\"14\"><text x=\"140\" y=\"47\">Systole</text><text x=\"427\" y=\"47\">Diastole</text><text x=\"13\" y=\"19\">Flow</text><text x=\"257\" y=\"220\">Time →</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"211\">S1</text><text x=\"315\" y=\"211\">S2</text></g></svg><p>Conceptual shape, not measured patient data: left-ventricular compression restricts flow during systole; relaxation allows a marked early-diastolic increase. Right coronary flow may be less strongly diastolic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>In systole, the left ventricle squeezes its own arteries shut.</strong> The coronary branches run through the muscle wall. When the muscle contracts hard, it <strong>compresses</strong> them, and left ventricular coronary flow almost stops.</p>\n<p><strong>In diastole, the muscle relaxes and the arteries open.</strong> Aortic pressure is still high, so blood <strong>rushes in</strong>.</p>\n<p>So <strong>the left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole.</strong> The right ventricle, which squeezes far less hard, is fed in both systole and diastole.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 2. Myocardial compression explains diastolic left-coronary flow</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"During systole intramyocardial vessels are compressed; during diastole their compression is relieved and left ventricular coronary flow increases\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"20\" width=\"270\" height=\"112\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fce9ec\" stroke=\"#bf6777\"/><rect x=\"338\" y=\"20\" width=\"270\" height=\"112\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f3ff\" stroke=\"#668dbf\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24364d\"><text x=\"147\" y=\"50\">Systole</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"50\">Diastole</text></g><g font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24364d\"><text x=\"147\" y=\"77\">Muscle squeezes vessels</text><text x=\"147\" y=\"102\">LV flow is restricted</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"77\">Muscle relaxes</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"102\">LV flow increases</text></g><path d=\"M289 78h40m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7\" stroke=\"#456d95\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></svg><p>Qualitative comparison; the amount of systolic flow depends on vessel depth, ventricular pressures and the territory being supplied.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The pressure that drives left coronary flow</strong> ≈ <strong>aortic diastolic pressure − left ventricular diastolic pressure.</strong> Anything that lowers aortic diastolic pressure (shock, aortic regurgitation) or raises left ventricular filling pressure (heart failure) cuts this driving pressure.</p>\n<h3>Why is the inner layer (subendocardium) most at risk?</h3>\n<p>The <strong>inner layer</strong> of the left ventricle is squeezed hardest, because it is closest to the high-pressure cavity. So it depends <strong>most</strong> on diastole, and it is the <strong>first to become ischaemic</strong> when perfusion falls. In <strong>aortic stenosis</strong>, for example, very high ventricular pressure both raises oxygen demand and squeezes the inner-layer vessels harder.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why must coronary flow rise when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The heart already extracts most of the oxygen from its blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The coronary arteries narrow during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood carries more oxygen during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart can store extra oxygen for later use</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> With about 70% extraction at rest, there is little more to take, so more oxygen needs more flow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does the left ventricle receive most of its coronary flow in diastole?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Aortic pressure is highest in diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its contracting muscle squeezes its own arteries in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The coronary arteries close their valves in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The right ventricle takes all the flow during systole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Contraction compresses the arteries running through the wall. When the muscle relaxes, they open and fill.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which part of the left ventricle becomes ischaemic first when perfusion falls?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The outer layer, nearest the surface arteries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The inner layer (subendocardium), squeezed hardest in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The papillary muscles only</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. All layers equally at the same time</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The inner layer is compressed most and depends most on diastolic flow.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-how-does-coronary-flow-match-changing-myocardial-work\">4. How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work?</h2>\n<p>The heart's oxygen demand rises with <strong>heart rate</strong>, <strong>contractility</strong> and <strong>wall tension</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/co-measurement?section=7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">→ Measuring Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Working heart muscle opens its own arteries.</strong> As it works harder, it releases local signals, chiefly <strong><span data-term=\"adenosine\">adenosine</span></strong> and also K⁺, low O₂ and <strong>nitric oxide</strong> from the endothelium. These relax the coronary arterioles, so flow rises to match the work. This is <strong><span data-term=\"active-hyperaemia\">active hyperaemia</span></strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Regulation</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic nerves: two effects, one winner.</strong> They can constrict coronary arteries directly (α receptors). But they also make the heart beat faster and harder (β₁), which raises its metabolism and releases dilating metabolites. <strong>The dilation wins</strong>, so in exercise coronary flow rises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia\">5. Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia?</h2>\n<p>A fast heart hits the heart muscle from <strong>both</strong> sides:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Demand rises:</strong> more beats per minute means more work.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply time falls:</strong> as heart rate rises, <strong>diastole shortens much more than systole</strong>. Diastole is when the left ventricle is perfused.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"diastole-clock\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Diastole clock</figcaption><p>A heart-rate slider (60–180 beats/min). One beat is drawn as a bar split into systole and diastole, to scale. Below it: one minute's worth of diastole, in seconds. Readout: \"At 70: 0.86 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.57 s. At 150: 0.40 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.16 s.\" A line warns when diastole falls below about a third of the beat: \"the left ventricle has little time to be perfused\".</p></figure>\n\n<p>In a healthy heart, the arterioles dilate enough to cope. But with a <strong>narrowed coronary artery</strong>, a <strong>low diastolic pressure</strong> or a <strong>high filling pressure</strong>, the shrinking diastole can tip the balance into <strong>ischaemia</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-coronary-flow-reserve-angina-and-myocardial-infarction\">6. Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png\" alt=\"Medical comparison of normal coronary artery flow and an artery narrowed by atherosclerotic plaque.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Normal coronary artery versus a narrowed vessel.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronary_Artery_Disease.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"flow-reserve\">Coronary flow reserve</span></strong> is how much coronary flow can rise above its resting level when the arterioles fully dilate. Normally, it can rise about 4–5 times.</p>\n<p><strong>Why <span data-term=\"angina\">angina</span> comes on with exertion, not at rest:</strong> behind a narrowed artery, the arterioles <strong>dilate at rest</strong> to keep flow normal. They have <strong>used up their reserve</strong>. When exercise raises demand, there is nothing left to give, so supply falls short and <strong>angina</strong> (ischaemic chest pain) appears. Resting cures it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Angina</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Myocardial infarction (heart attack)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>What happens</td>\n  <td>Temporary <strong>ischaemia</strong>: supply falls short of demand</td>\n  <td>Prolonged, severe ischaemia: <strong>heart muscle dies</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Reversible?</td>\n  <td><strong>Yes</strong>, when demand falls or flow returns</td>\n  <td><strong>No</strong>: dead muscle becomes scar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Usual cause</td>\n  <td>A fixed narrowing plus higher demand</td>\n  <td>A plaque ruptures and a clot blocks the artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What mainly dilates the coronary arterioles when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Parasympathetic nerves releasing acetylcholine</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Local signals such as adenosine from working heart muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Direct sympathetic stimulation of α receptors</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A rise in coronary venous pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Metabolic signals from the working muscle (adenosine, K⁺, low O₂, nitric oxide) relax the arterioles: active hyperaemia.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why can a heart rate of 160 cause ischaemia in a patient with coronary narrowing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It raises oxygen demand and shortens diastole, when the left ventricle is fed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It raises aortic diastolic pressure too high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It makes the coronary arteries contract more strongly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It increases the time the left ventricle is perfused</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Demand rises while the time available for left ventricular perfusion shrinks.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A man has chest pain on climbing stairs that goes when he rests. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A coronary artery is completely blocked, killing heart muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A narrowed artery has used up its reserve, so supply can't rise with demand</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. His heart muscle is extracting too little oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. His coronary veins are blocked during exercise</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The arterioles behind the narrowing are already dilated at rest. On exertion, supply can't rise, which gives stable angina.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"7-chapter-essentials\">7. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Coronary arteries</strong> arise from the aortic sinuses. The heart extracts about 70% of its oxygen at rest, so more demand needs <strong>more flow</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>LAD</strong> = front of the LV and the septum; <strong>circumflex</strong> = side of the LV; <strong>RCA</strong> = right ventricle and inferior wall. <strong>Dominance</strong> = which artery gives the posterior descending artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Venous blood</strong> drains via the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> to the right atrium.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole</strong>, because contraction squeezes its arteries. Driving pressure ≈ aortic diastolic − LV diastolic pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>The <span data-term=\"subendocardium\">subendocardium</span></strong> is the most vulnerable layer.</li>\n<li><strong>Active hyperaemia:</strong> adenosine and other metabolites dilate the arterioles. Sympathetic β₁ effects make the net result dilation.</li>\n<li><strong>Tachycardia</strong> raises demand and <strong>shortens diastole</strong>, so it can cause ischaemia in diseased arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Narrowed artery</strong> → reserve used up at rest → <strong>angina on exertion</strong>. <strong>Infarction</strong> = muscle death from prolonged ischaemia.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Coronary Circulation 1. Why does the heart need a separate blood supply? The blood inside the chambers can't nourish the thick ventricular walls: oxygen can only diffuse a fraction of a millimetre. So the heart has its own arteries, the right and left coronary arteries. They arise from the aortic sinuses, the small pouches just above the aortic valve cusps. The heart already takes out most of its oxygen at rest (about 70%, compared with about 25% for the body as a whole). So when it works harder, it can't simply extract more. Coronary blood flow has to rise. ( → Measuring Cardiac Output, §7) Oxygen delivered = coronary blood flow × oxygen content of the blood. If either falls, the heart muscle runs short. 2. Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage Figure. Anterior heart and ventricular coronary vessels. Image credit Blausen Medical Communications, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Figure. Coronary arteries in their anatomical locations. Image credit Patrick J. Lynch; derivative and labels by Fred the Oyster and Mikael Häggström, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. Left main coronary artery → divides into: the left anterior descending (LAD), running down the front, which supplies the front of the left ventricle and most of the septum; the left circumflex (LCx), curving round the left side, which supplies the side wall of the left ventricle. Right coronary artery (RCA) supplies the right ventricle and, usually, the bottom (inferior) wall of the heart. Dominance just means which artery gives off the posterior descending artery, which supplies the back of the septum. It is the RCA in most people (\"right dominant\"). It does not mean that artery supplies one whole side. Figure 1. Major coronary routes and drainage — simplified flow map Aortic root Left main coronary Right coronary LAD anterior + septal Circumflex lateral LV PDA origin defines coronary dominance Venous blood: cardiac veins → coronary sinus → right atrium Conceptual branches, not an anatomical map of coronary vessel positions. Dominance and individual territories vary. Drainage: most coronary venous blood collects in the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium. A little drains straight into the chambers. 3. Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole? Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle — qualitative waveform Systole Diastole Flow Time → S1 S2 Conceptual shape, not measured patient data: left-ventricular compression restricts flow during systole; relaxation allows a marked early-diastolic increase. Right coronary flow may be less strongly diastolic. In systole, the left ventricle squeezes its own arteries shut. The coronary branches run through the muscle wall. When the muscle contracts hard, it compresses them, and left ventricular coronary flow almost stops. In diastole, the muscle relaxes and the arteries open. Aortic pressure is still high, so blood rushes in. So the left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole. The right ventricle, which squeezes far less hard, is fed in both systole and diastole. Figure 2. Myocardial compression explains diastolic left-coronary flow Systole Diastole Muscle squeezes vessels LV flow is restricted Muscle relaxes LV flow increases Qualitative comparison; the amount of systolic flow depends on vessel depth, ventricular pressures and the territory being supplied. The pressure that drives left coronary flow ≈ aortic diastolic pressure − left ventricular diastolic pressure. Anything that lowers aortic diastolic pressure (shock, aortic regurgitation) or raises left ventricular filling pressure (heart failure) cuts this driving pressure. Why is the inner layer (subendocardium) most at risk? The inner layer of the left ventricle is squeezed hardest, because it is closest to the high-pressure cavity. So it depends most on diastole, and it is the first to become ischaemic when perfusion falls. In aortic stenosis, for example, very high ventricular pressure both raises oxygen demand and squeezes the inner-layer vessels harder. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why must coronary flow rise when the heart works harder? A. The heart already extracts most of the oxygen from its blood B. The coronary arteries narrow during exercise C. Blood carries more oxygen during exercise D. The heart can store extra oxygen for later use Show answer and explanation Answer: A. With about 70% extraction at rest, there is little more to take, so more oxygen needs more flow. 2. Why does the left ventricle receive most of its coronary flow in diastole? A. Aortic pressure is highest in diastole B. Its contracting muscle squeezes its own arteries in systole C. The coronary arteries close their valves in systole D. The right ventricle takes all the flow during systole Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Contraction compresses the arteries running through the wall. When the muscle relaxes, they open and fill. 3. Which part of the left ventricle becomes ischaemic first when perfusion falls? A. The outer layer, nearest the surface arteries B. The inner layer (subendocardium), squeezed hardest in systole C. The papillary muscles only D. All layers equally at the same time Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The inner layer is compressed most and depends most on diastolic flow. 4. How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work? The heart's oxygen demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall tension. ( → Measuring Cardiac Output, §7) Working heart muscle opens its own arteries. As it works harder, it releases local signals, chiefly adenosine and also K⁺, low O₂ and nitric oxide from the endothelium. These relax the coronary arterioles, so flow rises to match the work. This is active hyperaemia. ( → Local Regulation) Sympathetic nerves: two effects, one winner. They can constrict coronary arteries directly (α receptors). But they also make the heart beat faster and harder (β₁), which raises its metabolism and releases dilating metabolites. The dilation wins, so in exercise coronary flow rises. 5. Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia? A fast heart hits the heart muscle from both sides: Demand rises: more beats per minute means more work. Supply time falls: as heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. Diastole is when the left ventricle is perfused. Diastole clock A heart-rate slider (60–180 beats/min). One beat is drawn as a bar split into systole and diastole, to scale. Below it: one minute's worth of diastole, in seconds. Readout: \"At 70: 0.86 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.57 s. At 150: 0.40 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.16 s.\" A line warns when diastole falls below about a third of the beat: \"the left ventricle has little time to be perfused\". In a healthy heart, the arterioles dilate enough to cope. But with a narrowed coronary artery, a low diastolic pressure or a high filling pressure, the shrinking diastole can tip the balance into ischaemia. 6. Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction Figure. Normal coronary artery versus a narrowed vessel. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Coronary flow reserve is how much coronary flow can rise above its resting level when the arterioles fully dilate. Normally, it can rise about 4–5 times. Why angina comes on with exertion, not at rest: behind a narrowed artery, the arterioles dilate at rest to keep flow normal. They have used up their reserve. When exercise raises demand, there is nothing left to give, so supply falls short and angina (ischaemic chest pain) appears. Resting cures it. Angina Myocardial infarction (heart attack) What happens Temporary ischaemia: supply falls short of demand Prolonged, severe ischaemia: heart muscle dies Reversible? Yes, when demand falls or flow returns No: dead muscle becomes scar Usual cause A fixed narrowing plus higher demand A plaque ruptures and a clot blocks the artery Understanding Check: Sections 4–6 1. What mainly dilates the coronary arterioles when the heart works harder? A. Parasympathetic nerves releasing acetylcholine B. Local signals such as adenosine from working heart muscle C. Direct sympathetic stimulation of α receptors D. A rise in coronary venous pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Metabolic signals from the working muscle (adenosine, K⁺, low O₂, nitric oxide) relax the arterioles: active hyperaemia. 2. Why can a heart rate of 160 cause ischaemia in a patient with coronary narrowing? A. It raises oxygen demand and shortens diastole, when the left ventricle is fed B. It raises aortic diastolic pressure too high C. It makes the coronary arteries contract more strongly D. It increases the time the left ventricle is perfused Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Demand rises while the time available for left ventricular perfusion shrinks. 3. A man has chest pain on climbing stairs that goes when he rests. What is the best explanation? A. A coronary artery is completely blocked, killing heart muscle B. A narrowed artery has used up its reserve, so supply can't rise with demand C. His heart muscle is extracting too little oxygen D. His coronary veins are blocked during exercise Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The arterioles behind the narrowing are already dilated at rest. On exertion, supply can't rise, which gives stable angina. 7. Chapter Essentials Coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses. The heart extracts about 70% of its oxygen at rest, so more demand needs more flow. LAD = front of the LV and the septum; circumflex = side of the LV; RCA = right ventricle and inferior wall. Dominance = which artery gives the posterior descending artery. Venous blood drains via the coronary sinus to the right atrium. The left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole, because contraction squeezes its arteries. Driving pressure ≈ aortic diastolic − LV diastolic pressure. The subendocardium is the most vulnerable layer. Active hyperaemia: adenosine and other metabolites dilate the arterioles. Sympathetic β₁ effects make the net result dilation. Tachycardia raises demand and shortens diastole, so it can cause ischaemia in diseased arteries. Narrowed artery → reserve used up at rest → angina on exertion. Infarction = muscle death from prolonged ischaemia."},{"slug":"pulmonary","title":"Pulmonary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"30-32","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-two-circulations-in-one-lung","title":"1. Two circulations in one lung"},{"id":"2-same-flow-much-lower-pressure","title":"2. Same flow, much lower pressure"},{"id":"3-why-the-alveoli-stay-dry","title":"3. Why the alveoli stay dry"},{"id":"4-gravity-posture-and-the-lung-s-blood-reservoir","title":"4. Gravity, posture and the lung's blood reservoir"},{"id":"5-low-oxygen-constricts-lung-vessels","title":"5. Low oxygen constricts lung vessels"},{"id":"6-chapter-essentials","title":"6. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Pulmonary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-two-circulations-in-one-lung\">1. Two circulations in one lung</h2>\n<p>The lung receives blood from <strong>two separate systems</strong>, and they do different jobs:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Pulmonary circulation</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Bronchial circulation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Job</td>\n  <td><strong>Gas exchange</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Nourishes</strong> the airways and supporting tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Comes from</td>\n  <td>Right ventricle → pulmonary arteries</td>\n  <td><strong>Aorta</strong> → bronchial arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure</td>\n  <td>Low</td>\n  <td>Systemic (high)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Drains to</td>\n  <td>Pulmonary veins → left atrium</td>\n  <td>Partly into the <strong>pulmonary veins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does that last row matter?</strong> Bronchial blood has already given its oxygen to the lung tissue. When some of it drains into the pulmonary veins, it mixes a little oxygen-poor blood into the freshly oxygenated blood heading for the left atrium. This is one reason arterial blood carries slightly less oxygen than the air in the alveoli.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg\" alt=\"Anatomical illustration of the pulmonary circuit showing right ventricular outflow to pulmonary arteries, gas exchange in lungs and pulmonary venous return to the left atrium\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Pulmonary circuit anatomy.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-same-flow-much-lower-pressure\">2. Same flow, much lower pressure</h2>\n<p>The two sides of the heart are <strong>in series</strong>, so the right ventricle must pump the <strong>same output</strong> as the left: about 5 L/min at rest. Yet the pressures are very different:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Pulmonary</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Systemic</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Arterial pressure</td>\n  <td>About <strong>25/8</strong> mmHg (mean about 15)</td>\n  <td>About <strong>120/80</strong> mmHg (mean about 93)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure at the end of the circuit</td>\n  <td>Left atrium, about 5</td>\n  <td>Right atrium, about 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure drop across the circuit</td>\n  <td>About <strong>10</strong> mmHg</td>\n  <td>About <strong>90</strong> mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The same flow is pushed through by a pressure drop about <strong>one-ninth</strong> as large, so <strong>pulmonary resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic resistance</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics\">→ Haemodynamics</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why so low?</strong> The pulmonary vessels are <strong>short, wide, thin-walled and stretchy</strong>, and the capillary network is huge, with countless paths in parallel.</p>\n<p><strong>Exercise: more flow, little extra pressure.</strong> When cardiac output rises, two things happen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"recruitment\">Recruitment</span>:</strong> capillaries that were closed or barely perfused <strong>open up</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"distension\">Distension</span>:</strong> capillaries that were already open <strong>widen</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both lower the resistance, so pulmonary flow can rise several-fold while pulmonary arterial pressure rises only a little. That protects the thin capillaries, and the right ventricle, from high pressure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-the-alveoli-stay-dry\">3. Why the alveoli stay dry</h2>\n<p>The alveolar wall must be thin for gas exchange, so the lung can't afford fluid leaking into it. Compare the two main forces across a pulmonary capillary <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation\">→ Microcirculation</a>)</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Capillary hydrostatic pressure</strong>, pushing fluid out: only about <strong>10 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Plasma oncotic pressure</strong>, pulling fluid in: about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The outward push is unusually weak. So only a small amount of fluid filters into the lung's interstitium, and the <strong>lymphatics</strong> carry it away easily, long before it can reach the alveoli.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The full balance also includes the lung's interstitial forces (a slightly negative interstitial pressure and a fairly high interstitial protein level), which leave a small net outward filtration that the lymphatics clear. The lecture gives the two main values, 10 and 25 mmHg; Guyton and Hall give the full balance.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>When does this safety margin fail?</strong> In <strong>left heart failure</strong>, left atrial pressure rises and backs up into the pulmonary veins and capillaries. Once capillary hydrostatic pressure climbs past about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>, roughly the plasma oncotic pressure, filtration outruns lymph drainage. Fluid collects first in the interstitium, then floods the alveoli: <strong><span data-term=\"pulmonary-oedema\">pulmonary oedema</span></strong>, which thickens the barrier and impairs gas exchange. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure?section=3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up\">→ Heart Failure, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What is the main job of the bronchial circulation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. To nourish the airways and supporting lung tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. To carry blood from the right ventricle for gas exchange</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. To return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. To drain lymph from the alveoli into the bloodstream</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The bronchial arteries come from the aorta and supply the airways. Gas exchange is the job of the pulmonary circulation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The right and left ventricles pump the same output, but pulmonary arterial pressure is far lower. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Pulmonary vascular resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The right ventricle pumps a smaller volume with each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood flows through the lungs only during diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pulmonary arteries contain valves that lower the pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Same flow with a much smaller pressure drop means a much lower resistance: short, wide, stretchy vessels arranged in parallel.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In left heart failure, what makes fluid flood the alveoli?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Pulmonary capillary pressure rises until filtration outruns lymph drainage</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Plasma oncotic pressure rises and pulls fluid into the alveoli</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Bronchial arteries leak fluid directly into the alveolar spaces</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls, so capillaries collapse</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> High left atrial pressure backs up into the capillaries. Once the outward push beats the oncotic pull and the lymphatics, fluid accumulates.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-gravity-posture-and-the-lung-s-blood-reservoir\">4. Gravity, posture and the lung's blood reservoir</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>How upright lung perfusion changes from apex to base</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 630 240\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Upright lung outline with apex less perfused than base and conceptual zone 2 and zone 3 relationships, with zone 1 being a possible not normal resting zone\"><path d=\"M205 25C135 35 85 96 91 168C95 202 151 212 230 214V68Z\" fill=\"#e1f2fb\" stroke=\"#5e93ad\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M256 68V214C336 212 392 202 396 168C403 96 352 35 281 25Z\" fill=\"#e1f2fb\" stroke=\"#5e93ad\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><g stroke=\"#7d9cae\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\"><path d=\"M83 100h320M83 159h320\"/></g><g fill=\"#273d5c\" font-size=\"14\"><text x=\"423\" y=\"65\">Apex: less perfusion</text><text x=\"423\" y=\"119\">Zone 2 pattern</text><text x=\"423\" y=\"179\">Zone 3 pattern</text><text x=\"423\" y=\"216\">Base: greater perfusion</text></g><g fill=\"#28679e\"><path d=\"M120 50v116m-7 -12 7 12 7 -12\"/></g></svg><p>Illustrative upright distribution: blood flow generally increases toward dependent bases. Zone 1 (alveolar pressure exceeding arterial pressure) may develop at the apex in low-pressure or positive-pressure ventilation states, but is generally absent in healthy resting lungs. Zone positions shift with conditions.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Standing up:</strong> the lung apex sits above the heart and the base below it. Gravity adds pressure to the blood in the lower vessels and takes it away from the upper ones. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/gravity\">→ Gravity</a>)</em> So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At the <strong>base</strong>, vascular pressure is higher, more vessels are open and wider, and <strong>blood flow is greatest</strong>.</li>\n<li>At the <strong>apex</strong>, vascular pressure is lowest and <strong>blood flow is least</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"west-zones\">West zones</span>: compare three pressures.</strong> Whether a capillary is open depends on the pressure inside it versus the <strong>alveolar air pressure</strong> squeezing it from outside:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Zone</th>\n  <th>Pressures</th>\n  <th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Zone 1</strong></td>\n  <td>Alveolar &gt; arterial &gt; venous</td>\n  <td>Capillaries squeezed shut: <strong>no flow</strong>. Not present in healthy lungs; can appear after haemorrhage or with positive-pressure ventilation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Zone 2</strong> (upper lung)</td>\n  <td>Arterial &gt; alveolar &gt; venous</td>\n  <td>Flow depends on <strong>arterial − alveolar</strong> pressure, so it is intermittent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Zone 3</strong> (lower lung)</td>\n  <td>Arterial &gt; venous &gt; alveolar</td>\n  <td>Capillaries always open: <strong>continuous flow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>West zones are <strong>pressure relationships</strong>, not fixed stripes of lung. They shift with posture, blood pressure and breathing.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Functional West zones of upright lung perfusion</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 176\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three functional pressure regimes: zone one alveolar pressure exceeds arterial; zone two arterial exceeds alveolar exceeds venous; zone three arterial and venous exceed alveolar\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23384f\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"22\" width=\"195\" height=\"125\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff0e2\" stroke=\"#d99c51\"/><rect x=\"222\" y=\"22\" width=\"195\" height=\"125\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#edf2ff\" stroke=\"#718dc9\"/><rect x=\"436\" y=\"22\" width=\"195\" height=\"125\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e5f4eb\" stroke=\"#65a48d\"/><g font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"105\" y=\"50\">Zone 1</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"50\">Zone 2</text><text x=\"533\" y=\"50\">Zone 3</text></g><g font-size=\"17\"><text x=\"105\" y=\"83\">PA &gt; Pa &gt; Pv</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"83\">Pa &gt; PA &gt; Pv</text><text x=\"533\" y=\"83\">Pa &gt; Pv &gt; PA</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"105\" y=\"113\">Flow absent / minimal</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"113\">Waterfall flow</text><text x=\"533\" y=\"113\">Continuous flow</text></g></g></svg><p>Original conceptual comparison. PA = alveolar pressure, Pa = pulmonary arterial pressure, Pv = pulmonary venous pressure. Actual zones vary with posture and physiological conditions; zone 1 is ordinarily absent or minimal in healthy resting lungs.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Lying down:</strong> the whole lung is at about the level of the heart, so flow is spread more evenly. And about <strong>400 mL</strong> of blood moves from the body into the lungs. Standing up returns it to the circulation. So the pulmonary vessels act as a <strong>blood reservoir</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this matters: <span data-term=\"orthopnoea\">orthopnoea</span>.</strong> In left heart failure, the pulmonary capillaries are already near their limit. Lying flat adds that extra blood to the lungs, capillary pressure rises further, and the patient becomes <strong>breathless lying down</strong>. Sitting up moves blood back to the lower body and eases it. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure?section=3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up\">→ Heart Failure, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-low-oxygen-constricts-lung-vessels\">5. Low oxygen constricts lung vessels</h2>\n<p>In most tissues, low oxygen <strong>dilates</strong> the arterioles, bringing in more blood. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Regulation</a>)</em> The lung does the <strong>opposite</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"hpv\">Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</span>:</strong> when an alveolus has low oxygen, the small pulmonary arteries supplying it <strong>constrict</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the opposite?</strong> In the lung, low oxygen in an alveolus means that alveolus is <strong>poorly ventilated</strong>. Sending blood there would waste it, because the blood can't pick up oxygen. Constricting those vessels <strong>diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli</strong>, matching blood flow to air flow (the <strong><span data-term=\"vq-ratio\">ventilation–perfusion ratio</span></strong>). A rise in CO₂ (and H⁺) also constricts pulmonary vessels, which helps in the same way.</p>\n<p><strong>Local versus everywhere:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One poorly ventilated region</strong> (for example, a blocked airway): blood is redirected, and oxygenation improves.</li>\n<li><strong>Every alveolus hypoxic</strong> (high altitude, chronic lung disease): there is nowhere better to send the blood. Constriction everywhere <strong>raises total pulmonary resistance</strong>. If it persists, the result is <strong><span data-term=\"pulmonary-hypertension\">pulmonary hypertension</span></strong>, and the right ventricle strains against it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Before birth</strong>, the same response keeps fetal pulmonary resistance high, so blood bypasses the fluid-filled lungs. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/fetal?section=1-why-does-the-fetus-need-shortcuts\">→ Fetal Circulation, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nerves and nitric oxide:</strong> the pulmonary vessels have an extensive autonomic supply; sympathetic stimulation <strong>constricts</strong> them. <strong>Nitric oxide</strong> from the endothelium <strong>dilates</strong> them.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> In an upright person, why does the lung base receive more blood than the apex?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Gravity raises vascular pressure at the base, opening and widening vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Alveolar pressure is much higher at the base than at the apex</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The bronchial arteries supply the base but not the apex</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Hypoxic vasoconstriction opens the vessels at the base</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Below the heart, gravity adds pressure to the blood, so more capillaries are open and distended.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does a patient with left heart failure become breathless when lying flat?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. About 400 mL of extra blood moves into the already congested lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Lying flat lowers the pressure in the pulmonary capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Lying flat raises the plasma oncotic pressure in the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart pumps less blood into the lungs when lying flat</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The extra blood raises pulmonary capillary pressure further, pushing more fluid into the lung tissue. This is orthopnoea.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> At high altitude, why does pulmonary arterial pressure rise?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Every alveolus is hypoxic, so vessels constrict throughout the lung</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low oxygen dilates pulmonary vessels, so more blood enters the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The bronchial arteries constrict and divert blood into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Gravity has a stronger effect on the lungs at high altitude</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> With hypoxia everywhere, there is no better-ventilated region to send blood to. Widespread constriction raises total resistance.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"6-chapter-essentials\">6. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Two circulations:</strong> pulmonary (gas exchange, low pressure) and bronchial (from the aorta, nourishes the airways, partly drains into pulmonary veins).</li>\n<li><strong>Same output as the left side</strong> at about one-tenth of the resistance: mean pulmonary arterial pressure about 15 mmHg.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> recruitment and distension let flow rise with little rise in pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Alveoli stay dry:</strong> capillary hydrostatic pressure is about 10 mmHg against an oncotic pressure of about 25; lymphatics clear the small leak. Above about 25 mmHg capillary pressure (left heart failure), pulmonary oedema develops.</li>\n<li><strong>Upright:</strong> most flow at the base. <strong>West zones</strong> compare alveolar, arterial and venous pressures.</li>\n<li><strong>Lying down</strong> moves about 400 mL of blood into the lungs, which explains <strong>orthopnoea</strong> in heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong> diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli. When hypoxia is everywhere, it causes <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Pulmonary Circulation 1. Two circulations in one lung The lung receives blood from two separate systems, and they do different jobs: Pulmonary circulation Bronchial circulation Job Gas exchange Nourishes the airways and supporting tissue Comes from Right ventricle → pulmonary arteries Aorta → bronchial arteries Pressure Low Systemic (high) Drains to Pulmonary veins → left atrium Partly into the pulmonary veins Why does that last row matter? Bronchial blood has already given its oxygen to the lung tissue. When some of it drains into the pulmonary veins, it mixes a little oxygen-poor blood into the freshly oxygenated blood heading for the left atrium. This is one reason arterial blood carries slightly less oxygen than the air in the alveoli. Figure. Pulmonary circuit anatomy. Image credit OpenStax College, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. 2. Same flow, much lower pressure The two sides of the heart are in series, so the right ventricle must pump the same output as the left: about 5 L/min at rest. Yet the pressures are very different: Pulmonary Systemic Arterial pressure About 25/8 mmHg (mean about 15) About 120/80 mmHg (mean about 93) Pressure at the end of the circuit Left atrium, about 5 Right atrium, about 2 Pressure drop across the circuit About 10 mmHg About 90 mmHg The same flow is pushed through by a pressure drop about one-ninth as large, so pulmonary resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic resistance. ( → Haemodynamics) Why so low? The pulmonary vessels are short, wide, thin-walled and stretchy, and the capillary network is huge, with countless paths in parallel. Exercise: more flow, little extra pressure. When cardiac output rises, two things happen: Recruitment: capillaries that were closed or barely perfused open up. Distension: capillaries that were already open widen. Both lower the resistance, so pulmonary flow can rise several-fold while pulmonary arterial pressure rises only a little. That protects the thin capillaries, and the right ventricle, from high pressure. 3. Why the alveoli stay dry The alveolar wall must be thin for gas exchange, so the lung can't afford fluid leaking into it. Compare the two main forces across a pulmonary capillary ( → Microcirculation): Capillary hydrostatic pressure, pushing fluid out: only about 10 mmHg. Plasma oncotic pressure, pulling fluid in: about 25 mmHg. The outward push is unusually weak. So only a small amount of fluid filters into the lung's interstitium, and the lymphatics carry it away easily, long before it can reach the alveoli. Source note: The full balance also includes the lung's interstitial forces (a slightly negative interstitial pressure and a fairly high interstitial protein level), which leave a small net outward filtration that the lymphatics clear. The lecture gives the two main values, 10 and 25 mmHg; Guyton and Hall give the full balance. When does this safety margin fail? In left heart failure, left atrial pressure rises and backs up into the pulmonary veins and capillaries. Once capillary hydrostatic pressure climbs past about 25 mmHg, roughly the plasma oncotic pressure, filtration outruns lymph drainage. Fluid collects first in the interstitium, then floods the alveoli: pulmonary oedema, which thickens the barrier and impairs gas exchange. ( → Heart Failure, §3) Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. What is the main job of the bronchial circulation? A. To nourish the airways and supporting lung tissue B. To carry blood from the right ventricle for gas exchange C. To return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart D. To drain lymph from the alveoli into the bloodstream Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The bronchial arteries come from the aorta and supply the airways. Gas exchange is the job of the pulmonary circulation. 2. The right and left ventricles pump the same output, but pulmonary arterial pressure is far lower. Why? A. Pulmonary vascular resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic B. The right ventricle pumps a smaller volume with each beat C. Blood flows through the lungs only during diastole D. Pulmonary arteries contain valves that lower the pressure Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Same flow with a much smaller pressure drop means a much lower resistance: short, wide, stretchy vessels arranged in parallel. 3. In left heart failure, what makes fluid flood the alveoli? A. Pulmonary capillary pressure rises until filtration outruns lymph drainage B. Plasma oncotic pressure rises and pulls fluid into the alveoli C. Bronchial arteries leak fluid directly into the alveolar spaces D. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls, so capillaries collapse Show answer and explanation Answer: A. High left atrial pressure backs up into the capillaries. Once the outward push beats the oncotic pull and the lymphatics, fluid accumulates. 4. Gravity, posture and the lung's blood reservoir How upright lung perfusion changes from apex to base Apex: less perfusion Zone 2 pattern Zone 3 pattern Base: greater perfusion Illustrative upright distribution: blood flow generally increases toward dependent bases. Zone 1 (alveolar pressure exceeding arterial pressure) may develop at the apex in low-pressure or positive-pressure ventilation states, but is generally absent in healthy resting lungs. Zone positions shift with conditions. Standing up: the lung apex sits above the heart and the base below it. Gravity adds pressure to the blood in the lower vessels and takes it away from the upper ones. ( → Gravity) So: At the base, vascular pressure is higher, more vessels are open and wider, and blood flow is greatest. At the apex, vascular pressure is lowest and blood flow is least. West zones: compare three pressures. Whether a capillary is open depends on the pressure inside it versus the alveolar air pressure squeezing it from outside: Zone Pressures What happens Zone 1 Alveolar > arterial > venous Capillaries squeezed shut: no flow. Not present in healthy lungs; can appear after haemorrhage or with positive-pressure ventilation. Zone 2 (upper lung) Arterial > alveolar > venous Flow depends on arterial − alveolar pressure, so it is intermittent Zone 3 (lower lung) Arterial > venous > alveolar Capillaries always open: continuous flow West zones are pressure relationships, not fixed stripes of lung. They shift with posture, blood pressure and breathing. Figure. Functional West zones of upright lung perfusion Zone 1 Zone 2 Zone 3 PA > Pa > Pv Pa > PA > Pv Pa > Pv > PA Flow absent / minimal Waterfall flow Continuous flow Original conceptual comparison. PA = alveolar pressure, Pa = pulmonary arterial pressure, Pv = pulmonary venous pressure. Actual zones vary with posture and physiological conditions; zone 1 is ordinarily absent or minimal in healthy resting lungs. Lying down: the whole lung is at about the level of the heart, so flow is spread more evenly. And about 400 mL of blood moves from the body into the lungs. Standing up returns it to the circulation. So the pulmonary vessels act as a blood reservoir. Why this matters: orthopnoea. In left heart failure, the pulmonary capillaries are already near their limit. Lying flat adds that extra blood to the lungs, capillary pressure rises further, and the patient becomes breathless lying down. Sitting up moves blood back to the lower body and eases it. ( → Heart Failure, §3) 5. Low oxygen constricts lung vessels In most tissues, low oxygen dilates the arterioles, bringing in more blood. ( → Local Regulation) The lung does the opposite: Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: when an alveolus has low oxygen, the small pulmonary arteries supplying it constrict. Why the opposite? In the lung, low oxygen in an alveolus means that alveolus is poorly ventilated. Sending blood there would waste it, because the blood can't pick up oxygen. Constricting those vessels diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli, matching blood flow to air flow (the ventilation–perfusion ratio). A rise in CO₂ (and H⁺) also constricts pulmonary vessels, which helps in the same way. Local versus everywhere: One poorly ventilated region (for example, a blocked airway): blood is redirected, and oxygenation improves. Every alveolus hypoxic (high altitude, chronic lung disease): there is nowhere better to send the blood. Constriction everywhere raises total pulmonary resistance. If it persists, the result is pulmonary hypertension, and the right ventricle strains against it. Before birth, the same response keeps fetal pulmonary resistance high, so blood bypasses the fluid-filled lungs. ( → Fetal Circulation, §1) Nerves and nitric oxide: the pulmonary vessels have an extensive autonomic supply; sympathetic stimulation constricts them. Nitric oxide from the endothelium dilates them. Understanding Check: Sections 4–5 1. In an upright person, why does the lung base receive more blood than the apex? A. Gravity raises vascular pressure at the base, opening and widening vessels B. Alveolar pressure is much higher at the base than at the apex C. The bronchial arteries supply the base but not the apex D. Hypoxic vasoconstriction opens the vessels at the base Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Below the heart, gravity adds pressure to the blood, so more capillaries are open and distended. 2. Why does a patient with left heart failure become breathless when lying flat? A. About 400 mL of extra blood moves into the already congested lungs B. Lying flat lowers the pressure in the pulmonary capillaries C. Lying flat raises the plasma oncotic pressure in the lungs D. The heart pumps less blood into the lungs when lying flat Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The extra blood raises pulmonary capillary pressure further, pushing more fluid into the lung tissue. This is orthopnoea. 3. At high altitude, why does pulmonary arterial pressure rise? A. Every alveolus is hypoxic, so vessels constrict throughout the lung B. Low oxygen dilates pulmonary vessels, so more blood enters the lungs C. The bronchial arteries constrict and divert blood into the lungs D. Gravity has a stronger effect on the lungs at high altitude Show answer and explanation Answer: A. With hypoxia everywhere, there is no better-ventilated region to send blood to. Widespread constriction raises total resistance. 6. Chapter Essentials Two circulations: pulmonary (gas exchange, low pressure) and bronchial (from the aorta, nourishes the airways, partly drains into pulmonary veins). Same output as the left side at about one-tenth of the resistance: mean pulmonary arterial pressure about 15 mmHg. Exercise: recruitment and distension let flow rise with little rise in pressure. Alveoli stay dry: capillary hydrostatic pressure is about 10 mmHg against an oncotic pressure of about 25; lymphatics clear the small leak. Above about 25 mmHg capillary pressure (left heart failure), pulmonary oedema develops. Upright: most flow at the base. West zones compare alveolar, arterial and venous pressures. Lying down moves about 400 mL of blood into the lungs, which explains orthopnoea in heart failure. Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli. When hypoxia is everywhere, it causes pulmonary hypertension."},{"slug":"splanchnic","title":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"33-37","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-beds","title":"1. An unusual route through two capillary beds"},{"id":"2-the-liver-s-double-blood-supply-and-its-zones","title":"2. The liver's double blood supply and its zones"},{"id":"3-blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock","title":"3. Blood flow after meals, and in shock"},{"id":"4-why-does-fluid-collect-as-ascites","title":"4. Why does fluid collect as ascites?"},{"id":"5-chapter-essentials","title":"5. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-beds\">1. An unusual route through two capillary beds</h2>\n<p>The gut is supplied by three unpaired arteries from the aorta: the <strong><span data-term=\"coeliac-trunk\">coeliac trunk</span></strong>, the <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong> and the <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The unusual part is where the blood goes next.</strong> Venous blood from the gut and spleen does <strong>not</strong> go straight back to the heart. It collects in the <strong><span data-term=\"portal-vein\">portal vein</span></strong> (formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins) and passes through the <strong>liver first</strong>.</p>\n<p>So this blood crosses <strong>two capillary beds in a row</strong>: the gut's capillaries, then the liver's <strong><span data-term=\"sinusoid\">sinusoids</span></strong>. <strong>Why?</strong> Everything absorbed from the gut (nutrients, drugs, toxins, bacteria) is sent to the liver to be processed <strong>before</strong> it reaches the rest of the body.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portal_venous_system-en.svg\" alt=\"Labelled anatomical illustration of portal venous return from abdominal digestive organs to the liver\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Portal venous system.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portal_venous_system-en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-the-liver-s-double-blood-supply-and-its-zones\">2. The liver's double blood supply and its zones</h2>\n<p>The liver gets blood from <strong>two inflows</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Inflow</th>\n  <th>Share of the flow</th>\n  <th>Brings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Portal vein</strong></td>\n  <td>About <strong>75%</strong></td>\n  <td>Nutrient-rich, but partly deoxygenated, blood from the gut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Hepatic artery</strong></td>\n  <td>About <strong>25%</strong></td>\n  <td>Oxygen-rich arterial blood (about half the liver's oxygen)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both enter at the <strong><span data-term=\"portal-triad\">portal triad</span></strong> (portal vein branch + hepatic artery branch + bile duct). They <strong>mix in the sinusoids</strong>, the leaky, wide liver capillaries, and flow towards the <strong>central vein</strong> → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava. <strong>Bile flows the opposite way</strong>, towards the bile ducts in the triad.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_scheme1.jpg\" alt=\"Illustration of hepatic lobule organization, central vein, portal triads and acinar zones with oxygen delivery gradients\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Hepatic lobule and acinar oxygen zonation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">MarquardtM, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liver_scheme1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg\" alt=\"Detailed labelled anatomical illustration of liver vessels, portal tract and hepatic lobule structures\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Liver vascular and lobular anatomy.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Gwilz, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liver_vascular_anatomy.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Zones: why the centre suffers first.</strong> Along each sinusoid, liver cells take oxygen out as blood passes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zone 1</strong> (near the portal triad) gets the <strong>freshest, most oxygen-rich</strong> blood.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"hepatic-zones\">Zone 3</span></strong> (near the central vein) gets blood that has <strong>already given up much of its oxygen</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So when blood flow or oxygen falls (in shock or heart failure), <strong>zone 3 is damaged first</strong>.</p>\n<p>Between the sinusoid lining and the liver cells is a narrow gap, the <strong><span data-term=\"space-of-disse\">space of Disse</span></strong>, where plasma touches the cells directly. Fluid that collects here drains as hepatic lymph.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock\">3. Blood flow after meals, and in shock</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>After a meal</strong>, the gut works harder and its arterioles dilate, so splanchnic blood flow rises: <strong><span data-term=\"postprandial-hyperaemia\">postprandial hyperaemia</span></strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The splanchnic veins are a blood reservoir</strong>: stretchy, and holding a large volume.</li>\n<li><strong>In haemorrhage</strong>, sympathetic nerves <strong>constrict</strong> the splanchnic vessels. This sends stored blood back to the heart and diverts flow to the brain and heart. It helps hold up blood pressure, but if it lasts, the <strong>gut lining becomes ischaemic</strong> and can break down. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does gut venous blood pass through the liver before reaching the heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The liver processes absorbed nutrients and toxins first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The liver adds oxygen to the venous blood from the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The portal vein is too narrow to reach the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The gut veins have no valves, so they need the liver to pump</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The portal route sends everything absorbed from the gut to the liver to be processed before it enters the general circulation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> In shock, why are liver cells near the central vein (zone 3) damaged first?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They receive blood after upstream cells have used much of its oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They receive blood only from the portal vein</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They are closest to the hepatic artery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They do not have sinusoids</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Blood flows from the portal triad to the central vein, losing oxygen along the way, so zone 3 runs short first.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> How do the splanchnic vessels help in haemorrhage?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They dilate to receive more blood from the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They constrict, sending stored blood back to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They make new red cells to replace lost blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They stop the liver from releasing glucose</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Sympathetic constriction squeezes the splanchnic reservoir and diverts flow to vital organs, at the cost of gut perfusion.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-why-does-fluid-collect-as-ascites\">4. Why does fluid collect as ascites?</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"ascites\">Ascites</span></strong> is fluid in the peritoneal cavity. It usually develops when the liver is scarred (<strong><span data-term=\"cirrhosis\">cirrhosis</span></strong>), through several routes at once:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>High portal pressure:</strong> scarring blocks flow through the liver, so pressure backs up in the portal vein and sinusoids, pushing fluid out. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §4</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Much more hepatic lymph:</strong> the leaky sinusoids filter so much fluid that the lymphatics can't keep up, and the excess weeps into the abdomen.</li>\n<li><strong>The kidneys keep salt and water</strong>, sensing underfilled arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Low albumin</strong> (a failing liver makes less), so the pull holding fluid in the vessels weakens. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\">→ Oedema, §5</a>)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Right heart failure</strong> can cause ascites too: high pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver.</p>\n<p>The protein content of ascitic fluid <strong>varies with the cause</strong>: it is not always high, and not always low.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which change most directly starts ascites in cirrhosis?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Raised pressure in the portal vein and liver sinusoids</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Raised pressure in the hepatic artery</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Increased flow through the coeliac trunk</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Increased albumin production by the liver</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Scarring obstructs flow, raising portal and sinusoidal pressure. Lymph overflow, salt retention and low albumin add to it.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How can right heart failure cause ascites?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It raises albumin, pulling fluid into the abdomen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It blocks the portal vein directly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It stops the kidneys from retaining salt</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> High right atrial pressure is passed back into the hepatic veins and sinusoids, so filtration rises.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why do the liver's sinusoids produce so much lymph?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They are very leaky, so fluid and protein filter out easily</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They have no blood flow at all</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They contain muscle that pumps fluid out</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They are lined by thick, continuous capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries, so much fluid passes into the space of Disse and drains as hepatic lymph.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-chapter-essentials\">5. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Gut arteries:</strong> coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric.</li>\n<li><strong>Portal route:</strong> gut capillaries → portal vein → <strong>liver sinusoids</strong> → hepatic veins → IVC: two capillary beds in a row.</li>\n<li><strong>Two liver inflows:</strong> the portal vein (about 75% of flow) and the hepatic artery (about 25%, but about half the oxygen).</li>\n<li><strong>Zone 1</strong> (portal triad) is oxygen-rich; <strong>zone 3</strong> (central vein) is oxygen-poor and damaged first.</li>\n<li><strong>After meals</strong>, gut flow rises. <strong>In haemorrhage</strong>, splanchnic vessels constrict, mobilising blood but risking gut ischaemia.</li>\n<li><strong>Ascites:</strong> portal hypertension + lymph overflow + salt retention + low albumin (cirrhosis), or right heart failure.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation 1. An unusual route through two capillary beds The gut is supplied by three unpaired arteries from the aorta: the coeliac trunk, the superior mesenteric artery and the inferior mesenteric artery. The unusual part is where the blood goes next. Venous blood from the gut and spleen does not go straight back to the heart. It collects in the portal vein (formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins) and passes through the liver first. So this blood crosses two capillary beds in a row: the gut's capillaries, then the liver's sinusoids. Why? Everything absorbed from the gut (nutrients, drugs, toxins, bacteria) is sent to the liver to be processed before it reaches the rest of the body. Figure. Portal venous system. Image credit Jmarchn, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. 2. The liver's double blood supply and its zones The liver gets blood from two inflows: Inflow Share of the flow Brings Portal vein About 75% Nutrient-rich, but partly deoxygenated, blood from the gut Hepatic artery About 25% Oxygen-rich arterial blood (about half the liver's oxygen) Both enter at the portal triad (portal vein branch + hepatic artery branch + bile duct). They mix in the sinusoids, the leaky, wide liver capillaries, and flow towards the central vein → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava. Bile flows the opposite way, towards the bile ducts in the triad. Figure. Hepatic lobule and acinar oxygen zonation. Image credit MarquardtM, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. Figure. Liver vascular and lobular anatomy. Image credit Gwilz, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Zones: why the centre suffers first. Along each sinusoid, liver cells take oxygen out as blood passes: Zone 1 (near the portal triad) gets the freshest, most oxygen-rich blood. Zone 3 (near the central vein) gets blood that has already given up much of its oxygen. So when blood flow or oxygen falls (in shock or heart failure), zone 3 is damaged first. Between the sinusoid lining and the liver cells is a narrow gap, the space of Disse, where plasma touches the cells directly. Fluid that collects here drains as hepatic lymph. 3. Blood flow after meals, and in shock After a meal, the gut works harder and its arterioles dilate, so splanchnic blood flow rises: postprandial hyperaemia. The splanchnic veins are a blood reservoir: stretchy, and holding a large volume. In haemorrhage, sympathetic nerves constrict the splanchnic vessels. This sends stored blood back to the heart and diverts flow to the brain and heart. It helps hold up blood pressure, but if it lasts, the gut lining becomes ischaemic and can break down. ( → Shock) Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why does gut venous blood pass through the liver before reaching the heart? A. The liver processes absorbed nutrients and toxins first B. The liver adds oxygen to the venous blood from the gut C. The portal vein is too narrow to reach the heart D. The gut veins have no valves, so they need the liver to pump Show answer and explanation Answer: A. The portal route sends everything absorbed from the gut to the liver to be processed before it enters the general circulation. 2. In shock, why are liver cells near the central vein (zone 3) damaged first? A. They receive blood after upstream cells have used much of its oxygen B. They receive blood only from the portal vein C. They are closest to the hepatic artery D. They do not have sinusoids Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Blood flows from the portal triad to the central vein, losing oxygen along the way, so zone 3 runs short first. 3. How do the splanchnic vessels help in haemorrhage? A. They dilate to receive more blood from the heart B. They constrict, sending stored blood back to the heart C. They make new red cells to replace lost blood D. They stop the liver from releasing glucose Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Sympathetic constriction squeezes the splanchnic reservoir and diverts flow to vital organs, at the cost of gut perfusion. 4. Why does fluid collect as ascites? Ascites is fluid in the peritoneal cavity. It usually develops when the liver is scarred ( cirrhosis), through several routes at once: High portal pressure: scarring blocks flow through the liver, so pressure backs up in the portal vein and sinusoids, pushing fluid out. ( → Oedema, §4) Much more hepatic lymph: the leaky sinusoids filter so much fluid that the lymphatics can't keep up, and the excess weeps into the abdomen. The kidneys keep salt and water, sensing underfilled arteries. Low albumin (a failing liver makes less), so the pull holding fluid in the vessels weakens. ( → Oedema, §5) Right heart failure can cause ascites too: high pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver. The protein content of ascitic fluid varies with the cause: it is not always high, and not always low. Understanding Check: Section 4 1. Which change most directly starts ascites in cirrhosis? A. Raised pressure in the portal vein and liver sinusoids B. Raised pressure in the hepatic artery C. Increased flow through the coeliac trunk D. Increased albumin production by the liver Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Scarring obstructs flow, raising portal and sinusoidal pressure. Lymph overflow, salt retention and low albumin add to it. 2. How can right heart failure cause ascites? A. Pressure backs up through the hepatic veins into the liver B. It raises albumin, pulling fluid into the abdomen C. It blocks the portal vein directly D. It stops the kidneys from retaining salt Show answer and explanation Answer: A. High right atrial pressure is passed back into the hepatic veins and sinusoids, so filtration rises. 3. Why do the liver's sinusoids produce so much lymph? A. They are very leaky, so fluid and protein filter out easily B. They have no blood flow at all C. They contain muscle that pumps fluid out D. They are lined by thick, continuous capillaries Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries, so much fluid passes into the space of Disse and drains as hepatic lymph. 5. Chapter Essentials Gut arteries: coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric. Portal route: gut capillaries → portal vein → liver sinusoids → hepatic veins → IVC: two capillary beds in a row. Two liver inflows: the portal vein (about 75% of flow) and the hepatic artery (about 25%, but about half the oxygen). Zone 1 (portal triad) is oxygen-rich; zone 3 (central vein) is oxygen-poor and damaged first. After meals, gut flow rises. In haemorrhage, splanchnic vessels constrict, mobilising blood but risking gut ischaemia. Ascites: portal hypertension + lymph overflow + salt retention + low albumin (cirrhosis), or right heart failure."},{"slug":"skin","title":"Cutaneous Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"38-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-skin-blood-flow-is-mainly-about-heat-not-nutrition","title":"1. Skin blood flow is mainly about heat, not nutrition"},{"id":"2-skin-blood-flow-in-shock","title":"2. Skin blood flow in shock"},{"id":"3-the-triple-response-of-lewis","title":"3. The triple response of Lewis"},{"id":"4-chapter-essentials","title":"4. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-skin-blood-flow-is-mainly-about-heat-not-nutrition\">1. Skin blood flow is mainly about heat, not nutrition</h2>\n<p>Most tissues get the blood flow they need <strong>to feed their cells</strong>. The skin is different: its flow is controlled mainly to <strong>regulate body temperature</strong>. It can carry far more blood than its cells need.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hot:</strong> skin vessels <strong>dilate</strong>, so warm blood flows near the surface and heat is lost (flushed skin).</li>\n<li><strong>Cold:</strong> sympathetic nerves <strong>constrict</strong> skin vessels, so blood stays deep and heat is kept (pale, cool skin).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"ava\">Arteriovenous anastomoses (AVAs)</span></strong> are direct shortcuts from small arteries to veins, found in the fingers, toes, palms, ears and nose. When they open, a large volume of warm blood passes just under the skin, ideal for losing heat. They are controlled almost entirely by <strong>sympathetic</strong> nerves.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png\" alt=\"Detailed medical cutaway illustration of dermal circulation showing vessels in the skin and their relationship to superficial layers\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Blood vessels within the skin.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0802_Skin_DermalCirculation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-skin-blood-flow-in-shock\">2. Skin blood flow in shock</h2>\n<p>In haemorrhage, sympathetic nerves <strong>shut down skin blood flow</strong>, diverting blood to the brain and heart. That is why a bleeding patient has <strong>cold, pale, clammy skin</strong>, often <strong>before</strong> blood pressure falls much. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Septic shock</strong> can be different early on: toxins dilate the vessels, so the skin may be <strong>warm and flushed</strong>. <strong>Skin temperature alone can't diagnose shock</strong>; it must be read with the rest of the patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What mainly controls skin blood flow?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The oxygen needs of the skin cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The need to regulate body temperature</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The amount of sweat being produced</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The pressure in the skin's veins</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Skin flow can far exceed its nutritional needs. It is set mainly to keep or lose heat.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does a patient who is bleeding heavily have cold, pale skin?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Sympathetic nerves shut down skin flow to protect vital organs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Blood loss lowers body temperature everywhere equally</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The skin's arteriovenous anastomoses open widely</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Histamine constricts the skin vessels</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Sympathetic vasoconstriction diverts blood from the skin to the brain and heart.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Where are arteriovenous anastomoses mostly found, and what are they for?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the trunk, to supply nutrients to the skin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the fingers, toes, ears and nose, to lose or keep heat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In deep muscles, to supply oxygen in exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the scalp, to drain blood from the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> AVAs in the extremities shunt warm blood under the skin when heat must be lost.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-the-triple-response-of-lewis\">3. The triple response of Lewis</h2>\n<p>Draw a blunt point <strong>firmly</strong> across the skin of the forearm. Within a minute, three things appear, in this order:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Sign</th>\n  <th>What you see</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>1. Red line</strong></td>\n  <td>A red line exactly along the stroke, within seconds</td>\n  <td><strong>Local capillaries dilate</strong> where the skin was injured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>2. <span data-term=\"flare\">Flare</span></strong></td>\n  <td>A wider, blotchy red area spreading out around the line</td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"axon-reflex\">Axon reflex</span></strong>: sensory nerve endings send signals along their own branches, dilating nearby arterioles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>3. <span data-term=\"wheal\">Wheal</span></strong></td>\n  <td>A raised, pale swelling along the line</td>\n  <td><strong>Histamine</strong> makes the capillaries <strong>leaky</strong>, so fluid leaks into the tissue (local oedema)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Lewis triple response — what each visible sign means</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 210\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three skin response illustrations: red line at stroke, broad surrounding red flare, and raised wheal swelling\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#293d5b\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><rect x=\"221\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><rect x=\"432\" y=\"12\" width=\"197\" height=\"145\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2e8\" stroke=\"#caa18b\"/><path d=\"M36 90h145\" stroke=\"#d83e54\" stroke-width=\"7\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><ellipse cx=\"319\" cy=\"90\" rx=\"74\" ry=\"42\" fill=\"#ee8b8b\" opacity=\".6\"/><path d=\"M267 90h104\" stroke=\"#d83e54\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"530\" cy=\"94\" rx=\"67\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#e69e8d\" stroke=\"#cd7662\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M470 95Q530 51 590 95\" stroke=\"#fff6ed\" stroke-width=\"11\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"108\" y=\"180\">Red line</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"180\">Flare</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"180\">Wheal</text><text x=\"108\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Local dilation</text><text x=\"319\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Axon reflex</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"12\">Fluid swelling</text></g></svg><p>A schematic of three related features after firm stroking: narrow local redness, wider surrounding redness and raised oedema. The brief white response to light stroking is separate from Lewis's triple response.</p></figure>\n\n<h3>Why does the flare spread? The axon reflex, step by step</h3>\n<p>A single sensory nerve fibre in the skin doesn't end at one point. Near the skin it <strong>splits into many branches</strong>, like the fingers of a hand, and together they cover a patch of skin a few centimetres across.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The stroke stimulates one branch.</strong> The firm stroke damages a few cells and stimulates the sensory nerve endings in the line of the stroke.</li>\n<li><strong>The signal travels up that branch.</strong> It travels towards the spinal cord, as any sensory signal would.</li>\n<li><strong>At the branch point, it also travels back down the other branches.</strong> An impulse travels in both directions along a nerve fibre. So when it reaches the fork, it carries on up towards the spinal cord <strong>and also runs back down every other branch</strong>, into skin that was never touched.</li>\n<li><strong>The endings of those branches release vasodilators.</strong> They release <strong><span data-term=\"substance-p\">substance P</span></strong> and <strong><span data-term=\"cgrp\">CGRP</span></strong> (calcitonin gene-related peptide). These dilate the nearby arterioles directly, and substance P also makes nearby <strong><span data-term=\"mast-cell\">mast cells</span> release histamine</strong>, which dilates them further.</li>\n<li><strong>The arterioles across the whole patch dilate, giving the flare.</strong> The redness spreads as far as that fibre's branches reach, which is why the flare is <strong>wider than the stroke but stops a few centimetres out</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why &quot;axon&quot; reflex?</strong> The whole loop happens <strong>within the branches of one nerve fibre</strong>. There is no synapse, and the spinal cord and brain are not needed. That makes it different from a true reflex arc, which passes through the spinal cord.</p>\n<p><strong>How do we know?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>local anaesthetic</strong> that blocks the nerve abolishes the flare. The red line and the wheal still appear, because they come from local injury and histamine, not from the nerve.</li>\n<li>After the sensory nerve has been <strong>cut and has degenerated</strong>, the flare disappears.</li>\n<li>Cutting the nerve <strong>above</strong> the branch point, closer to the spinal cord, does <strong>not</strong> stop the flare, because the loop doesn't need the spinal cord.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure axon-reflex-figure\" id=\"cvs26-axon\"><figcaption>Figure. The axon reflex: why the flare spreads</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 420\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"One sensory nerve fibre runs up towards the spinal cord. Near the skin it splits into four branches. Step 1: a firm stroke stimulates the left branch. Step 2: the impulse travels up that branch to the branch point. Step 3: it also travels back down the other three branches into skin that was not touched. Step 4: their endings release substance P and CGRP, which dilate the nearby arterioles, so a red flare spreads across the whole patch. The spinal cord is not needed for this loop.\"><defs><marker id=\"ax-head\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"ax-arrowhead\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"150\" y=\"12\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" class=\"ax-cord\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"37\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"ax-lab-sm\">Spinal cord</text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"110\" class=\"ax-fibre ax-faint\"/><text x=\"212\" y=\"80\" class=\"ax-lab\">No spinal cord</text><text x=\"212\" y=\"97\" class=\"ax-lab\">needed</text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"190\" class=\"ax-fibre\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"end\" class=\"ax-lab-xs\">one sensory fibre</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"300\" width=\"360\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" class=\"ax-skin\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"335\" rx=\"172\" ry=\"28\" class=\"ax-flare\"/><g class=\"ax-fibre\"><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"300\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"300\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"300\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"300\"/></g><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"190\" r=\"5\" class=\"ax-node\"/><g class=\"ax-node\"><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"300\" r=\"4\"/></g><g class=\"ax-art\"><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"150\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"250\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/><ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"7\"/></g><path d=\"M44 314l8-8 8 8 8-8 8 8 8-8 8 8\" class=\"ax-stroke\"/><g class=\"ax-arrow\"><path d=\"M103 259L142 226\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/><path d=\"M190 232L173 271\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/><path d=\"M225 224L243 263\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/><path d=\"M251 221L297 260\" marker-end=\"url(#ax-head)\"/></g><g class=\"ax-step\"><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"364\" r=\"10\"/><circle cx=\"96\" cy=\"234\" r=\"10\"/><circle cx=\"314\" cy=\"232\" r=\"10\"/></g><g text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"ax-step-num\"><text x=\"70\" y=\"368\">1</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"238\">2</text><text x=\"314\" y=\"236\">3</text></g><text x=\"86\" y=\"368\" class=\"ax-lab-sm\">Stroke</text><text x=\"372\" y=\"372\" text-anchor=\"end\" class=\"ax-lab-xs\">skin</text><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"403\" r=\"10\" class=\"ax-step\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"407\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"ax-step-num\">4</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"408\" class=\"ax-lab-sm\">Substance P + CGRP dilate arterioles → flare</text></svg><p>① The stroke stimulates one branch. ② The impulse travels up to the branch point. ③ It also runs back down the other branches. ④ Their endings release substance P and CGRP, so arterioles across the whole patch dilate. Original DOMiNANCE diagram.</p></figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Substance P and CGRP as the axon-reflex mediators, and the local-anaesthetic and nerve-degeneration findings, are standard physiology (Lewis, 1927; Guyton and Hall). The lecture names the axon reflex as the cause of the flare.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>White line, not part of the triple response.</strong> A <strong>light</strong> stroke produces only a brief <strong>pale line</strong>: the local vessels constrict for a moment.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory hook:</strong> <strong>red line</strong> = local redness; <strong>flare</strong> = spreading redness; <strong>wheal</strong> = raised swelling.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Section 3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does the flare spread beyond the line of the stroke?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Histamine diffuses widely through the skin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. An axon reflex dilates arterioles through nearby nerve branches</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The brainstem sends dilating signals to the area</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The wheal pushes blood outwards into the surrounding skin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Sensory nerve branches carry the signal sideways and release dilators, without involving the spinal cord or brain.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What causes the raised wheal?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Local capillaries dilating with blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Histamine making capillaries leaky, so fluid leaks into the tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Sympathetic nerves constricting the local arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood clotting under the skin</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The wheal is local oedema from increased capillary permeability.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Does the axon reflex need the spinal cord?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Yes: the signal must reach the spinal cord to be relayed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Yes: the brainstem must coordinate the flare</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. No: the signal stays within the branches of one sensory nerve fibre</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. No: the response is caused by histamine alone, with no nerves</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The signal travels from one branch of a sensory neuron back down its other branches, so no central connection is involved.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> A local anaesthetic is injected into the forearm skin before a firm stroke. Which part of the triple response is lost?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The red line along the stroke</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The flare spreading around the line</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The raised wheal along the line</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. All three parts of the response</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The flare depends on the axon reflex, so blocking the nerve stops it. The red line and the wheal come from local injury and histamine.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-chapter-essentials\">4. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Skin blood flow regulates heat</strong>, not just nutrition. It can far exceed what the skin needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Hot → dilate</strong> (lose heat); <strong>cold → sympathetic constriction</strong> (keep heat).</li>\n<li><strong>Arteriovenous anastomoses</strong> in the fingers, toes, ears and nose shunt warm blood near the surface to lose heat.</li>\n<li><strong>Haemorrhage → cold, pale skin</strong> (sympathetic shutdown); early septic shock may give warm skin.</li>\n<li><strong>Triple response:</strong> <strong>red line</strong> (local dilation) → <strong>flare</strong> (axon reflex) → <strong>wheal</strong> (histamine, leaky capillaries, oedema).</li>\n<li><strong>Flare = axon reflex:</strong> a stroked branch sends impulses back down the fibre's other branches, which release substance P and CGRP to dilate arterioles. No spinal cord needed.</li>\n<li>A light stroke gives only a brief <strong>white line</strong> (local constriction), which is not part of the triple response.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response 1. Skin blood flow is mainly about heat, not nutrition Most tissues get the blood flow they need to feed their cells. The skin is different: its flow is controlled mainly to regulate body temperature. It can carry far more blood than its cells need. Hot: skin vessels dilate, so warm blood flows near the surface and heat is lost (flushed skin). Cold: sympathetic nerves constrict skin vessels, so blood stays deep and heat is kept (pale, cool skin). Arteriovenous anastomoses (AVAs) are direct shortcuts from small arteries to veins, found in the fingers, toes, palms, ears and nose. When they open, a large volume of warm blood passes just under the skin, ideal for losing heat. They are controlled almost entirely by sympathetic nerves. Figure. Blood vessels within the skin. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. 2. Skin blood flow in shock In haemorrhage, sympathetic nerves shut down skin blood flow, diverting blood to the brain and heart. That is why a bleeding patient has cold, pale, clammy skin, often before blood pressure falls much. ( → Shock) Septic shock can be different early on: toxins dilate the vessels, so the skin may be warm and flushed. Skin temperature alone can't diagnose shock; it must be read with the rest of the patient. Understanding Check: Sections 1–2 1. What mainly controls skin blood flow? A. The oxygen needs of the skin cells B. The need to regulate body temperature C. The amount of sweat being produced D. The pressure in the skin's veins Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Skin flow can far exceed its nutritional needs. It is set mainly to keep or lose heat. 2. Why does a patient who is bleeding heavily have cold, pale skin? A. Sympathetic nerves shut down skin flow to protect vital organs B. Blood loss lowers body temperature everywhere equally C. The skin's arteriovenous anastomoses open widely D. Histamine constricts the skin vessels Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Sympathetic vasoconstriction diverts blood from the skin to the brain and heart. 3. Where are arteriovenous anastomoses mostly found, and what are they for? A. In the trunk, to supply nutrients to the skin B. In the fingers, toes, ears and nose, to lose or keep heat C. In deep muscles, to supply oxygen in exercise D. In the scalp, to drain blood from the brain Show answer and explanation Answer: B. AVAs in the extremities shunt warm blood under the skin when heat must be lost. 3. The triple response of Lewis Draw a blunt point firmly across the skin of the forearm. Within a minute, three things appear, in this order: Sign What you see Why 1. Red line A red line exactly along the stroke, within seconds Local capillaries dilate where the skin was injured 2. Flare A wider, blotchy red area spreading out around the line Axon reflex: sensory nerve endings send signals along their own branches, dilating nearby arterioles 3. Wheal A raised, pale swelling along the line Histamine makes the capillaries leaky, so fluid leaks into the tissue (local oedema) Lewis triple response — what each visible sign means Red line Flare Wheal Local dilation Axon reflex Fluid swelling A schematic of three related features after firm stroking: narrow local redness, wider surrounding redness and raised oedema. The brief white response to light stroking is separate from Lewis's triple response. Why does the flare spread? The axon reflex, step by step A single sensory nerve fibre in the skin doesn't end at one point. Near the skin it splits into many branches, like the fingers of a hand, and together they cover a patch of skin a few centimetres across. The stroke stimulates one branch. The firm stroke damages a few cells and stimulates the sensory nerve endings in the line of the stroke. The signal travels up that branch. It travels towards the spinal cord, as any sensory signal would. At the branch point, it also travels back down the other branches. An impulse travels in both directions along a nerve fibre. So when it reaches the fork, it carries on up towards the spinal cord and also runs back down every other branch, into skin that was never touched. The endings of those branches release vasodilators. They release substance P and CGRP (calcitonin gene-related peptide). These dilate the nearby arterioles directly, and substance P also makes nearby mast cells release histamine, which dilates them further. The arterioles across the whole patch dilate, giving the flare. The redness spreads as far as that fibre's branches reach, which is why the flare is wider than the stroke but stops a few centimetres out. Why \"axon\" reflex? The whole loop happens within the branches of one nerve fibre. There is no synapse, and the spinal cord and brain are not needed. That makes it different from a true reflex arc, which passes through the spinal cord. How do we know? A local anaesthetic that blocks the nerve abolishes the flare. The red line and the wheal still appear, because they come from local injury and histamine, not from the nerve. After the sensory nerve has been cut and has degenerated, the flare disappears. Cutting the nerve above the branch point, closer to the spinal cord, does not stop the flare, because the loop doesn't need the spinal cord. Figure. The axon reflex: why the flare spreads Spinal cord No spinal cord needed one sensory fibre 1 2 3 Stroke skin 4 Substance P + CGRP dilate arterioles → flare ① The stroke stimulates one branch. ② The impulse travels up to the branch point. ③ It also runs back down the other branches. ④ Their endings release substance P and CGRP, so arterioles across the whole patch dilate. Original DOMiNANCE diagram. Source note: Substance P and CGRP as the axon-reflex mediators, and the local-anaesthetic and nerve-degeneration findings, are standard physiology (Lewis, 1927; Guyton and Hall). The lecture names the axon reflex as the cause of the flare. White line, not part of the triple response. A light stroke produces only a brief pale line: the local vessels constrict for a moment. Memory hook: red line = local redness; flare = spreading redness; wheal = raised swelling. Understanding Check: Section 3 1. Why does the flare spread beyond the line of the stroke? A. Histamine diffuses widely through the skin B. An axon reflex dilates arterioles through nearby nerve branches C. The brainstem sends dilating signals to the area D. The wheal pushes blood outwards into the surrounding skin Show answer and explanation Answer: B. Sensory nerve branches carry the signal sideways and release dilators, without involving the spinal cord or brain. 2. What causes the raised wheal? A. Local capillaries dilating with blood B. Histamine making capillaries leaky, so fluid leaks into the tissue C. Sympathetic nerves constricting the local arterioles D. Blood clotting under the skin Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The wheal is local oedema from increased capillary permeability. 3. Does the axon reflex need the spinal cord? A. Yes: the signal must reach the spinal cord to be relayed B. Yes: the brainstem must coordinate the flare C. No: the signal stays within the branches of one sensory nerve fibre D. No: the response is caused by histamine alone, with no nerves Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The signal travels from one branch of a sensory neuron back down its other branches, so no central connection is involved. 4. A local anaesthetic is injected into the forearm skin before a firm stroke. Which part of the triple response is lost? A. The red line along the stroke B. The flare spreading around the line C. The raised wheal along the line D. All three parts of the response Show answer and explanation Answer: B. The flare depends on the axon reflex, so blocking the nerve stops it. The red line and the wheal come from local injury and histamine. 4. Chapter Essentials Skin blood flow regulates heat, not just nutrition. It can far exceed what the skin needs. Hot → dilate (lose heat); cold → sympathetic constriction (keep heat). Arteriovenous anastomoses in the fingers, toes, ears and nose shunt warm blood near the surface to lose heat. Haemorrhage → cold, pale skin (sympathetic shutdown); early septic shock may give warm skin. Triple response: red line (local dilation) → flare (axon reflex) → wheal (histamine, leaky capillaries, oedema). Flare = axon reflex: a stroked branch sends impulses back down the fibre's other branches, which release substance P and CGRP to dilate arterioles. No spinal cord needed. A light stroke gives only a brief white line (local constriction), which is not part of the triple response."},{"slug":"fetal","title":"Fetal Circulation and Birth Transitions","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"42-45","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-fetus-need-shortcuts","title":"1. Why does the fetus need shortcuts?"},{"id":"2-trace-the-fetal-circuit","title":"2. Trace the fetal circuit"},{"id":"3-fetal-haemoglobin-grabbing-oxygen-from-the-mother","title":"3. Fetal haemoglobin: grabbing oxygen from the mother"},{"id":"4-what-closes-after-delivery","title":"4. What closes after delivery?"},{"id":"5-chapter-essentials","title":"5. Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-fetus-need-shortcuts\">1. Why does the fetus need shortcuts?</h2>\n<p>Before birth, the <strong><span data-term=\"placenta\">placenta</span></strong>, not the lungs, does gas exchange. The fetal lungs are filled with fluid, and their vessels are tightly constricted, so <strong>pulmonary resistance is very high</strong>. Pumping all the blood through them would be wasted effort.</p>\n<p>So the fetal circulation has <strong>three shortcuts (shunts)</strong> that send oxygen-rich blood where it is needed, mainly the heart and brain, and route most of it <strong>past the liver and lungs</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg\" alt=\"Anatomical illustration tracing placenta, umbilical veins and arteries, fetal heart, ductus venosus, foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Complete fetal circulation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-trace-the-fetal-circuit\">2. Trace the fetal circuit</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Placenta → umbilical vein:</strong> the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus (yet still only about 80% saturated) travels up the <strong>single umbilical vein</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 1: <span data-term=\"ductus-venosus\">ductus venosus</span>.</strong> Much of it <strong>bypasses the liver</strong>, going straight to the inferior vena cava.</li>\n<li><strong>Into the right atrium.</strong> The stream from the IVC is aimed straight across the atrium at an opening in the septum.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 2: <span data-term=\"foramen-ovale\">foramen ovale</span>.</strong> This oxygen-rich stream crosses <strong>from the right atrium to the left atrium</strong>, then goes to the left ventricle → aorta → <strong>heart and brain first</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SVC blood</strong> (oxygen-poor, from the head) goes mainly into the right ventricle → pulmonary trunk.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 3: <span data-term=\"ductus-arteriosus\">ductus arteriosus</span>.</strong> Pulmonary resistance is high, so most of this blood <strong>bypasses the lungs</strong>, crossing <strong>from the pulmonary trunk into the descending aorta</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Back to the placenta:</strong> the <strong>two umbilical arteries</strong> (branches of the internal iliac arteries) carry blood back to be reoxygenated.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Three fetal vascular shortcuts</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 171\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Ductus venosus bypasses liver sinusoids, foramen ovale connects right atrium with left atrium, and ductus arteriosus connects pulmonary trunk with descending aorta\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24354d\"><rect x=\"7\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ecf3ff\" stroke=\"#6f97c8\"/><rect x=\"229\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf5ee\" stroke=\"#76a988\"/><rect x=\"451\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff1dc\" stroke=\"#bb914f\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"107\" y=\"53\">Ductus venosus</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"53\">Foramen ovale</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"53\">Ductus arteriosus</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"107\" y=\"84\">Umbilical vein → IVC</text><text x=\"107\" y=\"107\">Partial liver bypass</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"84\">Right atrium → left</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"107\">atrium</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"84\">Pulmonary trunk →</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"107\">descending aorta</text></g></g></svg><p>Original route schematic; arrows show preferential fetal flow, not every stream or complete anatomical connection. Increased pulmonary flow after birth changes the pressure gradients that maintain these pathways.</p></figure>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Shunt</th>\n  <th>Connects</th>\n  <th>Bypasses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n  <td>Umbilical vein → inferior vena cava</td>\n  <td>The liver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></td>\n  <td>Right atrium → left atrium</td>\n  <td>The lungs (via the right ventricle)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n  <td>Pulmonary trunk → aorta</td>\n  <td>The lungs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does blood flow right-to-left through the shunts?</strong> Because, before birth, the <strong>right side</strong> has the higher pressure: pulmonary resistance is high, while the placenta keeps systemic resistance low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-fetal-haemoglobin-grabbing-oxygen-from-the-mother\">3. Fetal haemoglobin: grabbing oxygen from the mother</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"fetal-haemoglobin\">Fetal haemoglobin (HbF)</span></strong> holds oxygen <strong>more tightly</strong> than adult haemoglobin (HbA), because it binds the molecule <strong><span data-term=\"bpg\">2,3-BPG</span></strong> less strongly. 2,3-BPG normally loosens haemoglobin's grip on oxygen. So, at the low oxygen levels in the placenta, fetal blood can <strong>pull oxygen across from the mother's blood</strong>. After birth, HbF is gradually replaced by HbA over several months.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why is most blood diverted away from the fetal lungs?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The fetal lungs are fluid-filled and have very high vascular resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The fetal lungs already receive oxygen from amniotic fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The fetal heart is too weak to pump blood through the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The lungs would take oxygen away from the placenta</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Gas exchange happens in the placenta. The fluid-filled lungs' constricted vessels resist flow, so blood takes the shunts.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which shunt carries blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ductus venosus</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The foramen ovale</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ductus arteriosus</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The umbilical artery</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The ductus arteriosus links the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta, bypassing the lungs.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does fetal haemoglobin help the fetus take oxygen from the mother?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It binds 2,3-BPG more strongly, so it releases oxygen easily</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It holds oxygen more tightly because it binds 2,3-BPG less strongly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It carries carbon dioxide instead of oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is found only in the placenta, not in fetal blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> With less 2,3-BPG bound, HbF has a higher affinity for oxygen, so it loads oxygen from maternal blood.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-what-closes-after-delivery\">4. What closes after delivery?</h2>\n<p>Two events at birth flip the pressures:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The first breaths:</strong> the lungs fill with air and oxygen rises, so the lung vessels <strong>dilate</strong> and <strong>pulmonary resistance falls sharply</strong>. Lung blood flow surges, and much more blood returns to the <strong>left atrium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Clamping the cord:</strong> the placenta, a <strong>low-resistance</strong> circuit, is cut off, so <strong>systemic resistance rises</strong>. The flow up the umbilical vein stops.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>At birth — two changes that redirect circulation</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"First breaths decrease pulmonary vascular resistance and increase left atrial return, while cord clamping removes low resistance placenta and raises systemic resistance, leading to functional shunt closure\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#253e5b\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"12\" width=\"315\" height=\"78\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e4f2ff\" stroke=\"#76a1c8\"/><rect x=\"337\" y=\"12\" width=\"315\" height=\"78\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf5e9\" stroke=\"#75a884\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"40\">First breaths</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"40\">Cord clamping</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"65\">Pulmonary resistance falls</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"65\">Placental circuit removed</text></g><rect x=\"8\" y=\"130\" width=\"315\" height=\"73\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f0f6ff\" stroke=\"#76a1c8\"/><rect x=\"337\" y=\"130\" width=\"315\" height=\"73\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f1f8ed\" stroke=\"#75a884\"/><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"156\">Lung flow and left atrial</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"174\">return increase</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"156\">Systemic resistance rises</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"174\">Umbilical venous flow stops</text></g><g stroke=\"#53778c\" stroke-width=\"3\"><path d=\"M165 90v39M158 120l7 9 7 -9M495 90v39M488 120l7 9 7 -9\"/></g></g></svg><p>These simultaneous changes shift atrial pressures, favor functional foramen ovale closure and contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure occurs later and varies by structure.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png\" alt=\"Medical illustration of neonatal heart circulation showing postnatal pulmonary blood flow and altered fetal shunt pathways\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Circulation after birth.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Now <strong>the left side has the higher pressure</strong>, and each shunt closes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Shunt</th>\n  <th>Why it closes</th>\n  <th>Becomes (anatomical remnant)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></td>\n  <td>Left atrial pressure now <strong>exceeds</strong> right, which presses the flap shut</td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"fossa-ovalis\">Fossa ovalis</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Higher oxygen</strong> and <strong>less prostaglandin</strong> (from the placenta) make it constrict</td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"ligamentum-arteriosum\">Ligamentum arteriosum</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n  <td>No more umbilical venous flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Ligamentum venosum</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Umbilical vein</td>\n  <td>No more flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Ligamentum teres</strong> (of the liver)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Umbilical arteries</td>\n  <td>No more flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Medial umbilical ligaments</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Closure happens in two steps:</strong> <strong>functional</strong> closure within minutes to days, as flow stops; then <strong>anatomical</strong> closure over weeks to months, as tissue seals the channel.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does the foramen ovale close at birth?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Prostaglandin levels rise after the cord is clamped</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Left atrial pressure becomes higher than right atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The umbilical vein carries more blood after birth</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pulmonary resistance rises when the lungs fill with air</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> More blood returns from the lungs to the left atrium, and its pressure rises above the right, pressing the flap shut.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What makes the ductus arteriosus constrict after birth?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Falling oxygen and rising prostaglandins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A rise in pulmonary vascular resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Increased flow through the umbilical arteries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Oxygen constricts the ductus, and the prostaglandins that kept it open fall once the placenta is gone.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> At birth, pulmonary resistance falls while systemic resistance rises. Why do both happen?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both are caused by the first breath alone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Breathing dilates the lung vessels; cord clamping removes the low-resistance placenta</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Both are caused by clamping the cord alone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The ductus arteriosus closing raises both resistances</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> They are two separate events: lung aeration lowers pulmonary resistance, and losing the placenta raises systemic resistance.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-chapter-essentials\">5. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The placenta</strong> does fetal gas exchange. The fluid-filled lungs have high resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Three shunts:</strong> ductus venosus (bypasses the liver), foramen ovale (right → left atrium), ductus arteriosus (pulmonary trunk → aorta).</li>\n<li><strong>The best-oxygenated blood</strong> goes through the foramen ovale to the heart and brain.</li>\n<li><strong>Before birth the right side has the higher pressure</strong>, so flow through the shunts is right-to-left.</li>\n<li><strong>HbF</strong> binds oxygen more tightly (less 2,3-BPG binding), so it loads oxygen from the mother.</li>\n<li><strong>At birth:</strong> the first breaths lower pulmonary resistance; cord clamping raises systemic resistance; <strong>left pressure &gt; right</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Remnants:</strong> fossa ovalis, ligamentum arteriosum, ligamentum venosum, ligamentum teres, medial umbilical ligaments.</li>\n</ol>\n","plain":"Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth 1. Why does the fetus need shortcuts? Before birth, the placenta, not the lungs, does gas exchange. The fetal lungs are filled with fluid, and their vessels are tightly constricted, so pulmonary resistance is very high. Pumping all the blood through them would be wasted effort. So the fetal circulation has three shortcuts (shunts) that send oxygen-rich blood where it is needed, mainly the heart and brain, and route most of it past the liver and lungs. Figure. Complete fetal circulation. Image credit OpenStax College, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. 2. Trace the fetal circuit Placenta → umbilical vein: the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus (yet still only about 80% saturated) travels up the single umbilical vein. Shortcut 1: ductus venosus. Much of it bypasses the liver, going straight to the inferior vena cava. Into the right atrium. The stream from the IVC is aimed straight across the atrium at an opening in the septum. Shortcut 2: foramen ovale. This oxygen-rich stream crosses from the right atrium to the left atrium, then goes to the left ventricle → aorta → heart and brain first. SVC blood (oxygen-poor, from the head) goes mainly into the right ventricle → pulmonary trunk. Shortcut 3: ductus arteriosus. Pulmonary resistance is high, so most of this blood bypasses the lungs, crossing from the pulmonary trunk into the descending aorta. Back to the placenta: the two umbilical arteries (branches of the internal iliac arteries) carry blood back to be reoxygenated. Figure. Three fetal vascular shortcuts Ductus venosus Foramen ovale Ductus arteriosus Umbilical vein → IVC Partial liver bypass Right atrium → left atrium Pulmonary trunk → descending aorta Original route schematic; arrows show preferential fetal flow, not every stream or complete anatomical connection. Increased pulmonary flow after birth changes the pressure gradients that maintain these pathways. Shunt Connects Bypasses Ductus venosus Umbilical vein → inferior vena cava The liver Foramen ovale Right atrium → left atrium The lungs (via the right ventricle) Ductus arteriosus Pulmonary trunk → aorta The lungs Why does blood flow right-to-left through the shunts? Because, before birth, the right side has the higher pressure: pulmonary resistance is high, while the placenta keeps systemic resistance low. 3. Fetal haemoglobin: grabbing oxygen from the mother Fetal haemoglobin (HbF) holds oxygen more tightly than adult haemoglobin (HbA), because it binds the molecule 2,3-BPG less strongly. 2,3-BPG normally loosens haemoglobin's grip on oxygen. So, at the low oxygen levels in the placenta, fetal blood can pull oxygen across from the mother's blood. After birth, HbF is gradually replaced by HbA over several months. Understanding Check: Sections 1–3 1. Why is most blood diverted away from the fetal lungs? A. The fetal lungs are fluid-filled and have very high vascular resistance B. The fetal lungs already receive oxygen from amniotic fluid C. The fetal heart is too weak to pump blood through the lungs D. The lungs would take oxygen away from the placenta Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Gas exchange happens in the placenta. The fluid-filled lungs' constricted vessels resist flow, so blood takes the shunts. 2. Which shunt carries blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta? A. The ductus venosus B. The foramen ovale C. The ductus arteriosus D. The umbilical artery Show answer and explanation Answer: C. The ductus arteriosus links the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta, bypassing the lungs. 3. Why does fetal haemoglobin help the fetus take oxygen from the mother? A. It binds 2,3-BPG more strongly, so it releases oxygen easily B. It holds oxygen more tightly because it binds 2,3-BPG less strongly C. It carries carbon dioxide instead of oxygen D. It is found only in the placenta, not in fetal blood Show answer and explanation Answer: B. With less 2,3-BPG bound, HbF has a higher affinity for oxygen, so it loads oxygen from maternal blood. 4. What closes after delivery? Two events at birth flip the pressures: The first breaths: the lungs fill with air and oxygen rises, so the lung vessels dilate and pulmonary resistance falls sharply. Lung blood flow surges, and much more blood returns to the left atrium. Clamping the cord: the placenta, a low-resistance circuit, is cut off, so systemic resistance rises. The flow up the umbilical vein stops. At birth — two changes that redirect circulation First breaths Cord clamping Pulmonary resistance falls Placental circuit removed Lung flow and left atrial return increase Systemic resistance rises Umbilical venous flow stops These simultaneous changes shift atrial pressures, favor functional foramen ovale closure and contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure occurs later and varies by structure. Figure. Circulation after birth. Image credit BruceBlaus, Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Now the left side has the higher pressure, and each shunt closes: Shunt Why it closes Becomes (anatomical remnant) Foramen ovale Left atrial pressure now exceeds right, which presses the flap shut Fossa ovalis Ductus arteriosus Higher oxygen and less prostaglandin (from the placenta) make it constrict Ligamentum arteriosum Ductus venosus No more umbilical venous flow Ligamentum venosum Umbilical vein No more flow Ligamentum teres (of the liver) Umbilical arteries No more flow Medial umbilical ligaments Closure happens in two steps: functional closure within minutes to days, as flow stops; then anatomical closure over weeks to months, as tissue seals the channel. Understanding Check: Section 4 1. Why does the foramen ovale close at birth? A. Prostaglandin levels rise after the cord is clamped B. Left atrial pressure becomes higher than right atrial pressure C. The umbilical vein carries more blood after birth D. Pulmonary resistance rises when the lungs fill with air Show answer and explanation Answer: B. More blood returns from the lungs to the left atrium, and its pressure rises above the right, pressing the flap shut. 2. What makes the ductus arteriosus constrict after birth? A. Rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins B. Falling oxygen and rising prostaglandins C. A rise in pulmonary vascular resistance D. Increased flow through the umbilical arteries Show answer and explanation Answer: A. Oxygen constricts the ductus, and the prostaglandins that kept it open fall once the placenta is gone. 3. At birth, pulmonary resistance falls while systemic resistance rises. Why do both happen? A. Both are caused by the first breath alone B. Breathing dilates the lung vessels; cord clamping removes the low-resistance placenta C. Both are caused by clamping the cord alone D. The ductus arteriosus closing raises both resistances Show answer and explanation Answer: B. They are two separate events: lung aeration lowers pulmonary resistance, and losing the placenta raises systemic resistance. 5. Chapter Essentials The placenta does fetal gas exchange. The fluid-filled lungs have high resistance. Three shunts: ductus venosus (bypasses the liver), foramen ovale (right → left atrium), ductus arteriosus (pulmonary trunk → aorta). The best-oxygenated blood goes through the foramen ovale to the heart and brain. Before birth the right side has the higher pressure, so flow through the shunts is right-to-left. HbF binds oxygen more tightly (less 2,3-BPG binding), so it loads oxygen from the mother. At birth: the first breaths lower pulmonary resistance; cord clamping raises systemic resistance; left pressure > right. Remnants: fossa ovalis, ligamentum arteriosum, ligamentum venosum, ligamentum teres, medial umbilical ligaments."}];
